window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric A phase-by-phase guide for moving a Power BI Premium or Pro estate onto Microsoft Fabric. It separates the part that is mandatory and deadline-driven (moving capacity) from the part that creates lasting value (rationalizing, rebuilding and governing what runs on it), and gives you the decisions, templates and checks for both. Audience platfo"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#what-this-covers","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"What this playbook covers","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Most Power BI estates grew report by report: a PBIX file with its own Power Query logic, its own import copy of the data, and its own DAX. Fabric changes the unit of design. Data lands once in OneLake, is shaped by pipelines, notebooks and a warehouse, and is exposed through a small number of governed semantic models that many reports share. A migration is therefore two different jobs that happen to share a project p"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#when-to-use","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"When to use this playbook","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Use it when Use something else when You run Power BI Premium (P SKU) capacities, Premium Per User, or Pro workspaces and are adopting Fabric. You only need to move workspaces between P SKUs and F SKUs and rebuild nothing: the capacity runbook on the strategy page is enough. You want to retire import-heavy, per-report data preparation in favor of a lakehouse or warehouse foundation. Your data already lives in a well-m"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#executive-summary","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"Executive summary","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Each row is a position this playbook takes. The detail, the trade-offs and the failure modes are on the page in the right-hand column. Theme Position Page Strategic case Frame the move as a shift from isolated BI to a platform that manages the whole data lifecycle on OneLake, not as a license renewal. The same framing drives funding for the rebuild. Strategy Licensing and cost Map each P SKU to its F SKU equivalent, "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#execution-map","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"Execution map","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The work is organized into four phases. Phases overlap in practice, but each has an exit gate that should be met before the next phase consumes significant effort. Foundation and planning Decide, size, design, govern, assess. Implementation Environments, reverse-engineering, data layers, semantic layer, orchestration. Validation and go-live QA, UAT, deployment, parallel run, decommission. Change and support Communica"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#prerequisites","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"Prerequisites and outcomes","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#before-you-start","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"Before you start","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"An executive sponsor and a named business owner per domain. Azure subscription access to register the Microsoft.Fabric resource provider and create capacities. Fabric administrator access, or a service principal allowed to call the read-only admin APIs, for the inventory. The end date of your Premium agreement, confirmed with your Microsoft account team. A Git provider (GitHub or Azure DevOps) and a decision on who o"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#what-you-end-up-with","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"What you end up with","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Workloads running on an F SKU, with the P SKU cancelled. A signed rationalization matrix and a migration backlog. Governed workspaces, certified semantic models and thin reports. A repeatable CI/CD path from development to production. A support and optimization model owned by named people."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#page-map","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"Page map","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"02 Strategy, licensing and capacity Migration paths, F SKU rules, cost levers, throttling and the capacity runbook. 03 Architecture and environments Medallion, pattern decision matrix, workspace strategy and CI/CD foundation. 04 Governance with Fabric and Purview Catalog, domains, endorsement, labels, data loss prevention and audit. 05 Assessment and rationalization Inventory, usage, decision tree and the matrix temp"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#related-playbooks","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"Related playbooks","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"This playbook is the strategic spine. It summarizes and links to the detailed playbooks rather than repeating them. Data movement and layer design: Data Load Strategy , Medallion Architecture , Delta Lake Partitioning . Orchestration: Cross-Workspace Orchestration , Control-Table-Driven Pipelines . Lifecycle management: GitHub Integration , Deployment Pipelines , Variable Libraries , Deployment Checklist . Delivery a"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/index.html#refs","t":"Migrating from Power BI to Microsoft Fabric","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric migration overview Power BI Premium P SKU to Fabric F SKU migration decision guide Migrate workspaces from Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric Microsoft Fabric licenses What is Microsoft Fabric?"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Strategy, licensing and capacity migration Why the move is worth framing as a platform decision, the three paths an asset can take, how F SKU licensing and capacity behavior change the cost model, and the runbook for moving workspaces off Premium capacity without disruption. Phase I · Foundation Audience sponsors, platform leads, capacity owners Output capacity plan and migration path"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#strategic-case","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"The strategic case","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"A migration funded as \"the old SKU is going away\" produces a new invoice and an unchanged estate. A migration funded as a platform decision produces both. The case worth making rests on four changes, each of which you can later check against a measurable outcome. One platform for the data lifecycle. Ingestion, engineering, warehousing, science and BI share one SaaS surface, one capacity pool and one governance model."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#migration-paths","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Three migration paths, plus retire","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Every asset in the inventory takes exactly one of these paths. Deciding per asset, and recording the decision in the rationalization matrix , is what keeps the project from rebuilding everything or rebuilding nothing. Path What you do Effort Choose it when Reassign as is Move the workspace from the P SKU to an F SKU capacity. Reports, semantic models, dataflows, apps, schedules, deployment pipelines and Git integrati"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#licensing","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Licensing: Premium to F SKU","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#retirement","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"What is being retired, and what is not","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Power BI Premium per-capacity SKUs (P1 to P5) are being retired: Microsoft no longer sells new P SKUs, and each P subscription ends at the end of its current agreement term. The supported path for those workloads is a Fabric capacity (F SKU). Several things are explicitly out of scope of the retirement. Per-user licenses are unchanged. Power BI Pro and Premium Per User (PPU) continue. PPU does not provision a Fabric "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#sku-mapping","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"SKU mapping","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Capacity units (CU) are the common currency. Each P SKU v-core corresponds to 8 CU, which gives a one-to-one starting point. P SKU F SKU equivalent CU Power BI v-cores Typical fit P1 F64 64 8 Departmental and small-enterprise estates P2 F128 128 16 Distributed reporting across several teams P3 F256 256 32 High concurrency, large refresh windows P4 F512 512 64 Enterprise scale and heavy refresh volume P5 F1024 1,024 1"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#viewer-licensing","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"The F64 line: who needs which license","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"F64 is the smallest SKU on which a user with only a free Fabric license and the Viewer role can view Power BI content, which matches P SKU behavior. Below F64 every viewer needs Pro or PPU. That rule drives the single most consequential cost decision in the plan. Viewer licensing. The authoring rule does not change with capacity size. Only viewing does. To compare the options, put one formula in the business case. It"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#purchase-models","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Purchase models and cost levers","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Lever How it works Best for Watch out for Pay-as-you-go No commitment. Billed per second with a one-minute minimum; pricing is regional. Dev and test, pilots, spiky or unproven workloads. Left running around the clock it costs more than the equivalent P SKU did. Reservation Commit to a CU quantity for a term at a discount. It discounts capacity usage, not OneLake storage or networking. Capacities that run around the "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#capacity-behavior","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"How capacity behaves under load","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"A P SKU model is \"size for the peak.\" Fabric sizes for the average, and absorbs peaks through two mechanisms you need to understand before you size anything. Bursting lets an operation use more compute than the SKU provides while it runs, so work finishes fast. Smoothing spreads the CU consumed by that operation over future 30-second timepoints so the burst does not count as a spike. Interactive operations (report qu"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#surge-protection","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Guardrails: surge protection and isolation","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Capacity-level surge protection rejects new background operations when the capacity's 24-hour background percentage reaches a threshold you set, and resumes below a recovery threshold. It protects interactive users from background surges; it does not stop jobs already running. Workspace-level surge protection caps what one workspace may consume in a rolling 24-hour window, can block an offender automatically, and let"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#monitoring","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Monitoring and sizing tools","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Tool Use it for Fabric Capacity Metrics app Utilization, throttling charts, system events and per-operation drill-down. Install it before the migration and capture the baseline from your P capacities; it only holds a limited history, so export what you need. Fabric SKU Estimator Forward-looking sizing from a workload profile, to complement observed metrics. Capacity notifications and Real-Time hub capacity events Ale"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#capacity-strategy","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"How many capacities, and where","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Pattern When it fits Trade-off One shared capacity Small estate, single team, early pilot. A runaway notebook or refresh storm hits report users. Production and non-production The default for most organizations. Production is reserved; non-production is pay-as-you-go and paused when idle. Two bills to size and two capacity admin groups. By domain or business unit You need chargeback, different SLAs or independent adm"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#capacity-runbook","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Capacity migration runbook","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"This runbook follows Microsoft's guidance for same-region, same-tenant migration, which is the recommended default. The move happens per workspace; there is no tenant-wide switch. Safe sequence. Buy the F SKU first, move, validate, and only then cancel. Fabric does not decommission the P SKU for you; cancellation is a manual step in the Microsoft 365 admin center. Inventory workspaces and items Record workspace name "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#runbook-scripts","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Scripts for steps 1 and 4","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The inventory call is a starting point. For large tenants prefer the scanner (metadata scanning) API described on the assessment page , which returns item-level metadata in batches of up to 100 workspaces. The Bicep tab uses a generally available resource API version; the resource reference lists newer versions and optional properties such as overage settings."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#special-cases","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Cross-region, multigeo and cross-tenant","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Scenario Complexity What changes Same tenant, same region Low Recommended default. Reassign each workspace; the only downtime is any refresh running at that moment. Same tenant, different region Moderate to high Standard Power BI items survive. Large storage format semantic models and every Fabric item (lakehouses, warehouses, notebooks, pipelines, eventstreams, Dataflow Gen2 and their hidden staging items) do not: c"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#runbook-troubleshooting","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"Troubleshooting the move","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix Cannot reassign a workspace You are not an admin of the target capacity, or the resource provider is not registered. Add yourself as capacity admin; register Microsoft.Fabric . For F capacities the capacity admin must also be a workspace admin, unless you are a Fabric administrator. Region error on reassignment The workspace contains large storage format models or Fabric items. Follow the cro"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/strategy-licensing.html#refs","t":"Strategy, licensing and capacity migration","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric migration overview Migration decision guide (SKU mapping, right-sizing, timeline) Migrate workspaces from Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric Premium to Fabric migration FAQ Microsoft Fabric licenses Buy a Microsoft Fabric subscription Understand capacity throttling and smoothing Manage surge protection Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app Fabric SKU Estimator Pause and resume y"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"Target architecture and environments","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Target architecture and environments The layers your data moves through, the choice between an All-Lakehouse and a Hybrid pattern, how to cut workspaces so access and releases stay manageable, and the Git and deployment foundation you need before the first asset is rebuilt. Phase I and II · Foundation, Implementation Audience architects, platform leads Output architecture decision rec"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#medallion-layers","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"Medallion layers and where they live","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The medallion pattern organizes data into layers that progressively improve structure and quality. In Fabric the layers are a convention you implement with items and workspaces, not a product feature. This playbook uses five layer codes so that workspace and item names read the same way across the whole estate. Layer Code Contents Item types Written by Read by Bronze BRZ Source data as delivered: unaltered, append-on"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#pattern-decision","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"All-Lakehouse or Hybrid","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The only real architectural fork is the technology used to shape the Gold and Consumption layers. Bronze and Silver are lakehouses in both patterns. Pattern 1, All-Lakehouse. Gold is a lakehouse written by Spark. Consumers reach it through the lakehouse's SQL analytics endpoint (read-only T-SQL) or through a Direct Lake semantic model. Pattern 2, Hybrid. Gold-and-above is a Fabric Warehouse built with T-SQL (CTAS, IN"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#decision-matrix","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"Architectural pattern decision matrix","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Criterion All-Lakehouse (Spark) Hybrid (T-SQL Warehouse) Primary skills PySpark, Spark SQL, data engineering T-SQL, dimensional modeling, BI development Typical use Mixed BI and data science, large-scale ETL Enterprise BI, reporting, SQL-first analytics Transformation engine Spark notebooks and jobs Warehouse T-SQL: CTAS, INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, MERGE, stored procedures DML on the serving tables Delta MERGE , UPDATE , "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#decision-rules","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"How to decide","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Start with the people who will maintain the star schema. If they are BI developers fluent in T-SQL and migrating from import models, choose Hybrid. If they are Spark engineers who also serve data science, choose All-Lakehouse. Forcing an unfamiliar paradigm on the team is the main risk in either direction. Do not make it a religious choice. One domain can be Hybrid while another is All-Lakehouse. Both write Delta to "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#workspace-strategy","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"Workspace strategy","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Workspaces are the unit of access control, capacity assignment, Git connection and deployment. There are two common ways to cut them. Option A: one workspace per environment Option B: one workspace per layer per environment Shape DEV_SALES , TST_SALES , PRD_SALES hold every layer as items. DEV_SALES_BRZ through PRD_SALES_DLV : five layers by three environments. Strengths Few workspaces and pipelines; simple lineage; "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#naming","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"Naming convention","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Decide names once, enforce them in code review, and put them in the Git repository so the next team inherits them. Fabric item names are part of lineage, search and every error message. Object Pattern Examples Workspace <ENV>_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER>[_<Qualifier>] ; ENV is DEV, TST or PRD; LAYER is BRZ, SLV, GLD, CNSM or DLV DEV_SALES_BRZ , PRD_SALES_GLD , PRD_FINANCE_CNSM Item <ARTIFACT>_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER>_<Function> LH_SALES"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#domains-capacity-access","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"Domains, capacity and access","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Domains. Assign every workspace to a Fabric domain that matches the business area (Sales, Finance, HR). Domains give you federated ownership: domain admins can be delegated some governance settings, including who may certify items. See Governance . Capacity. Assign by environment first: production workspaces to a production capacity, DEV and TST to a non-production capacity that can be paused. Isolate heavy engineeri"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#environment-cicd","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"Environment and CI/CD foundation","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Do this before the first rebuilt asset. Retrofitting source control onto a team that has been publishing PBIX files from desktops is harder than starting with it, and a team that bypasses Git undermines every deployment guarantee downstream. Release path. Developers work in branch workspaces and merge by pull request; only DEV tracks main . TST and PRD are populated by a deployment pipeline or an automation workflow,"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#release-options","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"Three ways to move content between stages","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Mechanism How it works Choose when Watch out for Deployment pipelines Fabric-native stages with comparison, selective promotion and rules. BI-heavy teams who want a visual release process. Rules cover only dataflows Gen1, semantic models, paginated reports, mirrored databases and notebook default lakehouse. Everything else needs variables. Code-first from Git (API or fabric-cicd) A workflow publishes item definitions"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#cicd-example","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"Example: GitHub Actions with fabric-cicd","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"This sketch publishes one workspace folder to TST on merge and to PRD after an environment approval. The repository layout ( workspaces/SALES_SLV ) and secrets are placeholders. Verify against Microsoft Learn Git integration supports a specific list of item types, and individual types move between preview and general availability. Check Overview of Fabric Git integration for the current list and the tenant settings i"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/architecture.html#refs","t":"Target architecture and environments","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Implement medallion lakehouse architecture in Fabric T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse Transactions in Fabric Data Warehouse Direct Lake overview Overview of Fabric Git integration Development process using a branch workspace Create deployment rules Automate deployment pipelines with APIs and DevOps Governance and compliance in Microsoft Fabric Choose a Microsoft Fabric deployment pattern fabric-cicd docume"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Governance with Fabric and Purview A single copy of data in OneLake is read by Spark, SQL and the Power BI engine. Controls therefore have to be defined once and applied consistently. This page sets out a govern-first baseline: who decides, what Fabric provides natively, what Microsoft Purview adds, and how trust is earned through endorsement. Phase I · Foundation Audience governance "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#govern-first","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Why govern first","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"In a Power BI estate each PBIX file was its own island, so governance meant policing sharing. In Fabric the same table is reachable through several engines, workspaces are created by many people, and semantic models fan out to hundreds of reports. Retrofitting controls after teams have built habits means renegotiating with every one of them. A small baseline put in place before the first production workload costs far"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#roles","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Step 1: Define objectives and form the governance team","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Before configuring any technology, write down what governance must achieve in terms the business recognizes: protect sensitive data, make trusted data easy to find, keep cost visible, and be able to answer \"who changed or viewed what.\" Map those to your actual regulatory and contractual obligations; do not copy a compliance statement from a template. Role Owns Executive sponsor Mandate, funding, escalation for policy"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#tenant-baseline","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Step 2: Set the tenant-setting baseline","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Tenant settings decide who can create workspaces and Fabric items, share, export, publish externally, use service principals, apply labels and certify content. Decide them as a set, record the reason for each, and delegate to domains or capacities only where you have named owners. The full reference, with recommended values and the reasoning, is in the Tenant Settings Reference . Who may create workspaces, and who ma"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#domains","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Step 3: Organize with domains and workspaces","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Domains group workspaces by business area and are the basis for federated governance. Create one per business area that has an owner, map every workspace to its domain, and let domain administrators manage their contributors. This is how federated governance is realized in practice. A domain with no owner is a signal to merge it, not to leave it. Use the workspace naming convention from Architecture so that the domai"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#labels-dlp","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Step 4: Classify and protect with labels and data loss prevention","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Define a small label taxonomy with the security team: typically four levels from public to highly confidential. Keep it small enough that people choose correctly. Then decide what each label does, because a label with no consequences is only decoration. Control What it does Apply it Sensitivity labels Classify items and, where configured, encrypt and restrict export. Set on Fabric items. With inheritance enabled, lab"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#endorsement","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Step 5: Earn trust with endorsement and certification","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Endorsement is how consumers tell the difference between a one-off and the model the finance team stands behind. Use the levels deliberately. Level Who can set it Meaning Promoted Any user with write permission on the item. \"Ready for others to use.\" A signal from the owner, not a quality guarantee. Certified Only designated reviewers; can be delegated to domain administrators. \"Reviewed against our criteria and auth"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#access","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Step 6: Access control, in the right place","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Apply each control at the layer designed for it. A common failure is to rely on a report filter for confidentiality or to give every consumer workspace access to raw layers. Need Mechanism Notes Who can work in a workspace Workspace roles (Admin, Member, Contributor, Viewer) via security groups Grant by layer; consumers do not need workspace access if they use an app. Who can use a specific item Item permissions: rea"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#monitor","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Step 7: Monitor the governance posture","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The OneLake catalog's Govern experience gives domain and tenant owners insights into label coverage, endorsement coverage, item curation and recommended actions without leaving Fabric. Purview adds the audit trail and policy reports. Track a short list of measures and review them on a fixed cadence. Measure Why it matters Source Share of items with a sensitivity label Unlabeled items are unprotected by policy. OneLak"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#governance-pitfalls","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"Common failure modes","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Symptom Cause Fix Certified models proliferate and nobody trusts the badge No published criteria or reviewer independence. Publish the checklist; require a reviewer who did not build it; recertify on material change. Everyone creates workspaces on a production capacity Default create-workspace permissions. Restrict to named groups; provision through a request process; assign by domain. DLP blocks legitimate work at l"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/governance.html#refs","t":"Governance with Fabric and Purview","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Use Microsoft Purview with Microsoft Fabric Governance and compliance in Microsoft Fabric Govern your Fabric data with the OneLake catalog Endorsement overview Information protection in Microsoft Fabric Configure data loss prevention policies for Fabric Track user activities in Microsoft Fabric Connect to and manage a Power BI tenant in Microsoft Purview Integrate Direct Lake security"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Legacy assessment and rationalization Before anything is rebuilt, know what exists, who uses it and what it costs to keep. This page shows how to automate the inventory, work around the short retention of activity data, decide per asset with an explicit tree, and capture the result in a matrix that business and IT sign. Phase I · Foundation Audience program managers, BI leads, busines"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#why","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Why this step is mandatory","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The most common migration mistake is to migrate every asset. It carries forward years of duplicated reports, abandoned models and logic no one can explain, and it makes the project several times larger than it needs to be. A data-driven rationalization is the cheapest scope reduction available, and it also gives the sponsor an auditable answer to \"why did you retire my report?\" Treat the inventory as a small data pro"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#inventory","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Step 1: Build the inventory automatically","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"No single source tells you everything. Combine four, each with its own strengths and limits. Source Gives you Limits to plan for Scanner (metadata scanning) API Workspaces, reports, semantic models, dataflows, dashboards; lineage; data sources; optionally table, column, measure and expression detail and item users. Up to 100 workspaces per request; a scan is requested, polled, then read; results are available for a l"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#retention","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"The retention trap","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"A common plan is to review report views over 90 to 120 days. The activity events API cannot supply that: it keeps roughly four weeks, and the built-in usage and adoption reports in the admin monitoring workspace reflect a rolling 30-day window. A periodic report (a quarterly pack, a year-end report) can look unused for the whole window and still be critical. Start the export on day one. The only way to build a 90-day"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#inventory-scripts","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Collection scripts","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"For an exact-date inventory snapshot, store the scan results as dated partitions in your lake. The scanner returns current state, so only your own history shows what changed."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#analysis","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Step 2: Usage and performance analysis","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Views are the headline signal, but not the only one. Combine them with what the scan and capacity data tell you. Signal What it tells you Blind spot Views and distinct viewers in the window Real demand. Many views from one person is a different story than many viewers. Misses seasonal reports and consumers who use apps, subscriptions, embedded views or Analyze in Excel. Last viewed, last modified, last refreshed Dorm"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#decision-tree","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Step 3: Decide with an explicit tree","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Four gates, five outcomes. This tree expands the per-asset decision from the strategy page : it adds the consolidation step and the deferral that keeps low-value, messy assets from consuming the rebuild budget."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#stakeholders","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Step 4: Validate with stakeholders","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Usage data is a strong argument, not the final one. For each business area hold a short working session with the report owners and a few heavy consumers. Agenda. Review the proposed outcome for each asset; surface low-use assets that are critical; agree the owner for every survivor; agree the decommissioning window and how users will be told. Outputs. Corrected decisions, an exception register of protected dormant as"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#matrix","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Step 5: The Report Inventory and Rationalization Matrix","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The matrix is the single source of truth for scope. Business and IT leadership sign it, and later changes go through a change log. The rows below are fictional examples; owner and date cells are placeholders. Report Workspace Business area Business owner Technical owner Last accessed Views in window Data sources Complexity Decision Priority Notes Quarterly Sales Summary Sales Analytics Sales Owner A Developer B <date"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#matrix-definitions","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Column and value definitions","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Field Definition and source Report, workspace From the scanner API. Use item IDs as the key; names change. Business area, owners From workshops and the domain list. Name a person or group, never a team alias that nobody monitors. Last accessed, views in window From your accumulated activity export or the audit log. State the window length in the sheet header. Data sources From scanner data-source details. Flag gatewa"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#assessment-pitfalls","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"Failure modes","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Symptom Cause Fix A retired report resurfaces as an escalation Window shorter than the reporting cycle; no owner confirmation. Extend the window or use the audit log; require owner confirmation; keep an exception register. Scope grows during the build Matrix signed without owners, or \"Defer\" used as a default. Sign with owners; allow one deferral with a trigger; change-control additions. Inventory counts do not match"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/assessment.html#refs","t":"Legacy assessment and rationalization","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Metadata scanning overview Run metadata scanning Set up metadata scanning in an organization Access the Power BI activity log Admin: Get Activity Events (REST) Admin: WorkspaceInfo PostWorkspaceInfo (REST) Manage audit log retention policies Track user activities in Microsoft Fabric Feature usage and adoption report Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Reverse-engineering legacy assets When the people who knew the logic have moved on, the logic still lives in the files. This page shows how to extract it systematically, in a form a developer can rebuild from and a tester can reconcile against, without copying screens or guessing from visuals. Phase II · Build Audience BI developers, analytics engineers, testers Output specification p"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#goal","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Extract intent, not appearance","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The best rebuild starts from current business requirements and a subject-matter expert who can say what a number should mean. That is a rebuild, not a lift and shift, and it is the preferred route whenever the expert exists. Reverse-engineering is the safety net: it is how you recover the rules when the expert does not exist, and how you cross-check the rules when they do. Looking at the visuals and recreating them i"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#toolbox","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"The toolbox and three routes","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Three routes reach the same artifacts. Choose per asset based on where the file lives and what the workspace allows. Three routes, one package. Route A needs the file and Power BI Desktop. Route B needs a workspace on a capacity with the XMLA endpoint available. Route C works on an open file or a connected model. Mix them: most estates use all three. Tool Use it for Notes Power BI Desktop project (PBIP) Saving a PBIX"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#layers","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Where the logic hides","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Business logic is spread across four layers. A complete extraction visits each one, because a rule moved from one layer to another during a past refactor is a classic source of numbers that nearly match. Four layers to visit. The first three are extractable by tool. The fourth needs usage data and a conversation: a measure nobody queries from a report may still feed a workbook."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#get-files","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Step 1: Get the definition","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Start by obtaining a faithful copy of each prioritized asset. Several paths exist, each with restrictions that decide which you can use. Path How Restrictions to check Local file Open the PBIX in Power BI Desktop; Save as a project (PBIP). You need the file. If the original is lost, download it from the service. Download from the service File, Download this file, as a report or from a semantic model. Needs at least C"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#extract-model","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Step 2: Extract the model","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The model is where most of the logic lives. Extract it to files you can diff and search, not to a document you retype. Pick one script style per team so outputs are uniform. Verify against Microsoft Learn Semantic Link function signatures are in the sempy.fabric reference ; PBIR file layout and JSON schemas are in Power BI Desktop project report folder . The Tabular Editor object names come from its own scripting doc"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#extract-queries","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Step 3: Extract Power Query logic","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Copying M from the Advanced Editor query by query is the slow way, and it is easy to miss shared functions and parameters. The M code is part of the model definition: it sits in the table partitions and shared expressions of the TMDL files, and the Tabular Editor and Semantic Link scripts above export it. For triage across the tenant, the scanner API can return dataset expressions when the detailed-metadata admin set"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#extract-visuals","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Step 4: Extract report logic and visual queries","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Report-level logic is easy to overlook because it feels like formatting. It is not: a visual-level filter that hides a status, a page filter that restricts the year, or a report-level measure defined only in the report all change the numbers. Inventory the visuals from files. Run the PBIR script above. You get every page, visual type and bound field in one table, which replaces image captures as the record of what th"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#model-health","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Step 5: Assess model health","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"While you hold the model open, measure it. Model health tells you what to fix rather than carry forward. Vertipaq Analyzer. Table and column sizes, cardinality, encoding and relationship cost. In DAX Studio it is the View Metrics feature. High-cardinality text columns and unused columns are the usual wins, and the figures feed capacity sizing and Direct Lake planning. Best Practice Analyzer (BPA). A rule-based scan o"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#baselines","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Step 6: Capture golden baselines","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Before you rebuild, record what the legacy asset says. A baseline is the result of a defined query under a defined filter context, saved together with a note of which model version and refresh it came from. When the rebuild is ready, replay the same queries and compare. This turns \"the numbers look right\" into an evidence trail, and it feeds the reconciliation checklist . Baselines close the loop. The comparison is c"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#spec-package","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Step 7: Assemble the specification package","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The output of this phase is a specification package per asset: a folder in the repository, not a Word document. It carries the extracted files plus the decisions someone made about them. Artifact Content Produced by Model definition TMDL folder or TMSL file, kept as the archive copy. PBIP, REST definition or get_tmsl Measure catalog Every measure with expression, format, folder, and a plain-language purpose written b"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#re-pitfalls","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"Failure modes","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Symptom Cause Fix The rebuilt number is off by a stable percentage A hidden filter in a query, a page filter or a role. Diff the query catalog and visual inventory against the new model; replay the baseline with and without each filter. Published model cannot be downloaded Incremental refresh, Direct Lake, automatic aggregations or a migrated model. Use the model definition API or XMLA; download only the report in li"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/reverse-engineering.html#refs","t":"Reverse-engineering legacy assets","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Power BI Desktop projects (PBIP) Power BI Desktop project report folder (PBIR) Work with TMDL view Work with DAX query view External tools in Power BI Desktop Download a report from the Power BI service to Power BI Desktop Items: Get Semantic Model Definition (REST) sempy.fabric package reference Explore and validate relationships with semantic link Use notebooks with a semantic model"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html","t":"Build the data layers","s":"Build the data layers","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Build the data layers The legacy estate did its ingestion and transformation inside Power Query and Dataflow Gen1. The new estate does it in a medallion architecture with a deliberate tool at each layer. This page maps old constructs to new ones, summarizes each layer for a migration, and covers dataflow options and orchestration. The deep dives live in the data engineering playbooks."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#what-replaces-what","t":"Build the data layers","s":"What replaces what","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"In a Power BI estate, one tool did several jobs: it connected to sources, cleansed data, applied business rules, joined tables, aggregated, and refreshed on a schedule. A migration separates those jobs and assigns each to the layer best suited to it. Use the query catalog and rebuild notes to decide where each rule lands. Unbundling the legacy tool. Anything that was done per report in M or DAX and is used by more th"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#tech-choice","t":"Build the data layers","s":"One tool per layer","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The recommended stack for a Power BI migration follows a clear progression: pipelines to ingest, Spark to conform, and a star schema served from the Warehouse. The choice of Gold technology follows the pattern decision : in the All-Lakehouse pattern, Spark builds Gold too. Layer Primary tool Also consider Why Bronze Data Factory pipelines with the Copy activity, landing Delta tables in a lakehouse. Copy job for simpl"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#bronze","t":"Build the data layers","s":"Bronze: ingest with fidelity","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The Bronze layer ingests source data as it is, reliably and repeatably. Its job is to be a trustworthy copy that you can reprocess from, not a place for business rules. Land as Delta. Delta gives ACID transactions, schema enforcement and time travel, so the raw layer is auditable. Keep the source column names and types except where the format forces a change. Add audit columns. Load identifier, source system and inge"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#silver","t":"Build the data layers","s":"Silver: conform and enforce quality","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Silver turns raw data into clean, conformed, business-ready tables. This is where the rules recovered from the legacy queries are applied, tested and made reusable. Apache Spark, run from notebooks, is the strongest tool for it: cleansing nulls and types, parsing strings, joining systems (for example CRM and ERP), applying rules that SQL expresses poorly, and quarantining records that fail checks. A pattern worth sta"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#gold","t":"Build the data layers","s":"Gold: model the star schema","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Gold takes conformed Silver data and builds the business-friendly star schema: central fact tables and surrounding dimensions. In the recommended Hybrid pattern the Gold layer lives in a Fabric Warehouse and is built with T-SQL. Use CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) for initial loads and aggregates, T-SQL MERGE for upserts, and stored procedures to keep each load modular and reusable. MERGE is a T-SQL feature in the Ware"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#scd2","t":"Build the data layers","s":"Slowly changing dimensions","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Type 2 dimensions preserve history, which matters for accurate historical reporting. The two-step pattern below closes changed rows and inserts new versions in one transaction. It is easy to read and test, and the null-safe comparison avoids the classic bug where a change to or from a null goes unnoticed. A single MERGE can implement the same pattern when the source is arranged to produce both the closing and the ins"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#dataflows","t":"Build the data layers","s":"What to do with Dataflow Gen1","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Dataflow Gen1 is in a legacy state: it keeps running but receives no new feature investment. For workspaces on a Fabric capacity, Dataflow Gen2 is the recommended successor. Gen2 requires a Fabric capacity or trial, so workspaces that stay on Pro or Premium Per User licensing can continue with Gen1 while you plan the move. Dataflow Gen1 decision flow. Shared staging dataflows are the best candidates to replace with t"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#orchestration","t":"Build the data layers","s":"Orchestrate the end-to-end flow","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"A single monolithic pipeline that chains every task is brittle and hard to change. The pattern that scales is a metadata-driven hub and spoke: a master pipeline (the hub) holds no data logic, and reads a control table that lists the modular child pipelines (the spokes), their order, dependencies and parameters. Adding a new source means adding spokes and rows, not editing the hub. Hub, spokes and a control table. Dep"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#control-table","t":"Build the data layers","s":"Control table and dependency management","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The control table is the plan and the lock. A minimal shape is below; the full pattern, including asset discovery and parameters, is covered in Control-table-driven pipelines and Cross-workspace orchestration . The hub begins a run by resetting statuses, then loops: select enabled steps that are Pending and whose parent is Succeeded, set them to Running, invoke the child pipeline, and set the outcome. A failed step b"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#schedule-alerts","t":"Build the data layers","s":"Schedule and alert","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Schedule the hub at the cadence the business needs, using the built-in scheduler. Align it with the refresh windows agreed during communication planning . Failure notifications on the schedule. Open the pipeline, choose Schedule, and add users or groups under Failure notifications. This is the simplest alert and covers scheduled runs. Activity-level alerts. Add an Office 365 Outlook or Teams activity on the failure p"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#build-order","t":"Build the data layers","s":"Sequencing the build","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Building every Bronze table, then every Silver table, then every Gold table delays the first thing a business owner can look at by months. A safer order is a vertical slice: take one high-value subject area from the matrix, build it end to end through a semantic model and report, reconcile it, and only then repeat. The first slice settles naming, folder layout, shared code, alerting and the reconciliation method; lat"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/build-data.html#refs","t":"Build the data layers","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Implement medallion lakehouse architecture in Fabric Partitioning for Delta tables Liquid clustering Warehouse performance guidelines Migrate from Dataflow Gen1 to Dataflow Gen2 Migrate to Dataflow Gen2 (CI/CD) using Save As Upgrade Dataflow Gen1 to Dataflow Gen2 using the Upgrade Wizard Dataflow migration scenarios Create alerts for pipeline runs Teams activity Office 365 Outlook activity"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"Semantic models and reports","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Semantic models and reports With a governed Gold layer in place, the last build step is what users actually touch. This page covers the decoupled model-and-report architecture, how to choose a storage mode (including Direct Lake and its fallback), how to rebuild and secure the model, and how to deliver thin reports, by rebinding or rebuilding. Phase II · Build Audience BI developers, "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#design","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"One model, many thin reports","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"A migration is the best chance you will get to enforce a decoupled architecture. Each domain has a small number of certified semantic models, owned by a named team. Reports contain only visuals and connect live to those models. Excel, paginated reports and apps use the same models. The result is one definition of every measure, no silent forks of the business logic, and reports that can be rebuilt or retired without "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#storage-modes","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"Choose the storage mode","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Storage mode is a property of each table in a semantic model. The choice drives refresh cost, latency, security design and what the model can contain. Four options matter in a migration. Mode How it serves queries Strengths Limits to plan for Import Data is copied into the model and queried in memory. Any connector; Power Query inside the model; works for self-service analysts who cannot change the source; any licens"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#fallback","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"Fallback and guardrails","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The two Direct Lake options behave differently at their limits, and this is the most common surprise in testing. Know what happens at the limit. On the SQL variant the behavior is controlled by the model's Direct Lake behavior setting; disable fallback in testing so that a silent fallback shows up as a failed query instead of a slow report. Guardrails depend on the capacity SKU. Most are evaluated per query, and the "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#rebuild-model","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"Rebuild the semantic model","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Use the specification package from reverse-engineering as the blueprint. The rebuild is not a copy-and-paste exercise: every measure is a chance to simplify, rename consistently and document what it means. Create the model on the Gold layer. Connect to the Gold tables as Direct Lake, or as Import where the decision above says so. Name the model by convention, for example SM_SALES_CNSM_Orders . Build the star schema. "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#security","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"Secure the model","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Row-level security (RLS) and object-level security (OLS) are defined in the semantic model from the security specification you extracted. Where the legacy estate relied on security at the source, decide explicitly where each rule will live now, because the Direct Lake options treat SQL-level security differently. Where the rule lives Direct Lake on OneLake Direct Lake on SQL endpoint Import Semantic model RLS and OLS"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#certify","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"Publish and certify","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Publish the model to the central consumption workspace and promote it through the release path in environments and CI/CD . Once validated, endorse it as Certified so that everyone can find the authoritative version. The flow, the tenant settings and who may certify are in governance . The model has a named owner and a contact in its description. Measures have descriptions and consistent formats; hidden keys and techn"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#thin-reports","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"Build thin reports","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Thin reports are report-only items that connect live to the certified model. Recreate the pages, visuals and interactions from the visual inventory, and apply the organizational theme and templates. Save reports as Power BI projects in PBIR format so that pages and visuals are separate, reviewable files, and commit them to Git. Work from the inventory, not from memory. The visual inventory lists what each page contai"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#rebind","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"Rebind existing reports instead of rebuilding","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"When the matrix says Rebind, the report is kept and pointed at a new model. This is the fastest way to move a sound report to the new platform, provided the new model has the same table and field names the report uses. Test one report manually first; field differences show up as broken visuals. Permissions. The caller needs Write permission on the report and Build permission on the target model. Cross-workspace. If t"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#delivery-pitfalls","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"Failure modes","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Symptom Cause Fix Reports are slow and the SQL endpoint is busy Direct Lake on SQL fell back to DirectQuery (SQL views, SQL-level RLS, guardrails). Check the Direct Lake behavior setting; replace views with tables; compact the Delta tables; move to Direct Lake on OneLake where acceptable. Model refresh fails after a data load Guardrail exceeded on Direct Lake on OneLake, or duplicate values in a relationship's one-si"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/semantic-delivery.html#refs","t":"Semantic models and reports","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Direct Lake overview How Direct Lake works Integrate Direct Lake security Specify a fixed identity for a Direct Lake model Cross-workload table maintenance and optimization Work with TMDL view Power BI Desktop project report folder Reports: Rebind Report In Group (REST) Endorsement overview"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"Validate, deploy and go live","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Validate, deploy and go live This is the gate between development and business value. It proves the new estate is accurate, fast and secure; wins the business's written acceptance; releases through the same path every time; runs old and new side by side; and then retires the legacy estate in a way that can be undone until the last step. Phase III · Validate and release Audience QA eng"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#gates","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"The release path and its gates","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Treat the path from build to retirement as a sequence of gates. Each gate has an approver and a recorded decision, and nothing passes because a date arrived. Four gates, four approvers. The QA gate belongs to the technical team, the sign-off gate to the business, the go-live gate to the release manager, and the retirement gate to the sponsor and platform owner."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#qa","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"Quality assurance","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Before any asset reaches a business user, the technical team validates it on four pillars: data accuracy, functional correctness, performance and security. Record the result per asset on a checklist, so the matrix row can show \"QA passed\" with evidence."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#reconciliation","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"Data reconciliation","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Reconciliation is the most important and most time-consuming test. It shows that the numbers in the new estate equal the numbers in the legacy estate for the same business question. A difference in a key metric can come from anywhere in the chain: ingestion, a Silver rule, a Gold aggregate, or the DAX measure. So reconcile in layers, from the cheapest broad check to the most specific one, and stop at the first layer "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#functional-perf-sec","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"Functional, performance and security testing","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Pillar What to test How Functional Every filter, slicer, button, bookmark, drillthrough and tooltip page behaves as the specification says. Walk the visual inventory page by page; record pass or fail per interaction. Performance Each visual loads in an acceptable time under realistic data volume and concurrency. Record with Performance Analyzer; copy the slowest visual queries to DAX Studio and compare time in the Fo"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#uat","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"User acceptance testing","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"UAT is where business users confirm the solution fits their work. It is as much about building confidence as finding defects, and it produces the business sign-off that unlocks production. Representative testers. Choose them with business leaders: power users who will scrutinize details, and occasional consumers who judge clarity and usability. Environment. The TST workspaces, loaded with production-like volumes so p"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#deploy","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"Production deployment","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Production deployment should be the execution of a rehearsed process, not an event. Rehearse it at least once into TST, with the same pipeline, rules and variables that production will use. The release mechanisms, Git workflow and automation options are described in environments and CI/CD ; the dedicated playbooks cover deployment , variable libraries , and the deployment checklist . Compare and promote. In the deplo"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#go-live","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"Go-live and parallel run","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Go-live starts with the communication plan: a formal announcement with direct links to the new apps, the training on offer and the support route. Then run the new and legacy estates in parallel for a defined period, typically two to four weeks. Why parallel. It gives users a safety net while they adapt, and gives the team a last window to reconcile on live production data. Hypercare. Heightened support availability d"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#decommission","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"Decommission the legacy estate","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Retiring the old estate is the step most often skipped, and skipping it leaves two sources of truth, user confusion, wasted capacity and ongoing technical debt. Use a protocol whose first steps are fully reversible and whose last step is not. Make the irreversible step the last one. The grace period turns a surprise into a reversible event: a user who objects finds a retired report, not a deleted one. Confirm there i"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#release-pitfalls","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"Failure modes","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Symptom Cause Fix UAT sign-off keeps slipping Vague scripts, no named approver, no severity rules. Scripts per scenario; one tracker; severity table agreed up front. Numbers match in test and differ in production Rules or variables not applied on deploy; different data volume. Verify deployment rules and variables after promotion; rerun reconciliation in PRD. Reports are fast in test and slow with real users No concu"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/validate-release.html#refs","t":"Validate, deploy and go live","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Analyze query processing for Direct Lake semantic models Work with DAX query view External tools in Power BI Desktop Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app Introduction to deployment pipelines Set up and manage workspace retention Retention and recovery in Fabric Download a report from the Power BI service to Power BI Desktop"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Change, support and project plan","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Playbook 01 · Power BI to Fabric Change, support and project plan A migration succeeds when people use the new platform and trust it, not when the last pipeline runs. This page covers communication and training, the support and optimization model that follows go-live, and a project plan template you can adapt: a phased task table and a timeline in relative weeks, with no calendar dates. Phase IV · Adopt and support, "},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#change","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Change management","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"People adopt a platform when they understand why it is changing, see the benefit for their own work, and feel supported while they learn. Plan this from the start of the project, not from the week before go-live. The plan has two halves: a communication plan that says what to tell whom, and a role-based training curriculum that gives each group what it needs."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#comms","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Communication plan","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Identify every audience, from executive sponsors to occasional consumers, and define for each the key messages, channels and cadence across the four phases of the program. Phase Audience Key messages Channels Cadence Before migration Sponsors, business owners, all users Why the change, the benefits (faster insight, more reliable data, new capabilities), the rough timeline and what is expected of users. Sponsor email,"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#training","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Role-based training","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"One course for everyone does not work. Tailor it to the persona and deliver it through a mix of live workshops, on-demand material and hands-on labs, both before and after go-live. Persona What they need Format Report consumers (largest group) Find and open the new apps; use filters and slicers; subscribe to reports and export data; know where to ask for help. Quick-start guide, short on-demand videos, a recorded wal"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#support","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Post-migration support and optimization","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Go-live is the start of the platform's operational life, not the end of the project. Move from a project mindset to a product mindset: the analytics platform has an owner, a roadmap, a backlog and a release rhythm. The operating loop. Support tickets and metrics feed one backlog; changes ship through the same controlled path as the original build; and each release is announced through the community channel."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#channels","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Support channels","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"A help desk or ticketing system for logging and tracking issues, with categories that match the platform (access, data question, defect, change request) and target response times. Office hours with the BI team, scheduled and published, where users get live help. A community of practice in a dedicated Teams channel or Viva Engage community, for peer help, announcements and sharing. Escalation paths to the platform own"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#monitor","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Monitoring adoption, performance and cost","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"User adoption. Active users, report views, the share of views on the new estate versus any legacy remainder, and self-service content created. Use the activity events you started exporting during the assessment, the audit log, and the built-in usage reports. Low adoption points to a training or promotion gap. Capacity and performance. Use the Capacity Metrics app to watch utilization, smoothing, throttling and the it"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#improvement","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Continuous improvement and retrospective","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Keep one prioritized backlog fed by user feedback, tickets and monitoring, and work through it with the same agile and CI/CD practices you used to build the platform. Hold a project retrospective with the core team and key stakeholders: what went well, what to change, and the lessons that apply to every future data initiative. Record them in the same repository as the specification packages, so the next project start"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#project-plan","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Project plan template","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The template below is a starting point, not a schedule. Tasks marked \"per area\" repeat for each business area or wave; \"once\" tasks happen once for the whole program; \"case by case\" tasks depend on the area. It allows for multiple consumption layers after Gold, as a multi-domain implementation needs."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#waves","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Plan in waves, not in one pass","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"A single pass (every extract, then every build, then every test) puts the first thing a business owner can see far into the program. Instead, group the signed-off matrix into waves by business area and value. Wave 1 is a pilot that goes through the whole chain and settles naming, shared code, alerting, the reconciliation method and the release path. Later waves reuse all of it and overlap. Overlapping waves. Each wav"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#timeline","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Timeline in relative weeks","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"The chart shows one plausible shape for a mid-sized program, in weeks from project start. Use it to discuss dependencies, then replace the numbers with an estimate based on your matrix: the number of waves, the complexity of the rebuild assets, and the capacity of the team. Nothing here is a benchmark. One plausible shape, in relative weeks. Build, test and deploy bars repeat per wave and overlap. The dashed lines ma"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#plan-phase-1","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Phase I · Foundation and strategic planning","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Task Repeat Key activities Deliverable Weeks Project initiation and stakeholder alignment Once Define objectives, scope and success metrics. Identify and engage executive sponsors, business leads, IT and data teams. Form the team and define roles. Project charter 1 to 2 Licensing, cost and capacity Once Analyze licensing, cost structure and user-access model changes. Confirm renewal and retirement terms for any Premi"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#plan-phase-2","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Phase II · Data engineering, semantic and delivery build","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Task Repeat Key activities Deliverable Weeks Environment setup Per area Provision DEV, TST and PRD workspaces; create the repository and template; connect Git; add automated test hooks; create the Bronze, Silver and Gold lakehouses and warehouses; configure Fabric environments. Configured environments and pipelines 7 to 10 Reverse-engineering and data mapping Per area Arrange access to workspaces, documentation and e"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#plan-phase-3","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Phase III · Testing, deployment and go-live","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Task Repeat Key activities Deliverable Weeks Quality assurance Per area Reconcile data between legacy and new; test every interaction; test performance and Direct Lake behavior; verify RLS and OLS as a viewer. QA results and completed reconciliation checklist 22 to 38 User acceptance testing Per area Prepare the UAT environment and testers; write scripts and scenarios; run UAT and log defects; triage and resolve crit"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#plan-phase-4","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Phase IV · Change management and ongoing support","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Task Repeat Key activities Deliverable Weeks Communication plan Case by case Develop the strategy (messages, channels, cadence per audience) and execute it through the project: announcements, progress updates, go-live information. Communication plan and executed communications 1 to 48 User training and enablement Case by case Build a role-based curriculum; create quick-start guides, videos and documentation; schedule"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#roles","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Roles and ownership","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Role Owns Executive sponsor Objectives, funding, the retirement gate, escalations. Program manager Plan, waves, risks, status, the release calendar in relative weeks. Solution architect Pattern, naming, workspace strategy, standards. Data engineers Bronze, Silver, Gold, orchestration, reconciliation of the pipeline. BI developers Semantic models, reports, security specification, baselines. Fabric administrator Capaci"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#checklists","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Phase exit checklists and success measures","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Use these as the entry criteria for the next phase. If an item cannot be ticked, the phase is not finished."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#exit-1","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Phase I exit","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Capacity is in place and the reassignment runbook has been rehearsed on a non-critical workspace. Architecture, naming, branching and governance decisions are recorded and approved. The rationalization matrix is signed by business and IT leadership; the wave plan exists. A daily activity export is running and the exception register is open."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#exit-2","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Phase II exit (per wave)","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Every rebuilt asset has a specification package and baselines. Bronze, Silver and Gold tables trace to the data-mapping document; rejected rows are quarantined and reviewed. Models are certified candidates, with tested security and a recorded storage-mode decision. The hub pipeline runs the wave end to end and alerts reach a monitored destination."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#exit-3","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Phase III exit","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Reconciliation is within tolerance in production; no Critical or unaccepted High defects. Written business sign-off exists for each wave. The parallel run met its exit criteria; adoption is confirmed from usage data. Legacy content is archived, then deleted, and the matrix reflects the outcome."},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#exit-4","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Phase IV exit","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Support channels, owners and response targets are published and in use. Adoption, performance and cost reports run on a regular cadence. A backlog and release rhythm exist; the retrospective is held and its lessons are filed. Dimension Measure Where it comes from Adoption Active users and views on the new estate; share of legacy views remaining. Activity events and usage reports Quality Reconciliation pass rate; defe"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#principles","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"Principles to hold throughout","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Strategic foundation first. Licensing and cost analysis, a deliberate architecture pattern based on team skills, and governance are prerequisites, not parallel work. Rationalize, do not just migrate. A data-driven decision per asset is the cheapest scope reduction and the best risk control. Embrace engineering discipline. Source control and release pipelines make the platform maintainable, and the team needs training"},{"u":"playbooks/01-powerbi-to-fabric-migration/adoption-plan.html#refs","t":"Change, support and project plan","s":"References","p":"Power BI to Fabric Migration","x":"Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app Feature usage and adoption report Track user activities in Microsoft Fabric Create alerts for pipeline runs Power BI implementation planning Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric migration overview Microsoft Fabric Toolbox (community accelerators, including FUAM)"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"Data Load Strategy","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Playbook 02 · Data Engineering Data Load Strategy A table-by-table framework for deciding how data enters and moves through a Fabric medallion lakehouse: when a destructive full reload is the right answer, when you need incremental loads, how to detect changes at the source, and which Fabric tool should do the work at each layer. Audience data engineers, architects Level intermediate Reading time 80 min Pages 8"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#purpose","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"Purpose and scope","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"How you load data decides three things you will live with for years: how fresh the data is, how much capacity the platform burns to keep it fresh, and how many ways the pipeline can quietly go wrong. A full reload is simple and self-healing but cost grows with table size. An incremental load is cheap per run but is only as correct as the change signal behind it. Most platforms need both, chosen per table. This playbo"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#when-to-use","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"When to use this playbook","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Use it when you are Designing ingestion for a new lakehouse or warehouse Deciding between a nightly rebuild and incremental processing for a growing table Debugging missing deletes, duplicate rows or drifting aggregates Choosing between pipelines, Copy job, Dataflows Gen2, notebooks or mirroring Preparing Gold tables for Direct Lake Look elsewhere when you need Workspace and layer layout, which the medallion architec"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#audience","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"Audience and prerequisites","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Primary readers Data engineers and architects who own ingestion and transformation, and the analytics engineers who consume Gold tables. You should know What a Fabric lakehouse and a Delta table are, how Bronze, Silver and Gold layers divide responsibilities, and basic PySpark or Spark SQL. Environment A Fabric capacity, a lakehouse per layer (for example LH_SALES_BRZ , LH_SALES_SLV , LH_SALES_GLD ) and a notebook ru"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#strategy-picture","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"The strategy in one picture","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Every hop in the medallion flow has a default pattern. Departures from the default are deliberate, per-table decisions that the following pages walk through. Default pattern per hop. Silver is highlighted because it is where most correctness decisions live: deduplication, upserts and delete handling all happen in the Bronze-to-Silver step."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#principles","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"Six principles behind the framework","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Ask the source first The first design question is not \"how do we build it\" but \"what change information does the source give us\": log-based CDC, a reliable modified-timestamp or row version, or nothing but snapshots. If you skip this, you discover missing deletes in production. Bronze keeps what was delivered Append every delivery, whether it was a full extract or a delta, and tag it with a batch id and load type. Br"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#defaults","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"Default patterns at a glance","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Layer Default load pattern Depart from the default when Bronze Append every delivery, files or Delta, tagged with batch metadata Tiny, non-historical lookup data can be replaced; transient staging tables need no history Silver MERGE upsert from the new Bronze batch; SCD2 for history-bearing dimensions The table is small and fast to rebuild, history is not required, or business logic changed and must be re-applied to "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#outcomes","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"What you will be able to do","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Classify every table as full-load or incremental using explicit thresholds rather than habit. Select a change tracking mechanism from what the source exposes, and know what each one cannot see. Implement append-only Bronze with batch metadata, including the mixed full-and-incremental feed case. Write idempotent Delta MERGE upserts, an SCD Type 2 dimension and delete handling that matches the change signal. Refresh Go"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#map","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"Map of this playbook","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Page What it answers Full vs incremental loads Is this table small and quiet enough for a full reload? What does each approach cost? Change tracking options How do I find out what changed at the source, and what can each mechanism not detect? Bronze layer patterns Files or Delta? How do I land mixed full and incremental feeds without losing history? Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes How do I upsert safely, keep dimensi"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#related","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"How this fits with the other playbooks","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Medallion Architecture defines the layers and workspace boundaries this playbook loads into. Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy covers the physical table layout that replaceWhere , OPTIMIZE and Direct Lake all depend on. Control-Table-Driven Pipelines shows how to keep per-table load patterns and watermarks in metadata instead of hard-coding them. Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration sequences the load jobs described"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/index.html#refs","t":"Data Load Strategy","s":"References","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Microsoft Fabric decision guide: choose a data movement strategy What is Copy job in Data Factory Direct Lake overview Cross-workload table maintenance and optimization Delta Lake: table deletes, updates and merges"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/load-strategy.html","t":"Full vs incremental loads","s":"Full vs incremental loads","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Playbook 02 · Data Load Strategy Full vs incremental loads The choice between replacing a table and applying changes to it is a strategic trade-off between simplicity, freshness and cost. This page defines both patterns, gives explicit thresholds for when a full load is still the right answer, and lists how each approach fails."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/load-strategy.html#why-it-matters","t":"Full vs incremental loads","s":"Why this decision matters","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Load style is not an implementation detail. It sets how fresh the data can be, how much capacity each refresh consumes, how much logic you must build and monitor, and how the pipeline recovers from a bad run. The wrong default is expensive in both directions: incremental logic on a tiny lookup table is maintenance for no benefit, and a nightly full reload of a billion-row fact table eventually stops fitting in its wi"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/load-strategy.html#full-load","t":"Full vs incremental loads","s":"Full load (destructive refresh)","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"A full load extracts the entire dataset from the source and uses it to replace the contents of the target table, so the target becomes a point-in-time snapshot of the source. In a Delta lakehouse there are three ways to do it, and they are not equivalent. Technique What happens Use it when Overwrite ( mode(\"overwrite\") or INSERT OVERWRITE ) One atomic Delta commit. Readers see the previous version or the new one, nev"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/load-strategy.html#eligibility","t":"Full vs incremental loads","s":"Full-load eligibility: all of these must hold","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Keep a table on full loads only while every one of the following is true. These thresholds are practitioner rules of thumb, not Fabric limits: calibrate them against your own capacity size, run windows and SLAs, and re-check them as tables grow. Factor Rule of thumb How to check Small footprint Under roughly 20 to 30 GB or 50 million rows, with small daily growth DESCRIBE DETAIL for sizeInBytes , plus a row count tre"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/load-strategy.html#incremental-load","t":"Full vs incremental loads","s":"Incremental load (delta processing)","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"An incremental load identifies, extracts and applies only the data created or changed since the last successful run. It preserves what is already in the target and changes it by appending new records, updating existing ones or marking records deleted. That is what makes it cheap and frequent, and also what makes it fragile: the target is only as correct as the change signal and the logic that applies it. Efficiency. "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/load-strategy.html#failure-modes","t":"Full vs incremental loads","s":"How incremental loads fail","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Failure mode Effect Mitigation Missed changes (late commits, clock skew, watermark on a column that is not always updated) Rows silently absent or stale Overlap window plus an idempotent MERGE ; prefer a row version or log-based CDC; periodic reconciliation Duplicate delivery after retry or rerun Duplicate rows in Bronze or Silver Batch ids, a unique merge key, deduplicate before MERGE Out-of-order updates An older v"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/load-strategy.html#comparison","t":"Full vs incremental loads","s":"Comparison","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Feature Full load (destructive refresh) Incremental load (delta processing) Data scope The entire dataset moves from source to target each cycle Only new or modified data since the last successful load Performance and speed Slow for large tables and degrades as volume grows Fast, because it processes a small slice Resource use High CU, memory and network consumption, and high load on the source Low consumption, provi"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/load-strategy.html#hybrids","t":"Full vs incremental loads","s":"The trade-off, and the hybrids that resolve it","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"A full load buys ease and integrity at the cost of performance, resource use and latency. An incremental load buys speed and freshness at the cost of complexity and a higher inherent risk of inconsistency when change detection and reconciliation are not designed and monitored with care. Real platforms rarely pick one for everything. Four hybrids cover most needs. Incremental plus periodic full resync. Run frequent in"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/load-strategy.html#refs","t":"Full vs incremental loads","s":"References","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Incremental copy in Copy job Understand Direct Lake query performance VACUUM Delta tables Delta Lake time travel Delta Lake: table batch reads and writes"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Change tracking options","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Playbook 02 · Data Load Strategy Change tracking options An incremental pipeline is only as reliable as its answer to one question: what changed? This page covers the mechanisms available in a Fabric platform, from a simple high-watermark to log-based CDC, mirroring and Delta change data feed, and gives you a way to choose between them."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#source-first","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Start with the source, not the tool","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The feasibility and reliability of an incremental load depend entirely on how accurately you can identify changed data. That capability is set by the source system, not by your pipeline tool, so the first design question is \"what change information does the source provide?\" Adopting this source-first view prevents the most common production surprise: an incremental pipeline that works for months and then reveals that"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#watermark","t":"Change tracking options","s":"High-watermarking (HWM)","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The high-watermark method tracks progress with a column in the source table, such as a last_modified timestamp, a SQL Server ROWVERSION or a monotonically increasing integer. Each run uses the highest value seen last time as a bookmark. Read the stored high-water mark Fetch the maximum watermark value from the last successful run out of a durable control table. On the first run there is none, which means a full initi"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#watermark-pipeline","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Watermark in a pipeline","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"In a data pipeline the pattern is a Lookup activity that reads the stored value, a Copy activity whose source query uses it, and a final activity that stores the new value on success. Copy job implements the same state handling for you (see Copy job incremental modes ), so hand-built pipelines are worth it only when you need custom queries or orchestration around the copy. Set RunUpperBound to the pipeline's start ti"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#watermark-notebook","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Watermark in a notebook","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"For Bronze-to-Silver processing, track progress with an ingestion batch id that the orchestrator assigns in increasing order, not with wall-clock time. Concurrent ingestion runs can finish out of order, and a timestamp watermark would then skip the slower run's rows."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#watermark-pitfalls","t":"Change tracking options","s":"What goes wrong with watermarks","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Pitfall Why it happens Fix Hard deletes are invisible A deleted row no longer exists to be selected Periodic full comparison, or move to CDC (see delete handling ) Late-committing transactions A long transaction stamps rows with an early time but commits after your run passed that time Prefer ROWVERSION or a commit-ordered feed; otherwise re-read an overlap window (for example the last few hours) and rely on an idemp"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#cdc","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Change data capture (CDC)","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Change data capture records every insert, update and delete directly from the source database, usually by reading its transaction log, and delivers an ordered stream of change events. It is more accurate than a watermark because deletes are first-class events, and it puts little load on the source because it reads the log rather than querying tables. The price is setup and operations: the source must have the feature"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#copy-job","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Copy job incremental modes","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Copy job is a Data Factory item that moves data from many sources to many destinations without building a pipeline. It supports full copy and incremental copy, and manages the state of the last successful run for you. For database sources it chooses between two incremental modes per table. Watermark-based incremental copy. You pick an incremental column. Supported types include ROWVERSION , datetime, date, strings in"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#cdf","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Delta change data feed (CDF)","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Once data is in a Delta table you can get row-level change tracking from Delta itself. Delta change data feed records, for each commit, which rows were inserted, updated or deleted. It is off by default and records only changes made after you enable it. _change_type insert , update_preimage , update_postimage or delete . The pre-image is the row before an update, which lets you correct aggregates keyed on an old valu"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#cdf-bronze","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Where CDF helps, and where it does not","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"CDF is most valuable on tables that are updated and deleted, typically Silver tables maintained with MERGE , feeding Gold. It is also how materialized lake views decide to refresh incrementally (see materialized lake views ). On an append-only Bronze table it adds little, because every change is an insert. To process only new Bronze rows without a hand-written watermark, read Bronze as a stream and let the checkpoint"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#comparison","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Comparing the mechanisms","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Mechanism Source prerequisite Inserts Updates Deletes Notes Watermark Monotonic modified column or row version Yes Partly No Cheap and simple. Updates are seen only if the column is bumped on every change; risk of late commits and missed updates Log-based CDC (through Copy job or Eventstreams) CDC enabled on the source and supported by the connector Yes Yes Yes Low source load; setup and log retention to manage Datab"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#choosing","t":"Change tracking options","s":"Choosing a mechanism","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The ideal mechanism is log-based CDC because it gives a complete, unambiguous stream of changes and simplifies every downstream transformation. If CDC is not available, a watermark is a viable alternative with one structural constraint: it cannot see hard deletes, so you must combine it with a periodic full comparison to resynchronize the target. If neither CDC nor a reliable watermark exists, true incremental loadin"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/change-tracking.html#refs","t":"Change tracking options","s":"References","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Incremental copy in Copy job Change data capture in Copy job What is Mirroring in Fabric? Open mirroring Data movement decision guide Delta Lake: change data feed Tutorial: incrementally load data from a warehouse to a lakehouse"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Bronze layer patterns","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Playbook 02 · Data Load Strategy Bronze layer patterns Bronze is the raw, auditable foundation of the medallion architecture. The decisions you make here, files or Delta, append or replace, how you tag a batch, set what you can reprocess later and how complicated Silver has to be. This page covers the patterns and the metadata that make Bronze rebuildable."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#mandate","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Mandate: raw, immutable, auditable","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Bronze ingests data from source systems in its original, unaltered form and keeps it as a historical archive. It is the single place from which Silver and Gold can be rebuilt if business logic changes or a defect is found downstream. To do that job, Bronze is treated as immutable and loaded append-only: every delivery from the source is kept, in the order it arrived, so a complete chronological record exists for audi"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#files-vs-delta","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Landing as files vs loading into Delta tables","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The foundational Bronze decision in a lakehouse is whether to keep raw data as files in the Lakehouse Files area (Parquet, CSV, JSON in their native form) or to load it straight into Delta tables in the Tables area. Approach Strengths Weaknesses Files Maximum fidelity, a true 1:1 copy of the source. Resilient to schema drift, because validation is deferred to the Bronze-to-Silver step and ingestion does not fail on u"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#landing-zone","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"The landing zone pattern (pre-Bronze)","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The trade-off between architectural purity and operational efficiency has a well-established resolution: do both. Extend the layer in two stages. The ingestion tool lands data as files in a dedicated landing area, which satisfies the requirement for a pure, replayable archive. A Spark step then reads those files and appends them to append-only Bronze Delta tables, which enables SQL access, time travel and efficient i"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#landing-layout","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Landing layout and conventions","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Treat the landing area as an API between ingestion and processing. Predictable paths let any tool write and any notebook read, and let you tell full and incremental deliveries apart without opening a file. One batch, one folder. A batch id from the orchestrator, increasing and never reused, is the unit of load, replay and cleanup. Encode the load type in the path. Silver needs to know whether a batch is a delta or a "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#append-code","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Appending a batch to Bronze","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The notebook receives the batch id and load type as parameters from the pipeline, reads the batch folder, adds standard metadata columns and appends. The metadata columns are what let Silver deduplicate, order and interpret batches later. Delta also supports idempotent writes through the txnAppId and txnVersion writer options, which make a repeated commit of the same version a no-op; see the Delta documentation if yo"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#metadata-columns","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Standard metadata columns","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Column Purpose _batch_id Unit of load, ordering, replay and remediation; also the watermark for Bronze-to-Silver processing _load_type full or incremental ; tells Silver whether absent rows can imply deletes _ingest_ts When the row reached Bronze; breaks ties when the same key arrives more than once _source_file Row-level lineage back to the landed file _source_system Needed when several systems feed one conformed en"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#load-patterns","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Loading patterns for Bronze","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The loading pattern follows from Bronze's role: preserve a complete historical record. Pattern Verdict Details Incremental append Default, recommended All incoming data, whether it came from a full or an incremental extraction, is appended. Nothing in Bronze is updated or deleted. Files go to a new folder per batch; Delta tables use append mode. The result is a re-playable log of what the source delivered over time. "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#mixed-feeds","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Handling mixed full and incremental source feeds","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"A common real-world situation: a source sends daily incremental files containing only new and changed records, plus a periodic full file that represents the complete table. The problem appears when reconciliation logic built for full extracts meets a small incremental file. A MERGE with WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE treats every row missing from the small file as deleted, and wipes the target. Keep Bronze ig"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#remediation","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Remediating a bad batch","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Because each batch is tagged and written to its own files, undoing a bad load is routine, but the method depends on where the batch lives. Where Remediation Notes Landing files Delete the batch folder and re-land Nothing else is affected Bronze Delta table DELETE FROM bronze.orders WHERE _batch_id = <n> Cheap when each batch occupies its own files. It writes a delete commit, so streaming readers of Bronze need to be "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#table-settings","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"Table settings and layout for Bronze","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Bronze is write-heavy and rarely queried interactively, so tune for ingestion. Do not partition by default: a small daily append into many partitions produces small files, and Fabric guidance is to avoid partitioning unless a specific requirement justifies it (see the Delta partitioning playbook ). Enable auto compaction so small appends are consolidated without a separate job, and do not rely on V-Order for Bronze; "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/bronze.html#refs","t":"Bronze layer patterns","s":"References","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"What is a lakehouse in Microsoft Fabric? Lakehouse and Delta Lake tables Compacting Delta tables Cross-workload table maintenance and optimization Delta Lake: table batch reads and writes Delta Lake: table streaming reads and writes"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Playbook 02 · Data Load Strategy Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes Silver turns raw Bronze history into a single, trustworthy current state. This is where deduplication, upserts, dimension history and delete handling happen, and where most data-correctness bugs are made. The patterns below are idempotent, and each delete strategy is tied to the change signal that makes it safe."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#goal","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Goal: cleansed, conformed, integrated","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Silver takes the append-only data in Bronze and improves its quality and usability. Typical work falls into three groups. Cleansing. Handle nulls and missing values, correct data types, and validate against business rules. Route failing rows to a quarantine table with a reason code instead of dropping them silently. Conforming. Standardize formats and build master entities such as customers and products by merging re"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#full-reload","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Full and partition-scoped reloads","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"A full Silver reload is appropriate only while a table meets every eligibility check: small footprint, short run time, low downstream ripple, no history to protect, a friendly source and calm capacity. For small reference tables it is the simplest correct answer, and the overwrite pattern is on the full load section . A wider class of reloads is worth doing on large tables too, scoped to a table or a slice. Use them "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#merge","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Incremental upserts with Delta MERGE","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"MERGE is the workhorse of Bronze-to-Silver loading. It compares a source dataset (the new Bronze rows) with a target (the Silver table) on a join condition and runs different actions depending on whether a match is found. WHEN MATCHED The key exists in both. Typically UPDATE the Silver row with the new values, guarded so that only a newer version wins. WHEN NOT MATCHED The key exists in the source only. INSERT a new "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#merge-rules","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Rules that keep MERGE correct and cheap","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Concern What to do Unique source keys If several source rows match one target row, the merge fails. Deduplicate on the merge key first, as above. Also guard against NULL keys, and normalize case and whitespace in keys during cleansing. Do not rewrite unchanged rows Condition the update on a version column ( modified_at ) or a hash of tracked attributes, so replays and no-op updates do not rewrite files. Reduce files "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#scd2","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Slowly changing dimensions (Type 2)","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"For many dimensions it is not enough to overwrite old values. Analyzing sales by the customer's address at the time of the order , not just the current one, needs history. Type 1 updates in place and loses history. Type 2 keeps it by: Expiring the old record. Mark the existing row as no longer current: set is_current = false and close its validity interval with a valid_to timestamp. Inserting a new record. Add a new "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#deletes","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Handling deletes","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Propagating source deletions to Silver is one of the hardest parts of incremental loading, and the right approach depends entirely on the change detection mechanism you used at ingestion. A delete strategy that does not match the change signal produces a Silver table that quietly stops representing the source. Change signal What Silver receives Delete strategy Soft delete flag in the source An update with is_deleted "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#soft-deletes","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Soft deletes","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"If the source flags rows as inactive instead of removing them, the delete arrives as an ordinary update and the merge handles it. You choose whether Silver keeps the flag (consumers filter on it) or removes the row. To keep the flag instead, drop the first clause and the condition on the insert clause; the is_deleted column then flows through UPDATE SET * like any other attribute. Keeping flagged rows is safer for au"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#hard-deletes-cdc","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Hard deletes with CDC","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"When ingestion used log-based CDC, the change stream carries an explicit operation per row. Keep the last event per key, then apply deletes, updates and inserts in one merge."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#hard-deletes-hwm","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"Hard deletes with a watermark: the snapshot merge","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"A watermark cannot see deleted rows, so the only way to find them is to compare a complete source extract with Silver and remove the rows that are missing. That is a MERGE with WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE , where the source is the full extract. A watermark-based pipeline therefore needs a hybrid: frequent incremental merges for inserts and updates, plus a less frequent, heavier snapshot merge dedicated to deletes. The"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/silver.html#refs","t":"Silver: MERGE, SCD2 and deletes","s":"References","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Delta Lake: table deletes, updates and merges Delta Lake: concurrency control Concurrency control for Delta tables in Fabric Deletion vectors for Delta tables Change data capture in Copy job (SCD Type 2) VACUUM Delta tables"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Playbook 02 · Data Load Strategy Gold layer and Direct Lake Gold is the business-ready layer, modeled for analytics and consumed by semantic models. The central loading decision is how to keep aggregates and facts correct without recomputing everything, and the consumption layer, especially Direct Lake, adds constraints that reach back into how you write."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#objective","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Objective: optimized for analytics and reporting","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Gold takes conformed Silver data and prepares it for specific business uses. The work is usually a mix of three things. Aggregation. Summarize to a coarser grain, such as daily or monthly sales from individual order lines. Data modeling. Shape the data into a dimensional model, most often a star schema: central fact tables of measurements surrounded by dimension tables of descriptive attributes. That design matches t"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#aggregation","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"The aggregation dilemma","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The core Gold decision is how to create and maintain aggregated, modeled tables. There are two classic answers and one practical middle path. Full recalculation: pros Simple logic, highly reliable Exactly correct each run, including complex or non-additive measures Doubles as the periodic integrity refresh Full recalculation: cons Slow and capacity-hungry when Silver is large Too slow for high-freshness or near real-"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#slice-recompute","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"The middle path: recompute the affected slice","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"\"Incremental\" does not have to mean adding deltas to existing numbers, which is fragile. A more robust approach finds which slices of the Gold table (dates, periods, regions) the new Silver changes touch, recomputes those slices completely from Silver, and replaces them atomically. Because each affected slice is rebuilt from source rows, the result is correct for any measure, additive or not, and rerunning is safe. A"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#measure-types","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Which measures can be incremental?","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Measure type Examples Merge deltas? Recommended approach Additive Revenue, units, line counts Yes, with signed deltas Slice recompute, or delta merge if volumes demand it Distinct counts Customers per day or per month No Counts cannot be summed across slices. Recompute the whole affected period at the target grain, for example the affected month Ratios Margin percent, average order value No: ratios cannot be summed S"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#gold-options","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Options for keeping Gold current","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Option How it works Watch for Spark slice recompute Notebook finds affected slices, recomputes, replaceWhere You own the change detection and the state Materialized lake views Declarative SQL definitions that Fabric refreshes on a schedule, choosing no refresh, incremental or full Incremental needs CDF on all sources; window functions, DISTINCT and non-deterministic functions force a full refresh (see tooling ) Wareh"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#direct-lake","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Direct Lake: what it needs from your loads","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Direct Lake is a Power BI semantic model storage mode that loads columns from Delta tables in OneLake into memory on demand and queries them with the same VertiPaq engine as Import mode. It avoids an import refresh, but it ties query performance to the physical state of your Delta tables, so Gold loading decisions become BI performance decisions. Five concepts explain nearly all of it. Transcoding On the first query "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#direct-lake-fallback","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Fallback control and diagnosis","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"For Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints, the Direct Lake behavior model property controls what happens when Direct Lake conditions are not met: Automatic (default) falls back silently, DirectLakeOnly fails the query so you find and fix the cause, and DirectQueryOnly always uses DirectQuery. Use DirectLakeOnly in development and consider keeping fallback available in production for user continuity. To see which tab"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#guardrails","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Guardrails","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Each capacity SKU caps files, row groups and rows per table, evaluated per query, plus a model size limit on smaller SKUs. For Direct Lake on OneLake, exceeding a guardrail makes refresh fail and blocks queries until you optimize; on SQL analytics endpoints the model falls back to DirectQuery if fallback is enabled. A single table over a limit disables Direct Lake mode for the entire model. SKU Parquet files per tabl"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#direct-lake-layout","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Table layout Direct Lake rewards","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Factor Guidance V-Order Apply it to Gold tables that Direct Lake reads. It improves compression and loading speed. New Fabric workspaces default to the write-heavy resource profile with V-Order off, so enable it explicitly: set spark.sql.parquet.vorder.default to true, use the readHeavyForPBI resource profile, or set the delta.parquet.vorder.enabled table property. Existing files keep their layout until rewritten (fo"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#update-patterns","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Write patterns: what keeps framing incremental","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Parquet files are immutable, so an update or delete is a rewrite. What matters to Direct Lake is how much of the table's file set changes in one commit. Why overwrite hurts Direct Lake. Framing compares the Delta log: files that survive keep their in-memory data. A full overwrite leaves no survivors. Update pattern Effect on Direct Lake Guidance Incremental append Existing files and row groups untouched; incremental "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#calculated-columns","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"Business logic: what must be materialized","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Direct Lake on SQL analytics endpoints does not support calculated columns or calculated tables on Direct Lake tables, and Direct Lake on OneLake supports them only in limited, preview form (calculated columns with user context only). Plan as if derived columns must be produced upstream and stored in the Gold Delta table. The practical rule is more precise than \"pre-compute everything\": Materialize row-level and attr"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#after-the-load","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"After the load: reframing","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"With automatic updates on, the model reframes itself when it sees new Delta versions, which suits independent tables and small models. When a load writes several tables that must be consistent (a fact and its aggregates), a model that reframes between the writes can show a mixed state. In that case turn automatic updates off, run the whole load, and finish the pipeline with a refresh of the semantic model through a p"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/gold-direct-lake.html#refs","t":"Gold layer and Direct Lake","s":"References","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Direct Lake overview How Direct Lake works Understand Direct Lake query performance Cross-workload table maintenance and optimization Delta Lake table optimization and V-Order Configure resource profile configurations Optimal refresh for materialized lake views Incremental refresh in Dataflow Gen2"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Choosing the right tool","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Playbook 02 · Data Load Strategy Choosing the right tool Fabric offers several items that can load data. They overlap, so the choice is rarely about capability alone: it is about the layer, the volume, the change-tracking source, the team's skills and how much of the state you want to own."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#landscape","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"The landscape","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Think of the tools in two groups. One group moves data from a source into the lake with little or no transformation. The other transforms data that is already in the lake. A data pipeline sits above both as the orchestrator. Using each tool for its own job, rather than stretching one tool across everything, is what keeps pipelines maintainable. Two jobs, one orchestrator. Movement tools bring data in, transformation "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#pipelines","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Data pipelines and Copy activity","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Strengths The orchestration engine: sequence notebooks, dataflows, Copy activities and stored procedures with dependencies, parameters, retries and alerts Copy activity is built for high-throughput, metadata-driven movement with minimal transformation The natural first choice for landing raw files from a wide range of sources into Bronze Weaknesses Copy activity transformation is limited to type conversion, column ma"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#table-actions","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Lakehouse table actions","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"When the Copy activity writes to a Lakehouse table, the table action decides the load pattern. This is where a pipeline can implement Bronze loads directly, with no notebook. Table action What it does Fits Caution Append Adds rows to the table Incremental Bronze, one batch per run Not idempotent: a retried run appends twice unless you guard it Overwrite Replaces the data and the schema with the new values; previous v"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#orchestration","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Orchestration patterns","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"A pipeline run is the natural unit of a load: it owns the batch identifier, passes it to every step, and decides what happens on failure. A typical structure per source or domain is a Lookup of the last committed watermark, a Copy activity to the landing zone, a notebook activity that loads Bronze and then Silver, a notebook or stored procedure for Gold, and a final step that stores the new watermark and refreshes th"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#copy-job","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Copy job","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Copy job is a wizard-driven item for repeatable copies: choose the source tables, the destination and a mode (full, or incremental through a watermark column or native change data capture), then schedule it. It keeps the incremental state for you, which removes the Lookup, query and update activities from the pipeline you would otherwise build. Use it when You need incremental copy or replication of many tables with "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#dataflows","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Dataflows Gen2","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Strengths Low-code Power Query authoring with 300+ transformation functions and 150+ connectors Productive for analysts and data engineers who know Power Query and need transformation logic quickly Built-in incremental refresh with automatic bucketing, so common date-driven patterns need no code Weaknesses Rarely the right choice at terabyte scale, where tuned Spark code usually performs better Visual development int"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#dataflow-incremental","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"How Dataflow Gen2 incremental refresh behaves","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"You pick a date or datetime column, how far back to extract, a bucket size, and a column whose maximum value signals change. At each run the dataflow compares maximum values per bucket, fetches only changed buckets, and for each one deletes the bucket's rows in the destination and inserts the fresh rows . Data outside the bucket range is untouched. That is the affected-slice pattern from the Gold page , implemented a"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#spark","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Spark notebooks","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Strengths Maximum power, flexibility and scale: the right place for Delta MERGE , SCD Type 2, delete handling and heavy aggregation Code-first: version control, review, automated tests and CI/CD fit naturally Direct access to Delta features: change data feed, replaceWhere , Structured Streaming with checkpoints Parameterizable and callable from pipelines, with exit values for hand-offs Weaknesses Requires Python, Sca"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#warehouse","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Warehouse T-SQL and T-SQL notebooks","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"If the Gold layer is a Fabric Warehouse, set-based T-SQL is often the simplest and most performant tool: stored procedures that load dimensions and facts, MERGE for upserts, and CTAS for rebuilds. Fabric Warehouse supports MERGE , including WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE , and multi-statement transactions. A period-scoped refresh is simply a delete and insert for the same range in one transaction. Parameters. Call proced"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#mlv","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Materialized lake views","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"A materialized lake view (MLV) is a declarative way to build a Silver or Gold table. You write the SQL query once; Fabric persists the result as a Delta table, tracks dependencies between views, orders refreshes and enforces data quality rules. It turns a hand-built chain of notebooks into a dependency graph that refreshes itself. On each scheduled refresh the engine picks one of three strategies: No refresh No new D"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#mlv-incremental","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"What incremental refresh supports","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Construct Incremental? Notes Filters, projections, deterministic built-in functions Yes rand() , uuid() and current_timestamp() are not supported Inner joins, UNION ALL , CTEs Yes Subqueries and CTEs must use only supported clauses Left outer and left semi joins Conditionally Only when the right-hand table does not change in the cycle; otherwise full refresh SUM , MIN , MAX , COUNT (no DISTINCT ) with GROUP BY Yes No"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#other","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Other options that remove or reduce a load","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Option What it does Consider when Mirroring Continuously replicates a supported operational database into OneLake as Delta, managed by Fabric You want a current-state copy with low latency and no pipeline to maintain; history is still your job (see Change tracking ) Shortcuts Reference data in another location in place, with no copy The data already sits in OneLake or a supported store and does not need reshaping Eve"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#matrix","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Tool choice matrix","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Use the matrix as a starting point for each task in the medallion flow. The primary column is the default; the alternative applies when the primary does not fit the team, volume or source. Task Primary Alternative Rationale and key considerations Raw ingestion to the landing zone (files) Data pipeline with Copy activity Copy job; Spark for sources without a connector; Eventstreams for events Optimized for high-scale,"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#choose-flow","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"A decision flow for the tool","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The flow reflects the matrix: first decide whether the step moves or transforms data, then walk down the questions until one answers yes. The fall-through at the bottom of each column is the safe default for that kind of step. Move or transform, then the first question answered yes wins. Teal outcomes move data and purple outcomes transform it. A data pipeline orchestrates whichever you land on."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#selection","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Selection criteria beyond capability","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Criterion Question to ask Leans toward Team skills Who will maintain this at 2 a.m.? Power Query team: Dataflow Gen2. SQL team: Warehouse T-SQL or MLV. Python or Spark team: notebooks. Volume and growth Does the table reach hundreds of millions of rows or terabytes? Spark, or a Spark-backed managed feature, rather than Dataflow Gen2 Change-tracking source Do you have CDC, a trustworthy watermark, or neither? CDC: Cop"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#anti-patterns","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"Common anti-patterns","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Anti-pattern Why it hurts Instead Dataflow Gen2 for a multi-terabyte Silver MERGE Replace-only destination semantics and limited scale Spark notebook with Delta MERGE Copy activity Overwrite to a large Direct Lake Gold table on every run Every file is replaced and every column segment evicted Append, or replace only the changed slice from Spark Two tools writing the same Delta table (dataflow plus notebook, or Copy j"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/tooling.html#refs","t":"Choosing the right tool","s":"References","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Fabric decision guide: copy activity, Copy job, dataflow, Eventstream or Spark Data movement decision guide Configure a lakehouse in a copy activity What is Copy job Incremental refresh in Dataflow Gen2 What are materialized lake views Optimal refresh for materialized lake views T-SQL support in Fabric notebooks MERGE (Transact-SQL) in Fabric Mirroring in Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/cheat-sheet.html","t":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","s":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Playbook 02 · Data Load Strategy Decision matrix cheat sheet The whole framework on one page. Start from the defaults by layer, adjust for the source and the table, then run the checklist before you ship. The reasoning behind every row is on the earlier pages."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/cheat-sheet.html#defaults","t":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","s":"Defaults by layer","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The optimal strategy is a hybrid, chosen table by table and layer by layer. A small, static reference table does not deserve an incremental pipeline; a fact table with billions of rows cannot be fully reloaded. These are the defaults to start from. The defaults on one grid. Each row restates a layer's rule: keep the raw record immutable, upsert with guards, recompute the smallest correct slice."},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/cheat-sheet.html#matrix","t":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","s":"The decision matrix","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Layer Data characteristics Source capability Latency History Primary strategy Fallback Bronze Any volume or velocity Any (CDC, watermark, none) Low (batch) High: full history Incremental append. Append every full or incremental batch to keep a complete, auditable record. None: append-only is the core principle Silver Small volume, low velocity Any Low (batch) Low to medium Full load (replace). Simple, reliable and ef"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/cheat-sheet.html#by-source","t":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","s":"Pick by what the source exposes","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Source gives you Bronze Silver Watch for Log-based CDC or mirroring Append every change event with its operation and sequence MERGE applying inserts, updates and deletes in order Out-of-order events: dedupe by key, keep the latest sequence. Mirroring is a current-state replica, not history. Reliable modified-timestamp or batch id, deletes flagged Append new and changed rows MERGE with a soft-delete flag column Late a"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/cheat-sheet.html#by-table","t":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","s":"Pick by table type","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Table Pattern Why Small reference or lookup (countries, status codes) Full replace Cheaper than maintaining incremental logic; always correct Large append-only fact (events, transactions never edited) Incremental append in Bronze, append in Silver No updates to reconcile; friendliest pattern for Direct Lake Large fact with late changes (orders that are amended) MERGE on the business key with a version guard Idempoten"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/cheat-sheet.html#rules","t":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","s":"Rules of thumb","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Treat the figures below as starting points. Only the Direct Lake and Delta defaults are documented platform values; the load thresholds are practitioner guidance to calibrate against your own capacity and SLAs. Stay on full load while The table is under roughly 20 to 30 GB or 50 million rows, runs end to end in roughly 10 to 15 minutes, and has no history to protect. Reassess as it grows. Watermark overlap Re-read a "},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/cheat-sheet.html#operations","t":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","s":"Operations cheat sheet","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Check When How Alert on Source to Bronze reconciliation Every run Compare extracted row count with rows appended for the batch Any mismatch, or a batch with zero rows when rows are expected Watermark lag Every run Compare stored watermark with the latest source change time Lag exceeding the SLA Duplicate business keys in Silver Every run Group by key, count above one Any row returned MERGE outcome Every run Read oper"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/cheat-sheet.html#checklist","t":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","s":"Design checklist for each table","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"The table is classified as full or incremental against explicit thresholds, and the decision is written down. The change signal is identified (CDC, watermark column, snapshots) and its blind spots, especially deletes, are documented. Bronze appends with batch id, load type, source file and ingest time, and a rerun of the same batch changes nothing. Silver deduplicates the source before MERGE , guards updates with a v"},{"u":"playbooks/02-data-load-strategy/cheat-sheet.html#refs","t":"Decision matrix cheat sheet","s":"References","p":"Data Load Strategy","x":"Data movement decision guide Fabric decision guide: copy activity, Copy job, dataflow, Eventstream or Spark Cross-workload table maintenance and optimization Understand Direct Lake query performance Delta Lake table VACUUM"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"Medallion Architecture","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Data Engineering Medallion Architecture How to lay out a Fabric analytics platform as five isolated layers, one workspace each, and how to move data between them with shortcuts, notebooks, MERGE utilities, a warehouse and semantic models, using one naming standard from the first workspace to the last report. Audience data engineers, architects, platform and security teams Level intermediate Reading time"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#purpose","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"Purpose and scope","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The medallion pattern says data should improve in stages: raw in Bronze, cleansed and conformed in Silver, modeled for the business in Gold. Most teams stop there and put all three stages in one workspace. This playbook takes a stricter position that Microsoft's own guidance points toward: each layer gets its own workspace , and so do the two layers that sit after Gold, Consumption (warehouse, semantic models, AI/ML "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#when-to-use","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"When to use this playbook","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Use it when you are Standing up a new Fabric platform and deciding workspace boundaries Separating duties between ingestion, transformation, modeling and BI teams Handling regulated or sensitive data that raw-layer users must not see transformed or shared Writing the first reusable Silver and Gold notebooks and want tested patterns Standardizing names, Spark settings and notebook structure across teams Look elsewhere"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#audience","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"Audience and prerequisites","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Primary readers Data engineers and solution architects who design and build the lakehouse layers, plus the platform and security teams who own workspaces, roles and capacities. Also useful for BI developers (the Consumption and Delivery pages) and delivery leads who need the checklist. You should know What a Fabric workspace, lakehouse and Delta table are, basic PySpark and Spark SQL, and the idea of a medallion arch"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#design-at-a-glance","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"The design at a glance","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The diagram shows one domain in one environment. Each dashed band is a workspace; the boxes inside are its items. Nothing reads across more than one hop: Silver reads Bronze, Gold reads Silver, Consumption reads Gold. Delivery reads Consumption. The strictly isolated medallion. Every dashed band is its own workspace with its own roles, capacity assignment and deployment lifecycle. Data moves one hop at a time: each l"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#principles","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"Design principles","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"One workspace per layer. The workspace is where roles, capacity, Git and deployment are set, so it is the right place to draw a layer boundary. One hop at a time. A layer reads only the layer immediately before it, through shortcuts that appear as local tables. No skip-level reads, no reads from a later layer. Each layer owns its writes. Only the notebooks and pipelines of a layer write to that layer's lakehouse. Dow"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#outcomes","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"What you will have built","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Workspaces for each layer, domain and environment, named by one standard and assigned to capacities Lakehouses with shortcuts that expose each upstream layer as local, read-only tables A reusable full-load function and an incremental MERGE utility for Silver, parameterized per table A config-driven Gold engine with built-in reconciliation checks A Warehouse loaded from Gold shortcuts, a semantic model on top of it, a"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#map","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"How the pages fit together","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Page Read it when you need to Layers and strict isolation Explain or defend the design: layer contracts, the case for isolation, its cost, the options compared and security controls Workspace pattern and naming Create the workspaces and name everything: inventory per layer, naming standard, abbreviations, capacities, promotion summary Fabric notebooks Set notebook standards before anyone writes code: capabilities, si"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#reading-paths","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"Reading paths by role","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Role Start with Then Solution architect Layers and strict isolation Workspace pattern, then the deployment playbook Platform or security engineer Layers and strict isolation (security controls) Workspace pattern (capacity, naming), Environment Data engineer Fabric notebooks, Environment Bronze to Silver, Incremental loads, Silver to Gold BI developer Consumption and delivery Power BI development standards playbook"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#implementation-checklist","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"Implementation checklist","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Decide the isolation level using the decision table and record the choice and the reasons Agree environment, domain and layer abbreviations; reserve the naming standard before the first workspace is created Create workspaces for one domain in development, assign roles by layer, and set the capacity Create one schema-enabled lakehouse per lakehouse layer and one Environment item per workspace; publish and set it as th"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#related","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"Related playbooks","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Data Load Strategy decides full versus incremental per table; this playbook supplies the code. Cross-Workspace Orchestration wires the layer workspaces into one run. GitHub Integration and Deployment Pipelines and Promotion govern how these workspaces move from development to production. Shared Python Libraries packages the helpers from the incremental-load pages. Delta Partitioning and Variable Libraries cover table"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/index.html#refs","t":"Medallion Architecture","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Implement medallion lakehouse architecture in Fabric OneLake shortcuts Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric Lakehouse schemas Create, configure, and use an environment in Fabric Modern data warehouse medallion architecture in Fabric (Azure Architecture Center)"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Layers and strict isolation","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Architecture Layers and strict isolation A medallion layer becomes enforceable when it is a workspace. This page defines the five layers, makes the case for isolating them, states the cost honestly, compares the alternatives in a decision table and maps the security controls onto the design. Audience architects, security teams Focus design decisions Pages 2 of 10"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#medallion-on-fabric","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"The medallion pattern on Fabric","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"A medallion architecture organizes data into layers of increasing quality. On Fabric each layer is usually a lakehouse : Delta tables stored in OneLake, readable by Spark, the SQL analytics endpoint and Power BI. Microsoft's medallion guidance recommends a separate lakehouse for each layer, and recommends placing each lakehouse in its own workspace for better control and governance at the layer level, while noting th"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#strict-isolation","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Strict isolation","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Strict isolation means one Fabric workspace per layer: Bronze, Silver, Gold, Consumption and Delivery, repeated for each domain and each environment. It works because the workspace is the unit to which Fabric attaches the controls that matter for governance. Control Scoped to the workspace by What it means for a layer Access roles Admin, Member, Contributor and Viewer assignments Ingestion engineers can be Contributo"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#layers","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"The five layers","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Each subsection states the layer's purpose, what it contains, how it is written and read, and the most common mistake. The workspace pattern page lists the items to create in each."},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#bronze-layer","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Bronze layer: raw data","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Purpose Hold data as it arrived from the source, so any downstream layer can be rebuilt from it. Contents Source-shaped Delta tables (optionally raw files in the Files section). Only minimal technical changes: schema alignment, type casting where the source type is ambiguous, ingestion metadata such as a load timestamp. Written by Ingestion pipelines, Dataflows Gen2, Copy jobs and notebooks. Append-heavy; keep the hi"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#silver-layer","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Silver layer: cleansed and enriched data","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Purpose One validated, deduplicated, conformed version of each entity: correct types, standardized codes, soft-deleted rows handled, keys unique. Contents Delta tables at source grain, one per entity ( customers , orders , order_lines ). Shortcuts to Bronze live in a separate schema so owned and borrowed tables never mix. Written by Silver notebooks: full reloads for small tables, incremental MERGE for large ones. Re"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#gold-layer","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Gold layer: business-ready data","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Purpose Curated, modeled data at business grain: star-schema facts and dimensions, aggregates and derived measures that downstream consumers can trust. Contents Delta tables shaped for consumption: surrogate or durable keys, conformed dimensions, pre-computed attributes, partition and file layout suited to Direct Lake and SQL reads. Written by Gold notebooks, with reconciliation against Silver. Read by Consumption-la"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#consumption-layer","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Consumption layer: serve","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Purpose Present Gold data through the engine and shape each consumer needs, without changing the data's meaning. Contents A Fabric Warehouse for T-SQL workloads, semantic models for BI, and a lakehouse of Gold shortcuts for AI/ML. Split into several workspaces ( _WH , _SM , _LH qualifiers) when the teams or security models differ. Written by Warehouse developers and BI modelers. The Warehouse is loaded from Gold shor"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#delivery-layer","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Delivery layer: user-facing interfaces","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Purpose Give people reports, dashboards and apps, and give external systems an interface, without exposing the data layers. Contents Power BI reports bound to shared semantic models, dashboards and workspace apps; optionally pipelines that call REST APIs for system-to-system delivery. Written by Report authors. Read by Business users, through an app or report permission rather than a workspace role. Typical tools Pow"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#why-isolate","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Why isolate: the rationale","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Isolation is not tidiness; each reason below corresponds to a control you can test. Reason What isolation gives you How to verify it Security boundary Raw data that may hold sensitive fields sits in a workspace few people can enter; downstream layers hold masked or aggregated data Pick an analyst account; confirm it cannot see Bronze items or read Bronze tables through any engine Role separation Engineers, analysts a"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#cost-of-isolation","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"What isolation costs","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The benefits above are real, and so are the costs. Plan for them or the design will be abandoned under delivery pressure. What strict isolation multiplies. Three domains, five layers, three environments. The count is small to reason about but large to create by hand, which is why workspace creation and role assignment should be scripted. Cost What you experience Mitigation Object count Dozens of workspaces to create,"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#isolation-options","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"The options compared","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Strict isolation sits at one end of a range. The alternatives trade isolation for simplicity, and the right choice depends on who must be kept apart, how big the team is and what regulators require. Where the isolation boundary sits. Dashed lines are workspace boundaries, the only boundary that carries its own roles, capacity, Git connection and deployment stage. The schema option has no boundary between layers at al"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#decision-table","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Decision table","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Use the matrix to choose. Green cells are strengths, orange are partial, red are weaknesses. Criterion A. Workspace per layer B. Merged workspaces C. One workspace, lakehouse per layer D. One lakehouse, schemas Hard separation of duties between ingestion, transformation and BI Strong Partial Weak Weak Raw data hidden from most engineers and all analysts Strong Strong for Bronze Needs OneLake security roles Needs OneL"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#security-controls","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Security controls in this design","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Workspace roles are the coarse control. Fabric adds finer controls, and a layered design uses the finest control that fits the requirement. Controls from coarse to fine. Workspace roles decide who may enter a layer; OneLake security, SQL permissions and model security decide what they can read once inside. Use the finest control that fits and keep workspace roles tight. Requirement Control Notes Keep a team out of a "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#shortcut-security","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"How access works through a shortcut","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"A shortcut to another OneLake location is authorized at the target. For a same-tenant OneLake shortcut, the identity of the calling user is used to authorize access, and that user must have permissions at the target; the Silver role alone is not enough. The effective access is therefore the narrower of what the identity is allowed on the shortcut path and on the target path. Roles you hold in the consuming workspace "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#moving-data","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"Ways to move data between layers","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Shortcuts make the previous layer visible; something must still transform and write the next layer. This playbook uses notebooks because they handle arbitrary logic, MERGE and reconciliation. The alternatives suit narrower cases. Mechanism Good for Trade-off Shortcut plus notebook (this playbook) Any transformation, incremental MERGE, custom QA You own the code and its tests Direct cross-workspace read with a four-pa"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/architecture.html#refs","t":"Layers and strict isolation","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Implement medallion lakehouse architecture in Fabric Modern data warehouse medallion architecture in Fabric (Azure Architecture Center) Fabric governance and security baselines (Cloud Adoption Framework) Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric OneLake shortcuts Secure and manage OneLake shortcuts OneLake security roles, permissions, and scopes Lakehouse schemas Overview of materialized lake views"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Workspace pattern and naming","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Architecture Workspace pattern and naming Strict isolation only stays manageable if every workspace and item name can be generated and parsed. This page fixes the workspace pattern, the naming grammar, the abbreviation tables and the rules for environments, capacity and shortcut edges. Audience platform engineers, architects Focus structure and standards Pages 3 of 10"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#workspace-pattern","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"The workspace pattern","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"For one domain in one environment the pattern has seven workspaces. Three are data engineering layers (Bronze, Silver, Gold), three are Consumption workspaces split by purpose, and one is Delivery. Sources sit outside and feed Bronze. A separate orchestration hub ( <ENV>_ORCH_HUB ) sits above all domains and is covered in Cross-Workspace Orchestration . The workspace pattern for one domain. One domain in one environm"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#workspace-contents","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"What lives inside a layer workspace","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Every layer workspace follows the same internal shape: one primary store, the compute items that write only into that store, and the supporting items that standardize them. Keeping the shape identical makes new layers and new domains copy-paste work. What lives inside a layer workspace. The Silver lakehouse holds two kinds of tables: owned tables in the default schema and read-only shortcuts to Bronze in a separate s"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#who-works-where","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Who works where","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Assign roles to security groups, never to individuals, and give each persona the least access that lets them work. Reading an upstream layer is not the same as writing it: the engineer who builds Silver needs read access to Bronze data, not a Contributor role in the Bronze workspace. Persona Upstream layer Own layer Other layers Ingestion engineer Source systems Bronze: Contributor None Silver engineer Bronze: read a"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#naming-conventions","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Naming conventions","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Names carry meaning in three places: workspaces, items and the objects inside lakehouses. Each has a grammar, so a name can be built by a script and parsed back into its parts."},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#workspace-names","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Workspace names","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Pattern: <ENV>_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER>[_<Qualifier>] Part Values Notes <ENV> DEV , TST , PRD Environment type <DOMAIN> 2 to 5 capital letters, for example SALES , FIN , HR Matches the Fabric domain the workspace is assigned to <LAYER> BRZ , SLV , GLD , CNSM , DLV Medallion layer [_<Qualifier>] Optional, for example WH , SM , LH Splits a layer into several workspaces by purpose Examples: PRD_FIN_GLD , DEV_SALES_BRZ , TST_HR_"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#item-names","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Item names","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Pattern: <ARTIFACT>_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER>_<Function> Part Values Notes <ARTIFACT> Item type abbreviation from the table below Capital letters <DOMAIN> , <LAYER> Same values as the workspace Repeated on purpose so an item is identifiable outside its workspace, in logs and search results <Function> PascalCase purpose, for example LoadCustomers , CostAnalysis Describes what the item does, not how Examples: NB_SALES_SLV_LoadC"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#schema-and-table-names","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Schema and table names","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Object Convention Example Owned tables Default schema dbo ; lowercase snake_case entity names dbo.customers , dbo.order_lines Shortcut schema One schema per upstream layer, named with the layer abbreviation in lowercase brz in Silver, slv in Gold Shortcut name Same as the upstream table, so lineage reads by name brz.customers Gold tables dim_ and fact_ prefixes with a singular entity for dimensions dim_customer , fac"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#other-names","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Other names","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Object Pattern Example Deployment pipeline DPLY_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER> DPLY_SALES_SLV Orchestration hub workspace <ENV>_ORCH_HUB PRD_ORCH_HUB Security group <DOMAIN>-<LAYER>-<role> in your directory's convention SALES-SLV-contributors"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#abbreviations","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Abbreviation tables","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#layer-abbreviations","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Layers and environments","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Layer Abbreviation Environment Abbreviation Bronze BRZ Development DEV Silver SLV Test TST Gold GLD Production PRD Consumption CNSM Delivery DLV"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#item-abbreviations","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Item types","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Item type Abbreviation Note Lakehouse LH Warehouse WH Notebook NB Data pipeline PL Dataflow Gen2 DFL Spark job definition SJ Eventstream ES Semantic model SM Replaces the older DS for \"dataset\" Report RPT Dashboard DSH Machine learning model MDL Machine learning experiment EXP Activator RFL Kept from the original abbreviation Database (SQL or KQL) DB Put the engine in the function part KQL queryset QS Environment ENV"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#qualifier-abbreviations","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Qualifiers","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Qualifier Used with Meaning WH CNSM Warehouse workspace SM CNSM Semantic model workspace LH CNSM Lakehouse workspace for AI and ML ORCH_HUB No layer Orchestration hub"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#shortcut-rules","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Shortcut edge rules","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Naming fixes what things are called. A short set of rules fixes which things may point at which, so the shortcut graph stays a straight line from Bronze to Consumption. Which shortcut edges are allowed. Arrows show the direction of data flow. A shortcut object always lives in the downstream workspace. Keep every edge one hop long and downstream, so lineage reads as a straight line. Rule Reason A shortcut lives in the"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#environments","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Environments","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Each layer repeats in three environments. The names differ only in the first token; what differs is below. Aspect DEV TST PRD Source control Workspace connected to a Git branch; developers work here Not connected; content arrives by deployment pipeline Not connected; content arrives by deployment pipeline Data Synthetic or masked samples Representative subset, masked where sensitive Production data Human access Engin"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#capacity","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Capacity placement","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Every workspace is assigned to a capacity, and capacities are the unit of compute, billing and throttling. Separate layer workspaces let you choose where each kind of work runs. A common recommendation is dedicated capacity for the key layer workspaces and a minimal one for the hub, because the hub only runs light orchestration. Capacity follows workload shape. A workspace is assigned to one capacity, so the layer sp"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#promotion-summary","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"Promotion in one page","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Each layer workspace is promoted on its own through a deployment pipeline. The full treatment is in Deployment Pipelines and Promotion , with Git setup in GitHub Integration and the release steps in Deployment Checklist . The summary below is the part that matters for the medallion design. Promotion is per layer. Each layer has its own deployment pipeline named DPLY_ _ , so a Silver change is promoted without touchin"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/workspace-pattern.html#refs","t":"Workspace pattern and naming","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Workspaces in Microsoft Fabric Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric Domains in Microsoft Fabric Lakehouse schemas The deployment pipelines process Lakehouse git integration and deployment pipelines What is a variable library? Introduction to Git integration"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Engineering foundations Fabric notebooks in the medallion Every layer-to-layer move in this playbook is a notebook run by a pipeline. This page covers what notebooks can do, how to size and structure them, how to orchestrate several, which hooks to monitor and which hard limits apply. Audience data engineers Focus structure, limits, operations Pages 4 of 10"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#notebook-guidelines","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"What a Fabric notebook is","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"A Fabric notebook is a web-based, interactive item for developing and running Apache Spark code, with cells that mix code and Markdown. In a medallion it is the main transformation tool: it reads upstream tables through shortcuts, applies logic and writes Delta tables into its own lakehouse. A notebook is also a first-class workspace item. It can be placed under Git source control and deployed across environments wit"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#use-cases","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"Typical use cases by layer","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Use case Layer Notebook shape Batch ELT between layers: filter, conform, merge Bronze to Silver, Silver to Gold Thin notebook calling the merge engine , one table or a short list per notebook Reconciliation and quality checks Silver, Gold Read-only checks that raise on failure so the pipeline stops; see reconciliation Feature engineering and ML training Consumption lakehouse for AI and ML Exploratory notebooks and ex"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#sizing-guidelines","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"Structure and sizing guidelines","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"These guidelines protect specific limits (listed in the hard limits table) and keep runs retryable. Thin notebooks over shared libraries. The notebook holds only parameters, a call and an exit value. Business logic lives in a library attached through the Environment, so it is tested once and reused by every table. Parameters in and the exit value out stay small. Guideline Why it matters How One responsibility per not"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#workload-shapes","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"Sizing the work, not just the cluster","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Table shape Approach Notebook shape Small reference tables Full reload with the reusable function Many tables in one notebook, looped Large, slowly changing tables Incremental merge on a watermark One notebook per table or per small group Very large facts Incremental merge with a lookback window, partition-aware layout One notebook per table; see Delta Partitioning and Shared Libraries Small data manipulation outside"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#parameters-and-orchestration","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"Parameters and orchestration","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#child-notebook","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"A parameterized child notebook","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The first cell is marked as the parameter cell. Its values are defaults; a pipeline or a parent notebook overrides them at run time. The last cell hands a small result back as a string. Keep exit at the top level Do not call notebookutils.notebook.exit() inside a try and except block: the exit does not take effect when wrapped in exception handling. Put it alone in its own cell, because it also overwrites that cell's"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#run-options","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"Choosing how to call another notebook","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Mechanism Session and scope Parallel Best for Pipeline notebook activity Its own Spark session per activity unless you configure session sharing; separate retry, timeout and monitoring row per activity Yes, by pipeline design Layer-level steps, anything that needs isolation or visible per-step status %run Same session; variables of the referenced notebook become available; same workspace only; depth up to five No Sha"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#run-multiple","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"An orchestrator with a dependency DAG","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Orchestrating notebooks inside one session. Dimensions load first and in parallel, then facts in order. All children share the orchestrator's Spark session, so the pool must be sized for the concurrency you set. Timeouts and retries are per activity in the DAG. The DAG runs the two dimension loads in parallel, then the order facts, then the order lines. validateDAG catches duplicate names, missing dependencies and cy"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#monitoring-hooks","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"Monitoring and governance hooks","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Hook What it shows Use it for Notebook run snapshot Cell outputs and status of that run, kept with the run history Debugging a single failed run Spark application details and Spark UI Jobs, stages, executors, skew Performance investigation Fabric Capacity Metrics app Capacity use, throttling and backlog on the capacity that runs the notebooks Sizing capacity, spotting load windows that collide Workspace Spark setting"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#do-and-dont","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"Do and do not","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Do Keep rendered outputs tiny and behind a debug flag Checkpoint data to Delta between steps Modularize through libraries in an Environment Keep parameters small and typed as simple values Stop sessions at the end of automated runs Watch executed-cell counts and nesting depth Set per-cell timeouts and retries per DAG activity Test with the identity that the schedule will use Do not Pass large JSON payloads into noteb"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#hard-limits","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"Hard limits to plan around","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Limits are grouped by where they apply. The figure shows how they stack for a notebook run by a pipeline; the table gives values and the design response. Where each limit bites. A notebook run inherits the limits of everything around it. When a pipeline calls a notebook, the pipeline activity limits sit on top of the notebook limits, and the smallest applicable value is the one to design to. Limit Value Design respon"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/notebooks.html#refs","t":"Fabric notebooks in the medallion","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Develop, execute, and manage notebooks NotebookUtils notebook run and orchestration Fabric Notebook known limitation Notebook activity in pipelines Data Factory limitations overview Activity overview Configure high concurrency mode for notebooks Use Python experience on Notebook Manage the resources in a Fabric environment Workspaces in Microsoft Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html","t":"The Environment item","s":"The Environment item","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Engineering foundations The Environment item An Environment item is the execution contract for a layer: the runtime, Spark properties, compute sizes and libraries that every notebook in that workspace runs with. Set it once as the workspace default and the setup snippets disappear from notebooks. Audience platform and data engineers Focus Spark configuration Pages 5 of 10"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#environment-item","t":"The Environment item","s":"What the Environment item is","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"An Environment is a workspace item that defines Spark session configuration for notebooks and Spark job definitions. You can run without one, using the workspace-level Spark settings, but an Environment makes the configuration reusable, governed and versionable as a single artifact. It replaces the session-builder snippet that otherwise gets pasted at the top of every notebook, where it drifts. What an Environment it"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#runtime-choice","t":"The Environment item","s":"Choosing a runtime","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The runtime sets the Spark and Delta Lake versions, the operating system and the preinstalled libraries. The environment pins it, so a change of runtime is an explicit, reviewable change that you publish and promote like any other. Runtime Apache Spark Delta Lake Consider it when 1.3 3.5 3.2 Your libraries and code are validated on it, and you need continuity while you test the next runtime 2.0 4.1 4.2 on the runtime"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#environment-steps","t":"The Environment item","s":"Create and publish an Environment","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Prepare the Spark properties file. Choose the variant from the next section and keep it in the repository next to the code. Create the item. In the layer workspace, select New item, choose Environment, and name it with the item convention, for example ENV_SALES_SLV_Spark . Select the runtime. On the Home tab, pick the runtime from the Runtime dropdown. Configure compute. Choose the pool and, if the workspace admin ha"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#spark-properties","t":"The Environment item","s":"Spark properties","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Two variants cover the layers. Both are flat key and value pairs. The baseline sets the Parquet date and timestamp behavior; the merge-heavy variant adds two settings for merge-heavy and scan-heavy loads. Property What it does Trade-off spark.sql.parquet.datetimeRebaseModeInRead and ...InWrite Controls how DATE and TIMESTAMP values are read and written when files may come from older engines that used a hybrid Julian "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#resource-profiles","t":"The Environment item","s":"Resource profiles per layer","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Fabric Spark also provides resource profiles that preset Delta write behavior for a workload shape, selected with spark.fabric.resourceProfile in the Environment or at run time. A runtime setting takes precedence over the environment. Profile Intent Starting point for writeHeavy Frequent ingestion and writes, including ETL; the default profile for a workspace unless you change it Bronze and Silver readHeavyForSpark S"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#libraries","t":"The Environment item","s":"Libraries and publish modes","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Each runtime ships built-in libraries. The Environment adds public libraries from PyPI or Conda and custom libraries you build, which is where the shared merge engine and helpers belong. Package them as a wheel, version them, and attach them here so every notebook imports the same code; see Shared Libraries . Publish mode Publish time Session start Use for Quick About 5 seconds Libraries install when the session star"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#attach-rules","t":"The Environment item","s":"Attaching and inheriting","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"A notebook or Spark job definition can attach an Environment directly, or use Workspace default. With Set default environment off, Workspace default means the workspace-level Spark settings; with it on, Workspace default means the chosen environment item. Which configuration a session gets. Resolution goes from the most specific setting to the least specific. Session-level commands such as %%configure change the sess"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#git-and-deployment","t":"The Environment item","s":"Git and deployment pipelines","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Environment across stages. Git changes land in the staging state and need a publish. Deployment pipelines carry the published configuration onward without another publish. Custom pool references are workspace-scoped and do not carry over. Topic Behavior What Git tracks Libraries (public as a YAML representation, custom with their source files) and the Spark compute section including runtime and properties. Resources "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#verify-environment","t":"The Environment item","s":"Verify the environment","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Run a short cell at the top of a smoke-test notebook in each environment and stage. It prints the Spark version and fails if any expected property is missing or different. Symptom Likely cause Fix Properties are not applied Changes saved but not published, or default environment not turned on Publish; check Set default environment in the workspace Spark settings Publish fails A library or compute setting is incompati"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/environment.html#refs","t":"The Environment item","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Create, configure, and use an environment in Fabric Compute management in Fabric environments Library management in Fabric environments Manage the resources in a Fabric environment Fabric environment Git integration and deployment pipeline Migrate libraries and properties to a default environment Apache Spark runtime in Fabric Apache Spark runtime lifecycle in Fabric Runtime 1.3 in Fabric Runtime 2.0 in Fabric Config"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Bronze to Silver","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Implementation Bronze to Silver Moving data from Bronze to Silver takes two actions: make the Bronze tables visible in the Silver lakehouse with shortcuts, then run a notebook that filters, conforms and writes owned Silver tables. This page covers both, how to choose a load pattern per table, and the reusable full-load function with its per-table variants. Audience data engineers Focus shortcuts, full l"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#two-actions","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Two actions","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The Silver workspace never writes to Bronze and never copies Bronze tables. It borrows them through shortcuts in a dedicated schema, and the Silver notebook is the only process that materializes data in the Silver lakehouse. Keeping borrowed and owned tables in separate schemas means anyone reading the lakehouse explorer can tell at a glance which tables Silver is responsible for. Bronze to Silver in two actions. The"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#create-shortcuts","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Create shortcuts to the Bronze lakehouse","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"A OneLake shortcut is a pointer, not a copy. Reads through it go to the Bronze Delta files, so Silver always sees the current Bronze version and you pay for storage once. In a schema-enabled lakehouse you have two ways to create them. Option What you get Use when Schema shortcut A new schema ( brz ) that references a whole schema of the Bronze lakehouse; tables added to Bronze appear automatically Silver reads most o"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#shortcut-naming","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Naming the shortcuts","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Name each shortcut exactly like the Bronze table it points to and let the schema carry the meaning: brz.customers in Silver is the Bronze customers table. An earlier version of this pattern encoded the direction in a long prefix on every shortcut name (a \"shortcut to Bronze from Silver\" prefix). The schema makes the prefix redundant, keeps names short enough to read in lineage views, and means the notebook code diffe"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#shortcut-steps","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Steps in the lakehouse explorer","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Open the Silver lakehouse In DEV_SALES_SLV , open LH_SALES_SLV . Start a shortcut under Tables In the explorer, open the menu next to Tables (or next to an existing schema) and choose New schema shortcut for a whole schema, or New table shortcut for individual tables. Shortcuts for Delta tables belong under Tables; shortcuts under Files are treated as folders and are not registered as tables. Choose Microsoft OneLake"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#shortcut-automation","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Creating shortcuts as code","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"For more than a handful of tables, or to recreate the same shortcuts in test and production, create them with the OneLake shortcuts REST API from a deployment script or a notebook. The request below creates brz.customers in the Silver lakehouse; path is where the shortcut lives, target.oneLake.path is the Bronze table. Shortcuts are part of the lakehouse definition in Git integration and deployment pipelines, and dep"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#notebook-setup","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Set up the load notebook","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Create the notebook in the Silver workspace Name it by the standard, one notebook per table or per small group of related tables: NB_SALES_SLV_LoadCustomers , NB_SALES_SLV_LoadReference . Attach the Silver lakehouse as the default Unqualified names ( dbo.customers , brz.customers ) then resolve against LH_SALES_SLV . Keep the default lakehouse the same in every notebook of a run; child notebooks with a different defa"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#load-patterns","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Choosing a load pattern","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Two patterns cover nearly every table. A full load (destructive load) replaces the target with the current source content on every run. An incremental load applies only the rows that changed since the last run, using a watermark and a key. The choice is per table, and the Data Load Strategy playbook gives the full decision framework; the summary is below. Choosing a load pattern. Size is a guide, not a rule: reload w"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#full-load-function","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"The reusable full-load function","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Where a full load is appropriate, use one function for every table rather than writing a new cell per table. The same function works in any layer: Silver reading brz , Gold reading slv . The reusable full-load function. Overwrite is a single Delta commit, so readers see the old table or the new one and never a half-loaded state. The validation step runs a full scan on each side and belongs to development only. Except"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#full-load-changes","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"What changed from the original function","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Original behavior Problem Now Caught every exception and printed it A failed load reported success to the pipeline; nobody noticed until a report was wrong Exceptions propagate; the notebook activity fails and the run history shows why Built a SQL string, cached the DataFrame and counted it on every run Cache and count cost a full scan and memory for a number only development needs Reads with spark.table , filters wi"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#per-table-method","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Per-table method: Spark SQL or PySpark","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Some tables need more than a filter: renamed columns, derived values, a join to a reference table. Write those loads individually, in Spark SQL or PySpark, and follow the same four steps during development. Count the rows in the source shortcut. Count the rows the filter and transformation logic will keep, so you know what to expect. Transform and load the Silver table. Count the target and confirm it equals the numb"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#dev-checks","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"From development counts to automated reconciliation","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Row-count cells are a development tool. Once a load is tested, switch them off and rely on automated checks: a reconciliation step after each load (see reconciliation ), and post-deployment validation in test and production run as a pipeline step or from your CI/CD workflow. The QA Validation Frameworks playbook covers the check catalogue; the deployment playbook covers where the checks run during promotion."},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#troubleshooting","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix Shortcut appears under Tables as an unidentified folder The target is not a Delta table, or the shortcut points at a folder above the table Point the shortcut at the table folder itself, or at a schema for a schema shortcut Works interactively, fails under the pipeline with an access error The pipeline runs the notebook with a different identity that has no access to Bronze Grant that identit"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/bronze-to-silver.html#refs","t":"Bronze to Silver","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"OneLake shortcuts Create a OneLake shortcut Lakehouse schemas OneLake Shortcuts: Create Shortcut (REST API) Secure and manage OneLake shortcuts Load data into your lakehouse with a notebook Delta Lake: table batch reads and writes"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Incremental loads and MERGE","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Implementation Incremental loads and MERGE An incremental load applies only what changed since the last run. It is cheaper than a full reload and much easier to get subtly wrong. This page explains the two mechanisms that find the changes, gives a reusable Delta MERGE utility for Silver, shows how deletes reach Silver and lists the failure modes to design for. Audience data engineers Focus watermarks, M"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#watermark","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"The watermark column","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"A watermark column , or high-water mark, is a column in the source that records when a row was created or last changed, typically a last_modified_utc timestamp the source application sets on every insert and update. By remembering the highest value already loaded, a load can ask the source for only the rows above it. Monotonic Values only increase as rows change. A column that can be set backwards, or that a bulk fix"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#primary-key","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"The primary key approach","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Where there is no reliable modified timestamp but the table has a sequential, ever-increasing key such as an identity column, the key itself can act as the mark. Each run loads rows whose key is above the highest key already loaded. Example: the Contoso customers source has an auto-incrementing customer_id . The initial load takes every row; the highest id is 5000, so 5000 is stored. The next run asks for customer_id"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#watermark-vs-key","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Watermark column or primary key","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Feature Watermark column ( last_modified_utc ) Increasing key ( customer_id ) Change data capture or change feed Captures inserts Yes Yes Yes Captures updates Yes, if every change sets it No Yes Captures physical deletes No No Yes, as delete operations Depends on Source discipline in setting the column Keys never reused or assigned out of order CDC enabled at the source, or Delta change data feed on the upstream tabl"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#mark-storage","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Where to keep the mark","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Option How Strength Weakness Derived from the target max(coalesce(watermark columns)) on the Silver table at the start of each run Nothing extra to maintain; cannot drift from the data Needs the watermark column kept in the target; a full scan of that column unless file statistics answer it; deleting the newest rows moves the mark back Control table A Delta or warehouse table keyed by source table; read before, writt"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#merge-utility","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"The reusable incremental MERGE utility","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The utility performs an SCD type 1 upsert: the target keeps one current row per key and changes overwrite it. It is table-agnostic; everything table-specific is a parameter. Put the helpers and the function in one module, ideally a library attached through the Environment (see Shared Python Libraries ), or in the first code cell of the notebook while you develop. Inside the incremental MERGE utility. Steps 1 to 4 dec"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#merge-helpers","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Helper functions","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#merge-function","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"The function","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#merge-parameters","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Parameters","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Parameter Meaning Guidance key_candidates Ordered list of key column sets; the first set fully present in the source is the MERGE key List the true grain first. A fallback key that is not unique merges different rows together; prefer failing to guessing watermark_cols Ordered list; the first non-null value per row becomes _wm Keep the most reliable column first; every column in the list must mean \"last changed\" row_f"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#merge-usage","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Using it for several tables","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The per-table settings live in one dictionary, so adding a table is a configuration change, not new code. The examples show the three accepted filter shapes. The returned dictionary has mode ( bootstrap , merge or noop ), rows_in_slice , null_key_rows , last_watermark , new_watermark and, for a merge, the inserted, updated and deleted counts read from the Delta commit history. Write it to a run log table (see monitor"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#deletes","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Handling deletes","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"A watermark filter only returns rows that still exist. A row deleted in the source simply stops appearing, and Silver keeps it forever unless something tells Silver it is gone. Decide per table which signal you have. Three ways to see a delete. A watermark only sees rows that still exist. If the source deletes physically, you need one of these signals; without one, Silver keeps rows the source no longer has."},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#soft-deletes","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Soft-delete flags and change feeds","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"If the source marks deletions ( is_deleted = true ) or Bronze lands CDC records with an operation column, pass that as delete_condition . The flagged row carries a new watermark, so it arrives in the slice like any change; the MERGE deletes the matching Silver row and never inserts a flagged key. Do not filter flagged rows out with row_filter , or the delete never reaches the MERGE. If downstream layers need to see d"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#key-snapshot","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Key snapshot reconciliation","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"When the source deletes physically and there is no CDC, compare the full set of current source keys with Silver on a schedule, for example nightly, and flag or delete the Silver rows whose key has disappeared. Delta's WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE clause does this in one MERGE. Guard against a truncated key list If the Bronze extract failed halfway, the key list is short and the reconciliation flags or deletes rows that"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#failure-modes","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Failure modes","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Symptom Cause Design response Rows missing in Silver that exist in Bronze Rows committed late with a watermark below the stored mark lookback_minutes sized to the source's commit lag; periodic reconciliation Updates missing for some rows A batch job or manual fix in the source did not set the watermark Agree the contract with the source owner; schedule a periodic full comparison for affected tables Rows with a NULL w"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#operations","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"Keeping merged tables healthy","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Compaction. Frequent small MERGEs create small files. Run OPTIMIZE on a schedule, or rely on the table maintenance features of your runtime; see Delta Partitioning for layout. Retention. VACUUM removes files older than the retention period. Keep enough history for time travel during incident analysis and for change data feed readers. Low shuffle merge. Fabric's low shuffle merge setting reduces the rewrite cost of ME"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/incremental-merge.html#refs","t":"Incremental loads and MERGE","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Delta Lake: table deletes, updates and merges Delta Lake Python API (DeltaTable, DeltaMergeBuilder) Delta change data feed Low shuffle merge optimization on Delta tables Lakehouse table maintenance Apache Spark runtimes in Fabric Incrementally load data from a warehouse to a lakehouse (watermark pattern)"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Silver to Gold","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Implementation Silver to Gold Gold turns conformed Silver entities into facts and dimensions at business grain. The wiring repeats the Bronze to Silver pattern, a shortcut schema and a notebook, but Gold adds two things: business transforms that must be applied identically on every run, and reconciliation that proves the incremental loads still add up. Audience data engineers, analytics engineers Focus "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#gold-wiring","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Wiring the Gold workspace","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Silver to Gold with a reconciliation gate. The notebook is the only writer of the Gold tables. Reconciliation runs after the merge and raises on a mismatch, so a bad load stops the pipeline before the Consumption layer reads it. Create the Silver shortcuts in Gold In DEV_SALES_GLD , open LH_SALES_GLD and create a schema shortcut named slv to the Silver lakehouse's dbo schema, or table shortcuts in an slv schema, exac"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#full-load","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Full load: Spark SQL and PySpark","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The example builds dbo.fact_order_lines from the Silver order_lines table. Each Silver order line carries up to three charge slots, each with a type and an amount ( charge_1_type , charge_1_amount and so on). Gold pivots those slots into named measures, discount, shipping, tax and handling, so reports never need to know which slot held which charge. This positional-to-named pivot is a common Gold transform; the same "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#incremental-engine","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Incremental load: a config-driven engine","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"For large facts, Gold reuses the Silver incremental MERGE utility and adds three things around it: a shared watermark list, a registry of per-table transforms and settings, and reconciliation. Run the cells in this order: helpers and the Silver utility, transforms, configuration, engine, then the calls. The Gold engine, one table at a time. The engine itself has no table-specific code. Everything that differs between"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#global-watermark","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Global watermark and transforms","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"WATERMARK_COLS_DEFAULT is the priority list every Gold table shares: the first non-null value is the row's last-changed time. A single list works because Silver standardized these columns; if a table needs a different list, pass watermark_cols for that call. Transform functions take a DataFrame of changed Silver rows and return the Gold shape. Because the engine applies them to the incremental slice only, they must b"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#table-config","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Per-table configuration","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"source_table Silver shortcut to read, in the slv schema. key_candidates Ordered key sets, as in the Silver utility. The first must be the true grain of the Gold table. transform_fn The row-local transform for this table. delete_condition How Gold recognizes rows to remove; here the Silver soft-delete flag. qa_sum_columns Measures whose totals reconciliation compares. Choose the ones the business reports on. Optional "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#engine-code","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"The engine","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The loop runs every configured table, collects the metrics and raises once at the end if any reconciliation failed. That way one bad table does not hide the results of the others, and the pipeline activity still fails. To fail fast instead, pass raise_on_mismatch=True to qa_reconcile ."},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#reconciliation","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Reconciliation","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Reconciliation answers one question: does the Gold table hold what a full rebuild from Silver would produce? The incremental engine is correct only if every slice was complete, every key unique and every delete applied. Reconciliation catches a failure in any of those without knowing which one happened. What reconciliation compares. The expected side repeats the engine's logic over the whole source, so a match means "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#what-to-compare","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"What to compare","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Check Expected side Catches Cost Row count Latest row per key, minus deleted keys Missed slices, missed deletes, duplicates Full source scan Sum of key measures Same rows, transformed Missed updates, transform drift, type truncation Same scan, extra aggregates Duplicate keys Zero Wrong key choice, concurrent writers Group by key on the target Orphan foreign keys Zero, or a known unknown-member count Facts loaded befo"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#reconcile-changes","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Corrected from the original QA","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Original check Problem Now Raw source row count against target row count The two differ by design (duplicates per key, deleted rows), so a difference is normal and teams learn to ignore it Expected count is derived with the same deduplication and delete rules as the load Sum of a value column joined to a dimension filtered on one unit type Tied to one source's dimension and column names; a dimension change altered bo"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#reconcile-operations","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Running reconciliation in production","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Cost. The expected side scans the whole Silver table. For large facts run it nightly or per release with run_qa=False on intraday runs, or restrict both sides to recent partitions with the same filter. Tolerance. Integer counts must match exactly. Sums of decimals should match exactly too; the tolerance in the code only absorbs floating-point conversion in the comparison. Timing. If Silver is loading while Gold recon"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#gold-layout","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"Gold table layout for consumers","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Gold tables feed the Warehouse, Direct Lake semantic models and SQL readers, so their file layout affects every query. Compact after incremental loads; many small files slow Direct Lake framing and SQL scans and count against Direct Lake guardrails. Keep V-Order on for tables that Power BI reads; see the resource profile guidance on the Environment page . Partition only large tables, on a low-cardinality column consu"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/silver-to-gold.html#refs","t":"Silver to Gold","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Implement medallion lakehouse architecture in Fabric Lakehouse schemas Delta Lake: table deletes, updates and merges Delta Lake table optimization and V-Order Lakehouse table maintenance PySpark functions.when"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Consumption and delivery","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Implementation Consumption and delivery Consumption presents Gold to each kind of reader in the shape and engine it needs, without changing what the data means. Delivery gives business users reports and apps without giving them the data layers. This page builds both, from the warehouse load to the published app. Audience warehouse developers, BI developers, data scientists Focus serve and publish Pages "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#consumption-overview","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Three consumption paths","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"From Gold to the people who use it. Each dashed band is a workspace. Only shortcuts cross from Gold; the warehouse is loaded inside its own workspace, the semantic model is authored in its own workspace and reports in Delivery bind to it without copying data. Path Workspace Items Serves Warehouse DEV_SALES_CNSM_WH LH_SALES_CNSM (Gold shortcuts only), WH_SALES_CNSM T-SQL users and tools, typed and constrained tables, "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#warehouse","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Warehouse from Gold shortcuts","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"A warehouse can query a lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint in the same workspace with three-part names. It cannot reach a lakehouse in another workspace that way. That is why the warehouse workspace has its own lakehouse whose only content is shortcuts to Gold. Create the shortcut lakehouse In DEV_SALES_CNSM_WH , create LH_SALES_CNSM (schema-enabled). It holds no data of its own. Create the Gold shortcuts Create table "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#warehouse-ddl","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Create tables with explicit types","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"String columns read through a lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint appear as varchar(8000) . A table created with SELECT ... INTO or CREATE TABLE AS SELECT from a shortcut inherits those types, and the semantic model and every client then see 8,000-character columns. Declare the serving schema instead. Primary keys in a Fabric warehouse must be NONCLUSTERED and NOT ENFORCED . They document the grain and help the optimize"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#warehouse-load","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Load patterns","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Warehouse load patterns compared. The first pattern is the one this playbook started from. It works, but the table's types come from whatever the SQL endpoint reports, and outside a transaction readers can see an empty table. The other three fix one or both problems. The original pattern was a single script per table: if the table does not exist, create it with SELECT ... INTO (renaming columns to display names with "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#semantic-models","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Semantic models on the warehouse","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Fabric no longer creates a default semantic model for every warehouse or lakehouse. Create the models you need explicitly, in the semantic model workspace, and give each a name that says what it models. Open Power BI Desktop and sign in Sign in with your organizational account for the tenant that holds the workspaces. Open the OneLake catalog From the Home ribbon open the OneLake catalog and filter to warehouses. Sel"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#direct-lake","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Direct Lake variants","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Direct Lake loads column data straight from Delta files in OneLake into the model's memory: close to Import performance, without a scheduled import refresh. A refresh of a Direct Lake model is framing: the model repoints to the latest Delta version, which takes seconds. There are two variants, and the difference matters for security and for what happens when a query cannot be served in Direct Lake mode. The two Direc"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#direct-lake-choice","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Which variant for this design","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Default to Direct Lake on OneLake when the model's security is defined in the model itself or in OneLake security, and when the model benefits from tables in more than one item. Use Direct Lake on SQL endpoints when you rely on row-level, column-level or object-level security defined in the warehouse, or need views. Plan for fallback: set the Direct Lake behavior so that unexpected fallback is visible, and test perfo"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#ml-lakehouse","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"A Gold lakehouse for AI and ML","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Data scientists work best in Spark against Delta tables. Give them a lakehouse of Gold shortcuts in their own workspace rather than access to Gold itself: they read current Gold data, write features and experiment outputs to their own tables, and cannot change Gold. Create the lakehouse In DEV_SALES_CNSM_LH , use New item , choose Lakehouse and name it LH_SALES_CNSM_ML . Create a Gold shortcut schema Create a schema "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#delivery","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Publishing to the Delivery layer","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Thin reports in Delivery, one shared model. Reports carry no data of their own. Every report in Delivery binds to the same semantic model, so a measure is defined once. Users reach the model through the app, never through a workspace role. Start a report on the shared model In Power BI Desktop, open the OneLake catalog, filter to semantic models, select SM_SALES_CNSM_Orders in DEV_SALES_CNSM_SM and connect. The repor"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#consumption-checklist","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"Checklist","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Decided per domain whether the warehouse is needed, and recorded why Shortcut lakehouse in the warehouse workspace holds shortcuts only Warehouse tables created from versioned DDL with explicit types and NOT ENFORCED keys Each table load wrapped in a procedure, transactional, and called after a successful Gold load Semantic models created explicitly in the semantic model workspace and under Git Direct Lake variant ch"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/consumption-delivery.html#refs","t":"Consumption and delivery","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Query the SQL analytics endpoint or Warehouse T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse Transactions in Warehouse tables Data types in Fabric Data Warehouse Primary keys, foreign keys and unique keys in Fabric Data Warehouse MERGE (Transact-SQL) Create a semantic model from a warehouse Direct Lake overview How Direct Lake works Direct Lake in Power BI Desktop Publish an app in Power BI"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Appendix: loader internals","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Playbook 03 · Reference Appendix: loader internals Reference material for engineers who maintain the loaders: a generic full-load snapshot function with usage examples for filters and for transformations, and a walkthrough of the Silver and Gold merge engines that explains each step and what changed from the original code. Audience data engineers Focus code walkthrough Pages 10 of 10"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#generic-full-load","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Generic full load with filters","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"full_load_overwrite is a full-replace (snapshot) loader: a safer CREATE TABLE AS SELECT written in PySpark, with guardrails, an optional filter, an optional transform, schema control and optional metrics. It extends the simpler full-load function for tables that need more than a filter. The generic full-load loader. Dashed steps are optional. Filtering happens before the transform so the transform sees fewer rows, an"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#full-load-parameters","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Key parameters","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Parameter What it does Notes row_filter Predicate applied before the transform: a SQL string ( \"is_active = true\" ), a Column ( F.col(\"is_active\") & (F.col(\"score\") > 0) ) or a callable returning a Column Anything else raises a TypeError before reading data transform_fn A function from DataFrame to DataFrame; the default is the identity function Keeps the wiring stable while business logic changes, and is testable on"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#full-load-semantics","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Semantics","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Idempotent. The whole table is replaced in one Delta commit, so a retry produces the same result and readers never see a partial load. Fail fast. Missing required columns, a missing projected column and a malformed filter all raise before anything is written. Filter before transform. Rows the filter removes never reach the transform, which saves work and lets Spark push the predicate down to the file scan."},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#full-load-filter-examples","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Usage: filters and projection","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Generalized examples The original examples used three-part catalog names and a hard-coded calendar date in the filter. Fabric lakehouse notebooks address tables by schema and table name within the default lakehouse (or a four-part name across workspaces), and a fixed date in a production filter turns a rolling window into a shrinking one, so the examples use relative date functions."},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#generic-full-load-transform","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Generic full load with transformation","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"The same function covers tables that need shaping. transform_fn is the injection point: pass a named function for anything you want to unit test, or a lambda for a single derived column. The type hint Callable[[DataFrame], DataFrame] means exactly that: a function that takes a Spark DataFrame and returns one. Need Use Exact output columns, fail if any is missing select_cols after the transform Exact output types, tol"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#silver-engine","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Silver incremental MERGE: line by line","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"This section explains incremental_merge_scd1 and its helpers as shown on the incremental loads page . The function slices increments with a prioritized watermark list, picks a key from candidate sets, filters early, keeps the latest row per key and applies one MERGE that updates non-key columns, inserts new keys and, when configured, deletes flagged keys."},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#silver-helpers","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Helpers","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Function What it does Why it is written this way table_exists spark.catalog.tableExists(name) Public API, accepts schema-qualified names. The original called the internal JVM session, which can break between runtimes. resolve_columns Maps wanted names to the DataFrame's actual names, case-insensitively Spark resolves names case-insensitively by default, but MERGE expressions and dictionaries should use the real casin"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#silver-control-flow","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Control flow","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Existence. Checks the target; raises if it is missing and create_if_missing is off. Mark. Uses watermark_value if given (string or datetime), otherwise derives it from the target. Subtracts lookback_minutes . Slice. build_slice filters first so predicates can be pushed down, then keeps rows with _wm above the mark. One aggregate pass. Counts rows, finds the new maximum watermark and counts null keys in a single job. "},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#silver-session-settings","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Session settings the original changed","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Setting Original Now spark.microsoft.delta.merge.lowShuffle.enabled Set to true in every call Not set; documented as on by default and configured once in the Environment if needed spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled Set to true for the session Not set; schema evolution is explicit through evolve_schema spark.sql.files.maxPartitionBytes Set to 512m in every call Not set; a read-split size belongs in the En"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#silver-usage-notes","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Notes on the usage blocks","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Grain first. Put the true grain first in key_candidates . A fallback key that is coarser than the grain (a product and date for a table whose grain also includes the store) merges distinct rows; prefer an error. Filters and deletes. A filter such as \"not deleted\" belongs in Gold, or in delete_condition , not in row_filter ; otherwise deleted rows never reach the MERGE. Counts. count() is an exact full scan. The utili"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#gold-engine","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Gold engine: line by line","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#gold-constants","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Constants and transforms","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"WATERMARK_COLS_DEFAULT is the update-timestamp priority list shared by all Gold tables. The engine coalesces these in order and takes the first non-null value as the row's last-changed time, which lets the pipeline slice only new and changed rows and survive tables that lack the preferred column. If upstream does not set the first column reliably, the engine will miss or repeat changes; keep the list, and push the so"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#gold-config","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"TABLE_CONFIG","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"A dictionary from Gold table name to its settings: source, key candidates, transform, delete condition and the measures to reconcile. One generic function handles many tables without copied code; adding a table means adding an entry."},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#gold-functions","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"The two functions","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Step incremental_merge_with_qa 1 Merges the table's configuration with any overrides passed to the call. 2 Builds the shared arguments (filter, transform, delete condition, key normalization, tie-break) once, so the merge and the reconciliation use identical rules. 3 Calls the Silver utility, which slices, deduplicates, transforms and merges. 4 If run_qa , calls qa_reconcile and attaches the result under qa . Step qa"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#gold-design","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"Design features","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Incremental by design: watermark slicing and latest-per-key selection. Idempotent MERGE: safe to rerun; only changes after the mark, minus the lookback, are considered. Loose coupling: each table's shape and grain live in configuration; the engine does not know which table it is loading. Observable: merge metrics from the commit history and reconciliation results in one dictionary, ready for a run log table. Key take"},{"u":"playbooks/03-medallion-architecture/appendix.html#refs","t":"Appendix: loader internals","s":"References","p":"Medallion Architecture","x":"Delta Lake: table deletes, updates and merges Delta Lake: table batch reads and writes (overwriteSchema, partitioning) Delta Lake: table utility commands (history, operation metrics) PySpark Catalog.tableExists PySpark functions.row_number Low shuffle merge optimization on Delta tables"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Playbook 04 · Data engineering Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke) A pattern for running end-to-end data workflows that span many Fabric workspaces. One hub workspace owns order, retries and the run log; domain or layer spokes own their data and their pipelines. This playbook covers the design, the Invoke pipeline activity that makes it work, control flow, central monitoring, CI/CD, the alternative"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html#problem","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"The problem this pattern solves","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"A platform with one workspace per layer or per domain (see the medallion architecture playbook ) gives every team its own pipelines, schedules and capacity. It also removes the one thing a monolith gave you for free: a single place that knows what must run before what. Without a coordinating layer, teams fall back to schedules that guess. Silver is scheduled \"an hour after Bronze, because Bronze usually finishes by t"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html#how-it-works","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"How hub and spoke works in Fabric","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Hub and spoke in Fabric. The master pipeline invokes each spoke pipeline in turn and waits for it to finish; the hub owns order, retries and the run log, while every spoke keeps its own data, capacity and deployment lifecycle. Hub workspace A dedicated workspace named <ENV>_ORCH_HUB . It holds master pipelines, shared connections, the run log and alert rules. It does not hold business data or transformation logic. Sp"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html#when","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"When to use it, and when not to","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Use hub and spoke when Three or more workspaces have ordering dependencies between their jobs. Different teams own different spokes and need to deploy independently. One place must answer \"did the nightly load finish, and if not, where did it stop?\" Spokes run on separate capacities for isolation or chargeback. The same orchestration must run in DEV, TST and PRD with different targets. Prefer something simpler when E"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html#platform-state","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"What the platform supports","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Earlier guidance for this pattern described a \"preview\" Invoke pipeline activity with a list of bugs and a heavy reliance on REST workarounds. The platform has moved on, and several workarounds now have first-class replacements. Use this table as the starting point, then follow the link for the detail. Capability What it gives a hub-and-spoke design Status Detail Invoke pipeline (new activity) Cross-workspace invocat"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html#prerequisites","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"Audience and prerequisites","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"A Fabric capacity (trial or paid) and permission to create at least three workspaces on it. Working knowledge of Fabric pipelines: Copy data, Lookup, parameters and the expression language. For the workshop: Admin on the three workspaces, and either a tenant admin who can enable the service-principal API setting or an account you can sign in with for a user connection. For the CI/CD material: familiarity with Git int"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html#outcomes","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"What you will be able to do","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Decide whether hub and spoke is the right orchestration pattern, and cut spokes by domain, layer or both. Configure the Invoke pipeline activity with a durable identity, correct permissions and explicit parent context. Build sequential, parallel, conditional and metadata-driven flows with retries, timeouts and failure policy that surface errors instead of hiding them. Centralize run logging and alerting so one dashbo"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html#map","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"How this playbook is organized","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"01 Hub-and-spoke architecture Roles, the hub-spoke contract, how to cut spokes, capacity and permissions. Design 15 min 02 The Invoke pipeline activity Legacy versus new, identities, wait on completion, limits and the REST fallback. Reference 20 min 03 Control flow and failure handling Sequence, fan-out, ForEach, retries, failure semantics and safe re-runs. Patterns 20 min 04 Logging, monitoring and CI/CD Central run"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html#related","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"How it fits with other playbooks","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Medallion architecture decides which layers get their own workspace; this playbook decides how those workspaces run in order. Data load strategy defines what a spoke pipeline does (full, incremental, merge); the hub only decides when and in which order. Control-table pipelines supplies the metadata that drives the ForEach fan-out described here. Variable libraries and deployment pipelines solve the environment-specif"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/index.html#refs","t":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","s":"References","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Invoke pipeline activity Activity overview Choose how to monitor pipeline runs in Fabric Data Factory Data Factory limitations overview Choose a Microsoft Fabric deployment pattern (Azure Architecture Center)"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Playbook 04 · Hub and spoke Hub-and-spoke architecture What the hub and the spokes each own, why the contract between them is the part that needs governance, how to decide where to draw spoke boundaries, and how capacity and permissions fit together."},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html#network-paradigm","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"From network topology to data topology","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Hub and spoke comes from network design: a central hub virtual network hosts shared services such as the firewall, DNS and gateways, and workload networks (spokes) connect to it. Policy is centralized, workloads are isolated, and adding a spoke does not disturb the others. The same shape maps well onto Fabric workspaces, with one important difference. Same shape, different payload. A network hub carries the traffic; "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html#roles","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"Roles and responsibilities","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The pattern works only if both sides stay on their half of the line. The most common failure is a hub that slowly accumulates transformation logic, or a spoke that quietly starts calling its neighbour. Hub workspace Spoke workspace Owns Execution order, dependency gates, retry and failure policy, run logging, notifications Domain or layer data, transformation logic, data quality rules, its own deployment Contains Mas"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html#contract","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"The contract between hub and spokes","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Hub and spoke is often described as giving teams full autonomy. In Fabric it gives managed decentralization . The hub's Invoke pipeline activity references a spoke pipeline by workspace and item ID and passes parameters by name. That is tight coupling: rename a parameter or recreate a pipeline and the hub breaks. Spoke teams therefore have to treat the signature of a spoke pipeline the way an API owner treats an endp"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html#cutting-spokes","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"How to cut spokes","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"There is no single correct granularity. Choose the boundary that matches how you deploy and who owns the work, then let the hub express the dependencies. The naming convention <ENV>_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER> supports both layouts below. Where the sequencing logic lives. With layer spokes the hub holds the medallion order; with domain spokes the order moves inside each domain's own pipeline and the hub holds only cross-domain "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html#capacity-permissions","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"Capacity and permissions","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html#capacity","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"Capacity","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"For a small platform one capacity can host the hub and every spoke. As load grows, the usual move is to give heavy spokes (typically ingestion) their own capacity so a burst of copy activity cannot throttle modelling or reporting. Microsoft's deployment-pattern guidance describes both options: multiple workspaces on one capacity is simpler to administer, multiple workspaces on separate capacities gives performance is"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html#permissions","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"Permissions","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The hub does not need to read any spoke data. It needs the right to start and observe pipeline runs in each spoke, granted to a single non-human identity. Principal Where Minimum role Why Hub connection identity (service principal or workspace identity) Each spoke workspace Contributor Learn states Contributor is sufficient for most scenarios when a workspace identity invokes a pipeline in another workspace. Do not g"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html#benefits-costs","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"Benefits and what they cost","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"What you gain Governance: one place for order, failure policy, audit trail and alert routing. Reuse: parameterised spoke pipelines serve many callers; a new spoke is one more invoke edge, not a rewrite. Autonomy within limits: spoke teams build and deploy independently while the contract holds. Isolation: roles, capacity and Git history per spoke. What you pay Coupling: hub and spokes share IDs and parameter names, s"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/architecture.html#refs","t":"Hub-and-spoke architecture","s":"References","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Choose a Microsoft Fabric deployment pattern Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric Give users access to workspaces Workspace identity Understand Microsoft Fabric licenses and capacity Pricing for pipelines"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"The Invoke pipeline activity","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Playbook 04 · Hub and spoke The Invoke pipeline activity The activity that lets a pipeline in one workspace start and track a pipeline in another. This page separates the two activities that share the name, explains the connection and identity model, and turns the old list of \"preview limitations\" into what to do about each one."},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#two-activities","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Two activities with the same name","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Fabric has two Invoke pipeline activities. Pipelines built early on use the legacy activity, which only reaches pipelines in the same workspace. The new activity adds a connection and a workspace selector, and with them cross-workspace calls and child-run monitoring. A hub-and-spoke design needs the new one; the old one is still useful for modularising logic inside a single workspace. What the new activity adds. A co"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#configure","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Configure the activity","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"On the pipeline canvas, add Invoke pipeline from the Activities tab and open its Settings tab. Setting What it does Guidance for hub and spoke Type Fabric, Azure Data Factory or Azure Synapse Analytics Fabric for spokes. ADF and Synapse are for hybrid estates that still run pipelines there. Connection Reusable connection object holding the identity used to call Fabric One connection per environment, owned by the plat"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#identity","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Connection and identity","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Every new Invoke pipeline activity needs a connection, and the connection's identity is what Fabric authorizes when the hub starts a child run. When you create the connection you choose an Authentication kind : organizational account, service principal, or workspace identity. Authentication kind Strengths Weaknesses Use for Organizational account Fastest to set up; fine for a first test Tied to one person. A password"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#wi-setup","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Set up a workspace identity connection","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Create the workspace identity In the hub workspace, open Workspace settings, select the Workspace identity tab and choose + Workspace identity . Only a workspace admin can do this, and the identity should live in the same workspace as the pipeline that will use it. Enable the tenant setting A Fabric admin turns on Service principals can call Fabric public APIs (disabled by default) and scopes it to a security group t"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#wait-and-parameters","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Wait on completion, parameters and results","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#wait","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Wait on completion","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"This one checkbox decides whether a dependency means anything. With it on , the activity stays running until the child finishes and takes the child's outcome, so the On success path really means \"the child succeeded\". With it off , the activity completes as soon as the child is started, and the On success path means only \"the child was started\". Time runs left to right. Without waiting, every downstream dependency fi"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#parameters","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Parameters and parent context","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Parameters on the activity map to parameters declared on the child pipeline, by name. Treat the list as the spoke's contract (see the contract ) and always pass explicit parent context, so the child can log which orchestrated run it belongs to even if system variables are empty. Child parameter Value the hub passes (pipeline expression) Purpose runDate @variables('runDate') The logical data window, computed once per "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#return-values","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Return values","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The Set variable activity can set a pipeline return value , and the intent is that a calling pipeline can consume it. The documentation does not describe how the new Invoke pipeline activity exposes it, and community reports describe the value as missing from the new activity's output. Verify against Microsoft Learn Test a return value end to end in your tenant before using one. Reference: Set Variable activity . Unt"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#limits","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Limits that shape the design","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Limit Value Design consequence Activities per pipeline (including activities inside containers) 120 Each Invoke counts as one. Split large flows into spoke pipelines instead of growing one canvas. Parameters per pipeline 50 Pass a small contract, not a configuration dump. Read configuration from a control table. Characters per expression 8,192 Keep expressions short; compute in a Set variable step. Activity timeout D"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#known-issues","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"Early limitations, rewritten","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Guidance written when the cross-workspace activity first appeared listed three problems: token expiry, missing parent context, and settings that could not be parameterized. Here is each one, plus the others people hit in practice, with the response that holds today. Reported issue What to do User tokens in the connection expire, so runs fail intermittently Use a service principal or workspace identity. If you must ke"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#rest-fallback","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"When the native activity is not enough: the REST fallback","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Some cases fall outside what the activity handles: calling from outside Fabric (an external scheduler, a release pipeline), starting a run without any pipeline in between, or needing custom polling. The Fabric REST API for running an item on demand covers them. Run-on-demand returns 202 Accepted immediately with a Location header for the job instance, so a caller that must wait has to poll. Fire, then poll. The same "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/invoke-pipeline.html#refs","t":"The Invoke pipeline activity","s":"References","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Invoke pipeline activity Authenticate with workspace identity Workspace identity Expressions and functions for Data Factory in Microsoft Fabric Set Variable activity Data Factory limitations overview Job Scheduler: Run On Demand Item Job REST API capabilities for pipelines in Fabric Data Factory Fabric actions activity"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Control flow and failure handling","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Playbook 04 · Hub and spoke Control flow and failure handling How the hub decides what runs, in what order, how often and what happens when something fails. The page builds from dependency connectors to metadata-driven fan-out, then covers retries, failure semantics, idempotent re-runs and run conditions for cases where a central master is the wrong tool."},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#dependency-model","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"The dependency model","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"A hub pipeline is a graph of activities joined by connectors. Each activity has four output paths, and which one fires decides what runs next. Output path Next activity runs when the previous one Typical use in a hub On success succeeded Advance to the next stage On fail failed Log the failure, notify, fail the hub with a clear message On completion finished, whether it succeeded or failed Best-effort steps such as n"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#sequential","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Sequential execution","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The foundation of a medallion flow: Silver must not start until Bronze has finished successfully. In a hub it is expressed by connecting the On success output of one Invoke pipeline activity to the next, with Wait on completion enabled on each. Without the wait, \"success\" would mean only that the child was started (see Wait on completion ). Order by dependency, not by schedule offset. Green connectors carry the happy"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#parallel","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Parallel execution: fan-out and fan-in","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Independent work should not queue behind itself. Ingesting three domains is the classic case: the three Invoke activities have no dependency on each other, so they start together, and the stage that needs all three has three On success connectors, so it waits for the slowest. Fan-out is the absence of a connector; fan-in is several connectors into one activity. The branches finish in any order and the join waits for "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#conditional","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Conditional execution and dynamic parameters","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"If condition and Switch let the hub choose at run time. The inputs are expressions over parameters, variables, system values and earlier activity outputs. Need Activity Expression Full refresh on one weekday, incremental otherwise If condition @equals(dayOfWeek(utcnow()), 0) (Sunday is 0) Skip the stage when nothing new arrived Lookup, then If condition @greater(activity('LookupNewRows').output.firstRow.new_rows, 0) "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#dynamic-params","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Where parameter values come from","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Spoke pipelines should be generic black boxes with no hard-coded server names, paths or tables; the hub supplies the concrete values on every call. The values can come from: Hub pipeline parameters , set by the trigger or a manual run (for example runDate when re-running a past window). Hub variables , set by a Set variable activity earlier in the run and used for values computed once. Lookup outputs , for metadata-d"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#foreach","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Metadata-driven fan-out with ForEach","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Hard-wiring one Invoke activity per spoke does not scale past a handful, and every new spoke needs a hub deployment. The alternative is a control table that lists what to run, a Lookup activity that reads it, and a ForEach that invokes one pipeline per row. This is the pattern behind the control-table pipelines playbook , applied to orchestration. Batch count is a ceiling, not a promise. It caps how many iterations r"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#retries-timeouts","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Retries and timeouts","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Retry and timeout are per-activity policies on the General tab. For an Invoke pipeline activity they apply to the whole child run, which changes how you should use them. Setting Behaviour Guidance Timeout Default 12 hours, in D.HH:MM:SS format Set it to the child's SLA plus margin. A hung child that holds the default for half a day blocks every downstream stage and its capacity. Enable retries / Retry Number of retri"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#failure-handling","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Failure handling and pipeline status","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"How a pipeline ends depends on how you wired its failure paths, and the obvious wiring is wrong. The engine decides pipeline status from the leaf activities: it evaluates every leaf (an activity with no outgoing connectors), looks at the parent instead when a leaf was skipped, and reports success only if everything it evaluates succeeded. The Azure Data Factory documentation of the same engine names three shapes. A h"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#idempotency","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Idempotency and re-runs","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"In a distributed batch, re-running is routine: a transient fault, a late file, a fixed bug. Design every spoke so that running it twice with the same parameters leaves the same result as running it once. Then a re-run is an operation, not an incident. Re-running is cheap only if the writes are safe. A re-run from the failed step skips what already finished; a re-run of everything repeats every write, so each write mu"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#run-conditions","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"Run conditions: dependencies without a central master","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Run conditions let a pipeline declare what it is waiting for before it starts, instead of a hub invoking it. They suit cases where spokes are scheduled by their own teams but still need to respect each other. Type Waits for Example Custom One or more upstream pipelines whose runs match a run dimension (processing date, region, batch, tenant) and a connection Transform after ingest and validation both finish for the s"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/control-flow.html#refs","t":"Control flow and failure handling","s":"References","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Activity overview (general settings, control flow activities) Activity retries in Fabric Data Factory ForEach activity Fail activity Expressions and functions for Data Factory in Microsoft Fabric Run conditions in Fabric pipelines (preview) Monitor pipeline runs in the new Monitoring hub (preview) Errors and conditional execution (Azure Data Factory)"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/operations.html","t":"Logging, monitoring and CI/CD","s":"Logging, monitoring and CI/CD","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Playbook 04 · Hub and spoke Logging, monitoring and CI/CD The two things that are painful to retrofit: one place that answers \"did it finish?\", and a way to promote hub and spokes between environments without hard-coded workspace and pipeline IDs. This page compares the monitoring options, shows an explicit run log, covers alerting, and then solves the item-ID problem."},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/operations.html#fragmentation","t":"Logging, monitoring and CI/CD","s":"The fragmentation problem","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Run history lives in the workspace where a run executes. In hub and spoke that means the hub shows that its master pipeline ran, each spoke shows its own runs, and nothing shows the end-to-end story unless you build it. Earlier guidance solved this by enabling workspace monitoring in the hub and streaming each spoke's job events through an Eventstream into the hub's Eventhouse. That still works, but there are now nat"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/operations.html#monitoring-options","t":"Logging, monitoring and CI/CD","s":"Choose how to centralize","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Option What you get Scope Watch out for Monitoring hub (legacy experience) Run list across items you can access, activity runs, a Gantt view, export of run data, rerun Across workspaces you can open Browsing, not alerting or analysis New Monitoring hub Preview Pipeline health table (last status, success rate, average duration), run history, upstream and downstream runs , rerun of the whole pipeline, failed activities"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/operations.html#run-log","t":"Logging, monitoring and CI/CD","s":"An explicit run log in a Warehouse","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Whatever the platform logs, keep a small table you control. It carries what no system log knows: the logical batch, the stage name your operations team uses, rows written, and the error text you chose to keep. The hub writes the envelope (started, succeeded, failed for each stage); spokes add their own metrics keyed by the same batchId . In the hub, call the procedure with a Stored procedure or Script activity before"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/operations.html#alerts","t":"Logging, monitoring and CI/CD","s":"Alerts","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Mechanism Granularity Notes Failure notifications on a schedule One pipeline Add users or groups in the Schedule pane. They apply to scheduled runs only; on-demand runs do not notify. Teams or Outlook activity One activity, inside the pipeline Good for a rich message from the failure path. These OAuth-based activities need a manual sign-in after promotion to a higher environment. Activator on job events One pipeline "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/operations.html#object-ids","t":"Logging, monitoring and CI/CD","s":"The item-ID problem","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The most awkward technical problem in hub and spoke is that Fabric item IDs are not stable across workspaces. A pipeline deployed from development to test becomes a new item with a new ID. A hub pipeline whose Invoke activity carries the development workspace ID and pipeline ID will, after promotion, happily call the development spoke from the test hub, which is both a silent correctness bug and a data-isolation prob"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/operations.html#deploying","t":"Logging, monitoring and CI/CD","s":"Deploying hub and spokes","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Order matters because the hub's references must resolve to things that already exist. Deploy foundations first, spokes second, the hub last, then verify before anything runs on a schedule. Promotion order. Steps 1 and 2 are not Fabric items and are never deployed by promotion; script them once per environment. Foundations Create the workspaces, assign capacity, set roles, create the workspace identity (or register th"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/operations.html#refs","t":"Logging, monitoring and CI/CD","s":"References","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Choose how to monitor pipeline runs in Fabric Data Factory Monitor pipeline runs in the new Monitoring hub (preview) What is workspace monitoring (preview)? Enable workspace monitoring for pipelines Create alerts for pipeline runs Explore Job events in Fabric Real-Time hub CI/CD for pipelines in Data Factory Variable library integration with pipelines Variable library item reference type Microsoft Fabric Capacity Met"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"Alternatives and recommendations","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Playbook 04 · Hub and spoke Alternatives and recommendations Hub and spoke is one way to orchestrate Fabric, not the only one and often not the cheapest. This page compares it honestly with notebook DAGs, Apache Airflow jobs, event-driven triggers and run conditions, gives a decision path, and closes with the recommendations, anti-patterns and checklist for a production hub."},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#decision-table","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"Decision table","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Choose per problem, not per organization. The rows are not exclusive: most mature platforms use two or three of them together (see the hybrid pattern ). Approach Best fit Crosses workspaces Visual run history Code-first Main drawback One pipeline, one workspace One team, one deployment unit Partly Yes No Does not give teams independent release cycles Hub and spoke with Invoke pipeline Several teams or layers, orderin"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#notebook-orchestration","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"Notebook orchestration","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Fabric notebooks can call other notebooks with notebookutils.notebook.run and run many of them as a dependency graph with notebookutils.notebook.runMultiple . When the whole job is Spark code, this removes the per-activity overhead of a pipeline and keeps the logic in one reviewable place. A notebook DAG is parallel threads inside one Spark session. It is efficient for many short Spark steps and cannot give any step "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#notebook-cons","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"What to know before you choose it","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Shared compute Child notebooks share one Spark session. A heavy child starves the others, and the DAG cannot give a step its own pool or environment. Lakehouse rule A referenced notebook runs only if it uses the same lakehouse as the parent, inherits it, or defines none. A child with a different default lakehouse is blocked unless you pass useRootDefaultLakehouse: True in the arguments. Cross-workspace calls Supporte"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#airflow","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"Apache Airflow job","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The Apache Airflow job item hosts managed Airflow, so a team that already writes Python DAGs can orchestrate Fabric items without running its own cluster. A Microsoft-provided operator runs Fabric items (pipelines, notebooks, Spark job definitions, copy jobs, semantic model refresh and others) in any workspace the connection's service principal can reach. What Learn documents today: Airflow version 2.10.5 and Python "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#event-driven","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"Event-driven triggers","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"A pipeline can start when something happens instead of on a clock. Learn lists three event families: file events (OneLake and Azure Blob Storage), job events (a Fabric item run changing state) and workspace events. The trigger is built on Eventstream and Activator: you choose Trigger on the pipeline's Home ribbon, define the event in the Set alert panel, and the result is an Activator (Reflex) item that starts the pi"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#decision-path","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"A decision path","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Work down the questions and stop at the first yes. If you reach the bottom, hub and spoke is the default for cross-team, cross-workspace work. Stop at the first yes. The order encodes cost: the simplest tool that fits wins, and hub and spoke is what remains when teams, workspaces or capacities genuinely differ. Add the event trigger and the notebook DAG on top of the hub when they fit part of the work."},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#hybrid","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"The hybrid pattern","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Real platforms combine these. Events start the day, the hub orders the layers and logs the outcome, and each spoke uses whichever engine suits its work. Use each mechanism where it is strongest. The hub never needs to know that the Silver spoke runs a notebook DAG; it sees one pipeline that succeeds or fails."},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#recommendations","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"Recommendations","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The whole playbook condensed into decisions. Each line points to the page that explains it. Design Make the hub a control plane only: it orders, passes parameters, logs and alerts, and moves no data. Treat each spoke pipeline's parameters and failure behaviour as an API contract and version it. See the contract . Granularity Invoke at the unit a team can own and re-run independently (a layer or a domain), not at the "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#anti-patterns","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"Anti-patterns","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Anti-pattern Why it hurts Do this instead Hard-coded workspace and pipeline IDs in the hub Promoted copies silently call the previous environment Variable library value sets or deployment-time replacement A personal account on the Invoke connection Scheduled runs fail when the token expires or the person leaves Service principal or workspace identity with Contributor on spokes A \"god hub\" that holds business logic Ev"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#checklist","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"Production-readiness checklist","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The hub moves no data and holds no business logic; spokes' parameters and outcomes are documented as a contract. The Invoke connection uses a service principal or workspace identity, is named per environment, and has Contributor on every spoke. The tenant setting that lets service principals call Fabric public APIs is enabled and scoped to a security group. The scheduled run, not only a manual run, has been tested en"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/alternatives.html#refs","t":"Alternatives and recommendations","s":"References","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"NotebookUtils notebook run and orchestration NotebookUtils (former MSSparkUtils) for Fabric What is an Apache Airflow job? Run a Fabric item using Apache Airflow DAGs Run, schedule, or use events to trigger a pipeline Run conditions in Fabric pipelines (preview) Explore Job events in Fabric Real-Time hub REST API capabilities for pipelines in Fabric Data Factory"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Playbook 04 · Hub and spoke Workshop: build hub and spokes Build a small but real hub and spoke: two spoke workspaces that each copy a CSV file into a Delta table, and a hub workspace whose master pipeline invokes them in order. Then replace your personal sign-in with a workload identity, break a spoke on purpose to prove the hub turns red, and keep the troubleshooting table for later. About 90 minutes."},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#goal","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"What you will build","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The spokes do almost nothing on purpose. The workshop teaches orchestration mechanics: invoking across workspaces, waiting for completion, passing a parameter and propagating failure. Domain logic can be added to the spokes later without changing the hub, which is the point of the pattern. Target design. Two spoke pipelines own the data movement; the hub owns the order. No data passes through the hub. Role Workspace "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#request","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Request workspaces and permissions","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"In most organizations someone else controls capacity, tenant settings and Entra objects, so start the requests first. The hub's connection identity is the one that matters most: it needs Contributor on every spoke, and that role assignment is what you will test in Stage 5. Who needs what. The identity that the connection authenticates as is the identity every spoke authorizes. Nothing is needed on the hub except the "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#sample-data","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Sample data","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Save these two files locally. They are synthetic and deliberately tiny."},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#stage-spokes","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Stage 1: Create the spoke workspaces and lakehouses","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"End of Stage 1. Two workspaces, two lakehouses, one source file each. Create the workspaces In the Fabric portal choose Workspaces, then + New workspace . Create DEV_SALES_BRZ and DEV_SALES_SLV , and assign each to the Fabric capacity you were given (Advanced settings, License mode). If designated spoke workspaces already exist, use them instead. Create a lakehouse in each In each workspace choose + New item , then L"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#stage-spoke-pipelines","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Stage 2: Build the two spoke pipelines","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Each spoke pipeline is a single Copy data activity with one parameter. The parameter is the pipeline's public contract: the hub will pass it explicitly. The spoke pipeline. The destination table name is not typed into the activity; it comes from the parameter, so the caller decides it. Create the pipeline In DEV_SALES_BRZ choose + New item , Data pipeline, name it PL_SALES_BRZ_Ingest and choose Create. When the start"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#stage-hub","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Stage 3: Create the hub and its master pipeline","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The master pipeline. Two Invoke activities, one dependency, and the contract ( sinkTable ) passed explicitly. Create the hub workspace Choose Workspaces, + New workspace , name it DEV_ORCH_HUB , add a description such as \"Control plane for the sales workshop\", assign the capacity and choose Apply. Create the master pipeline In DEV_ORCH_HUB create a Data pipeline named PL_ORCH_HUB_SalesDaily . Add the first Invoke act"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#stage-run","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Stage 4: Run and monitor","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"One hub run, two linked child runs. The Gantt view in the Monitoring hub draws exactly this shape; each child run also lives in its own spoke workspace. Run Open the Output tab first so you can watch from the start, choose Save, then Run (accept Save and run if prompted). Watch the Output tab You should see Invoke Bronze in progress, then Succeeded, then Invoke Silver in progress, then Succeeded. Total time is the su"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#stage-identity","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Stage 5: Replace your account with a workload identity","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"The run above worked because you are Admin everywhere and your sign-in is on the connection. That is the configuration that fails at 02:00, when your token has expired or you have left. Swap it now, using the permission map from the request section . Create the workspace identity In DEV_ORCH_HUB open Workspace settings, the Workspace identity tab, and choose + Workspace identity . Only a workspace Admin can do this, "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#stage-failure","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Stage 6: Prove that failure propagates","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"A hub that stays green while a spoke fails is worse than no hub. Test both failure positions. Both experiments must end in a red hub run. If experiment B shows the hub as Succeeded, a connector or a catch path is masking the failure. Experiment A: break Silver In PL_SALES_SLV_Conform change the source file path to orders_missing.csv and save. Run the hub. Expected: Bronze succeeds, Invoke Silver fails with the child'"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#stage-extend","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Stage 7 (optional): Log runs and make the dependency real","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Evolve a spoke without touching the hub. Silver now genuinely depends on Bronze data, and the hub still just invokes one pipeline and waits. Add the run log Create a Warehouse in DEV_ORCH_HUB , run the ops.pipeline_run_log script from the run log section , and add a Stored procedure activity before and after each Invoke (Started, Succeeded, and Failed on the On fail path). Re-run the failure experiments and check the"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#troubleshooting","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix A spoke workspace is missing from the Workspace list in the Invoke activity The connection's identity has no role there, or the workspace is not visible to it Add the identity as Contributor on the spoke and reopen the list Forbidden or \"not authorized\" on the child run Contributor missing on one spoke, or the tenant setting does not cover the identity Check the role on both spokes and the se"},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#cleanup","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"Clean up","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Delete the schedule you created in Stage 5, or confirm it is off. Remove the workspace identity or service principal's Contributor role from the spokes if the workshop identity will not be reused. Delete the workshop connections you no longer need. Delete the three workspaces if they were created only for this workshop. Key takeaways The spoke pipeline's parameter is its contract: the hub passes sinkTable explicitly "},{"u":"playbooks/04-cross-workspace-orchestration/workshop.html#refs","t":"Workshop: build hub and spokes","s":"References","p":"Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration (Hub and Spoke)","x":"Invoke pipeline activity Copy activity in Data Factory for Microsoft Fabric Run, schedule, or use events to trigger a pipeline Choose how to monitor pipeline runs Workspace identity in Microsoft Fabric Authenticate with workspace identity Give users access to workspaces OneLake shortcuts"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/index.html","t":"GitHub Integration and Branching","s":"GitHub Integration and Branching","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook 05 · GitHub Integration GitHub Integration and Branching A Fabric workspace is a shared runtime, and nothing in it is versioned until you connect it to Git. This playbook shows how to connect workspaces to GitHub without losing work, branch the way GitHub Flow intends, lock the repository down with rulesets, and give automation its own identity instead of borrowing a developer's. Audience platform leads, Dev"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/index.html#what-it-solves","t":"GitHub Integration and Branching","s":"What this playbook solves","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Fabric Git integration works at workspace level: you connect a workspace to one branch and one folder of a repository, and Fabric keeps item definitions (notebooks as .py , semantic models as TMDL, pipelines as JSON, and so on) in sync with that folder. That gives you history, review and rollback for analytics assets. It does not, on its own, give you a safe way of working. Teams that stop at \"we connected the worksp"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/index.html#model","t":"GitHub Integration and Branching","s":"The model in one picture","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"One change, three stages. Developers work in a branched workspace, GitHub decides what reaches main , and only main feeds the DEV workspaces that deployment promotes onward. Nothing is edited directly in TST or PRD."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/index.html#principles","t":"GitHub Integration and Branching","s":"Operating principles","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Every later page is an application of one of these. Git is recoverable; a workspace is not. Whenever a direction has to be chosen, choose the one that overwrites the workspace only after the workspace content is safely in Git or in a backup workspace. One workspace, one branch, one folder. A workspace connects to a single branch and a single folder. Keep that mapping boring and documented. main is the only branch tha"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/index.html#when","t":"GitHub Integration and Branching","s":"When to use it, and when not to","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A good fit when More than one person edits Fabric items and you need review and history. You promote content through DEV, TST and PRD and want the definition of \"what is deployed\" to be a commit. Your organization already runs GitHub (including GitHub Enterprise Cloud) for code. You want a single place to review notebooks, pipelines, models and reports as text. Think twice when Your GitHub is self-hosted (GitHub Ente"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/index.html#prerequisites","t":"GitHub Integration and Branching","s":"Prerequisites at a glance","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A Fabric capacity and workspaces assigned to it, with a named workspace admin per workspace. A Fabric admin who can enable the Git integration tenant settings (the GitHub-specific one is off by default). A GitHub organization and repository (or a plan to create them), with a GitHub plan that supports rulesets and environments on private repositories. A decision on who creates workspaces and repositories. If only a ce"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/index.html#page-map","t":"GitHub Integration and Branching","s":"How the pages fit together","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"02 Prerequisites and authentication Tenant settings, permissions matrix, token types, GitHub Enterprise Cloud and the two-tier identity model. Reference 12 min 03 Repository and workspace layout Item folders, one repository versus many, the recommended layout and the limits that shape it. Design 12 min 04 Connecting safely Preflight, the first-sync decision, step-by-step connection and the folder-change hazard. Proce"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/index.html#related","t":"GitHub Integration and Branching","s":"How this fits with the other playbooks","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook Relationship 07 · Deployment Pipelines and Promotion Takes over where main ends: promotion from DEV to TST and PRD, deployment rules and approvals. 09 · Variable Libraries The recommended way to make items environment-aware, which is also what makes feature workspaces safe. 14 · Deployment Checklist The release-day checklist that assumes the repository and branching model set up here. 17 · Pull-Request Notif"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/index.html#refs","t":"GitHub Integration and Branching","s":"References","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"What is Microsoft Fabric Git integration? Get started with Git integration Development process in Microsoft Fabric CI/CD workflow options in Fabric GitHub Flow"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Prerequisites and authentication","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook 05 · GitHub Integration Prerequisites and authentication Most failed first connections come down to one of four things: a tenant switch that is still off, a workspace role that is too low, a token with the wrong scope, or a GitHub plan that quietly lacks a feature you planned to rely on. This page is the checklist that prevents all four, and the reasoning behind the two-tier identity model used for the rest "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#concepts","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Core concepts in three sentences","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Git integration keeps the definition of each supported item in a Git folder and gives developers branches, history and review. Deployment moves those definitions between environments, either with Fabric deployment pipelines or with code (the Fabric Git and Items REST APIs, or the fabric-cicd library). Automation is whatever triggers that movement, here GitHub Actions. The first is the subject of most of this playbook"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#providers","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Supported Git providers and GitHub variants","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Fabric supports Azure DevOps and GitHub, and in both cases only the cloud-hosted products. The details matter when your organization runs GitHub in a non-default way. GitHub flavour Supported? What to know GitHub.com (Free, Pro, Team, Enterprise Cloud) Yes The repository URL is optional when you add the account; if you leave it blank the connection can reach any repository the token can. GitHub Enterprise Cloud with "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#tenant-settings","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Tenant settings checklist","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"These switches live in the Fabric admin experience under tenant settings. They are organization-wide unless you scope them to security groups, which is usually the right call for Git integration: start with a pilot group, then widen. Setting (label in the portal) Why you need it Notes Users can create Fabric items Required whenever the workspace holds Fabric (non-Power BI) items such as notebooks, lakehouses and pipe"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#capacity","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Capacity and workspace prerequisites","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The workspace is assigned to a Fabric capacity (a trial capacity works). Power BI Premium capacities can be used, but some Power BI SKUs only support Power BI items. The workspace is a regular workspace. My workspace cannot connect to Git, and neither can a workspace with template apps installed. There is capacity headroom for feature workspaces. Branching out creates a workspace and must have capacity available. Wor"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#permissions","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Who needs what: workspace role and GitHub permission","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Every Git operation is gated twice: by the person's role in the workspace, and by what the stored GitHub credential is allowed to do in the repository. The table combines both, using fine-grained token permissions for the GitHub column. Operation Workspace role Fine-grained token (Contents) Connect, disconnect or change the connection Admin Read (disconnect needs nothing) Initial sync Admin Read to import; read and w"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#authentication","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Authenticating the Git connection","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"When a developer connects a workspace to GitHub, Fabric stores a credential for that developer. The credential is a personal access token (PAT). The documented options are narrower than the GitHub ecosystem as a whole. Credential Use for Fabric Git connection? Notes Fine-grained personal access token Recommended Scope it to the one repository and grant Contents: read and write . A token scoped to a single repository "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#create-token","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Create the fine-grained token","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Open the token settings In GitHub, go to Settings > Developer settings > Personal access tokens > Fine-grained tokens and choose to generate a new token. Choose the resource owner Select the organization that owns the repository, not your personal account. A token is bound to one resource owner. If the organization requires approval for fine-grained tokens, the token stays pending, and can only read public resources,"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#two-tiers","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"The two-tier identity model","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Because the Git credential for GitHub is a user token, the cleanest design splits identities by purpose instead of trying to make one identity do both jobs. Different identities for different jobs. People need a GitHub credential because Fabric pushes commits on their behalf. Automation can deploy from a checked-out repository and needs no GitHub credential in Fabric at all. Why the automation lane skips the Git sync"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#constraints","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Constraints that surface late","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"MFA parity. The authentication method in Fabric must be at least as strong as the one required for Git. If GitHub requires multifactor authentication, Fabric must require it too, through Entra ID conditional access. Guest users. B2B guests are not supported in Git integration scenarios. Workspace identity. An item that uses a workspace identity and is committed to Git can only be updated back into a workspace that ha"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#github-plan","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"What your GitHub plan decides","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Several controls recommended in this playbook are plan-dependent for private repositories. Find out which plan you are on before you design the hardening, not after. Capability Private repositories need CODEOWNERS and required code-owner review GitHub Pro, Team or Enterprise Rulesets GitHub Pro, Team or Enterprise Cloud Environments, environment secrets, deployment branch rules GitHub Pro, Team or Enterprise Required"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#operating-model","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"Who does what during setup","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Many organizations restrict workspace creation and repository creation to a central team. That is a good control, and it needs a defined hand-off, otherwise the branch-out workflow stalls on the first feature. Step Fabric admin Workspace admin GitHub org owner or repo admin Developers Enable tenant switches for a pilot group Owns Informed - - Create repository, teams and rulesets Consulted Consulted Owns - Create wor"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/foundations.html#refs","t":"Prerequisites and authentication","s":"References","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Git integration tenant settings Get started with Git integration Git integration process: permissions and limitations Automate Git integration by using APIs Git - Connect (REST API) Developer admin settings Managing your personal access tokens (GitHub) Setting a personal access token policy for your organization (GitHub)"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"Repository and workspace layout","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook 05 · GitHub Integration Repository and workspace layout The folder structure in your repository is not a documentation choice. Fabric mirrors it, in both directions, so it decides what a commit touches, what a pull request reviewer sees, what an Update can delete and which dependencies survive a deployment. Decide it before you connect the first workspace. Audience architects, platform leads, workspace admin"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html#mapping","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"The mapping rules","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A workspace connects to one branch and one folder . Inside that folder, every Git-supported item is a subfolder, and workspace subfolders become Git subfolders. The relationship is symmetrical: items you save in a workspace folder appear in the matching Git folder on commit, and items added to the Git folder appear in the workspace on update. For a medallion layout with one workspace per layer (see playbook 03 ), the"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html#item-folders","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"How an item is stored","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Each item becomes a directory named {display name}.{public facing type} , containing the item's definition files and a .platform file that Fabric generates. Knowing this layout is what lets reviewers read a pull request and lets you write sensible CODEOWNERS rules (see playbook 17 ). The format varies by item type: a notebook is a .py file; a semantic model is a definition.pbism plus a definition/ folder of TMDL file"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html#platform-file","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"The .platform file and logical IDs","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The logicalId is what links an item in a workspace to its folder in Git. Items with the same logical ID are assumed to be the same item, even if the name or folder changed. A branch can be synced to several workspaces, so the same logical ID can exist in different workspaces, but never twice in one workspace. Three practical rules follow: Never edit a logical ID or the type field (it is case sensitive). When you copy"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html#one-vs-many","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"One repository or many?","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The repository is the unit of access control, of pull request scope and of CODEOWNERS. Choose it by asking what must change together and who must approve it, not by how many workspaces you have. Atomic change. Layers depend on each other, so a schema change that ripples from Bronze to Gold is one logical change. A repository per workspace splits it into reviews that can merge out of order. Option Best when Strengths "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html#layout","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"Recommended repository layout","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Folder names follow the workspace convention minus the environment. SALES_GLD serves DEV_SALES_GLD , TST_SALES_GLD and PRD_SALES_GLD . Non-workspace folders start with an underscore. They sort together and are visibly not connected to anything. Keep custom files out of item folders. When Fabric commits an item it deletes files inside that item's folder that are not part of the definition. Files outside item folders a"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html#workspace-folders","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"Folders inside a workspace","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Workspace subfolders are mirrored into Git, up to 10 levels deep. Empty folders are not copied to Git, and empty folders in Git are removed automatically. Two operational rules matter more than the depth limit: Use shallow, functional subfolders (for example ingest/ , transform/ , orchestration/ ) and keep them stable. Moving items between folders changes paths, produces noisy diffs and breaks any CODEOWNERS pattern "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html#dependencies","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"Dependencies across workspaces","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A layered design puts related items in different workspaces, and that is where Git integration is least forgiving. Fabric resolves dependencies through logical IDs, but only for references to items in the same workspace . A reference to an item in another workspace is stored as an object ID, and after a deployment it still points at the source workspace. Binding stops at the workspace boundary. Within a workspace Git"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html#limits","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"Limits that shape the layout","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A workspace holds at most 1,000 items. If a Git branch holds more than that for the connected folder, syncing to the workspace fails; split by workspace or by folder. Folder structure is kept to 10 levels, the full path of a file may not exceed 250 characters, and a branch name may not exceed 244 characters. The directory you connect to cannot begin or end with a space or tab and cannot contain \" / : < > \\ * ? | . It"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/repository-design.html#refs","t":"Repository and workspace layout","s":"References","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Git source code format Git integration process: folders and limitations Understand dependency binding in cross-workspace deployment Notebook source control and deployment"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html","t":"Connecting safely","s":"Connecting safely","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook 05 · GitHub Integration Connecting safely The moment you click Connect and sync is the only point in the integration where a single wrong choice can delete a workspace's content with no way back. Everything after it is recoverable from Git. This page is the procedure that makes the first sync boring: back up, connect to an empty folder, commit a baseline at once, and only then turn on the guard rails. Audien"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#first-sync","t":"Connecting safely","s":"What the first sync does","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"When you connect, Fabric makes the workspace and the branch folder match. The direction depends on what each side holds. If one side is empty and the other has content, content is copied from the non-empty side to the empty one. If both have content, you must choose, and the choice overwrites one of them. Four outcomes. Three are harmless. The fourth, with content on both sides, is where workspaces are lost: choosing"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#preflight","t":"Connecting safely","s":"Preflight safeguards","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Do these before the first connection to a workspace that already holds work. For a brand-new, empty workspace most of them do not apply."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#backup-clone","t":"Connecting safely","s":"1 · Clone the workspace as a safety net","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The most dependable backup that works before source control exists is a deployment pipeline: deploy the DEV workspace into a new, differently named workspace (for example BAK_DEV_SALES_BRZ ). Fabric copies the item definitions into the target workspace. Data is the catch: a deployed lakehouse arrives as a new, empty lakehouse, and shortcut definitions are synced while their contents are not, so the clone is only a tr"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#capture-outside-git","t":"Connecting safely","s":"2 · Record what Git will not hold","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Workspace access (roles and groups), capacity assignment and workspace-level settings. Deployment pipelines are separate objects and are not in the repository. Data: lakehouse files and tables, warehouse tables. Git stores definitions, not data. Items that are not supported for Git. They stay in the workspace, appear in the source control pane, and cannot be committed. Inventory them now (see the supported items sect"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#freeze","t":"Connecting safely","s":"3 · Freeze changes and check the size","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Announce a short freeze: nobody edits the workspace during the connection and baseline commit. Confirm the workspace is under 1,000 items and that no single commit will exceed 50 MB for GitHub. If it might, plan to commit in several batches. Confirm the repository exists, main exists (initialize the repository with a README at the root), and the folder you will use does not exist yet . Confirm the person connecting h"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#connect-steps","t":"Connecting safely","s":"Establishing the connection","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Run these steps as the workspace admin, once per workspace. The example connects DEV_SALES_BRZ to the SALES_BRZ folder of main . Open Git integration In the workspace, open Workspace settings and select Git integration . Choose GitHub as the provider. If GitHub is missing, the GitHub tenant switch is still off for you (see the tenant settings ). Add your account Select Add account . Enter a display name that is uniqu"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#medallion-order","t":"Connecting safely","s":"Connecting a layered estate","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"With one workspace per layer, connect them in dependency order (Bronze, Silver, Gold, then consumption and delivery) so a failed step does not leave downstream workspaces connected to nothing. After the last one, the repository should look like the layout in the previous page : one folder per workspace, plus the folders and files you added by hand that sit outside any workspace folder. Mark the state with a Git tag ("},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#folder-hazard","t":"Connecting safely","s":"The folder-change hazard","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Workspace subfolders are part of the synchronized structure. If you create or reorganize folders in the workspace and the Git folder does not yet have them, the two are considered different. Update then lets the Git structure overwrite the workspace structure, which removes the new folders and, with them, the items inside. Commit structure before you update. The right-hand sequence is also how a developer reorganizes"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#recovery","t":"Connecting safely","s":"If a workspace was already overwritten","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Situation First move Notes Items disappeared after an Update against an empty folder Stop. Do not commit from the workspace. A commit now would push the emptied state into Git. Work from the backup workspace and from Git history instead. A backup clone exists Deploy from the clone back to the live workspace, or reconnect from the clone's items. Hydrated data in the clone saves the most time. Re-point connections and "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#connect-errors","t":"Connecting safely","s":"Connection symptoms and fixes","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix GitHub is not offered as a provider The GitHub tenant switch is off, or you are outside the group it applies to. Ask the Fabric admin; check delegation at capacity and workspace level. Repository or branch not listed Token not scoped to the repository, token pending organization approval, SSO authorization missing, or token expired. Review the token's resource owner and repository access; app"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/connect-safely.html#refs","t":"Connecting safely","s":"References","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Get started with Git integration Git integration process: connect and sync, folders, limitations Resolve conflicts with Git integration Troubleshoot CI/CD Lakehouse Git integration and deployment pipelines"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"Sync model and supported items","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook 05 · GitHub Integration Sync model and supported items After the first sync, day-to-day work is a loop of commits and updates. The rules of that loop explain almost every message the source control pane shows you, and most of the surprises: why Commit greys out, why a file changed that nobody touched, why an item is silently missing from the repository. Audience developers, workspace admins Use it for unders"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html#directions","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"Two directions, never at the same time","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Commit moves workspace changes to Git. Update moves Git changes into the workspace. You can only do one at a time, and the two are asymmetric in ways that matter: Commit (workspace to Git) Update (Git to workspace) Granularity You choose which changed items to commit. Some items also allow committing individual files from the compare experience (a preview capability). Always the whole branch folder, up to the tip of "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html#status","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"The life of an item: Git status","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Status is a state machine. Commit is only available from Uncommitted changes, Update only from Update required, and a Conflict blocks both until you resolve it. Status Meaning What to do Synced The item is the same in the workspace and in the connected branch. Nothing. Uncommitted changes The workspace item differs from Git; the change is not yet committed. Commit it, or undo it. Update required The branch has a newe"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html#conflicts","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"Resolving conflicts","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A conflict occurs only when the same item changed in both places. Choose the lightest resolution that is safe. Lightest safe option first. Reconnecting is not a conflict resolution: it overwrites all content on one side, not only the conflicted items, and does not return anything to the last synced state. Accept incoming changes overwrites the workspace item; the workspace change is lost and the status becomes Synced"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html#branch-ops","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"Branch operations in the Source control pane","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The Branches tab collects the operations that change which branch a workspace is attached to. They are easy to confuse, and the difference between them is whether they change the workspace content, the connection, or both. Operation What it does Workspace content Who can Branch out to another workspace Creates a new branch from the tip of the source workspace's connected branch, then creates a new workspace (or uses "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html#quirks","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"Behaviors that look like bugs","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"You see Why What to do A file shows as modified though you changed nothing logically The service reformats definitions on commit. Renaming a semantic model column, for example, moves it to the end of the columns array in the model definition. Accept it; review for substance. Do not fight the formatter. Every file in a PBIP item shows a whole-file diff The service writes LF line endings. Files committed with CRLF are "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html#supported-items","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"Supported and unsupported items","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Git integration supports a growing but incomplete list of item types, grouped by workload. An item that is not supported does not block the connection: it stays in the workspace, appears in the source control pane, and is ignored by commit and update. The consequence for design is simple: anything that must be reproducible from Git has to be an item type on the list. Inventory the unsupported items in each workspace "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html#unsupported","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"What is not supported","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Item or scenario Detail Power BI dashboards Not on the supported list. Semantic model types Push datasets, live connections to Analysis Services, and the older model v1 are excluded. Streaming datasets are not supported. Reports bound to unsupported models Reports connected to Azure Analysis Services or SQL Server Analysis Services models, and reports from Power BI Desktop that depend on models in My workspace. Works"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html#limits","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"Limits and general limitations","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Limit or restriction Value or rule Combined size of one commit (GitHub) 50 MB. Commit in batches if you exceed it. Maximum size of one file 25 MB. Items per workspace 1,000. Syncing a branch with more fails. Path, branch name, folder depth 250 characters, 244 characters, 10 levels. Submodules Not supported. GitHub variants Cloud only. No Enterprise Server, no IP allow list. Sovereign clouds, B2B guests Not supported."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/sync-and-items.html#refs","t":"Sync model and supported items","s":"References","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Git integration process Resolve conflicts with Git integration Development process using Branch-Out experience Overview of Fabric Git integration Git source code format Lakehouse Git integration and deployment pipelines Compare and commit changes"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook 05 · GitHub Integration GitHub Flow for Fabric One branch is always deployable, every change starts on a short-lived branch, and nothing reaches that branch without a pull request. Fabric adds one twist: every branch that holds work in progress also gets its own workspace, and that workspace is where the work is actually done. Audience developers, reviewers, workspace admins Use it for daily workflow, branch"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#github-flow","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"GitHub Flow, adapted to workspaces","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"GitHub Flow is deliberately small. It has three rules, and they fit analytics development well because most Fabric changes are modest, frequent and independently shippable: main is always deployable. It is the source of truth, and anything merged into it is safe to promote. All work happens on a descriptive, short-lived branch created from main . Changes return to main only through a pull request , which is where rev"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#naming","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"Branch naming","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A branch name is the first thing a reviewer sees. Make it carry the purpose and a pointer to the work item, and keep it boring enough to type. Prefix Use for Example feature/ New capability or an enhancement feature/1234-add-churn-model bugfix/ Defect fix for something not yet in production bugfix/1250-fix-sales-report-filter hotfix/ Urgent production fix, expedited review hotfix/1301-orders-load-timeout chore/ House"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#feature-workspaces","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"Getting a feature workspace","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"There are two ways to end up with a branch and a workspace connected to each other. Both produce the same result; they differ in who needs which permission. Option A: Branch out Option B: Pre-created workspace How In the source control pane, Branch out to another workspace . Fabric creates the branch from the connected branch, creates the workspace, connects them and populates it. Create the branch in GitHub, create "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#feature-loop","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"The feature loop","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"One loop per piece of work. If reviewers request changes, the loop returns to step 3: commit more to the same branch and the open pull request updates itself. Start from a current DEV workspace. Before branching out, make sure the DEV workspace shows no pending updates. A feature that starts from an old commit begins life with conflicts. Branch out. Name the branch with the pattern above and the workspace with the qu"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#pull-request","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"The pull-request process","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The pull request is where the repository's rules become visible. It is the one place where the author, the reviewers, the automated checks and the ruleset all meet. Gates in order. The ruleset on main enforces the red conditions; reviewers and automation supply the evidence for each. Code owner review is covered in the CODEOWNERS playbook ."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#open-pr","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"Opening the pull request","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"In the repository, open Pull requests and choose New pull request , or use the Compare & pull request prompt that appears after a push. Set the base to main and the compare branch to your feature branch. Write a title that states the outcome, and fill in the description (template below). Check the Files changed tab yourself first. A surprise in your own diff, such as a deleted folder or a changed logicalId , is cheap"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#review-focus","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"What reviewers look for in a Fabric diff","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Item definitions are text, so they can be reviewed, but the formatter produces noise. Knowing where the signal is makes reviews short. Look at Why Folders added or removed Deleting an item folder deletes the item in DEV at the next update. For a lakehouse, that includes its data. .platform changes A changed logicalId on an existing item breaks dependency binding. A changed display name renames the item and its folder"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#merge-method","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"Merging","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Method Result on main Fit Squash One commit per pull request, with a message you write. Default for feature branches. Keeps history readable and makes a revert a single step. Merge commit All branch commits plus a merge commit. When individual commits carry meaning and you want them preserved. Rebase Branch commits replayed onto main , no merge commit. Linear history, but the commit IDs change, which confuses anyone "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#after-merge","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"After the merge","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Update the DEV workspace. Open Source control on the DEV workspace. It shows Update required . Review the Updates list, choose Update all , and confirm every item returns to Synced . Update is whole-branch, so the list also shows other people's merged work. Promote. DEV now matches main . Promotion to TST and PRD is the job of deployment pipelines or automation, covered in the deployment playbook and verified with it"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#stay-current","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"Keeping a long-running branch current","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Short branches rarely need this. If main moves while your pull request is open, GitHub shows the branch as out of date. Use Update branch on the pull request to merge main into the feature branch, then run Update all in the feature workspace to receive the result. If two people changed the same item, resolve it as described on the conflict section ; prefer small, frequent pull requests over heroic merges of notebooks"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#data-not-copied","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"Data is not copied","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Branch-out copies definitions, never data A new feature workspace has item definitions, and lakehouses and warehouses in it are empty. A notebook that is bound to a specific lakehouse or workspace by ID keeps pointing at the original, so a test run in the \"isolated\" feature workspace can read, and write, shared DEV data. Isolation is something you build. Definitions branch automatically; data and bindings do not. Wit"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/branching-workflow.html#refs","t":"GitHub Flow for Fabric","s":"References","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Development process using branch workspace Manage branches in Fabric workspaces Basic concepts in Git integration Best practices for lifecycle management in Fabric GitHub flow About merge methods on GitHub"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Access model and repository hardening","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook 05 · GitHub Integration Access model and repository hardening The workflow on the previous page is only as safe as the controls behind it. This page covers who gets which role, how identity flows from Entra ID, and how to make main impossible to change except through a reviewed pull request. Audience repository admins, platform and security owners Use it for role design, SSO, rulesets, hardening, scanning"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#least-privilege","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Least privilege, in two tiers","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A Fabric developer needs two independent sets of permissions: a GitHub role that decides what they can do in the repository, and a Fabric workspace role that decides what they can do in the workspace. Neither implies the other (see the two-tier identity model ). Design both from the same functional roles, and give each person the smallest role that lets them do their job: Developers and analysts write item changes on"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#team-structure","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Teams and repository roles","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Team Role Who and why fabric-admins Admin Platform owners and DevOps leads: repository settings, rulesets, environments and secrets, team access. Keep this to a few people and review it regularly. fabric-maintainers Maintain Tech leads who manage merge settings and day-to-day repository hygiene without access to security settings, secrets or the rulesets. fabric-developers Write Data engineers and analysts: push to f"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#identity","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Identity: Entra ID, single sign-on and team sync","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Individual role assignments do not scale and leave stale access behind. Drive membership from Microsoft Entra ID instead: Capability What you get Single sign-on People sign in to GitHub with their corporate identity and the policies attached to it, such as MFA and conditional access. SCIM provisioning Joiners are provisioned and leavers are deprovisioned automatically, which closes the most common gap: a departed dev"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#access-review","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Review access on a schedule","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Review membership of the admin and maintainer groups, and the bypass lists of every ruleset, at least quarterly. Use Entra access reviews for the groups. List outside collaborators and installed GitHub Apps and remove those without an owner. Review active personal access tokens and approved token requests for the organization. Check that nobody holds access through an individual assignment that a team already covers."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#rulesets","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Rulesets: protecting main","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A ruleset is a named set of rules that applies to a set of branches or tags. It is the current replacement for classic branch protection rules, and it should be your only mechanism for protecting main ."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#rulesets-vs-protection","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Why rulesets","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Layering. Several rulesets can apply to the same branch at once. GitHub aggregates them and, where they overlap, the most restrictive rule applies. Classic protection allowed only one rule per pattern. Visibility. Anyone with read access can view the active rulesets, so a developer can see why an action is blocked without asking an admin. Manageability. Rulesets have an enforcement status (active, disabled and, on pl"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#ruleset-setup","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Creating the ruleset for main","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Open the rules. In the repository, go to Settings , then Rules , then Rulesets , and choose New ruleset followed by New branch ruleset . Name it and choose the enforcement status. Name it for what it protects, such as protect-main . Start as Active if the baseline is already committed (see the callout below); use Evaluate first on plans that offer it if you want to see what would be blocked. Keep the bypass list near"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#hardening","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Hardening the repository","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The ruleset protects one branch. The rest of the repository and its surroundings need protecting too, and each control assumes the others may fail. Defence in depth. The ruleset is the repository layer; most real incidents come from the identity, content and pipeline layers around it."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#org-controls","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Organization controls","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Require two-factor authentication or single sign-on for every member, and remove members and outside collaborators who no longer need access. Set the base repository permission to No permission , so access comes only from team grants. Set a personal access token policy: allow fine-grained tokens, restrict or disable classic tokens, and require approval of tokens that reach organization resources. See the authenticati"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#repo-controls","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Repository controls","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Keep one default branch ( main ), enable Automatically delete head branches so merged branches do not accumulate, and disable forking for private repositories. Add a CODEOWNERS file that assigns each item folder, and the .github/ folder that holds workflows, to the right team. Workflow files are code that runs with your credentials, so they deserve the strictest review. Add a .gitignore that keeps local tooling outpu"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#scanning","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"Optional automated scanning","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Automated scanning catches mistakes that reviewers miss, and a ruleset can make the result a merge requirement. Control What it catches Fabric relevance Secret scanning and push protection Tokens, keys and connection strings committed to the repository; push protection stops them before they land. The most likely leak is a credential pasted into a notebook cell or a parameter file. Fabric commits are pushes, so push "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/security-hardening.html#refs","t":"Access model and repository hardening","s":"References","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"About rulesets Available rules for rulesets Creating rulesets for a repository Repository roles for an organization Synchronizing a team with an identity provider group About code owners Secure use reference for GitHub Actions Basic concepts in Git integration"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Service principals and Actions","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook 05 · GitHub Integration Service principals and Actions Developers use their own identity in the Fabric UI. Everything that runs without a person, such as validation, deployment to TST and PRD and scheduled housekeeping, needs an identity of its own. This page shows how to give automation exactly the access it needs and no stored password at all. Audience platform and DevOps engineers, security reviewers Use "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#identities","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Who authenticates to what","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A pipeline run touches three separate trust relationships. Keeping them separate is what keeps the blast radius small. Step in the run Credential Lifetime and scope Check out the repository, post a status GITHUB_TOKEN , issued automatically for each run Expires when the job ends. Scope is whatever the workflow's permissions block grants. Sign in to Microsoft Entra ID A GitHub OIDC token, presented by azure/login Minu"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#create-sp","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Creating and authorising the service principal","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Create one service principal per environment, for example sp-fabric-deploy-tst and sp-fabric-deploy-prd . A credential that can only reach TST cannot damage PRD, and each identity shows up separately in audit logs. Register the application. In Microsoft Entra ID, register a single-tenant application. Record the Application (client) ID and the Directory (tenant) ID . These are identifiers, not secrets. Do not create a"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#oidc","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"OIDC federation instead of a stored secret","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"With OpenID Connect, GitHub issues each job a signed token that states exactly where the job came from. Entra ID trusts that token if its claims match a federated credential configured on the application. Nothing needs to be stored in GitHub, so there is nothing to leak, rotate or expire. Client secret OIDC federated credential Stored in GitHub Yes, as a secret Only the client and tenant IDs, which are identifiers Li"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#federated-credential","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Configuring the federated credential","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"On the application registration, add a federated credential for GitHub Actions deploying Azure resources . The values that matter are the issuer, the audience and the subject: Field Value Issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com Audience api://AzureADTokenExchange Subject, job that targets an environment repo:<org>/<repo>:environment:<environment> Subject, job on a branch repo:<org>/<repo>:ref:refs/heads/ma"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#secrets","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"GitHub secrets: scope matters","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"GitHub stores encrypted secrets at three scopes. Choosing the wrong one is the most common way production credentials end up readable by people who should never see them. Scope Readable by Use for For Fabric Repository Every workflow in the repository, on any branch Low-sensitivity values needed everywhere Not for deployment credentials. A workflow pushed to a feature branch could read them. Environment Only jobs tha"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#environments","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Environments with required reviewers","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"A GitHub environment is a named deployment target with its own secrets, variables and protection rules. A job that declares environment: prd pauses until the rules are satisfied, and only then receives the environment's secrets and an OIDC token carrying that environment in its subject. Every gate is enforced by the platform, not by convention. The PRD credentials do not exist for a job until a reviewer approves, and"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#environment-setup","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Setting up an environment","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Create it. Repository Settings , then Environments , then New environment . Create tst and prd . Names are not case sensitive and must be unique in the repository. Required reviewers. Add the people or teams who approve deployments to that environment, up to six; one approval is enough to proceed. These reviewers approve deployments , not pull requests: pull-request review is governed by the ruleset. Prevent self-rev"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#mechanisms","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Choosing how the pipeline deploys","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Fabric can reach TST and PRD by several routes. The service principal and OIDC pattern above is the same for all of them; the difference is what the job does once it is signed in. Mechanism What it does Fits when Watch for Deployment pipelines (Fabric) Promote content between stages inside Fabric, with deployment rules per stage. You want Fabric-native promotion and stage-level rules. Deployment rules and supported i"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#workflows","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Workflows","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Two workflows are enough to start. Both live in .github/workflows/ , which is outside every connected workspace folder, so Git sync never touches them."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#pr-validation","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Validation on pull requests","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"This workflow needs no Fabric access at all: it reads the repository and reports a status. Name the job validate and add it as a required status check in the ruleset. Grow it with the checks only you know to write: forbidden item types, hard-coded workspace names, a unit-test run for the notebook helper code in _tests/ ."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#deploy-workflow","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Deployment after merge","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The deployment workflow runs on every push to main . It deploys to TST automatically, then waits at the prd environment's gate. The folders are listed in dependency order and run one at a time, so a layer never deploys before the layer it reads from. id-token: write only on the deploy jobs. Every other job gets read-only access. Without this permission azure/login cannot request the OIDC token. allow-no-subscriptions"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#deploy-script","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"The deployment script","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The script signs in through the Azure CLI session that azure/login created, then publishes one workspace folder. It lives in _deploy/ , outside every workspace folder. Environment-specific values are swapped at publish time by a parameter file in the folder, so the item definitions in Git stay identical across environments: Orphan cleanup deletes items unpublish_all_orphan_items removes items from the target workspac"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#git-apis","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"Driving the Git APIs from automation","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Everything the source control pane does has a REST equivalent. That is useful for reconnecting a rebuilt workspace, scripting branch-out, or keeping a downstream workspace connected to Git. It comes with one catch for GitHub. Connect, initialize, then act. Commit and update are long-running operations: the call returns an operation ID, and you poll that operation until it reports success or failure. Connecting a work"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/service-principals-automation.html#refs","t":"Service principals and Actions","s":"References","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Identity support for the Fabric REST APIs Automate Git integration by using APIs Git - Connect (REST API) Git integration with service principal Best practices for lifecycle management in Fabric Managing environments for deployment Configuring OpenID Connect in Azure Secure use reference for GitHub Actions azure/login action fabric-cicd documentation"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Operations, rollback and checklist","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Playbook 05 · GitHub Integration Operations, rollback and checklist Everything so far builds the road. This page covers driving on it: how the pieces fit together, how to undo a bad change without making things worse, what to review regularly, what to check when something breaks, and what must be true before you go live. Audience everyone who runs or supports the setup Use it for incidents, reviews, troubleshooting, "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#unified","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"The whole picture","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The diagram below combines the workflow, the controls and the automation from the previous pages. Read it left to right along the top, then down to the promotion row. Fabric workspaces GitHub repository Automation Controls Recovery Two paths from main. DEV is updated from Git because DEV is where developers work; TST and PRD are deployed by a pipeline that proves its identity with OIDC and waits for approval before p"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#rollback","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Rolling back a bad change","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Version control makes a bad change recoverable, but only if you pick the right tool. The deciding question is whether anyone else has already pulled the commit. Default to revert. Reset exists for the narrow case where the history is yours alone; everywhere else, undoing by adding a commit is both safer and easier to audit."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#revert-vs-reset","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"git revert versus git reset","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"git revert git reset --hard What it does Adds a new commit that applies the inverse of an earlier one. Moves the branch pointer back and discards the commits after it. History Preserved. The mistake and its undo are both visible. Rewritten. The discarded commits disappear from the branch. Shared branches Safe. Dangerous. Everyone who pulled the old commits now has a diverged copy. Needs a force push No. Yes. Allowed "},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#revert","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Method 1: revert (the default)","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Because main is protected, the revert travels the same road as any other change. Find the change. Open the merged pull request that introduced the problem. If it was squash-merged it is a single commit; if it was merged with a merge commit, you will revert that merge commit. Create the revert. On the merged pull request choose Revert . GitHub creates a branch and opens a new pull request containing the inverse change"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#restore-item","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Restoring one item from an earlier commit","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Often only one notebook or pipeline needs to go back. Restore that folder from a known-good commit onto a new branch, and send it through review as a normal change. This works for a changed item and for one that was deleted."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#reset","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Method 2: reset (private branches only)","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Reset suits one case: a feature branch that only you use, where you want to throw away the last few commits and start over from a good one. It rewrites history, so it needs a force push, and the ruleset will not allow that on main . Prefer --force-with-lease to --force . It refuses to overwrite commits you have not seen. Hard reset discards uncommitted work in your clone. Check git status first. The feature workspace"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#restore-fabric","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Restoring in Fabric after the branch is fixed","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Rollback is a normal release. It goes through the same pull request, the same deployment and the same approval as any other change, only faster. Once the branch is correct, bring Fabric to match: Open the Fabric workspace connected to the branch, then Source control , then the Updates tab. Fabric detects that the branch moved and lists the items that will change. Read the list. Items the reverted change added will be"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#operations","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Running it day to day","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#monitoring","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Auditing and monitoring","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"GitHub. Review the audit log for changes to repository settings, team membership, rulesets, environments and secrets, and for any use of a bypass. Fabric. Use the monitoring hub for run history and the activity log for who did what, including calls made by the service principals. Entra ID. Watch sign-in logs for the service principals: unexpected locations or failures are the earliest sign of a misused or broken cred"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#cadence","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Recurring tasks","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Cadence Task Owner Weekly Review failed workflow runs and stuck approvals; remove merged branches and finished feature workspaces. Platform team Monthly Review uses of ruleset bypass and administrator environment bypass; bump pinned actions and library versions; check the supported-item list for items your design needs. Platform and security Quarterly Access review of admin, maintainer and automation groups; audit fe"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#testing","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Testing as a gate","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Unit tests for helper code used by notebooks run in pr-validation with a test framework such as pytest, and the result is a required status check. Data quality checks run against test data in TST after each deployment. Add a test job between deploy-tst and deploy-prd so PRD is only offered for approval when the checks pass. Regression tests are added with every fix, so the same incident cannot recur silently."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#scale","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Scaling to many repositories","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Define the baseline once in an organization-level ruleset and apply it to every Fabric repository. Package the deployment steps as a reusable workflow that each repository calls, so every team deploys the same way and a fix lands everywhere at once. Keep a template repository with the standard layout, CODEOWNERS , workflows and pull-request template, and create new repositories from it. Keep one service principal per"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#troubleshooting","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Symptom Likely cause What to do Commit from Fabric is rejected A ruleset on the connected branch requires pull requests, or the token cannot write to the repository. Commit to a feature branch, not main . Check the token's repository access and its SSO authorization. Update required never clears New commits keep arriving, or an item is in conflict. Run Update all ; if an item stays in conflict, resolve it as describe"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#checklist","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Go-live checklist","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#check-foundation","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Foundation","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Fabric tenant settings for Git integration are enabled for the right groups, and the service principal API setting is scoped to the automation group. The GitHub organization enforces single sign-on and two-factor authentication, with teams synced from Entra ID groups. A token policy exists, developers use fine-grained tokens, and the owner of each Fabric GitHub connection is documented."},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#check-repository","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Repository","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The folder layout matches the workspace layout, and each connected folder was empty or intentionally non-empty before the first sync. The baseline is committed to main , and the safety-net clone is in place until the baseline is proven. The ruleset on main is active: pull request, approvals, stale approvals dismissed, conversations resolved, code owners, no force push, no deletion. CODEOWNERS covers every item folder"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#check-automation","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Automation","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"One service principal per environment, in the automation group, with the lowest workspace role that works. Federated credentials are bound to environment subjects, and no client secret exists. Environments tst and prd exist with required reviewers, prevent self-review, and deployment branches limited to main . Workflows declare minimal permissions , pin actions to commit SHAs, and the validation job is a required sta"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#check-operations","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"Operations","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"The recurring review calendar exists with named owners. Developers know the workflow: branch out, small commits, pull request, delete the feature workspace afterwards. The rollback procedure, including who approves a fast-track revert, is written down where the on-call team will find it. Key takeaways One picture: developers work in feature workspaces, GitHub enforces review on main , DEV follows main, and a pipeline"},{"u":"playbooks/05-github-integration/operations-rollback.html#refs","t":"Operations, rollback and checklist","s":"References","p":"GitHub Integration and Branching","x":"Basic concepts in Git integration Resolve conflicts with Git integration Best practices for lifecycle management in Fabric Troubleshoot Fabric lifecycle management issues git-revert documentation git-reset documentation git-restore documentation Reverting a pull request Reviewing the audit log for your organization"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Playbook 06 · Shared Python Libraries Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark Package the utilities your teams keep pasting into notebooks as a versioned Python wheel, test it in CI, and deliver it through a Fabric Environment so every notebook and pipeline run imports the same vetted code. Audience data engineers, platform engineers Level intermediate Reading time about 80 minutes"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html#problem","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"The problem: utilities copied between notebooks","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Every team that writes more than a handful of Fabric notebooks ends up with the same helper cells: a full-reload function, a merge wrapper, a column validator, a row-count logger. Without a shared library each notebook carries its own copy. The copies diverge within weeks. One gets a bug fix, one gets an unrelated edit, one is never touched again. Nobody can answer \"which version of the merge helper ran last night?\","},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html#approach","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"The approach in one paragraph","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Write the utilities as an ordinary Python package, declare it in pyproject.toml , build a wheel ( .whl ) once in CI, upload that wheel to a Fabric Environment as a custom library, publish the Environment, and attach it to notebooks and Spark job definitions (or set it as the workspace default). Notebooks then contain a single import line instead of the helper code. Pipelines do not install anything: they run notebook"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html#when","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"When to use a shared library (and when not to)","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"A shared library pays for itself when the same mechanics appear in several notebooks and someone is willing to own them. The rule of thumb is share mechanics, not business logic : loading, merging, validating, logging and auditing belong in the library; the transformation that turns orders into a gold fact table stays in the notebook (or in a domain library owned by that domain). Put it in the library when Keep it in"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html#prerequisites","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"Prerequisites","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"A Fabric workspace on a Fabric capacity where you can create Environment items. A Git repository (the examples use GitHub and contoso-org/fabric-analytics ) with branch protection on the default branch. Python 3.11 or later on the build machine, matching the Python version of the Spark runtime your Environment selects. For automation: a Microsoft Entra service principal with a federated credential for GitHub Actions,"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html#outcomes","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"What you will have at the end","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"A package repository with a src/ layout, a PEP 621 pyproject.toml , unit tests and a reproducible wheel build. A small, neutral API ( full_load_overwrite , merge_upsert , max_value ) you can extend. An Environment per stage with the wheel published, attached to notebooks, and verified by a smoke test. A GitHub Actions workflow that tests, builds once and promotes the same wheel through DEV, TST and PRD with approvals"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html#map","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"Playbook map","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"02 Designing the library The transform, projection and call pattern; the reusable load and merge functions; API rules that keep the library safe to share. Design 15 min 03 Packaging and building pyproject.toml, src layout, the complete module, unit tests and a local wheel build and validation. Implementation 20 min 04 Installing in an Environment Upload, publish, attach; Quick versus Full mode; why not %pip in pipeli"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html#facts","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"Key facts at a glance","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Artifact A pure-Python wheel ( py3-none-any ). No compiled extensions, no platform tag. Where it lives in Fabric Environment item, Custom libraries . Fabric accepts .whl , .py , .jar and (R only) .tar.gz ; use .whl for Python packages. Publish modes Full resolves dependencies and builds a snapshot (typically 3 to 6 minutes to publish, plus 1 to 3 minutes of session start). Quick publishes in seconds and installs at n"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html#related","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"Related playbooks","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Data Load Strategy explains when to use full versus incremental loads; the helpers here implement both. Medallion Architecture shows where these utilities are called from (bronze to silver, silver to gold notebooks). GitHub Integration and Branching covers the branch model that the library repository follows. Deployment Pipelines and Promotion covers promoting notebooks and Environments between stages. Key takeaways "},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/index.html#refs","t":"Shared Python libraries for Fabric Spark","s":"References","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Manage Apache Spark libraries in Microsoft Fabric Manage libraries in Fabric environments Create, configure, and use an environment in Fabric Writing your pyproject.toml (Python Packaging User Guide)"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html","t":"Designing the library","s":"Designing the library","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Playbook 06 · Shared Python Libraries Designing the library Before packaging anything, decide where the seam between library and notebook runs. This page defines the transform, projection and call pattern, the load and merge helpers that implement it, and the API rules that keep a shared library from breaking its callers."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html#pattern","t":"Designing the library","s":"The notebook pattern the library comes from","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Most silver and gold notebooks follow the same three steps: a transformation function that derives columns, a projection list that fixes the output columns, and a call to a load function that reads, filters, writes and reports. The first two are specific to a table. The third is identical everywhere, which makes it the natural thing to lift into a library."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html#pattern-transform","t":"Designing the library","s":"Step 1: the transformation (stays in the notebook)","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"A transformation is a pure function from DataFrame to DataFrame. It reads nothing, writes nothing and uses no globals, so it can be unit tested with a three-row DataFrame."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html#pattern-projection","t":"Designing the library","s":"Step 2: the projection (stays in the notebook)","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"The projection is data, not code: the ordered list of columns the target table must have. Keeping it explicit makes schema changes visible in review and lets the load function fail fast when a column goes missing."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html#pattern-call","t":"Designing the library","s":"Step 3: the call (one line of intent)","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Everything the library cannot know (which columns, which filter, which transformation) arrives as an argument. Everything the library can standardize (reading, validating, writing, counting) lives behind the function. The seam. Green arrows down are arguments the notebook supplies; the green arrow up is the metrics dictionary it gets back. Nothing table-specific is hard-coded inside the library."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html#full-load","t":"Designing the library","s":"The reusable full-load function","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"full_load_overwrite reads a source table, optionally validates, filters, transforms and projects it, and overwrites a Delta target. The complete module code is on the packaging page ; this table is the contract. Parameter Type Default Behavior source_table , target_table str required Names resolved by Spark. Qualify them ( lakehouse.schema.table ) for cross-lakehouse work. transform_fn DataFrame to DataFrame identity"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html#changes","t":"Designing the library","s":"What changes when the code moves out of the notebook","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Copying the cell into a module verbatim does not work and, in a few places, would not be good enough if it did. These are the deliberate differences from the notebook version. Notebook version Library version Why Uses the notebook's global spark Takes an optional spark argument and falls back to the active session A module has no global spark ; the import would work and the first call would raise NameError . Injectio"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html#incremental","t":"Designing the library","s":"Incremental helpers","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Full overwrite is the right default for small dimensions and for tables that are cheap to rebuild. For large facts you need an incremental path. Two small helpers cover most of it. max_value reads a high-water mark from the target. merge_upsert applies a deduplicated change set to a Delta table with MERGE . Behavior How it is implemented Failure it prevents Duplicate keys in the change set fail early Group by key_col"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html#api-rules","t":"Designing the library","s":"API design rules","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"A shared library is consumed by people who cannot read its source quickly and who run it unattended. These rules keep it safe to depend on. Rule What it looks like What happens if you ignore it Keyword-only arguments def f(*, source_table, target_table, ...) Adding or reordering a parameter silently changes the meaning of positional calls No hidden globals Take a spark argument; never read spark , mssparkutils or not"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/library-design.html#refs","t":"Designing the library","s":"References","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Delta Lake: upsert into a table using MERGE Apache Spark runtime in Fabric (Spark, Delta Lake and Python versions per runtime) Develop, execute, and manage notebooks"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"Packaging and building a wheel","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Playbook 06 · Shared Python Libraries Packaging and building a wheel The artifact Fabric consumes is a wheel. This page declares the project in pyproject.toml , shows the repository layout and the complete module, adds tests that run without Fabric, and builds and validates the wheel on your machine."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#pep621","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"PEP 621 and pyproject.toml in practice","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"PEP 621 defines how a project declares its core metadata (name, version, dependencies, Python requirement, authors, license) in a [project] table of pyproject.toml . Every mainstream build backend reads the same table, so the metadata is portable and the backend is a swappable detail. The living specification is maintained by the Python Packaging Authority, and the Packaging User Guide is the place to check current f"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#backend","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"Choosing a build backend","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"For a pure-Python library of utilities, any of the standard backends produces an identical wheel. Pick the one your team already operates. Backend build-backend Choose it when setuptools setuptools.build_meta Default choice. Familiar, reads [project] , supports the src layout and optional dynamic versioning through setuptools-scm . hatchling hatchling.build You want sensible defaults with almost no configuration and "},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#repo-structure","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"Repository structure","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Use the src layout: the importable package lives under src/ , tests live beside it, and nothing build-related is committed. Do not commit build output Folders such as dist/ , build/ and *.egg-info/ belong in .gitignore . A committed dist/ goes stale the moment the code changes, and people will upload the stale wheel because it is sitting right there. The only wheel that should reach an Environment is one built by CI "},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#pyproject","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"The pyproject.toml","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Field Decision Why name contoso-fabric-utils (distribution) versus contoso_fabric_utils (import) Tools normalize hyphens, underscores and dots to the same project name, and wheel file names use underscores. Keep one canonical spelling in documentation and notebooks: import with underscores. version Static, bumped in the release pull request The wheel file name carries the version, so Environments, logs and changelogs"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#versioning","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"Versioning options","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Strategy How Strengths Weaknesses Static version version = \"1.4.0\" edited in the release pull request; CI fails if the Git tag does not equal v plus the project version Obvious, no extra tooling, wheel name is known before the build A human must remember to bump it; the CI check turns forgetting into a failed build instead of a wrong release Dynamic from a tag dynamic = [\"version\"] with setuptools-scm in build-system"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#module-code","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"The module code","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"This is the library's public surface. It is the notebook function from the previous page with the changes explained there: injectable session, safer defaults and metrics returned instead of printed. How the metrics are read The merge result is read back from the table's Delta history (the commit made by this call), not from a second scan. The helper compares the table version before and after so that a merge that com"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#tests","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"Tests that run without Fabric","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"The library imports pyspark and delta , both of which the test extra installs locally. A session-scoped local Spark session with the Delta extensions is enough to test real behavior on tiny DataFrames. The tests run on a laptop or a GitHub runner in a minute or two once Spark's Delta jars are cached. Add a test for every transformation you share, using three-row DataFrames that include a null, a duplicate and a bound"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#build","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"Build and validate the wheel locally","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"python -m build reads pyproject.toml , creates an isolated build environment containing only build-system.requires , builds a source distribution first, then builds the wheel from that source distribution. If the sdist is missing a file the wheel needs, the build fails here and not in production. Release check on your machine. The wheel is the artifact; dist/ stays out of Git. CI repeats exactly these steps, so a gre"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#inspect","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"What to check in the output","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Check Expected If not File name contoso_fabric_utils-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl : py3-none-any means pure Python, no platform tag A platform tag means a compiled extension crept in; a wheel like that will not install on the Fabric runtime's Linux image unless it matches Listing from python -m zipfile -l contoso_fabric_utils/__init__.py , loads.py , py.typed and a .dist-info folder with METADATA , WHEEL and the license Mi"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/packaging.html#refs","t":"Packaging and building a wheel","s":"References","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Writing your pyproject.toml src layout vs flat layout Single-sourcing the project version PEP 621: Storing project metadata in pyproject.toml PEP 639: Improving license clarity with better package metadata Apache Spark runtime in Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/environment.html","t":"Installing in a Fabric Environment","s":"Installing in a Fabric Environment","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Playbook 06 · Shared Python Libraries Installing in a Fabric Environment An Environment turns the wheel into a durable, workspace-visible dependency. This page walks through upload, publish and attach, explains what Full and Quick publish modes cost you, and shows why pipelines should never depend on %pip install ."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/environment.html#concepts","t":"Installing in a Fabric Environment","s":"What an Environment is, and where it applies","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"A Fabric Environment is a workspace item that defines the configuration of a Spark session: the runtime, the compute settings, the libraries and a small set of resource files. Libraries come from three places: the built-in set that ships with the runtime, public or private repositories ( External repositories ), and files you upload under Custom libraries . For a Python library the custom file is a .whl (Fabric also "},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/environment.html#steps","t":"Installing in a Fabric Environment","s":"Create the Environment and install the wheel","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Do this first in the DEV workspace. Use an Environment per stage and the same item name in each (for example ENV_SALES_SLV_SharedLibs ). Create the Environment In the workspace select New item , search for Environment , name it, and select Create . Select the runtime On the Environment's runtime dropdown choose the runtime you tested the wheel against (the one whose Python and Spark versions match requires-python and"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/environment.html#publish-modes","t":"Installing in a Fabric Environment","s":"Publish modes and their effect on session start","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"When you add custom libraries you pick a publish mode. The choice decides when dependency work happens: once at publish time, or on every session start. Full mode Quick mode What publish does Resolves dependencies, validates compatibility and creates a stable library snapshot Skips dependency processing Publish time Typically 3 to 6 minutes About 5 seconds Session start Adds roughly 1 to 3 minutes to deploy the snaps"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/environment.html#pip-in-pipelines","t":"Installing in a Fabric Environment","s":"Why not %pip install in pipelines?","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"%pip install (and %conda install ) are inline commands. They install into the current session of the current notebook , restart the Python interpreter, and are not touched by Environment publishing. That is perfect for a one-off experiment and wrong for unattended work, for four reasons. Disabled by default. Inline library commands are turned off in pipeline notebook runs. You can switch them on by passing a boolean "},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/environment.html#use-in-notebook","t":"Installing in a Fabric Environment","s":"Use the library in a notebook","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"With the Environment published and attached, the notebook change is mechanical: delete the cell that defines the helper, add an import, and leave the transformation, projection and call cells as they are. Run the notebook once against a scratch target table first. If the import fails, work through this list. Symptom Likely cause Fix ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'contoso_fabric_utils' The notebook is not attac"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/environment.html#across-stages","t":"Installing in a Fabric Environment","s":"Environments across stages and workspaces","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Treat the Environment as a deployable item with its own lifecycle. There are three ways to get the same library set into DEV, TST and PRD, and you should choose exactly one writer per Environment. Mechanism How it works Watch for CI uploads and publishes (recommended for libraries) The pipeline in the CI/CD page calls the Environment REST API in each stage Do not also edit the library list by hand; the next release w"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/environment.html#refs","t":"Installing in a Fabric Environment","s":"References","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Create, configure, and use an environment in Fabric Manage libraries in Fabric environments Manage Apache Spark libraries in Fabric Fabric environment Git integration and deployment pipeline Custom live pools overview Manage libraries with limited network access in Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/cicd.html","t":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","s":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Playbook 06 · Shared Python Libraries CI/CD with GitHub Actions Uploading a wheel by hand does not scale past one workspace and one person. This page automates the path from a tagged commit to a published Environment in each stage: tests, one build, OIDC sign-in with no stored secrets, upload and publish through the Fabric REST API, and approvals before TST and PRD."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/cicd.html#design","t":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","s":"Pipeline design: build once, promote the same file","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"The most important design decision is that the wheel is built exactly once , from a tag, and that same file is uploaded to every stage. Rebuilding per stage produces three artifacts that are supposed to be identical and sometimes are not (a changed dependency pin, a different Python patch version, a timestamp). If PRD runs a different file than the one TST approved, TST proved nothing. Pull requests run the tests onl"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/cicd.html#identity","t":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","s":"Identity, secrets and permissions","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"The workflow signs in to Microsoft Entra ID with OpenID Connect : GitHub issues a short-lived token for the job, Entra exchanges it for an access token against a federated credential on an app registration, and no client secret is ever stored in GitHub. The access token is then used as a bearer token against the Fabric REST API. Create an app registration per stage One service principal for DEV and TST and a separate"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/cicd.html#api","t":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","s":"The Fabric REST sequence","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Four documented Environment operations do all the work. The workflow calls them in this order. Step Call Notes List staged libraries GET /v1/workspaces/{workspaceId}/environments/{environmentId}/staging/libraries?beta=False Returns libraries with name and libraryType ( Custom or External ). Large lists are paged with a continuation token. Delete an old wheel DELETE .../staging/libraries/{libraryName} Use the full fil"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/cicd.html#script","t":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","s":"The publish script","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Keep the Fabric calls in one small script so you can run it locally while debugging. It needs only curl and jq , both present on GitHub-hosted Ubuntu runners. Three details are worth knowing. The script deletes every existing wheel of the same distribution before it uploads, which enforces \"exactly one version per Environment\" and makes a re-run of the same tag idempotent. It waits for the service's Retry-After inter"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/cicd.html#workflow","t":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","s":"The workflows","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"A caller workflow runs tests and the build, then calls a reusable deployment workflow three times. The reusable workflow declares the GitHub environment, which is what triggers the approval rule. Prove the release with a smoke notebook After the publish, run a tiny notebook in that stage's workspace through the Fabric Job Scheduler API (the Environment must be attached to it, or be the workspace default). Pass the ex"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/cicd.html#alternatives","t":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","s":"Alternatives to uploading the wheel yourself","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Option How it works Choose it when Constraints REST upload (this page) CI uploads the wheel to each Environment and publishes One platform, a handful of workspaces, you want full control of the sequence You own the script and the polling logic Private package feed Preview CI publishes the wheel to an Azure Artifacts feed; the Environment lists the package under External repositories and resolves it at publish time th"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/cicd.html#release-practice","t":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","s":"Release practice","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Keep a CHANGELOG.md and write breaking changes at the top of the release notes. Use pre-release versions ( 1.5.0rc1 ) to try a change in TST without promoting it. Roll back by re-running the deployment for the previous tag; the script replaces the newer wheel with the older one. Keep previous wheels as release assets so rollback does not depend on artifact retention. Allow only one release in flight per Environment ("},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/cicd.html#refs","t":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","s":"References","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Manage the Environment through public APIs Staging: Upload Custom Library Items: Publish Environment Items: Get Environment Long running operations Use the Azure Login action with OpenID Connect Developer admin settings (service principals and Fabric APIs)"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Options, operations, checklist","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Playbook 06 · Shared Python Libraries Options, operations, checklist A wheel in an Environment is the default, not the only answer. This page compares every realistic way to share code between Fabric notebooks, shows how to test without publishing anything, and covers the governance, troubleshooting and validation work that keeps a shared library trustworthy."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#options","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Distribution options compared","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"The right mechanism depends on three questions: does the code run unattended, how far does it need to travel, and how much process does it deserve. The matrix lists what each option gives you and what it costs. Option Safe in pipeline runs Versioned Beyond one workspace Notes Wheel in an Environment, Full mode Yes Yes Partly Every item that attaches the Environment gets the same snapshot. Publish takes minutes and se"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#when-each","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"When each option earns its place","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Environment, Full mode is the production answer for a single platform team and a handful of workspaces. Everything in this playbook assumes it. Environment, Quick mode is for the library's own developers: change, publish in seconds, retest in a notebook. Promote the validated version to Full mode. A private feed is worth the extra moving parts when the same package is consumed by Fabric and by something else (another"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#test-without-env","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Testing without creating an Environment","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"You can try a build before it has an Environment, or iterate on it without waiting for a publish. These techniques are session-scoped on purpose: they cannot affect any other notebook, and they disappear when the session ends."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#test-pip-builtin","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Install the wheel from notebook resources","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Upload the wheel to the notebook's Resources (the built-in folder), then install it in the first cells of the notebook. This is the quickest way to test a release candidate. The installation restarts the Python interpreter, so variables defined before it are lost; keep installs at the top. The package is available on the driver and the executors for that session only."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#test-sys-path","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Import source files straight from resources","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"For a tight edit loop you can skip packaging entirely: upload the package folder ( contoso_fabric_utils/ with its __init__.py ) to the notebook's built-in resources and put the resources folder on the import path. Put the parent folder on the path, never the package folder If you add builtin/contoso_fabric_utils itself to sys.path , every module inside it becomes importable by its bare name. A module called io.py wou"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#test-lakehouse","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Install from Lakehouse Files","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"If several people need to try the same candidate, put the wheel in a lakehouse folder (for example Files/libs/ ) and install from the mounted path. The lakehouse must be the notebook's default lakehouse so that it is mounted as a local path."},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#test-run","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Share a few functions with %run","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"%run executes another notebook in the current context, so everything it defines is available afterward. It works in interactive and pipeline runs, but only for notebooks in the same workspace, with a limited set of parameter types (integer, float, boolean, string) and a maximum nesting depth. It is also how copy-paste returns in disguise: there is no version number, and a change to the shared notebook affects every c"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#governance","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Workspace usage patterns and governance","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#attach-pattern","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Workspace default or per-item attachment","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Pattern Good for Trade-off Workspace default Environment Every notebook and Spark job in the workspace should get the library without anyone remembering to attach it Only a workspace admin can choose which Environment is the default, which is a useful control. Items inherit it, including ones that do not need it; if the default is ever changed or removed, imports fail in every item that relied on it Attach per item S"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#governance-rules","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Rules that keep it trustworthy","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Ownership. The library repository has named owners and a code-owners file, so a change to the public API cannot merge without them. See Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS . Semantic versions and a support policy. State which major and minor versions are supported (for example the current major and the previous minor) and how long a deprecated function stays. Review library code as production code. A notebook "},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#troubleshooting","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix ModuleNotFoundError in a notebook Environment not attached, default-environment toggle off, or session started before the publish finished Check the Environment shown in the notebook; confirm publish state; start a new session Interactive run works, pipeline run fails The interactive session used a %pip cell or Quick mode; the pipeline activity did not Publish in Full mode and delete inline i"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#checklist","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"Validation checklist","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Run through this list once per stage when you introduce the library and after every release. The Environment's Custom libraries list contains exactly one wheel of the distribution, at the intended version, and the publish state is Success . A new notebook session with the Environment attached imports contoso_fabric_utils and prints the expected __version__ . A test run of full_load_overwrite into a scratch table succ"},{"u":"playbooks/06-shared-libraries/distribution-and-ops.html#refs","t":"Options, operations, checklist","s":"References","p":"Shared Python Libraries","x":"Manage Apache Spark libraries in Fabric Manage libraries in Fabric environments How to use notebooks (notebook resources) Develop, execute, and manage notebooks (%run, magic commands) Custom live pools overview Apache Spark runtime in Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/index.html","t":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","s":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Playbook 07 · Deployment Pipelines Deployment Pipelines and Promotion Git versions your items; deployment pipelines promote them. This playbook shows how to run both together, how pairing, autobinding and deployment rules behave, and how to promote a medallion platform layer by layer without breaking references. Audience Architects, data engineers, release managers Level Advanced Reading time about 2 hours"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/index.html#what-it-solves","t":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","s":"What this playbook solves","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Fabric ships two lifecycle tools that are easy to conflate. Git integration stores item definitions in a repository so you get branches, pull requests and history. Deployment pipelines copy items between workspaces that stand for stages (development, test, production), compare the stages and apply per-stage configuration. Teams that adopt only one of them hit predictable walls: Git alone gives review and rollback but"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/index.html#at-a-glance","t":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","s":"The pattern at a glance","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Question Answer used throughout this playbook Where does development happen? In temporary feature workspaces connected to feature branches. The DEV workspace is connected to main . How do changes reach TST and PRD? Through a deployment pipeline. TST and PRD workspaces have no Git connection. What differs per stage? Connections, default lakehouses and parameters, set with deployment rules, variable library values or p"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/index.html#when-to-use","t":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","s":"When to use this pattern, and when not to","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Situation Fit Why Separate developers and release managers; stage-by-stage review with a visual compare Good fit The pipeline gives a compare view, a change review, a deployment history with notes and a single place to configure rules. Semantic models or paginated reports that must connect to different sources per stage Good fit Deployment rules exist for exactly these items. Medallion platform with several workspace"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/index.html#audience","t":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","s":"Audience and prerequisites","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Audience Platform architects who choose the lifecycle design, data engineers who build and promote items, release managers who run deployments, and reviewers who need to understand what a promotion changes. Capacity Every workspace assigned to a pipeline must be on a Fabric capacity. Roles Pipeline admin on the pipeline, plus at least Contributor on both the source and target workspaces of each deployment. Assigning "},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/index.html#outcomes","t":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","s":"What you will be able to do","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Choose between Git-only, hybrid and code-first promotion, and defend the choice with the trade-offs. Create a pipeline, assign workspaces and explain exactly when items become paired. Run a manual deployment, read the compare labels and review changes before and after. Predict which references autobind and which need a rule, a parameter or a variable library. Configure deployment rules for a notebook's default lakeho"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/index.html#map","t":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","s":"Map of this playbook","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"01 Deployment strategy The hybrid pattern, the alternatives and an honest decision table. Design 02 Pipeline setup and pairing Stages, workspace assignment, the two UIs, pairing and known issues. Steps 03 The manual deployment Compare, deploy, review, and what a deployment copies. Steps 04 Autobinding What re-points automatically, what does not and how to override it. Concepts 05 Deployment order and layers One pipel"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/index.html#related","t":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","s":"How this fits with the other playbooks","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Medallion architecture defines the layer workspaces that this playbook promotes. GitHub integration and branching covers the Git half of the hybrid pattern: branches, feature workspaces and pull requests. Variable libraries covers the per-stage configuration that complements deployment rules. Deployment checklist condenses this playbook into a runbook you can follow on release day. Key takeaways Use Git for developme"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/index.html#refs","t":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","s":"References","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Overview of Fabric deployment pipelines The deployment pipelines process Best practices for lifecycle management in Fabric Choose the best Fabric CI/CD workflow option"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/strategy.html","t":"Deployment strategy","s":"Deployment strategy","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Playbook 07 · Deployment Pipelines Deployment strategy Before you create a pipeline, decide what is the source of truth for each stage and what moves content between stages. This page defines the hybrid pattern, shows the trade-offs against the alternatives and lists the operating rules that stop it degrading into drift."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/strategy.html#two-tools","t":"Deployment strategy","s":"Two tools, two jobs","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Git integration and deployment pipelines overlap in marketing but not in mechanics. Keep the split in mind and most design questions answer themselves. Capability Git integration Deployment pipelines What it moves Item definitions between a repository branch and one workspace Item definitions between workspaces that represent adjacent stages Strength History, branching, pull requests, review, revert Stage compare, se"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/strategy.html#hybrid-pattern","t":"Deployment strategy","s":"The hybrid pattern","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"The pattern is documented by Microsoft as the option where Git is connected only up to the development stage and deployments from there are workspace to workspace. It separates developer concerns from release concerns, and it is the option that gives you deployment rules and autobinding for per-stage configuration. Connect only DEV to Git The DEV workspace tracks the main branch. All commits and pull-request merges f"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/strategy.html#why-it-works","t":"Deployment strategy","s":"Why the split matters","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Developers keep the full Git workflow without exposing TST and PRD to direct commits, force-pushes or accidental syncs. Release managers get a simple, auditable interface : compare two stages, deploy selected items, read the history with the notes. Per-stage configuration has a home. Deployment rules can only be defined in the pipeline, and they are what lets one definition connect to different sources per stage."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/strategy.html#decision","t":"Deployment strategy","s":"Choosing between the options","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Microsoft describes four workflow options: Git integration with a branch per stage, code-first deployments through the Fabric Items APIs, deployment pipelines, and a variant of code-first for ISVs. Most real platforms mix them, for example Git for development, a pipeline for platform items and code-first for items the pipeline does not cover. The table compares the three that matter to an enterprise data platform. Th"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/strategy.html#how-to-decide","t":"Deployment strategy","s":"How to decide","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"If the people who release are not the people who develop, and they want to see what will change before it changes, choose the hybrid . If policy demands that every production change comes from a reviewed commit and a CI run with gates, choose code-first , or hybrid with the pipeline triggered from CI. If your team already runs Gitflow and wants Git as the only record, choose Git-only , and plan the per-stage configur"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/strategy.html#operating-rules","t":"Deployment strategy","s":"Operating rules for the hybrid pattern","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Rule Why Protect main and require pull-request review DEV mirrors main , and DEV is what you promote. Review is the only quality gate before the pipeline. Never edit TST or PRD by hand A manual edit shows as Different in the compare and is overwritten by the next deployment, so the fix silently disappears. Fix forward through the whole path A production bug is fixed on a feature branch, merged, synced to DEV and prom"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/strategy.html#anti-patterns","t":"Deployment strategy","s":"Anti-patterns","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Anti-pattern What goes wrong Do this instead Connecting TST and PRD to Git branches while also using a pipeline Two mechanisms can change the same workspace, so you can no longer say which definition is live. Pick one mover per workspace. In the hybrid, only DEV has a Git connection. Deploying out of a feature workspace Unreviewed, unmerged content reaches TST and the repository no longer explains the environment. Me"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/strategy.html#refs","t":"Deployment strategy","s":"References","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"CI/CD workflow options in Fabric Overview of Fabric deployment pipelines Manage Git branches Best practices for lifecycle management in Fabric Deployment plan overview"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"Pipeline setup and pairing","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Playbook 07 · Deployment Pipelines Pipeline setup and pairing A pipeline is quick to create and hard to change: the stage count and names are permanent, and pairing is created once. This page covers the setup steps, the two user interfaces, how items become paired and a corrected list of known issues."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html#prerequisites","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"Prerequisites","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"You have a Fabric license and are an admin of at least one workspace; that is what makes the Deployment pipelines entry point visible. Every workspace you intend to assign resides on a Fabric capacity. Plan the capacity per environment before you create anything. You are admin of each workspace you assign, and at least Contributor on the workspaces in the adjacent stages. Workspace names follow the convention <ENV>_<"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html#create","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"Create the pipeline","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Open the creation dialog From the workspaces flyout choose Deployment pipelines , then Create pipeline (or + New pipeline ). You can also create a pipeline from inside a workspace you administer; that workspace is then assigned automatically. Name it Use DPLY_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER> , for example DPLY_SALES_SLV , and add a purpose suffix only if you need two pipelines for one layer. Name and description can be edited later "},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html#assign","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"Assign workspaces","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Pick the stage In an empty stage open Add content to this stage and choose the workspace. Only workspaces that meet the conditions in the prerequisites appear in the list. Assign Select Assign . Fabric scans the workspace and tries to pair its items with items in the adjacent stage. Repeat for each stage Assign DEV first, then TST and PRD. Because pairing happens at assignment, assign workspaces that already contain "},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html#uis","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"The original and the new UI","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"There are two interfaces for the same functionality. The new UI is the default and is in preview; a toggle in the upper right corner switches to the original UI, and your choice is remembered. Both support the same deployment rules, history and change review. Original (classic) UI New UI Perspective You deploy from the source stage and create rules from the target stage Everything is done from the selected target sta"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html#pairing","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"Item pairing","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Pairing is the stored link between an item in one stage and its counterpart in the adjacent stage. It decides whether a deployment overwrites an item or creates a second copy, and everything else (compare, autobinding, rules) builds on it. How pairing decides overwrite or duplicate. Pairing is a stored link, not a live name match. Paired items overwrite on deploy even if renamed; identical-looking items that were nev"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html#pairing-rules","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"How items become paired","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Moment What happens A workspace is assigned to a stage Fabric pairs items of the same type and name in the adjacent stage. Folder location breaks ties only when a stage holds duplicates (same name and type). If one item per stage matches, they pair regardless of folder. An unpaired item is deployed for the first time (a clean deploy) A copy is created in the target stage and paired with the source item. A paired item"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html#pairing-consequences","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"What this means in practice","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"There is no way to pair items manually. Pairing happens only through the two moments above, so align names and types before the first assignment or deploy. A rename does not unpair. Paired items can carry different names, and a rename in the source is applied to the target on the next deploy. Do not rename items directly in TST or PRD. Folders count as part of the name. Moving an item to another folder is a change th"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html#known-issues","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"Known issues and how to resolve them","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"The table below replaces the community-sourced list that circulates in many playbooks. Each row is a documented behaviour with its cause, so you can tell a permission problem from a product problem. The first row is by far the most common. Why is Deploy greyed out?. Walk left to right; the first answer that is no is the fix. Permissions on the pipeline and on the workspaces are granted separately. Symptom Cause Resol"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/pipeline-setup.html#refs","t":"Pipeline setup and pairing","s":"References","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Get started with deployment pipelines Assign a workspace to a deployment pipeline Overview of the new deployment pipelines UI The deployment pipelines process Troubleshoot the Fabric lifecycle management tools"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html","t":"The manual deployment","s":"The manual deployment","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Playbook 07 · Deployment Pipelines The manual deployment Learn the manual deployment before you automate it. Any automation has to reproduce the same gates: compare, select with dependencies, deploy with a note, then verify and hydrate. This page walks each one in both UIs and states what a deployment does to the target."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#before","t":"The manual deployment","s":"Before you deploy","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"The change is merged to main and DEV has been updated from Git (see the hybrid pattern ). Items you are about to promote have paired counterparts in the target stage, or you accept that a first deploy creates them. Upstream layers were already deployed to the target stage, hydrated and verified. See Deployment order and layers . Rule values for the target stage (connection strings, lakehouse IDs) are prepared. The ap"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#compare","t":"The manual deployment","s":"Compare the stages","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"The pipeline home page compares each stage with the stage it is deployed from. A green indicator means the metadata of all items is identical; an orange indicator means at least one item was changed, added or removed. Items are matched through pairing, so the compare is only as good as your pairing. Select the target stage (new UI) The source stage is shown in the drop-down next to Deploy . Every item in the target s"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#select","t":"The manual deployment","s":"Choose what to deploy","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Full, selective and backward deployment. Select related adds upstream dependencies so a report never travels without its semantic model. Backward deployment is meant for bootstrapping a pipeline from an existing production workspace, not for hotfixes."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#full","t":"The manual deployment","s":"Full deployment","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Deploy everything that is new or different in one go. This is the standard for a release: one sprint's worth of reviewed change. In the original UI select Deploy on the source stage card; in the new UI select the target stage, pick the source, select all items and deploy."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#selective","t":"The manual deployment","s":"Selective deployment","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Use it for a hotfix to one item, or when you must promote a layer in parts. Select Show more (original UI) or tick the items (new UI) and deploy; only the selected items move. The Deploy button shows how many items are selected, and a single deployment is limited to 300 items. Select related. Adds the upstream items the selected ones depend on, for example the semantic model behind a report. It works in the flat list"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#backward","t":"The manual deployment","s":"Backward deployment","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Deploys from a later stage to an earlier one. It is available only when the target stage is empty and only as a full deployment. Its legitimate use is bootstrapping: you assign an existing production workspace to the last stage and deploy backward to create TST and then DEV. It is not a hotfix path, because as soon as the earlier stage has a workspace the option is disabled."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#execute","t":"The manual deployment","s":"Execute the deployment","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Select the items and start the deploy A dialog lists every item you are about to deploy. It warns when items in the target will be replaced. Add a note Expand Add a note . State what is promoted and why, for example Release 12: order margin calculation, ticket 4821 . Notes are stored in the deployment history and are your audit trail. A note without a ticket or release reference is nearly useless in six months. Optio"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#dev-to-tst","t":"The manual deployment","s":"DEV to TST, then TST to PRD","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"The two hops are the same procedure with different consequences. Deploying DEV to TST is where you find missing pairs, unexpected Different items and rules that do not apply. Deploying TST to PRD should then be a repeat of a rehearsed change: compare the stages, expect the same set of differences you promoted to TST, deploy with a note that references the TST note, and verify. If the TST to PRD compare shows items yo"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#outcome","t":"The manual deployment","s":"What a deployment does to the target","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"What a deployment copies, and what it leaves alone. Green arrows overwrite the target. Everything behind a red dashed line stays as the target stage had it, which is why every promoted layer must be hydrated or refreshed. Deployment copies definitions and the connections between items, then applies the rules for the target stage. The overwrite changes an item's content only; the item's ID, URL and permissions stay. C"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#history","t":"The manual deployment","s":"Deployment history and change review","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Every deployment is logged and visible to pipeline admins: source and target stage, time, the user who started it, status and the note. The stage cards also show when content was last deployed to that stage. Use the history to answer \"what is in production and who put it there\"."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#review","t":"The manual deployment","s":"In-depth change review","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Open the compare for the stage pair Select the Compare icon (new UI) or hover over a different item and select Change review (original UI). Read the line-by-line diff The left side (red, minus) is the target content that will be removed or overwritten; the right side (green, plus) is the source content that will be applied. Switch between side-by-side and inline views. Remember the limits Change review supports schem"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#verify","t":"The manual deployment","s":"After the deployment","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Compare again: deployed items read Same as source . Run the load pipelines or notebooks that hydrate the layer, then check tables and shortcuts in the target workspace. Refresh dataflows first, then semantic models, and set credentials and the schedule on a new model. Open one report per deployed model and confirm it points at the target model. Record the outcome against the deployment note. The full per-layer proced"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/manual-deploy.html#refs","t":"The manual deployment","s":"References","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Deploy content using deployment pipelines Compare content in different stages The deployment pipelines process Deployment history Deployment plan overview"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/autobinding.html","t":"Autobinding","s":"Autobinding","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Playbook 07 · Deployment Pipelines Autobinding Autobinding is why a report deployed to TST reads the TST model instead of the DEV one. It is also the feature most often over-trusted: it covers a defined set of references inside one workspace, and it silently does not apply to cross-workspace references, Direct Lake models or Dataflow Gen2."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/autobinding.html#mechanism","t":"Autobinding","s":"How autobinding works","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Autobinding builds on item pairing . When you deploy an item, the pipeline looks at what the item depends on in the source stage and asks whether the paired counterpart of each dependency exists in the target stage. Identify dependencies Find the items the deployed item reads from in the source stage, for example the semantic model behind a report. Use View lineage on an item to see them. Look for the paired dependen"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/autobinding.html#cross-pipeline","t":"Autobinding","s":"Across pipelines","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"When reports and semantic models live in different workspaces, and therefore different pipelines, the pipelines can still bind them: a report in the test stage of pipeline B that reads a model in the test stage of pipeline A is recognised as connected, and a new deployment of the report reconnects it to the matching stage of pipeline A. Cross-pipeline autobinding follows the stage position. The match is by stage inde"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/autobinding.html#what-binds","t":"Autobinding","s":"Which references autobind","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Will this reference autobind?. Most surprises come from the first question. Cross-workspace references between Fabric items store object IDs, which are not rewritten on deployment. Reference Autobinds? Condition, or what to do Power BI report to semantic model Yes Same workspace, or same stage position across two pipelines. The model must exist in the target stage. Notebook to default lakehouse Yes, same workspace La"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/autobinding.html#across-layers","t":"Autobinding","s":"What this means for a layered platform","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"A platform with one workspace per layer has many references that cross a workspace boundary: a Silver notebook that reads a Bronze lakehouse, a warehouse that queries a Gold lakehouse, a model that reads a warehouse. Those references are not autobound, however the deployment order is arranged. Plan each one: Cross-layer reference Recommended handling Notebook reads a lakehouse in another workspace Resolve the lakehou"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/autobinding.html#override","t":"Autobinding","s":"Overriding autobinding","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Mechanism How it overrides Use it when Deployment rule Rules always take precedence over autobinding and are applied during the deploy. An item type has a rule: notebook default lakehouse, semantic model, dataflow gen1, paginated report, mirrored database data sources. Parameter rule When a parameter controls the connection, autobinding does not happen, even if the parameter holds an item or workspace ID. You rebind "},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/autobinding.html#limits","t":"Autobinding","s":"Limits and operating practice","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"A missing dependency fails the deployment. Deploy dependencies first, or select them with Select related . Circular or self dependencies fail the whole deployment. Do not delete and recreate. Deleting an item in the source does not remove its pair in the target; it becomes Not in source there. Recreating it in the source under the same name creates an unpaired item: the first deployment of a Power BI item fails if th"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/autobinding.html#verify-binding","t":"Autobinding","s":"Verify binding after a deployment","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Open the lineage On an item choose More options then View lineage and confirm that the upstream items are the target-stage items. Check notebooks Open a deployed notebook and read the attached lakehouse and environment in the explorer pane. A notebook still attached to the DEV lakehouse means the rule or binding did not apply. Check models Open the model settings and confirm the data source points at the target stage"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/autobinding.html#refs","t":"Autobinding","s":"References","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"The deployment pipelines process (autobinding) Understand dependency binding in cross-workspace deployment Notebook source control and deployment Create deployment rules Lakehouse Git integration and deployment pipelines"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/order-and-layers.html","t":"Deployment order and layers","s":"Deployment order and layers","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Playbook 07 · Deployment Pipelines Deployment order and layers A layered platform has many workspaces and many cross-references. This page defines the pipeline layout, the order that respects the dependencies, the deploy-hydrate-verify loop, and what each layer needs before the next can follow."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/order-and-layers.html#why-order","t":"Deployment order and layers","s":"Why the order matters","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Dependencies must exist in the target. A report cannot deploy without its semantic model, and a notebook binds to the lakehouse and environment that already exist in its workspace. Binding is evaluated against the target as it is when you deploy. Pairing, autobinding and rules all read the current state of the target stage; deploying out of order changes what they find. A deployment copies definitions, not data. A fr"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/order-and-layers.html#one-pipeline-per-layer","t":"Deployment order and layers","s":"One pipeline per layer","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Create a separate pipeline for each layer, named DPLY_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER> . Each has three stages, each stage holds exactly one layer workspace, and the workspace names follow <ENV>_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER> . Reference layout: one pipeline per layer. Each row is its own pipeline with three stages and three workspaces. Rows are deployed in order from the top; no pipeline mixes layers. Pipeline Workspace per stage Typical content"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/order-and-layers.html#sequence","t":"Deployment order and layers","s":"The deployment sequence","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Dependencies decide the order. Numbers give the default deployment order. If a Direct Lake model reads the consumption lakehouse, follow the dashed edge: deploy and hydrate that lakehouse before the model. Step Layer Deploy Needs first Then hydrate and verify 1 BRZ Lakehouse, environment, variable library; then notebooks, data pipelines, shortcuts Source connections owned by a shared identity Set the stage's variable"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/order-and-layers.html#loop","t":"Deployment order and layers","s":"The deploy, hydrate, verify loop","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Deploy, hydrate, verify, then move on. Odd steps deploy, even steps hydrate and verify. The same loop repeats from TST to PRD. A failed even step stops the sequence. Within each layer the order of items matters too, because binding looks for items that must already exist: Lakehouse and environment first Deploy the lakehouse, the environment and any variable library, and wait for success. Notebooks and Spark job defin"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/order-and-layers.html#lakehouse-behaviour","t":"Deployment order and layers","s":"What a lakehouse deployment does","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Aspect Behaviour What is deployed Metadata: name, description, shortcuts and, optionally, data access roles. Tables, views and files are never deployed or overwritten. First deployment A new, empty lakehouse with the same name is created in the target workspace. Notebooks and Spark job definitions are remapped to reference it. The SQL analytics endpoint and the default semantic model are provisioned with it. Internal"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/order-and-layers.html#environments","t":"Deployment order and layers","s":"Environments and promotion lanes","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Provision Create every layer workspace for DEV, TST and PRD, assign capacity and roles, and assign each to its pipeline stage. Run the full ordered deployment into TST Hydrate and verify after each layer, then run the end-to-end smoke and data quality checks. Complete acceptance Do user acceptance on TST outputs, with the TST semantic models and reports. Repeat the same sequence for PRD Use the same order and the PRD"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/order-and-layers.html#breaks","t":"Deployment order and layers","s":"What breaks when the order is broken","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Mistake What you see Why Report deployed before its semantic model The report deployment fails, or the report stays bound to another stage's model if the connection was deliberately pinned The paired model is missing in the target stage Notebooks deployed before the lakehouse or environment Notebook attached to a lakehouse in another stage, or a failed deployment Autobinding finds no paired lakehouse or environment i"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/order-and-layers.html#refs","t":"Deployment order and layers","s":"References","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Lakehouse Git integration and deployment pipelines Notebook source control and deployment Variable library CI/CD Deploy content using deployment pipelines Best practices for lifecycle management in Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Supported items and deployment rules","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Playbook 07 · Deployment Pipelines Supported items and deployment rules Support for lifecycle management is uneven across item types, and deployment rules cover only a handful of them. This page gives the support matrix, explains how rules behave, and walks the two scenarios every layered platform hits: notebooks that read another layer's lakehouse, and semantic models that must read the target stage's data."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#supported-items","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Supported items and lifecycle features","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"The matrix lists the items a data platform typically promotes. Git integration and deployment pipelines have separate support lists, and several Power BI items are still in preview in both. Rules exist for five item types only. Item Git integration Deployment pipelines Deployment rule Notes Data Engineering Lakehouse Yes Yes No Metadata and shortcuts only; tables and files are never versioned or overwritten. Environm"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#rules","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Deployment rules","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"A rule overrides one property of one item when it is deployed into a stage. Without rules, a deployed item keeps whatever the source stage had, including connections to DEV. How a deployment rule works. The rule belongs to the target stage and to one item, and only runs during a deployment into that stage. Until you deploy, compare keeps showing the item as different because the rule has not been applied."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#rule-types","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Rule types and the items that support them","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Item Data source rule Parameter rule Default lakehouse rule Dataflow Gen1 Yes Yes No Semantic model Yes Yes No Paginated report Yes No No Mirrored database Yes No No Notebook No No Yes Data source rule. Replaces a data source's details (for example server and database) with another of the same type. You cannot change the type, and one data source cannot appear in more than one rule. The list of data sources is taken "},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#rule-behaviour","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"How rules behave","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Create rules on the target stage, never on the first stage, and only as the owner of the item. The rule pane is greyed out for anyone else. Rules take effect only at the next deployment into that stage. Until then the item shows as Different in compare, and compare already reflects the rule. A rule is valid only while its parameter or data source still exists in the source item. Renaming or removing it makes the depl"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#prepare","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Prepare the target-stage values","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Collect the values for each target stage before you open the rule pane. The appendix contains a notebook that lists them for every workspace and a template to record them. Rule Values to collect Where to read them Notebook default lakehouse Lakehouse ID, lakehouse name, workspace ID of the target lakehouse The item URL https://app.fabric.microsoft.com/groups/<workspace-id>/lakehouses/<lakehouse-id> , or the Items RES"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#scenario-notebook","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Scenario 1: a notebook's default lakehouse","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"A Gold notebook NB_SALES_GLD_Load in DEV_SALES_GLD uses LH_SALES_SLV_Core in DEV_SALES_SLV as its default lakehouse. After deployment to TST_SALES_GLD it must use LH_SALES_SLV_Core in TST_SALES_SLV . A default lakehouse in the notebook's own workspace is autobound; this one is in another layer's workspace, so you need a rule. Scenario 1: a notebook's default lakehouse across stages. The default lakehouse here lives i"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#scenario-model","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Scenario 2: re-pointing a semantic model","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"A semantic model SM_SALES_GLD in DEV reads WH_SALES_CNSM in DEV_SALES_CNSM_WH . In TST it must read the TST warehouse, and in PRD the PRD warehouse. What you can do depends on how the model reads its data."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#direct-lake","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Direct Lake models","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"A Direct Lake model does not autobind: after deployment it stays bound to the lakehouse or warehouse of the source stage. The choice of rule depends on the Direct Lake variant. Which rule can re-point this semantic model?. Check the storage mode of the tables first. The rule applies to the connection the model uses, and Direct Lake on OneLake has no SQL connection for the rule to edit. Model reads Rule support What t"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#model-steps","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Data source rule for a SQL-based model","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Open the TST stage and select Deployment rules Select the semantic model (you must own it) and expand Data source rules . Add a rule for the data source The list shows the data sources of the model as they exist in the source stage, as a server and a database. Pick the one to change. Choose Other and enter the TST values Server is the SQL connection string of the TST warehouse or lakehouse endpoint. Database must be "},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#onelake-options","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Direct Lake on OneLake","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Option How it works Trade-off Parameterized expression with parameter rules The connection expression is built from Text parameters; a parameter rule per stage sets them at deploy time. Needs the model authored this way, and a parameter rule per stage. Rebind after deployment A post-deployment step re-points the model at the target lakehouse, for example with semantic link labs, TMDL or an XMLA script. One more step "},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#which-mechanism","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"Rule, variable library or parameter?","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Need Use Reason Notebook default lakehouse in another workspace Deployment rule Purpose-built; takes precedence over autobinding Semantic model source (SQL-based or Direct Lake on SQL) Data source rule Supported and applied by the pipeline Direct Lake on OneLake Parameter rule, or post-deploy rebind No data source rule exists Data pipeline connections and item references Variable library No rule type exists for pipel"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#rule-problems","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"When rules do not behave","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Symptom Cause Fix Rule options are greyed out You are not the owner of the item, or the item has no data source or parameter the rule could target Take over the item (ownership) or ask the owner; check that the model really has the data source I configured a rule but nothing changed Rules apply only when the item is deployed into the stage Deploy the item; afterwards it should no longer show as different A previously"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/deployment-rules.html#refs","t":"Supported items and deployment rules","s":"References","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Create deployment rules Overview of deployment pipelines (supported items) Overview of Git integration (supported items) Notebook source control and deployment Direct Lake overview Troubleshoot the Fabric lifecycle management tools"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"Automation, limits and permissions","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Playbook 07 · Deployment Pipelines Automation, limits and permissions Once the manual deployment is routine, script the same gates. This page shows the deploy API and its long-running behaviour, what to do for items the pipeline cannot configure, the limits worth planning around, and who needs which role."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#automate","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"Automating a deployment","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"The deployment pipeline is a Fabric feature, but it has a public REST API, so a CI system can call it. This keeps what the pipeline is good at (compare, rules, autobinding, history) and adds what CI is good at (approvals, tests, scheduling). The sequence is the same as the manual one: deploy, wait, verify, hydrate. A scripted deployment is a long-running operation. The POST returns immediately; the deployment runs on"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#deploy-api","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"The deploy call","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"The deploy operation is a POST to /v1/deploymentPipelines/{deploymentPipelineId}/deploy . It returns 202 Accepted with a Location header for the operation, an x-ms-operation-id and a Retry-After interval, because the deployment runs as a long-running operation. Body field Required Meaning sourceStageId , targetStageId Yes Stage IDs of two consecutive stages. Deploying to a previous stage works only into an empty stag"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#identity","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"Identity for automation","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Required roles. The caller needs the pipeline admin role and at least Contributor on both the source and the target workspace, exactly as for a person. Service principals are supported only when every item involved in the operation supports service principals. A single unsupported item makes the call fail, so test your real item mix, and keep a user-based fallback. Tenant settings. Allow service principals to call th"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#ci-gates","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"What to add around the call","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Gate before TST and PRD Use the approval feature of your CI system on the environment that runs the deploy step. Require the TST deployment note to be referenced in the PRD run. Verify after the deploy Run smoke checks and data quality tests as separate steps that fail the run. They are the automated form of the verification in the Deployment checklist . Hydrate in order Trigger the load pipelines through the Fabric "},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#code-first","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"When the pipeline is the wrong tool","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"A code-first release keeps definitions in Git and publishes them to each workspace with the Fabric item APIs. The open-source fabric-cicd library wraps that: you describe the workspace, which item types are in scope and which environment is targeted, and a parameter file holds per-environment replacements. You get You give up or must build Git as the only source of truth, with gated, testable releases The pipeline co"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#limits","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"Limitations and workarounds","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Limitation Effect Workaround Data pipelines have no deployment rules A promoted pipeline keeps the connections and item references of the source stage Use a variable library (next section). Do not hard-code connections. Dataflow Gen2: no autobinding, destinations are absolute references A promoted dataflow reads and writes the DEV items Use relative references for sources where possible and parameterize destinations;"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#variable-libraries","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"Per-stage values for data pipelines: variable libraries","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Older guidance makes pipelines environment-aware by switching on the current workspace ID and setting variables in each case. A variable library replaces that approach: it is a Fabric item with named variables and several value sets , exactly one of which is active per workspace. Pipelines read values through library variables , and the same library can supply connection and item references, shortcut targets and note"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#permissions","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"Permissions and roles","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Pipeline permission and workspace permission are granted separately. A pipeline has a single permission, Admin ; everything else comes from workspace roles. Action Requirement Create a pipeline A license that allows it (see Learn); pipeline creation can be restricted to a platform group Assign a workspace to a stage Pipeline admin, workspace admin of the workspace, and at least Contributor on the workspaces in adjace"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#role-design","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"A role design that works","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Developers are Contributor or Member on feature workspaces and DEV, and have no write role on TST or PRD. Release managers hold the pipeline admin role and Contributor on every stage workspace they deploy between. Use a security group so membership is reviewable. Automation identity is pipeline admin and Contributor on the stage workspaces, owner of the items whose rules it must manage, and owner of the connections t"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/automation-and-limits.html#refs","t":"Automation, limits and permissions","s":"References","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Deployment Pipelines - Deploy Stage Content (REST) Long running operations (REST) Automate your deployment pipeline using APIs and DevOps The deployment pipelines process (permissions, limitations) What is a variable library? Use a variable library in a data pipeline (tutorial) fabric-cicd and Azure DevOps tutorial"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/appendix.html","t":"Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values","s":"Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Playbook 07 · Deployment Pipelines Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values Deployment rules need workspace IDs, item IDs and SQL connection strings for every target stage. Collecting them by hand from URLs invites typos. This appendix collects them programmatically into a table and gives a template to record what you configured."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/appendix.html#inventory-notebook","t":"Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values","s":"Inventory notebook","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Run this PySpark notebook from a lakehouse you can write to. It lists the workspaces whose names match a pattern, reads the lakehouses, warehouses, semantic models and notebooks in them, adds the SQL endpoint details for lakehouses and warehouses, and writes the result to Delta tables with a prefix. The running identity needs at least Viewer on each workspace it reads. It sees only workspaces it has access to. The no"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/appendix.html#rule-queries","t":"Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values","s":"Queries for the rule values","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Run these in a notebook cell with the SQL magic, in the lakehouse that holds fabric_inventory . Replace the workspace names with the target stage workspaces. Dataflow Gen2 creates staging lakehouses and warehouses that you must not use for rules, hence the exclusion."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/appendix.html#q-notebook","t":"Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values","s":"Notebook default lakehouse rule","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/appendix.html#q-model-warehouse","t":"Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values","s":"Semantic model on a warehouse","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Use the database value in the same form the source stage shows in the rule's From field: the warehouse name, or its ID."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/appendix.html#q-model-lakehouse","t":"Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values","s":"Semantic model on a lakehouse SQL endpoint (Direct Lake on SQL)","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Verify against Microsoft Learn Data source rules are supported for Direct Lake on SQL endpoints and not for Direct Lake on OneLake; see Direct Lake models . Confirm the property names returned by Get Lakehouse and Get Warehouse in the Lakehouse REST reference and Warehouse REST reference if the notebook returns empty values."},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/appendix.html#template","t":"Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values","s":"Rule settings template","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Record what you configured for each target stage, so a lost rule (after an unassignment or a deleted item) can be recreated in minutes. Keep one copy per pipeline, with real values stored outside the repository. Rule Setting TST value PRD value Notebook default lakehouse Lakehouse workspace ID <tst-workspace-id> <prd-workspace-id> Lakehouse ID <tst-lakehouse-id> <prd-lakehouse-id> Lakehouse name LH_SALES_SLV_Core LH_"},{"u":"playbooks/07-deployment/appendix.html#refs","t":"Appendix: collecting IDs and rule values","s":"References","p":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion","x":"Workspaces - List Workspaces (REST) Items - List Items (REST) Lakehouse - Get Lakehouse (REST) Warehouse - Get Warehouse (REST) NotebookUtils for Fabric Create deployment rules"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Playbook 08 · Data Engineering Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy A decision guide for the physical layout of Delta tables in Microsoft Fabric: whether to partition at all, what to use instead when the goal is faster reads, how Direct Lake and the SQL analytics endpoint react to your choice, and how to operate the result so it stays healthy. Audience data engineers, architects Level intermediate Reading time 45 min Pag"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#purpose","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"Purpose and scope","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Partitioning is one of the oldest data-layout tools and one of the easiest to misuse. Done well, it lets an engine skip whole directories, keeps concurrent writers from colliding and limits how much data a rewrite disturbs. Done badly, it multiplies small files, slows every write, burdens the SQL analytics endpoint and forces Direct Lake to juggle thousands of tiny row groups. This playbook answers three questions: w"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#when-to-use","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"When to use this playbook","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Use it when you are Creating a large Delta table and deciding on its layout Seeing slow queries, a growing file count or slow SQL endpoint sync Planning concurrent pipelines that write to one table Preparing Gold tables for Direct Lake Reviewing an existing partitioned table that no longer fits its workload Look elsewhere when you need Load patterns such as MERGE and SCD2, covered in the data load strategy playbook W"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#audience","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"Audience and prerequisites","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Primary readers Data engineers who create and maintain lakehouse tables, architects who set layout standards, and platform owners who watch capacity and file health. You should know What a Delta table, a Parquet file and the Delta transaction log are, and basic Spark SQL or PySpark. Environment A Fabric lakehouse and Spark notebooks. Examples target Fabric Runtime 2.0, and each page notes where Runtime 1.3 behaves di"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#decision-tree","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"The decision in one picture","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Walk the questions from the top. The first question answered yes selects the layout. If none applies, the answer is the default: no partitioning. Partition for a reason, not by habit. Partitioning and liquid clustering cannot be combined on one table. If both the first and third questions apply, partition for the writers and use Z-Order inside the partitions for the filters."},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#principles","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"Six principles behind the guidance","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Start unpartitioned A well-sized file set with statistics already gives engines something to skip. Partitioning adds directories, and every directory is a place where small files can accumulate. Add it when a requirement names it. Partition columns are low-cardinality and aligned with how data is written Tens to hundreds of distinct values, each holding at least about 1 GB. A month, a region or a business unit qualif"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#defaults","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"Defaults at a glance","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Topic Default guidance Depart from it when Partitioning None Concurrent writers need isolation, or a large Direct Lake table gets rolling-window rewrites Read optimization Liquid clustering on 1 to 4 frequently filtered columns, Runtime 2.0 or later You are on Runtime 1.3 with a small table (clustering rewrites more), or the table is already partitioned (use Z-Order) File size Adaptive target file size, which selects"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#outcomes","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"What you will be able to do","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Decide with evidence whether a table should be partitioned, clustered or left alone. Estimate partition size and count before you commit to a granularity. Combine V-Order, optimize write, auto compaction and OPTIMIZE without conflicting settings. Choose a layout that fits Direct Lake, the SQL analytics endpoint and Warehouse consumers. Implement partitioned and clustered tables, selective overwrites and concurrent me"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#map","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"Map of this playbook","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Page What it answers Why and when to partition How pruning works, how to size partitions, and when concurrent writers justify the cost. Fabric layout features What V-Order, optimize write, auto compaction, OPTIMIZE , Z-Order and liquid clustering each do, and which combine. Patterns by layer and consumer What layout fits Bronze, Silver and Gold, and what Direct Lake, the SQL endpoint and Warehouse need. Implementatio"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#related","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"How this fits with the other playbooks","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Data Load Strategy decides how data arrives; this playbook decides how it is laid out once written, and why replaceWhere and slice replacement behave the way they do. Medallion Architecture defines the Bronze, Silver and Gold layers whose layouts are recommended here. See the glossary for terms such as V-Order, liquid clustering and framing. Key takeaways The default is no partitioning, with adaptive file sizes and a"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/index.html#refs","t":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","s":"References","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Partitioning for Delta tables Apply liquid clustering on Delta tables Cross-workload table maintenance and optimization Understand Direct Lake query performance SQL analytics endpoint performance considerations"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/concepts.html","t":"Why and when to partition","s":"Why and when to partition","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Playbook 08 · Delta Partitioning Why and when to partition Partitioning trades flexibility for the ability to skip, isolate and bound work. This page explains what you gain, what you pay, how to check the arithmetic before you commit, and why concurrent writers are now the main reason to partition at all."},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/concepts.html#pruning","t":"Why and when to partition","s":"What partitioning does","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Hive-style partitioning divides a Delta table into physical subdirectories by the values of one or more partition columns. Each distinct value (or combination of values) gets its own directory, such as order_month=2026-03/ . When a query filters on the partition column, the engine reads only the matching directories. This is partition pruning , and it can cut both latency and cost. Pruning in one picture. Partitionin"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/concepts.html#benefits","t":"Why and when to partition","s":"What you gain, and what you pay","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"What partitioning buys Pruning for queries that filter on the partition column Concurrent-writer isolation: operations on different partitions touch different files and do not conflict Bounded rewrites: an update, delete or OPTIMIZE limited to one partition leaves the rest untouched Cheap retention: a DELETE that matches whole partitions removes files without rewriting data What partitioning costs At least one file p"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/concepts.html#sizing","t":"Why and when to partition","s":"Size partitions before you create them","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Two rules of thumb from the Fabric documentation keep partitioned tables healthy: the partition column should have low to moderate cardinality (tens to hundreds of distinct values, not thousands), and each partition should hold at least about 1 GB of data. Larger tables can accommodate more partitions. Older Delta guidance often adds a total-size floor of roughly 1 TB below which partitioning is not worth it; treat t"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/concepts.html#writers","t":"Why and when to partition","s":"Concurrent-writer isolation","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Delta Lake uses optimistic concurrency control. Two operations that rewrite the same files conflict, and one of them fails or must retry. Concurrent appends generally do not conflict, but updates, deletes and merges can, even when they logically modify different rows, because the engine works at file granularity. Partitioning makes it possible for concurrent operations to touch disjoint file sets. Two MERGE operation"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/concepts.html#column","t":"Why and when to partition","s":"Choosing the partition column","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"The column is filtered on, or written by one writer at a time, in the large majority of operations. Its cardinality is low to moderate: tens to hundreds of values, with each value holding at least about 1 GB. Its values are stable. A column whose value changes after the row is written forces rows to move between partitions. Its values are known at write time, since the partition is chosen as the file is written. It d"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/concepts.html#pitfalls","t":"Why and when to partition","s":"Common pitfalls","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Pitfall Symptom Remedy High-cardinality partition column Thousands of directories, tiny files, slow writes and slow SQL endpoint sync Coarser grain, or liquid clustering for lookups Too many partition levels Cardinality multiplies (12 months by 50 regions is 600 partitions) One level, and Z-Order or clustering for the second dimension Frequent small writes into many partitions File count grows with every micro-batch "},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/concepts.html#refs","t":"Why and when to partition","s":"References","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Partitioning for Delta tables Concurrency control and conflict resolution Understand Direct Lake query performance SQL analytics endpoint performance considerations Delta Lake concurrency control"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Fabric layout features","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Playbook 08 · Delta Partitioning Fabric layout features Partitioning is only one of several layout controls. Fabric adds write-time features, automatic compaction, adaptive file sizing and liquid clustering, and most of the time they do more for you than partition columns can. This page explains what each one does, which combine and what changes with the runtime."},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#overview","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Three moments, three kinds of feature","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Layout features act at different times. Knowing which moment a feature acts at tells you where its cost lands and what it cannot fix. Cheapest first. Prevent small files at write time, let auto compaction repair them right after the commit, and keep scheduled jobs for the work that needs a window: clustering, Z-Order, purging and cleanup."},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#v-order","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"V-Order and resource profiles","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"V-Order is a write-time optimization for Parquet files. It reorganizes row-group distribution, encoding and compression to improve read efficiency across Fabric engines, and it pays off most in read-heavy patterns such as dashboards and repeated scans. Files remain open-source Parquet, and Delta features such as Z-Order stay compatible. The usual trade-off is slower writes, often around 15 percent on average. V-Order"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#optimize-write","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Optimize write","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Optimize write does pre-write compaction : it shuffles in-memory data into optimally sized bins before Spark writes the Parquet files, so the write produces fewer, larger files without a cleanup step afterwards. The shuffle costs time, so use it selectively. It is most valuable for: Partitioned tables, where each write otherwise creates at least one file per partition touched Tables with frequent small inserts, inclu"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#file-size","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Adaptive target file size","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Fabric can choose the target file size per table, so you do not have to tune it by hand. Adaptive target file size estimates the ideal size from table heuristics such as total size and updates it as the table grows: tables under about 10 GB target 128 MB files, scaling linearly up to 1 GB for tables beyond about 10 TB. Small tables thereby keep enough files to benefit from file skipping, and large tables keep files b"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#compaction","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Auto compaction and OPTIMIZE","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Auto compaction evaluates partition health after each write. If it finds excessive fragmentation (too many small files) it runs a synchronous OPTIMIZE immediately after the commit. Because it only runs when needed, it is the recommended default maintenance strategy for Spark-written tables and removes the need for a separate table-health check before maintenance jobs. Scenario Use Spark-written table, normal batch pa"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#vacuum","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"VACUUM and deletion vectors","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"VACUUM removes files that the Delta log no longer references once they are older than the retention period (seven days by default). It reclaims storage but does not improve the active file layout. Do not shorten the retention period without checking time-travel needs and concurrent readers, and, for Direct Lake, remember that a framed semantic model references a specific table version that must still exist until the "},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#z-order","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Z-Order","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Z-Order colocates related rows in the same files by applying a space-filling curve across one or more columns, tightening the file-level min and max ranges so selective filters skip more files. It is applied as part of OPTIMIZE , and the columns are not stored in table metadata : you specify them on every run. It has no incremental mode, so each run rewrites all eligible files unless you add a WHERE predicate. With p"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#liquid-clustering","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Liquid clustering","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Liquid clustering organizes data so rows with similar values in the clustering columns sit in the same files, with tight, rarely overlapping value ranges. Queries that filter on those columns read only the files whose ranges match. Unlike partitioning it creates no directories, handles high-cardinality columns without small-file problems and lets you change the columns later. For most read-performance workloads start"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#lc-runtime","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Runtime matters","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Runtime 1.3: use liquid clustering with caution On Runtime 1.3 every OPTIMIZE rewrites all files in each Z-Cube smaller than 100 GB, even those already well clustered, causing severe write amplification. Do not combine it with auto compaction on that runtime, and run OPTIMIZE deliberately and infrequently. Incremental liquid clustering, which processes only unclustered, small, unhealthy or deletion-vector files and r"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#warehouse-clustering","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Warehouse data clustering Preview","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Warehouse tables have their own, system-managed layout. Data clustering in Fabric Data Warehouse (Preview) groups rows by up to four columns with CLUSTER BY at table creation, applies during ingestion with no later maintenance, and cannot be added to or changed on an existing table. It suits large tables with repeated selective predicates on mid-to-high cardinality columns. It is a Warehouse feature and does not appl"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#matrix","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"What combines with what","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Feature With partitioning With liquid clustering Notes Partitioning Not applicable No Choose one scheme per table Z-Order Yes No On a partitioned table it is applied within each partition independently V-Order Yes Yes V-Order and clustering work on different axes: file-internal layout versus cross-file ranges Optimize write Yes Yes Especially useful for partitioned writes; for clustered tables it produces consolidate"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#runtime-diffs","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"Runtime 1.3 versus Runtime 2.0","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Capability Runtime 1.3 Runtime 2.0 Adaptive target file size Opt-in On by default File-level compaction targets Opt-in On by default Deletion vectors Opt-in On by default Liquid clustering Available, full-rewrite strategy; use with caution Incremental strategy by default; auto compaction compatible OPTIMIZE FULL , clusteringQuality() Not available Available Auto compaction onCheckpointOnly mode Not available Availabl"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/fabric-features.html#refs","t":"Fabric layout features","s":"References","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Optimize Delta Lake tables with V-Order Configure resource profile configurations Tune the size of Delta table data files Compacting Delta tables Apply liquid clustering on Delta tables Z-Order for Delta tables Delta Lake table VACUUM Data clustering in Fabric Data Warehouse"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"Patterns by layer and consumer","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Playbook 08 · Delta Partitioning Patterns by layer and consumer Bronze, Silver and Gold describe the purpose of data, not the layout it needs. This page turns the principles into layout recommendations for each layer, explains what Direct Lake, the SQL analytics endpoint and Warehouse each ask of a table, and works through neutral examples."},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#layers","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"Layout by medallion layer","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"The medallion layers do not determine whether a layout is user-managed or system-managed, and they do not require a separate copy of a table for each consumer. Use one shared table where its requirements are compatible, and let the layer tell you the priority: write throughput in Bronze, reuse in Silver, read performance in Gold. One rule per layer. The more a table is read by BI tools, the less partitioning you want"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#bronze","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"Bronze: landing and raw history","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Default to no partitioning. Bronze is append-heavy, so the priority is write throughput. Optimize write and auto compaction keep the file count in check. Ingest-month partitions (for example ingest_month ) are a good choice when retention is by month and old months are dropped with a partition-aligned DELETE , or when several source-specific pipelines write to one table. They are simple and robust, and they accept la"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#silver","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"Silver: conformed, reusable data","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Partition by the business time that drives most filters , but only when each partition is large enough: order_month for orders, posting_month for ledger entries, event_date for very high-volume telemetry. Otherwise cluster. If the table is smaller than the partition arithmetic allows, or the dominant filter is a high-cardinality column, use liquid clustering on one to four filter columns instead (Runtime 2.0 and late"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#gold","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"Gold: serving","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Keep partitioning minimal , often none and at most a month. Small aggregate tables should be unpartitioned. Favor file layout over more partitions: V-Order on tables that Direct Lake reads, regular OPTIMIZE , and liquid clustering or Z-Order on the hot predicates (for example region or product category) for the Spark and SQL consumers. Update by append or by replacing a slice , so Direct Lake can reuse what it has al"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#consumers","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"What each consumer needs","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Consumer What it wants from the layout Implication for partitioning Spark Right-sized files, statistics for skipping, no small-file overhead Adaptive file size and compaction first; clustering for selective filters SQL analytics endpoint A manageable file and partition count so metadata sync stays fast; the same layout as Spark Avoid high-cardinality partitions; do not enable V-Order or set static file sizes just for"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#direct-lake","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"Direct Lake","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Direct Lake loads whole columns from Delta tables into memory on demand, and the physical layout decides how fast that is and how much has to be reloaded after an update. Row groups become column segments in the semantic model, so many tiny row groups mean many tiny segments and many Parquet dictionaries to merge. Direct Lake does not use Delta or Parquet statistics to skip files or row groups while loading, so layou"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#sql-endpoint","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"SQL analytics endpoint","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Every lakehouse has a SQL analytics endpoint that a background process keeps in sync with the Delta tables. Under normal conditions the lag is under a minute, but it grows with the number of files and partitions the process has to scan. Parquet files are immutable, so updates and deletes add files over time, and without maintenance that adds read overhead and slows the sync. Avoid high-cardinality partition columns. "},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#warehouse","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"Warehouse","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Fabric Data Warehouse manages its own physical layout and compaction. You do not choose Hive-style partitions for Warehouse tables. Keep the default V-Order setting for read-oriented and mixed workloads, and consider data clustering (Preview) for large tables with recurring selective predicates. When a Warehouse reads Lakehouse tables through the SQL analytics endpoint or shortcuts, the lakehouse layout guidance on t"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#examples","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"Worked examples","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"The examples below use neutral retail, finance and telemetry data and assumed volumes. They show how the arithmetic and the decision tree turn into choices; replace the assumed sizes with yours. Table Assumed size and pattern Recommended layout Why orders fact (Silver) 600 GB over five years, merged daily; analysts filter by date and region Liquid clustering on (order_date, region) . If regional pipelines merge concu"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/patterns.html#refs","t":"Patterns by layer and consumer","s":"References","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Cross-workload table maintenance and optimization Understand Direct Lake query performance Direct Lake overview SQL analytics endpoint performance considerations Performance guidelines in Fabric Data Warehouse Data clustering in Fabric Data Warehouse"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/implementation.html","t":"Implementation examples","s":"Implementation examples","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Playbook 08 · Delta Partitioning Implementation examples The code behind the decisions: creating partitioned and clustered tables, appending with sensible settings, replacing one slice atomically, running concurrent merges that do not collide, and changing a layout without taking the table offline. The examples use a neutral orders domain: silver.orders partitioned by order_month , a gold.sales table for clustering a"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/implementation.html#create-tables","t":"Implementation examples","s":"Create tables","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/implementation.html#create-partitioned","t":"Implementation examples","s":"A partitioned table","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Declare the partition column as part of the schema. For a monthly grain, add a derived order_month column rather than partitioning by the raw date, and enable the write-time features that keep partitioned writes healthy."},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/implementation.html#create-clustered","t":"Implementation examples","s":"A liquid clustered table","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"When the goal is faster selective reads rather than writer isolation, cluster instead of partitioning. Clustering is applied by OPTIMIZE , so schedule it or rely on auto compaction on Runtime 2.0. Pick one scheme per table A table cannot be both partitioned and liquid clustered. Combining PARTITIONED BY with CLUSTER BY is not supported, and Z-Order is also incompatible with clustering. On Runtime 1.3, do not pair clu"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/implementation.html#append","t":"Implementation examples","s":"Append with the right settings","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Appends are the friendliest write pattern: they add files and leave existing ones alone. Derive the partition column in the pipeline so every row lands in the right directory, and set the session options that suit the table. When appending to an existing partitioned table, partitionBy must match the table or be omitted; a mismatch fails. Check for a null or malformed partition value before the write. A single bad dat"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/implementation.html#selective-overwrite","t":"Implementation examples","s":"Replace one slice atomically","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"To rebuild one month, use replaceWhere . It replaces only the rows matching the predicate, in a single atomic commit, and leaves every other file untouched, which is exactly what keeps Direct Lake's loaded data valid for the other months. Slice replacement in the Delta log. Only the files matching the predicate change. By default the DataFrame must contain only rows that satisfy the predicate, otherwise the write is "},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/implementation.html#concurrent-merge","t":"Implementation examples","s":"Run merges concurrently without conflicts","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Two merges into one table can proceed side by side when each targets a different partition and says so in the condition. Both the target and the source predicate belong in the ON clause, as literals or parameters, not only in the source data. Without the partition column in the condition Delta cannot prove the file sets are disjoint, and the second commit can fail even though the rows differ. Partitioning removes con"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/implementation.html#change-layout","t":"Implementation examples","s":"Change a layout with a rewrite","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Delta does not support changing partition columns in place. Moving from daily to monthly partitions, from one partition column to another, or between partitioning and liquid clustering is a rewrite. Treat it as a planned operation with validation, not an overwrite you run and hope for. Never overwrite the live table in place. Writing to a new name keeps the old table available for rollback, and lets you validate befo"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/implementation.html#refs","t":"Implementation examples","s":"References","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Partitioning for Delta tables Concurrency control and conflict resolution Apply liquid clustering on Delta tables Optimize Delta Lake tables with V-Order Delta Lake: selective overwrite with replaceWhere"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/operations.html","t":"Validation and operations","s":"Validation and operations","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Playbook 08 · Delta Partitioning Validation and operations A layout is only as good as its maintenance. This page shows how to measure file and partition health, confirm that pruning works, diagnose the common problems, and run maintenance on a cadence that matches each class of table."},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/operations.html#measure","t":"Validation and operations","s":"Measure layout health","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Start with the table's own metadata. DESCRIBE DETAIL reports the file count, total size, partition columns, clustering columns and table properties, including the evaluated delta.targetFileSize.adaptive target. DESCRIBE HISTORY shows every commit with its operation metrics, which is how you see what a write, merge or OPTIMIZE actually did. An average can hide skew between partitions or between recent and previously c"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/operations.html#pruning-check","t":"Validation and operations","s":"Confirm that pruning and skipping work","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Layout is only useful if queries benefit from it. Read the physical plan and look for the partition filters on the scan, then compare the files the scan read with the table's total. Liquid clustered tables. From Runtime 2.0 the Scala clusteringQuality() method reports per-column overlap and estimated skipping effectiveness. Compare before and after OPTIMIZE , and validate against files scanned in the Spark UI. Direct"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/operations.html#signals","t":"Validation and operations","s":"Health signals and what to do","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Treat these as indicators to investigate, not universal thresholds: validate them against the table's write pattern and its consumers' performance. Signal Likely cause Response File count rises faster than table size; average file size well below the adaptive target Frequent small writes, many partitions touched per batch, no compaction Run a one-time OPTIMIZE for the backlog, then enable optimize write and auto comp"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/operations.html#triage","t":"Validation and operations","s":"Triage for small files","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Treat the cause, not the symptom. Compaction repairs small files that a write pattern keeps creating; only a coarser layout stops partitions from being too small in the first place."},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/operations.html#sync","t":"Validation and operations","s":"Keep the SQL analytics endpoint in step","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"The SQL analytics endpoint reads the same Delta tables as Spark, kept in step by a background metadata sync. Normally the lag is under a minute, but spikes in file or partition counts lengthen it. Watch sync time alongside file counts after large loads, schedule regular maintenance rather than relying on the sync to cope, and use the on-demand metadata sync or the refresh SQL endpoint metadata REST API when a downstr"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/operations.html#cadence","t":"Validation and operations","s":"Operations cadence","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Match maintenance to how the table is written and read. The cadence below is a starting point: automatic compaction does the continuous work, and scheduled jobs handle what needs a window. Automatic first, scheduled second, reviewed on a rhythm. A quarterly review is where you decide whether a table has outgrown its layout. Table class Compaction Layout VACUUM and refresh Bronze, append-only, high volume Auto compact"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/operations.html#checklist","t":"Validation and operations","s":"Layout checklist","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"The decision tree was walked and the answer recorded next to the table: unpartitioned, partitioned (with the reason) or clustered. If partitioned: the partition column is low-cardinality, stable, known at write time, and each partition is at least about 1 GB. Partition values contain no sensitive identifiers, and null or malformed values are rejected before the write. Optimize write and auto compaction are on for Spa"},{"u":"playbooks/08-delta-partitioning/operations.html#refs","t":"Validation and operations","s":"References","p":"Delta Lake Partitioning Strategy","x":"Cross-workload table maintenance and optimization Compacting Delta tables Tune the size of Delta table data files SQL analytics endpoint performance considerations Understand Direct Lake query performance Lakehouse table maintenance Delta Lake table VACUUM Delta Lake table format interoperability"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Playbook 09 · Variable Libraries Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items A variable library is a Fabric item that holds the values that change between DEV, TST and PRD (workspace and item IDs, connections, batch sizes, flags) so that the pipelines, notebooks, dataflows and shortcuts that use them can be promoted unchanged. This playbook covers how it works, how to build and consume one, how "},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#problem","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"The problem: stage-specific values buried in items","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Every promoted solution carries a handful of values that must differ by stage: the lakehouse a copy activity reads, the connection it uses, the workspace an item points at, how many days of data a test run loads. When those values live inside the items, every promotion needs a human or a script to fix them, and every fix is a chance to ship the DEV value into PRD. The usual workarounds each break down in a different "},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#what-it-is","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"What a variable library is","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"A variable library is an item in a workspace that contains a list of variables and, for each variable, a value for every stage in the release path. Other items in the same workspace consume the variables. Each consumer resolves the value in its own context, when it runs, so the library does not have to be deployed in lockstep with its consumers. The design has four parts, covered in depth on the next page : Variables"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#not","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"What it is not","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Not a secret store. Anyone who can view the library can read its values (a workspace Viewer can view the item). Keep credentials in connections or a key vault and store only a reference. Not writable at run time. Notebooks can read variables but not change them. It is configuration, not shared state. Not cross-workspace. Consumers use libraries in their own workspace. A notebook cannot read a library from another wor"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#when","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"When to use a variable library","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Use it when Use something else when The same item must point to a different lakehouse, workspace or connection per stage The value is a credential or token: use a connection or a key vault reference A value changes with the stage but not with the run (batch size, lookback window, feature flag) The value changes with every run: use a pipeline or notebook parameter Several items in a workspace share one value and you w"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#prerequisites","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"Prerequisites","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"A workspace on a Fabric capacity per stage (the examples use DEV_SALES_SLV , TST_SALES_SLV and PRD_SALES_SLV ). The tenant setting Users can create Fabric items enabled for the people who build libraries. A workspace role of Contributor or higher to create and edit a library; Viewer is read-only. Items that support variable libraries (pipelines, notebooks, Dataflow Gen2 with CI/CD, lakehouse shortcuts, copy jobs and "},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#outcomes","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"What you will have at the end","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"A variable library per domain and layer with a value set per stage, built to a naming convention. Pipelines, notebooks and shortcuts reading their stage values from it, with no hard-coded IDs. A repeatable step that activates the right value set after each first deployment, with a guard that fails fast if a stage runs with the wrong one. A clear rule for when a variable, a deployment rule or autobinding is the right "},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#map","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"Playbook map","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"02 Concepts and anatomy Variable types, default and alternative value sets, why the active set is workspace state, naming, limits and permissions. Concepts 15 min 03 Create and consume variables Create a library and read it from pipelines, notebooks, dataflows, shortcuts, copy jobs and other items. Implementation 20 min 04 Promotion, Git and automation Deployment pipelines, the step after deployment, Git representati"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#facts","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"Key facts at a glance","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Scope A library and its consumers live in the same workspace. One library holds up to 1,000 variables and 1,000 value sets, within 10,000 alternative-value cells and 1 MB in total. Variable types Boolean, Integer, Number, String, DateTime (ISO 8601, UTC), Guid, plus item reference Preview and connection reference Preview . Active value set Exactly one per library per workspace; not part of the item definition; not ov"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#related","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"Related playbooks","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"GitHub Integration and Branching covers the repository and branch model in which the library is stored as a folder. Shared Python Libraries : keep environment values out of the wheel and pass them in from a variable library at call time. Deployment Pipelines and Promotion covers stages, deployment rules and autobinding, and the order in which the library is deployed and activated. Deployment Checklist is the release-"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/index.html#refs","t":"Variable libraries: per-stage configuration without editing items","s":"References","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Fabric application lifecycle management variable library Get started with variable libraries Lifecycle management of the variable library Create deployment rules"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"Concepts and anatomy","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Playbook 09 · Variable Libraries Concepts and anatomy Before you build anything, get four ideas straight: what types a variable can have, how default and alternative value sets resolve to one set of values, why the active value set belongs to the workspace and not to the item, and who is allowed to change what. Most variable-library incidents come from misunderstanding one of these."},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#anatomy","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"Anatomy of a library","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"A variable library is a single item that stores everything needed to configure its consumers in every stage. In its definition it has three kinds of content: the variables (name, type, optional note and the default value), one value set file per alternative set (holding only the values that differ from the default), and a small settings part that records the order in which value sets are listed. A platform file with "},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#types","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"Variable types","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Each variable has a name, a type, a default value and an optional note (up to 2,048 characters). The type decides how the value is validated and which consumers can use it. Type Category Holds Notes Boolean Basic true or false Appears as Bool in pipelines. Integer Basic Whole numbers Appears as Int in pipelines. Number Basic Decimals Not supported in pipelines. String Basic Text The safest type for IDs you hand to co"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#choosing-types","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"Choosing a type for the thing you want to vary","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"You want to vary Prefer Fall back to The lakehouse, notebook or pipeline an item points at Item reference, where the consumer supports it A String holding the item ID (and one for the workspace ID) The external connection an activity uses Connection reference, where the consumer supports it A String holding the connection ID A table name, folder or endpoint host String None needed A lookback window, batch size or ret"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#reference-types","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"Reference types are static pointers","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"An item reference stores raw workspace and item IDs. It does not follow the item when you deploy: after a deployment to a new stage, the reference still points at the original workspace until you change it. The same holds for a connection reference, which never auto-binds on deployment. That is exactly why they belong in a variable library: the value set for each stage holds that stage's target. The values across val"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#value-sets","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"Value sets","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Every library has a default value set . It always exists, holds every variable's baseline value and cannot be renamed. You add alternative value sets for the stages or scenarios you need. An alternative set is stored as a collection of pointers to the default values, with overrides where you changed them, so a set only records the differences. Value set names are unique within a library, follow the variable naming ru"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#default-choice","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"What belongs in the default value set","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Because every workspace starts with the default set active, the content of the default set decides what an unconfigured stage does. There are two defensible conventions: Convention Strength Risk Default holds the DEV values. Alternative sets TST and PRD override what differs. Smallest library; DEV works with no extra step; fewest cells against the 10,000 limit A stage where nobody activated its set silently runs with"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#active-set","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"The active value set is workspace state","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"This is the single most important behavior to understand. The active value set is not stored in the library's definition. It is a workspace-level setting of the item. Consequences: Deployment does not overwrite it. Deploying a library from TST to PRD copies variables and value sets; the PRD workspace keeps whichever set it had active. It does not appear in comparisons. Changing the active set does not make the item \""},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#naming-limits","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"Naming rules and limits","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Item Rules Library name Not empty; no leading or trailing spaces; starts with a letter; letters, numbers, underscores, hyphens and spaces; at most 256 characters Variable name Not empty; no leading or trailing spaces; starts with a letter or underscore; letters, numbers, underscores and hyphens; at most 256 characters; unique within the library Value set name Same rules as a variable name; unique within the library V"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#permissions","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"Permissions and trust","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"A variable library is a Fabric item, so it follows workspace roles or direct sharing: Workspace role What it can do with the library Viewer View the item (and therefore read every value) Contributor View, add, edit and delete Member View, add, edit, delete and reshare Admin View, add, edit, delete and reshare There is no permission at the level of a single variable: permission on a variable equals permission on the i"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#security-practices","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"Governance practices","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Grant write on a library only to people and automation you already trust with the consumers' behavior. Audit the assignments regularly. Have items reference only libraries that are designated as trusted, and enforce that by review of the item definitions in Git. Do not store secrets in variables. Because Viewers can see values, store connection references or key vault references instead. Track changes to libraries th"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#design","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"How to structure your libraries","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Question Recommendation How many libraries per workspace? One per concern: for example one config library per domain and layer ( VL_SALES_SLV_Config ). A single library for everything couples unrelated teams, widens write access and approaches the size limits. What goes in? Values that differ by stage: IDs, connection references, endpoints, sizes, windows and flags, and a stage name for guards. What stays out? Secret"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/concepts.html#refs","t":"Concepts and anatomy","s":"References","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Variable types in variable libraries Value sets in variable libraries Variable library item reference Variable library connection reference Variable library permissions Get started with variable libraries"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"Create and consume variables","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Playbook 09 · Variable Libraries Create and consume variables Create a library in a few minutes, then wire it into the items that need stage-specific values. Each consumer reads variables differently and has its own limits, so this page gives the procedure for each and a matrix of what to design around."},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#create","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"Create a variable library","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Do this in the development workspace, in the same workspace as the items that will consume the library. Libraries are workspace-scoped, so each stage gets its own copy through deployment, not through a shared library. Create the item In the workspace select + New item , scroll to Develop data and select Variable library . (From the Fabric home page you can also select Create and pick Variable library in the Data Fact"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#consumers","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"What can consume a library","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"A consumer resolves variables from the library in its own workspace , using the library's active value set. The table summarizes the supported consumers and the limits that most often decide a design. Consumer How it reads variables Types to expect Limits to design around Data pipeline A Library variables reference on the pipeline, used in dynamic content as @pipeline().libraryVariables.<Name> Boolean, DateTime, Guid"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#pipelines","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"Pipelines","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"A pipeline does not read the library directly in an expression. You first add a library variable reference to the pipeline, then use that reference in dynamic content. There is no need to create pipeline parameters and map them to the library: the reference is the link. Add the reference Open the pipeline and select an empty area of the canvas so that no activity has focus. In the bottom panel open Library variables "},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#connection-scenario","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"The connection scenario","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"The most common use is making a copy activity connect to the stage's own source and destination without editing the pipeline after deployment: Model the connection as a variable. Use a connection reference variable for an external connection, or a String variable holding the lakehouse and workspace IDs for a Fabric destination. Give each stage its value in its value set. Look up a connection's ID in Manage connection"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#notebooks","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"Notebooks","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Notebooks read a library with NotebookUtils at run time or inject values into the session configuration with %%configure . Both read the active value set of the library in the notebook's own workspace."},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#notebookutils","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"With NotebookUtils","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"An item reference resolves to an object from which you extract the two IDs. Use them to build a OneLake path to the item. The library must be in the same workspace as the notebook. Cross-workspace access is not supported, and a child notebook in a reference run cannot read libraries across workspaces. Variables are read-only from notebooks. Change them in the Fabric UI or through the API. Names are case sensitive: us"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#configure","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"With %%configure","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Use %%configure to set the session's default lakehouse from the library, so one notebook can run in every stage without edits. This is the variable-library alternative to a default-lakehouse deployment rule. Define all three variables as String , including the ID values. workspaceId is required when the lakehouse is in a different workspace from the notebook. Item reference variables are not supported through %%confi"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#passing-config","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"Passing values into shared code","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Keep library code (see Shared Python Libraries ) free of workspace names and IDs. Read the values in the notebook and pass them as arguments, so the same wheel works in every stage. Orchestration hubs read their own workspace A notebook resolves variables in the workspace where it runs. A hub workspace that invokes spoke pipelines (see Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration ) does not configure the spokes through its"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#dataflows","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"Dataflow Gen2","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Dataflow Gen2 with CI/CD can reference variables in filter, replace and column-transformation dialogs through the input widget (turn it on under View , Parameters , Always allow ), or directly in M with the variable functions. The identifier must use the $(/**/Library/Variable) format. Store each variable as a separate query that does not need staging; supported dialogs can then use it as a query input. The default i"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#shortcuts","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"Lakehouse shortcuts","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"A shortcut property such as the connection ID or the target location can be bound to a variable, so the same shortcut definition points at test data in TST and production data in PRD. Open the shortcut. In the lakehouse, right-click the shortcut and choose Manage shortcut . Edit the target. Select Edit target and choose the property to parameterize. Assign the variable. Pick a variable from the library. The variable "},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#others","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"Copy jobs and other items","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Copy job. Create connection variables (with each stage's connection ID in its value set), link the copy job's source and destination connections to them, and after deployment activate the matching value set in each workspace. User data functions. Read variables through the Python programming model, and use item or connection references to connect to data sources. Eventstream. Use connection references to rebind conne"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/build-and-consume.html#refs","t":"Create and consume variables","s":"References","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Get started with variable libraries Tutorial: use variable libraries Variable library integration with pipelines NotebookUtils variable library utilities Develop, execute and manage notebooks (%%configure) Use Fabric variable libraries in Dataflow Gen2 Assign variables to shortcuts CI/CD for copy job in Data Factory How to use notebooks (security context of a run)"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"Promotion, Git and automation","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Playbook 09 · Variable Libraries Promotion, Git and automation A variable library is only useful if it travels well. This page explains what a deployment pipeline and Git do and do not carry, the activation step that follows the first deployment into a stage, how variable libraries compare with deployment rules, and how to script activation through the REST API."},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#pipelines","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"Variable libraries in deployment pipelines","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"The variable library is a deployable item type, so a deployment pipeline copies the library, with all its variables and value sets, from one stage to the next like any other item. The behavior that matters: Every value set travels to every stage, but only one is active in a stage. The active set is chosen independently per stage and can be changed at any time. The first arrival defaults. When a library first lands in"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#compare","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"What shows up as a difference","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Change in one stage Registers as \"different from source\" in a comparison? Variable added, deleted or edited Yes Value set added or deleted Yes Variable name changed Yes Order of variables changed Yes Active value set changed No: it is part of the item configuration, not the definition The practical consequence is that a stage whose library was edited in place (a value tweaked in PRD) shows as different from TST, whic"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#after-deployment","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"The step after the first deployment","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"The first time a library is deployed into a stage, activate that stage's value set before anything that reads a variable runs. This is the only step that is not carried by the deployment and the one most often missed. The risky window. Between the deployment and the activation the stage runs on the default value set. Close that window by making activation the next step of the release, before any pipeline or notebook "},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#feature-workspaces","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"Feature workspaces and branch-out","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"A developer's feature workspace created from a branch receives the library with the default set active. Under the convention in which the default set holds the DEV values, that is the right behavior: feature workspaces work against development resources with no extra step, and they cannot accidentally point at TST or PRD."},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#git","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"Variable libraries and Git","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Like other Fabric items, a library is stored in the connected repository as a folder. Item permissions are checked when you commit and when you update from Git, so a user cannot change a library through Git without the permission they would need in the workspace. What Git carries. The definition round-trips between the workspace and the folder; the choice of active value set stays in the workspace. Part Holds variabl"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#json-shapes","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"Example files","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"These examples show the shape of each file with neutral names and placeholder IDs. Treat them as a guide and copy the exact shape from a library you commit from your own workspace. The order list can be empty or partial, cannot contain value set names that do not exist, and value sets missing from it are appended in alphabetical order. Because every value set is its own file, a pull request shows exactly which stage'"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#git-only","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"Git-only flows without deployment pipelines","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"If stages are driven only by Git (a branch or folder per stage, each synced to its workspace), the library definition arrives through the sync and the active value set still has to be set in each workspace, because Git does not carry it. Script that step with the API below and run it after the sync."},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#rules-vs-libraries","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"Variable libraries or deployment rules?","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Deployment rules and variable libraries both give a stage its own values. They differ in where the value lives, which items they cover and when they take effect. Aspect Deployment rules Variable libraries Where configured On the target stage of a deployment pipeline, per item In a library item in each workspace, shared by many items Item types Data source and parameter rules for dataflows Gen1, semantic models, pagin"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#rest","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"REST API and automation","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"The Fabric REST API manages variable libraries as items. These operations cover the lifecycle: Operation Request Notes List libraries in a workspace GET /v1/workspaces/{workspaceId}/variableLibraries Each entry includes properties.activeValueSetName . Pagination uses a continuation token. Set the active value set PATCH /v1/workspaces/{workspaceId}/variableLibraries/{variableLibraryId} with {\"properties\": {\"activeValu"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/cicd-promotion.html#refs","t":"Promotion, Git and automation","s":"References","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Lifecycle management of the variable library Value sets in variable libraries Create deployment rules Notebook source control and deployment pipelines Items - Update Variable Library (REST) Items - List Variable Libraries (REST) Variable library definition (REST) Use GitHub Actions to connect to Azure (OpenID Connect)"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/example-and-operations.html","t":"Worked example and operations","s":"Worked example and operations","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Playbook 09 · Variable Libraries Worked example and operations One end-to-end example for a fictional retailer: a library with a connection, an item reference and stage values; a pipeline and a notebook that consume it; a release that deploys and activates it in TST and PRD; and a guard that fails fast when a stage runs with the wrong value set. The page then covers anti-patterns, troubleshooting and a validation che"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/example-and-operations.html#scenario","t":"Worked example and operations","s":"The scenario","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Contoso's sales domain loads orders from an operational database into a bronze lakehouse. The bronze layer has three workspaces and one deployment pipeline: Stage Workspace Contents DEV DEV_SALES_BRZ LH_SALES_BRZ , PL_SALES_BRZ_LoadOrders , NB_SALES_BRZ_StageGuard , NB_SALES_BRZ_LoadOrders , VL_SALES_BRZ_Config TST TST_SALES_BRZ The same items, deployed from DEV PRD PRD_SALES_BRZ The same items, deployed from TST The"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/example-and-operations.html#build","t":"Worked example and operations","s":"Build it in DEV","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Create the library. In DEV_SALES_BRZ create VL_SALES_BRZ_Config and add the five variables with the DEV values as defaults (see the table above). For the two reference types, pick the DEV database connection and LH_SALES_BRZ in the picker. Save. Add the stage value sets. Add TST and PRD . In each, override only what differs: StageName , OrdersDb , BronzeLakehouse , and LookbackDays for TST. Save. Bind the pipeline. I"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/example-and-operations.html#guard","t":"Worked example and operations","s":"The stage guard","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Nothing in the platform stops a TST or PRD workspace from running with the default (DEV) value set. The guard turns that mistake into a failed first activity instead of a quiet load into the wrong place. It compares the stage the workspace's name declares (by the <ENV>_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER> naming convention) with the StageName the active value set reports. The guard depends on a disciplined naming convention. If your wor"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/example-and-operations.html#release","t":"Worked example and operations","s":"Release to TST and PRD","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Deploy In DPLY_SALES_BRZ compare DEV with TST and deploy the library together with the pipeline and notebooks. Expect the library to appear as a new or changed item. Activate the stage's value set Activate TST in the TST workspace by hand, or with the script from the REST section : Run the guard and a test load Run PL_SALES_BRZ_LoadOrders . The guard passes, the Copy activity reads from the TST database connection, a"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/example-and-operations.html#anti-patterns","t":"Worked example and operations","s":"Anti-patterns","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Anti-pattern Why it hurts Instead Secrets, tokens or keys stored as variables Anyone who can view the library can read them, and they sit in Git Use connection references and key vault references; store only identifiers One library for the whole tenant or workspace Couples unrelated teams, widens write access and approaches size limits One library per domain and layer Code that branches on the stage name ( if stage ="},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/example-and-operations.html#troubleshooting","t":"Worked example and operations","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Four questions, in order. Most wrong-value incidents end at the first or second question. The table lists the rest. Symptom Likely cause Fix TST or PRD runs with DEV values The default value set is still active after the first deployment Activate the stage's set; add the guard and the scripted activation The library is missing from a pipeline's variable picker The library is in another workspace, or the user has no a"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/example-and-operations.html#checklist","t":"Worked example and operations","s":"Validation checklist","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Use this list when you introduce a library and again for every first deployment into a stage. One library per domain and layer, named by convention ( VL_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER>_<Purpose> ), with a named owner. Value sets are named exactly after the stages, with one default-value-set convention across the organization. Every variable has a decided type and a note; reference types point at items and connections the editor can"},{"u":"playbooks/09-variable-libraries/example-and-operations.html#refs","t":"Worked example and operations","s":"References","p":"Variable Libraries","x":"Tutorial: use variable libraries Fabric application lifecycle management variable library Lifecycle management of the variable library Variable library integration with pipelines NotebookUtils variable library utilities NotebookUtils for Fabric (runtime context) Expressions and functions in pipelines Troubleshoot the Fabric lifecycle management tools"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/index.html","t":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","s":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Playbook 10 · Data engineering Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery Replace pipelines full of hard-coded notebook names and stored-procedure calls with a small set of generic pipelines that read their work from a Warehouse table, and keep the item IDs that table needs fresh with an automated inventory of everything in your tenant. Audience platform engineers, Fabric admins Level Advanced Runs on Data Fa"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/index.html#problem","t":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","s":"The problem this playbook solves","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Once a platform grows past a few dozen notebooks and SQL scripts, orchestration becomes the bottleneck. Every new load or transformation means opening a pipeline, dragging another activity onto the canvas, wiring dependencies by hand, and promoting the change through every environment. The same eight-activity pattern (log start, run, log end, notify) is copied dozens of times, and no two copies drift in the same way."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/index.html#what-changes","t":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","s":"What changes when work moves into a table","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The control-table pattern separates the what (a row that names a task, its type, its stage and its owner) from the how (a generic engine that knows how to run any task type, log it, and raise an alarm). Adding the twentieth notebook to the nightly run becomes an INSERT , not a pipeline change. Before and after. The red column repeats wiring and breaks when a notebook is renamed or recreated; the green column keeps on"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/index.html#when-to-use","t":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","s":"When to use it, and when not to","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A good fit when You orchestrate dozens or hundreds of notebooks, procedures or child pipelines with the same run, log and notify pattern. People other than pipeline authors need to add or disable tasks under change control. You need an audit trail of every attempt that you can query with SQL. Tasks live in several workspaces and you want one place that knows their IDs. A poor fit when You have fewer than a handful of"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/index.html#what-you-build","t":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","s":"What you build","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Asset Where it lives Role Page NB_ORCH_HUB_Inventory notebook DEV_ORCH_HUB workspace Calls the REST APIs, builds the inventory DataFrames and writes Delta tables 2 fabric_inventory and related tables LH_ORCH_HUB_Inventory lakehouse Queryable catalog of workspaces, items and connection strings 2 ctl.control_table , ctl.task_dependency WH_ORCH_HUB_Control warehouse The DAG as data: one row per task and its prerequisite"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/index.html#prerequisites","t":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","s":"Prerequisites in brief","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A Fabric workspace on a capacity that hosts the Warehouse, the inventory lakehouse, the notebooks and the pipelines (the hub workspace). Permission to create and run pipelines and to execute procedures in the Warehouse. A service principal (or, for activities that support it, a workspace identity) with access to every workspace you orchestrate, plus the tenant settings that let it call Fabric APIs. The details are on"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/index.html#map","t":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","s":"Map of the playbook","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"02 Asset discovery and inventory API options, permissions, the inventory notebook, table schemas and how the catalog is used. 03 Framework design and decisions Principles, topology, decision table, ordering, retries, concurrency and restart design. 04 Warehouse objects and seed data Stored procedures, control table, run log, views and the first rows. 05 Notebook dispatcher Four ways to run a notebook by ID, and the l"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/index.html#related","t":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","s":"How this fits with the other playbooks","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Playbook 03, Medallion Architecture : the ingest and transform notebooks that the control table orchestrates live in the layer workspaces defined there. The control table supplies the stage ordering across those layers. Playbook 04, Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration : the hub-and-spoke pattern decides where pipelines live. This playbook makes the hub data-driven, so spokes are invoked from rows instead of from c"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/index.html#refs","t":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","s":"References","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Lookup activity ForEach activity T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse Items - List Items (Fabric REST API) Data movement decision guide"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"Asset discovery and inventory","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Playbook 10 · Half A Asset discovery and inventory A scheduled notebook can enumerate every workspace and item you are allowed to see, capture where each one lives and how to connect to it, and publish the result as Delta tables. That catalog is the foundation for naming audits, cost attribution, dynamic orchestration and deployment scripts."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#purpose","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"What the inventory captures","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The discovery notebook connects to the Fabric REST APIs, walks the workspaces it can see and lists the items in each one: lakehouses, warehouses, notebooks, pipelines, semantic models, reports and every other item type the API returns. For each item it records three groups of facts. What it is Item type and display name, plus the description and folder when the API returns them. Where it is Workspace name and ID, the"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#api-options","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"Choose the right API","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Fabric exposes the same facts through four families of APIs. They differ in scope (what the caller can see), cost (how many calls a full inventory needs) and depth (whether you get schema and lineage). Option Endpoints Sees Limits to design for Use it for Core list APIs GET /v1/workspaces , GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/items , Get Lakehouse, Get Warehouse Workspaces the caller can access; items need at least the Viewer ro"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#identity","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"Identity, permissions and tenant settings","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A scheduled inventory should not run as a person. Use a service principal that belongs to a security group, grant the group the access it needs, and keep its secret in Azure Key Vault. Scenario Identity What it needs Tenant settings to enable Interactive exploration Your user Viewer (or higher) role in each workspace you want to list. None. Scheduled inventory of the workspaces your platform owns Service principal in"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#token-idioms","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"Token acquisition idioms","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Idiom Use when Watch out for notebookutils.credentials.getToken('pbi') The notebook runs as a user, or interactively. The audience keys are storage , pbi , keyvault and kusto ; pbi covers the Power BI and Fabric REST APIs. Under a service principal the token has limited scopes (a subset of read/write item scopes) rather than the full Fabric service scope. Tokens expire, so refresh them in long jobs. MSAL client crede"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#notebook-flow","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"How the notebook works","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Inventory notebook flow. Everything inside the dashed zone talks to the API and must tolerate throttling; everything after it is local Spark work. Three behaviors of the Fabric list APIs shape the code: Pagination. A response that has more data includes continuationToken and continuationUri ; the keys are absent on the last page. The original script's $top=5000 query option is not a documented parameter of the Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#notebook-code","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"The inventory notebook","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Create a notebook named NB_ORCH_HUB_Inventory in the hub workspace, attach LH_ORCH_HUB_Inventory as its default lakehouse, and split the code into cells as shown. The first cell is a parameter cell so a pipeline or schedule can override values."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#scanner-flow","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"When you need lineage and schema: the scanner flow","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The list APIs return item names and IDs. If you also need lineage, data source details or the tables and columns of semantic models, use the scanner, which is asynchronous and works on chunks of workspaces. Take a full scan first, then run incremental scans for workspaces that changed. Scanner flow. Chunk the workspace list by 100, keep no more than 16 scans in flight, and persist the result you need before the 24-ho"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#inventory-tables","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"Inventory tables","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Running the notebook creates the tables below in the default lakehouse. They are snapshots: each run overwrites the current tables and, when KEEP_HISTORY is on, appends the inventory to a history table so you can detect items that appeared or disappeared. Table Grain Key columns fabric_inventory One row per item workspace_id , workspace_name , capacity_id , item_id , item_type , item_name , logical_id , sql_connectio"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#inventory-pitfalls","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"Pitfalls","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix A workspace or its items are missing The identity has no role in it (core APIs need Viewer or higher). Add the service principal's security group to the workspace, or use the admin list APIs. 401 or 403 under a service principal Tenant switch off, principal outside the allowed group, or the app carries admin-consent Power BI permissions. Re-check the switches and the group; remove admin-conse"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#administration","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"How the inventory is used for administration","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A current, queryable inventory changes administration from workspace-by-workspace clicking into a data-driven practice. Five uses come up repeatedly. One dataset, five uses. Each consumer asks a different question of the same tables, which is why the inventory is worth keeping fresh."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#use-governance","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"1. Governance and auditing","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Complete asset visibility. The first question of any audit is \"what do we have and where?\". The inventory answers it with a query. Enforcing standards. Find workspaces and items that do not follow the naming convention, or workspaces created outside the standard process. Identifying unused assets. Join the inventory to activity data (the admin activity events, or monitoring data kept in your own tables) to flag noteb"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#use-orchestration","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"2. Automation and dynamic orchestration","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"This is the use that connects the two halves of the playbook. Instead of copying a notebook's ID into a pipeline by hand, you resolve it from the inventory when you seed or synchronize the control table. Control-table-driven pipelines. fabric_notebooks , fabric_pipelines and the other typed tables are the source for object_id and target_workspace_id in the control table. Cross-workspace operations. A central pipeline"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#use-cost","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"3. Cost management and optimization","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Resource sprawl. Count workspaces and items per capacity and per domain, and list workspaces that hold zero or one item. Capacity planning. The mix of item types (many large warehouses versus many small notebooks) is a first-order signal when you forecast capacity. The inventory gives you the mix; pair it with the capacity metrics for actual consumption."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#use-security","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"4. Security and compliance","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Defining the security scope. You cannot review access on assets you do not know exist. The inventory is the checklist of items whose permissions and sensitivity labels need review. Auditing footprint. Security teams can use the inventory (and, for lineage and sensitivity details, the scanner output) to confirm that every asset falls under the right governance policy."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#use-cicd","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"5. CI/CD and DevOps enablement","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A deployment script usually needs the ID of a target workspace or item. Instead of hard-coding it, query the inventory. When workspaces are connected to Git or deployment pipelines, the logical_id identifies the same item across environments, so you can translate a source ID to the target environment's ID. Verify against Microsoft Learn The item's logicalId is null when no logical ID has been assigned. Confirm in Ite"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/asset-inventory.html#refs","t":"Asset discovery and inventory","s":"References","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Workspaces - List Workspaces (Core) Items - List Items (Core) Items - List Items (Admin) Workspaces - List Workspaces (Admin) Run metadata scanning Enable service principal authentication for admin APIs NotebookUtils credentials utilities NotebookUtils (former MSSparkUtils) Service principal support for semantic link Fabric REST API throttling"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Framework design and decisions","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Playbook 10 · Half B Framework design and decisions Before you build, decide what the framework is, what it will and will not do, and how it behaves when tasks fail, overlap or need to be re-run. This page covers the principles, the end-to-end flow, prerequisites, the example topology, a decision table against alternatives and the design guidance that the later pages implement."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#principles","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Principles of control-table-driven orchestration","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"To orchestrate hundreds of PySpark notebooks and SQL scripts reliably, shift control flow out of hand-built pipeline wiring and into a configuration layer. By separating the what (a row in the control table that defines a task) from the how (a generic engine that executes any task), you get reuse, consistency and a platform that changes by data entry instead of by deployment. A control-table framework uses configurat"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#deliverables","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"What the playbook delivers","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A control table and a task-dependency table in a Warehouse, with seed scripts. The control table is the system of record for your DAG: each row is a task with its type, stage, order, target IDs, parameters and notification targets. An append-only run log that records every start and end event. Because it never updates in place, it avoids the snapshot-isolation update conflicts that appear when many parallel tasks wri"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#how-it-works","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"How it works, end to end","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"End-to-end flow. The control table decides what runs; the pipeline decides how. Each task writes its own start and end events, so the run log is a complete trail even when parallel tasks overlap. Declare tasks in the control table. Each row defines a task: task_type (Notebook, StoredProcedure, Pipeline), stage , sequence_no , the target IDs, and notify_to . This keeps orchestration data-driven and easy to review unde"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#why","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Why this approach","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Deterministic and auditable. A single table declares the DAG, the pipeline reads it exactly as it is at run time, and the append-only log is an immutable trail of every attempt and outcome. Parallel where it is safe, sequential where it is needed. ForEach settings balance throughput and dependencies, and the batch count caps concurrency. Rename-proof execution. Referencing items by ID instead of by display name remov"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#prerequisites","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Prerequisites","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A Fabric workspace with Data Factory and a Warehouse for the control table, logs and procedures (the hub workspace). Permission to run pipelines and to execute procedures in that workspace, and Contributor access (or higher) to every workspace whose notebooks and pipelines you will orchestrate. A mailbox or a Teams channel for failure notifications. With a user connection the Outlook activity sends from that account,"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#topology","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Scenario and topology","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The worked example uses one orchestration hub workspace and two data workspaces. The hub holds the Warehouse with the control table, run log and procedures, the master pipeline and the dispatcher. Ingest notebooks live in the Bronze workspace, the transform notebook in the Silver workspace (see Medallion Architecture for the layout), and the two stored procedures live in the hub Warehouse. Example topology. The hub s"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#decision-table","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Decision table: control table or something else?","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The control-table pattern is one of several reasonable ways to run work in Fabric. Pick it when its strengths line up with your constraints, not because it is the most flexible. Approach Strengths Limits Choose it when Hard-coded pipelines One pipeline per flow, activities and IDs set on the canvas Simplest to read and debug. Visual lineage on the canvas. Nothing extra to maintain. Duplicated logging and error handli"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#design-guidance","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Design guidance","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#ordering","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Ordering: stage, sequence and dependencies","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Model ordering explicitly. Three columns carry it: stage is a gate. All enabled tasks of stage n must succeed before stage n+1 starts. It maps to one Lookup and ForEach pair. sequence_no orders tasks inside a stage that runs sequentially. It is also the tie-breaker for parallel stages: order long-running tasks first so they start first. ctl.task_dependency records which task depends on which. By itself it documents t"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#idempotency","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Idempotency and restarts","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Assume every task can be run twice. Pipelines retry, people rerun, and a failed run is restarted. Write notebooks so that a repeat produces the same end state (overwrite a partition or MERGE on a key) and write procedures with TRUNCATE then insert, or MERGE , inside a transaction. The framework itself never promises exactly-once execution. To restart a failed run without repeating finished work, give the master pipel"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#retries","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Retries and timeouts","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Use the activity's own retry for transient errors. Pipeline activities can retry automatically (the retry count, interval and optional retry conditions are set per activity). By default a retry fires on any failure; retry conditions, which are in preview and supported for specific activity types including Notebook and Stored procedure, let you retry only on matching error codes or messages such as throttling. Retry c"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#concurrency","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Concurrency limits","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Set the ForEach batch count by the scarcest resource, not by the number of tasks. The scarce resource is rarely the pipeline. Resource Constraint What to do ForEach Parallel iterations are capped by the batch count (documented for Azure Data Factory as a maximum of 50, default 20). Start low and raise it while watching the next rows. Spark capacity Notebooks started by a pipeline queue first-in, first-out when the ca"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#dynamic-parameters","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Dynamic parameters and JSON configuration","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Tasks differ in small ways: a load window, a table name, a mode flag. Resist adding a column for each. A nullable parameters_json column holds a JSON object that the pipeline passes to the task as one string parameter; the notebook parses it with json.loads and applies defaults. Keep frequently queried settings ( load_mode , timeout_minutes ) as typed columns, and use JSON for the long tail. Warehouse tables cannot h"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#extensibility","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"Extensibility to new task types","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A task type is a contract: required columns plus one branch in the Switch. To add Spark job definitions, Dataflow Gen2 refreshes, Copy jobs, semantic model refreshes or web calls, you add the type name to the guard procedure's list of valid types, add a Switch case that maps row columns to the activity's settings, and add a row-level rule to the guard (for example, a Copy job task must have object_id ). The engine's "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/framework-design.html#refs","t":"Framework design and decisions","s":"References","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Activity overview (general settings, retries, activity limits) Lookup activity ForEach activity Invoke pipeline activity Notebook activity What is Copy job in Data Factory Data movement decision guide NotebookUtils notebook run and orchestration Job queueing in Apache Spark for Fabric Transactions in Fabric Data Warehouse Workspace identity"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Warehouse objects and seed data","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Playbook 10 · Half B · Steps 0 to 2 Warehouse objects and seed data Everything the framework stores lives in one Fabric Warehouse: a configuration table, a dependency table, an append-only run log, views over the log and a handful of stored procedures. This page turns your existing scripts into procedures (step 0), creates the objects (step 1) and seeds the first five tasks (step 2)."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#overview","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"What you create","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"All objects go into the hub Warehouse ( WH_ORCH_HUB_Control in the example). Configuration and history live in a ctl schema so they are easy to secure and to keep apart from the procedures that do real work, which stay in dbo with a usp_ prefix. Object model. Configuration is edited by people and versioned as a seed script. History is written only by usp_log_event and only ever appended to. The views turn events into"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#convert","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Step 0: convert T-SQL scripts into stored procedures","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The pipeline can run a stored procedure by name and observe whether it succeeded. It cannot do that with a saved query. So the first step is to turn each script you want to orchestrate into a procedure with the same body. Apply these rules: One script, one procedure. Name it dbo.usp_<verb>_<noun> . The guard rejects any other pattern, which also keeps the name safe to use in generated SQL. Remove client-only statemen"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#create-objects","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Step 1: create the control table, run log and procedures","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#schema","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Schema","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Run this once. It fails if the schema already exists."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#control-table","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Control table","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Each row is a task. The table holds configuration only, so it carries no status or retry counters that the pipeline would have to update. Runtime state belongs in the run log. NOT ENFORCED means metadata only Warehouse accepts primary key and unique constraints only as NOT ENFORCED (and NONCLUSTERED for a primary key), and the scripts add them with ALTER TABLE . The engine uses them as metadata and will not reject a "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#task-dependency","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Task dependency table","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The source design stored dependencies as a comma-separated list inside the task row. A separate table is easier to validate, query and join, and needs no string parsing."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#run-log","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Run log","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The run log is the key design decision on this page. It is insert-only : a task writes a Started row when it begins and one end row ( Succeeded , Failed or Skipped ) when it finishes. Nothing is ever updated. Parallel iterations therefore never write to the same row, and the Warehouse's snapshot isolation never has an update conflict to report. From events to status. Status is derived, not stored. Because the log is "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#procedures","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Stored procedures","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Three procedures belong to the framework itself. The two example task procedures were created in step 0. usp_log_event The only writer of the run log. The pipeline calls it through a Stored procedure activity with typed parameters, which avoids building SQL strings from error messages that contain quotes. usp_validate_control_table The guard. Run it at the top of every master pipeline run. Each rule throws with the n"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#views","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Status views","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Three views turn the event stream into status. Each is a separate batch. A run that is between stages has no task in Running state for a moment, so vw_run_summary can briefly show Succeeded for a run that is still going. For pipeline-level state use the Monitor hub, or compare the number of logged tasks with the number of enabled tasks."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#warehouse-rules","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Warehouse behaviors that shaped these scripts","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"If you adapt scripts from SQL Server or Azure SQL, expect these differences. Behavior What the scripts do about it Primary key and unique constraints are accepted only as NOT ENFORCED . A guard procedure checks uniqueness before every run. No nvarchar , tinyint or datetime columns. uniqueidentifier values cannot be read through a SQL analytics endpoint. Text is VARCHAR , flags are BIT , times are DATETIME2(6) and IDs"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#seed","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Step 2: seed the control table","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The seed script is idempotent: it deletes the five example rows by name and inserts them again inside one transaction, so you can run it repeatedly. It holds names, not IDs . IDs are filled in afterwards by the sync procedure, which is what keeps one script valid for development, test and production. Replace alerts@example.com with your team's shared mailbox or distribution list."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#resolve-ids","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Resolve the IDs","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"With the inventory in place, resolve and validate in one go: The guard finishes without an error, and the notebook rows now carry lower-case GUIDs. If the guard throws, it names the task to fix. If you have no inventory yet, set the IDs by hand. Take them from the item's URL in the browser or from the inventory notebook's output. The values below are placeholders: The seeded plan, as the master pipeline will read it:"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#day-two","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"Changing the plan later","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"After the engine exists, the plan changes with data entry. Each of these is a single-table statement: Keep the seed script in source control and treat direct edits in production as emergency changes: record them in the script afterwards, bump config_version , and rerun the guard. Resolve IDs at sync time, not at run time Do not look up IDs from the inventory inside the master pipeline. An inventory that is a few hour"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/warehouse-objects.html#refs","t":"Warehouse objects and seed data","s":"References","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse Data types in Fabric Data Warehouse Tables in Fabric Data Warehouse (constraints) Transactions in Fabric Data Warehouse Collation in Fabric Data Warehouse Query the Warehouse (cross-database queries) Source control with Warehouse CREATE PROCEDURE (Fabric) CREATE VIEW (Fabric) MERGE (Fabric) THROW (Fabric)"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Playbook 10 · Half B · Step 3 Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher) A control table that stores notebook names breaks the day someone renames a notebook. This page explains why the framework stores IDs instead, compares the ways a pipeline can start a notebook by ID, and builds a dispatcher notebook that starts a notebook through the Fabric job scheduler API, waits for it, and reports the outcome."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#why-ids","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"Why IDs and not names","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A display name is a label that anyone with the right role can edit. An item ID is assigned when the item is created and never changes. Orchestration that depends on a name is therefore only as stable as the team's naming discipline. Event Reference by name Reference by ID, resolved at sync time Someone renames a notebook Breaks. The next run cannot find it. Keeps working. The next sync updates the stored name. Notebo"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#options","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"Ways to start a notebook from a control-table row","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Choosing how to start a notebook. Prefer the native Notebook activity wherever it accepts the IDs you store. Use the dispatcher when you need row-level timeout, attempts or outcome handling, or when dynamic IDs are not accepted. Option Rename-proof Queued when capacity is full Per-row timeout and attempts Cost and monitoring Native Notebook activity with IDs from the row Yes , if IDs are accepted as dynamic content Y"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#identity","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"Identity, permissions and tenant settings","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The dispatcher calls the Fabric REST API as whichever identity runs the dispatcher notebook. When a pipeline's Notebook activity starts it, that is the identity of the activity's connection. Permission. The identity must be allowed to run the target notebook. Add a service principal or workspace identity to the target workspace as Contributor, Member or Admin. The job scheduler lists the delegated scopes Item.Execute"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#dispatcher-flow","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"How the dispatcher works","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Dispatcher sequence. The dispatcher holds a session open while the target runs, which is the price of row-level control. It waits at least the first Retry-After before polling, then polls with growing intervals. Job statuses reported by the scheduler are NotStarted , InProgress , Completed , Failed , Cancelled and Deduped . The last means another instance of the same job type was already running and this request was "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#notebook-code","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"The dispatcher notebook","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Create NB_ORCH_HUB_RunNotebookById in the hub workspace. A Python notebook is enough, because the dispatcher only makes HTTP calls, and it keeps the extra session small. Split the code into the three cells below. Mark the first as the parameter cell. Verify against Microsoft Learn The job scheduler documents a generic endpoint ( .../items/{itemId}/jobs/{jobType}/instances ) and a notebook-specific one ( .../notebooks"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#child-contract","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"The contract for orchestrated notebooks","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"For the three run parameters to take effect, the target notebook must declare them in a parameter cell with the same names. The parameter list is not broadly supported for every item type; if the API returns FeatureNotAvailable , the target item type does not accept it. One string parameter keeps the pipeline expression stable while the set of settings evolves. Adding a setting means changing the notebook and the row"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#test","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"Test the dispatcher before wiring it in","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Run it by hand. Set TARGET_WORKSPACE_ID and NOTEBOOK_ID to a harmless notebook (one that only prints and returns) and run all cells. Expect a Completed status and a JSON summary. Break it on purpose. Use a random ID (expect item-not-found with no retries), a notebook that raises (expect a failed run and, with MAX_ATTEMPTS above 1, a retry) and a one-minute timeout against a sleeping notebook (expect a cancel). Run it"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#capacity","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"Capacity and concurrency","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Two sessions per task. A parallel ForEach with batch count n keeps up to n dispatcher sessions and n child sessions alive. A Python dispatcher is small, but it is not free; size the batch count with both in mind. No queue for REST-started runs. A pipeline-started notebook queues when the capacity is full. A run started through the REST API does not queue and is rejected, which is why the dispatcher retries HTTP 430 w"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#troubleshooting","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Symptom Likely cause What to do 403, insufficient privileges The running identity cannot run the target notebook. Add it to the target workspace (Contributor or higher) and confirm the tenant setting for service principals. 404, item not found Wrong ID or workspace, or the notebook was deleted. Run usp_sync_object_ids and the guard. Do not paste IDs by hand into the pipeline. 429 on start Too many run requests. Lower"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/dispatcher.html#refs","t":"Notebook dispatcher (GUID launcher)","s":"References","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Job Scheduler: Run On Demand Item Job Job Scheduler: Get Item Job Instance Manage and execute notebooks with APIs NotebookUtils notebook run and orchestration Job queueing in Apache Spark for Fabric Notebook activity Workspace identity NotebookUtils credentials"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Build the master pipeline","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Playbook 10 · Half B · Step 4 Build the master pipeline The master pipeline is the only pipeline that knows the framework. It validates the configuration, reads the tasks for one stage, loops over them, runs the right activity for each task type, and records every outcome. This page builds it activity by activity, explains the failure semantics that decide whether a stage blocks the next one, and adds a reusable noti"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#overview","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"The pipeline at a glance","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"One pipeline, PL_ORCH_HUB_Master , in the hub workspace. It has a guard, then one Lookup and one ForEach per stage. Every arrow between stages is an On success dependency, so a failed stage stops everything after it. Canvas layout. Guard, then Lookup and ForEach per stage. Stages 2 and 3 run their tasks one after another; stage 1 runs its ingests in parallel. Activity Type Runs after Purpose Guard_Validate Stored pro"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#setup","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Before you build","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The Warehouse objects exist, the control table is seeded and the guard runs clean ( previous page ). The dispatcher notebook exists, or you have confirmed the native Notebook activity accepts dynamic IDs ( dispatcher page ). One Warehouse connection to WH_ORCH_HUB_Control for the Lookup, Script and Stored procedure activities. A connection for the Notebook and Invoke pipeline activities, using the identity chosen on "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#guard","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Guard step","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Add a Stored procedure activity named Guard_Validate that runs dbo.usp_validate_control_table . It has no parameters. Every later activity depends on its success. Give it both outcome paths: On failure: Log_Guard_Failed (Stored procedure, dbo.usp_log_event ) with task_name set to (pipeline) , event Failed and the guard's error as the message, then Notify_Guard_Failed (Invoke pipeline, the notification pipeline ) addr"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#stage-lookups","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Stage lookups and ForEach settings","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Each stage has a Lookup and a ForEach . The Lookup returns the stage's enabled tasks; the ForEach iterates them. Settings for Lookup_Stage1 : connection to the Warehouse, Use query set to Query , and First row only turned off . The COALESCE calls fill defaults once, in SQL, so the expressions inside the loop never meet a missing or null property. The restart clause is empty-safe: with no restart_run_id the subquery r"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#iteration","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"The per-task body","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Everything inside a ForEach is the same, apart from the stage suffix. It has five activities: log the start, run the right activity for the task type, then either log success or log failure and notify. One task, one body. The Switch isolates the three task types. Logging and notification sit outside it, so every type is treated the same way."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#log-activities","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Logging activities","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"All three logging activities are Stored procedure activities that call dbo.usp_log_event through the Warehouse connection, with typed parameters. Typed parameters avoid the quoting problems of building SQL text from an error message. Click Import to load the parameter list, then set the values: Parameter Type Value run_id String @pipeline().RunId task_name String @item().task_name stage Int32 @item().stage activity S"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#switch","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Switch on task type","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Add a Switch activity named Run_Task_S1 . Set Expression to @item().task_type . Cases match the exact text, so they are case-sensitive and must equal the values the guard allows. Case Activity inside Reads from the row Notebook Run_Notebook_S1 object_id , target_workspace_id , parameters_json , timeout_minutes , max_attempts StoredProcedure Run_Procedure_S1 object_name Pipeline Run_Pipeline_S1 object_id , target_work"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#notebook-case","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Case Notebook","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Two ways to build Run_Notebook_S1 . Choose with the decision path on the dispatcher page. Through the dispatcher. A Notebook activity that runs NB_ORCH_HUB_RunNotebookById . Base parameters: Parameter Type Value TASK_NAME String @item().task_name RUN_ID String @pipeline().RunId TARGET_WORKSPACE_ID String @item().target_workspace_id NOTEBOOK_ID String @item().object_id PARAMETERS_JSON String @item().parameters_json TI"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#procedure-case","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Case StoredProcedure","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Use a Script activity named Run_Procedure_S1 on the Warehouse connection, with Script type set to NonQuery and this script: The Stored procedure activity binds a procedure when you author it, and its parameter list is fixed at that point, so it cannot serve a table of different procedures. The Script activity can. The name comes from a table, which is why the guard insists that every procedure follows dbo.usp_* and e"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#pipeline-case","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Case Pipeline","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Use an Invoke pipeline activity named Run_Pipeline_S1 . Set Type to Fabric , choose the connection, and point the workspace and pipeline fields at @item().target_workspace_id and @item().object_id if dynamic content is accepted. Pass the same three values every orchestrated pipeline should accept: Parameter Value task_name @item().task_name run_id @pipeline().RunId parameters_json @item().parameters_json Make sure th"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#default-case","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Default case","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Add a Fail activity named Fail_UnknownType_S1 . Message: @concat('Unknown task_type ', item().task_type, ' for task ', item().task_name) ; error code: UnknownTaskType . The guard should have caught this already. The default case is the backstop for a configuration that bypassed it."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#outcome","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Success and failure paths: what decides the stage result","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The most important property of this design is not visible on the canvas: whether a failed task stops the next stage. It is decided by how you wire the two outcome paths. Two wirings, two outcomes. With only a failure path the failure is handled and the stage reports success. With both paths, the skipped success leaf hands the verdict back to the failed task, so the stage fails. The rule, as documented for Azure Data "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#notifications","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Notifications","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Put every notification in one reusable pipeline, PL_ORCH_HUB_Notify , and invoke it from Notify_Failure_S1 (and from the guard). The Fabric documentation recommends exactly this composition for email: a dedicated email pipeline that owns the activity and its connection, called through an Invoke pipeline activity. One place to fix. Callers never hold an email or Teams connection. When a connection expires or a deploym"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#notify-pipeline","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Build the notification pipeline","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Create PL_ORCH_HUB_Notify with four string parameters. to , task_name , run_id and error_message . Add an Office 365 Outlook activity. Choose a connection. To : @pipeline().parameters.to (a semicolon-separated list works). Subject : @concat('Pipeline failure: ', pipeline().parameters.task_name) . Build the body from the parameters. Use line breaks that render in the tenant's mail client. The expression below assumes "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#notify-identity","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Identity and limits","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Who sends the mail. The Outlook activity supports user (OAuth), workspace identity and service principal connections. With a user connection the content is sent from that user's account, so use a team-owned account. A workspace identity or service principal needs application-level Microsoft Graph permissions with administrator consent, and may need Exchange Online mailbox restrictions. Those permissions can allow sen"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#dependency-gate","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Optional: a dependency gate","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"A stage gate blocks a whole stage when anything before it failed. If independent branches share a stage and one branch's failure should not stall the others, check each task's prerequisites individually. Four changes make the pipeline do that: Check prerequisites per task. Add a Lookup before Run_Task_S1 , with First row only on: Branch on the result. Because Switch and If cannot nest, change the Switch expression so"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#overlap","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Optional: guard against overlapping runs","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Two runs of the master pipeline writing to the same tables will conflict. A schedule that overlaps with a manual run is the usual cause. Add a Lookup after the guard and an If Condition that fails the run: The condition is @greater(activity('Check_Overlap').output.firstRow.running_runs, 0) . This is a heuristic. A cancelled run leaves a Started row with no end row, which looks like a run in progress until the time wi"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#child-pipeline","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Variant: extract the per-task body into a child pipeline","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Building the body three times, once per stage, works and is easy to follow, but the copies drift. Once the design is stable, move the body into a child pipeline. Create PL_ORCH_HUB_RunTask with parameters run_id , stage , task_name , task_type , object_name , object_id , target_workspace_id , parameters_json , timeout_minutes , max_attempts , notify_to and default_notify_to . Move the body into it, replacing @item()."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#first-run","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"Validate, run and inspect","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Validate and save. Use the editor's Validate button and fix any missing connection or dependency. Run it. Run the pipeline with the default parameters. In the Monitor hub the stage ForEach activities run in order and the ingests overlap. Read the log. Replace the run ID in the queries below with the pipeline run ID shown in the Monitor hub. Run a failure drill. Point one notebook at an ID that does not exist, run aga"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/master-pipeline.html#refs","t":"Build the master pipeline","s":"References","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Lookup activity ForEach activity Switch activity Script activity Stored procedure activity Notebook activity Invoke pipeline activity Fail activity Office 365 Outlook activity Teams activity Expressions and functions in pipelines Pipeline failure and error message (Azure Data Factory)"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"Extended scenario and workshop","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Playbook 10 · Half B · Scale-out Extended scenario and workshop The first plan has five tasks. This page grows it to twenty without touching the pipeline, which is the point of the framework: new work is a data-entry step. It shows the full plan, the script that populates the control table, what each stage lookup returns, and what the run looks like on a timeline. It ends with a workshop outline you can use to teach "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#initial-plan","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"The initial plan","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The first plan is the five tasks seeded on the Warehouse objects page : two ingests in parallel, one transform after both, and two stored procedures in sequence. It is small enough to read on one screen and large enough to exercise every part of the framework: a parallel stage, a dependency between stages and a sequential stage of a different task type. Stage Tasks Type Runs 1 ingest_customers , ingest_products Noteb"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#expanded-plan","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"The expanded plan","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The extended scenario adds fifteen tasks: seven notebooks that simulate data-preparation steps and eight stored procedures that build a small dimension table. They do trivial work on purpose, so the whole run takes minutes and the timeline is easy to read. Twenty tasks, three stages. The pipeline from the previous page runs this plan unchanged. Only the control table grew. Task Type Stage Seq Item Depends on schema_i"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#populate","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"Populate the control table","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Four things have to exist before the rows can be inserted. Create the demo items, then run the populate script."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#demo-notebooks","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"1. Demo notebooks","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Create seven notebooks with the names in the table above: four in the Bronze workspace and three in the Silver workspace. Each has the same two cells, which simulate work so the timeline has something to show. The first cell is the parameter cell and matches the orchestrated-notebook contract . The sleep length comes from parameters_json , so you can make one task slow, or force one to fail, without opening the noteb"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#demo-procedures","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"2. Demo procedures","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Create a small log table, then eight procedures that each insert one row. The procedures run in sequence, so there is no write conflict. Create the other seven from the same template, changing the name in both places: usp_insert_staging_data , usp_apply_data_type_cast , usp_calculate_aggregate , usp_apply_hardcoded_filter , usp_parameterized_load , usp_update_dim_flag and usp_cleanup_staging_table ."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#demo-inventory","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"3. Refresh the inventory","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Run the inventory notebook so the new notebooks appear in fabric_inventory . The sync procedure resolves IDs from there."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#populate-script","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"4. Insert the fifteen rows","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The script follows the same pattern as the seed: delete by name, insert inside one transaction, no IDs. Populate script for tasks T6 to T20 (T-SQL) Then resolve the IDs, run the guard and count the plan: The count should be 6 notebooks in stage 1, 4 notebooks in stage 2 and 10 stored procedures in stage 3. If the guard throws, read which task it names: the usual causes are a notebook that is not in the inventory yet,"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#review-lookups","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"Review the lookups","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Before running, confirm what each stage lookup now returns. Run the three queries from the master pipeline page in the Warehouse query editor, or open each Lookup activity and use Preview data . Lookup Rows Order returned Lookup_Stage1 6 ingest_customers , ingest_products , schema_inference , data_ingestion_sim , column_renamer , row_count_logger Lookup_Stage2 4 transform_sales , type_caster , filter_duplicates , tem"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#timeline","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"Run it and read the timeline","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Run the master pipeline. When it finishes, the log answers three questions: what ran, in what order and how long each part took. An illustrative timeline. The durations are made up to show the shape: waves of three in stage 1, a staircase in stages 2 and 3. Your own run will differ with capacity and session start-up time. How to read it: Stage 1 is a plateau. The bars overlap, limited by the batch count. Raising the "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#workshop","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"Turn it into a workshop","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The pipeline is small enough to build by hand in a half day, and building it is the best way to understand it. This outline works for a group of data engineers. It does not depend on a pre-built pipeline file: participants build the pipeline from the master pipeline page and compare it with the description of the finished canvas. A pipeline exported from source control is tied to one workspace's item IDs and connecti"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#facilitator","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"Facilitator notes","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Set up shared mail early. The notification lab needs a mailbox or Teams channel that every participant can send to, and an admin may have to approve the connection. Give each participant their own hub workspace. Shared control tables make parallel exercises collide. Watch capacity. Twenty participants each running six parallel notebooks will saturate a small capacity. Stagger the extension lab, or keep stage 1 at two"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#cleanup","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"Clean-up","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Disable the demo tasks ( is_enabled = 0 ) when the workshop ends, rather than deleting them, so the timeline and run history stay readable. Drop the demo tables and notebooks when the workspace is retired. Key takeaways Growing the plan from five to twenty tasks was a data-entry step: rows in the control table and the dependency table, plus a sync and a guard run. Order belongs in sequence_no . Never rely on names, w"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/extended-scenario.html#refs","t":"Extended scenario and workshop","s":"References","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Lookup activity ForEach activity Browse pipeline runs in the Monitoring hub Choose how to monitor pipeline runs T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Operations, security and checklist","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Playbook 10 · Half B · Production Operations, security and checklist A framework that runs well on the first day still needs someone to watch it, secure it, promote it between environments and fix it at two in the morning. This page covers the health checks to run every day, the monitoring layers, a troubleshooting table, the security model, CI/CD with variable libraries and a go-live checklist."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#health-checks","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Quick health checks","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"All of these read the run log or its views. Save them in the Warehouse query editor, or build a small report on the views. First, one more view that answers \"what happened last time\" for every task across all runs: What is the latest status of every task, enabled or not? What happened in the last hour? What failed in the last day, and why? Is anything running longer than its timeout? Which tasks are getting slower? W"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#monitoring","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Monitoring and observability","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Use two layers and join them on the pipeline run ID. The run log tells you what the business process did, task by task. The platform's telemetry tells you how the pipelines and capacity behaved. Two layers of observability. The log is yours: you control its shape and retention. The platform layer is Microsoft's. Neither replaces the other. Question Where to look Which task failed, and with what message? The run log a"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#troubleshooting","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Symptom Likely cause What to do The guard fails at the top of the run A configuration rule is violated. The message names the first offending task. Fix the row, rerun the guard in the query editor, and rerun the pipeline. A Lookup returns no rows No enabled task in that stage, a wrong stage number, or a restart_run_id that excludes everything. Run the Lookup query by hand with the same parameter value. A task failed "},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#security","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Security","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The control table is a powerful object: whoever can edit it can make the platform run any notebook or procedure the pipeline's identity can reach. Treat edits as code changes. Limit who can write the control table. Keep the seed script in Git and apply it through a reviewed pull request and a release step. Give day-to-day operators read access to the views, not write access to the tables. Workspace roles take precede"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#cicd","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"CI/CD of the framework","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The framework is a set of items (a Warehouse, pipelines, notebooks and a variable library) plus a body of configuration data. Promote the items through your normal path, and treat the data as environment-specific. Promotion. Items travel through the deployment pipeline. The five steps below the stages are the part a deployment does not do for you."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#where-config-lives","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Where each setting lives","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Setting Lives in Why What runs, in which stage and order, with which dependencies The control table and dependency table, seeded from a script in Git It is the same in every environment, so it is reviewed as code. Workspace IDs and item IDs Derived into the control table by the sync procedure They differ in every environment. Workspace names, when they carry an environment prefix A seed-script variable, or logical_id"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#variable-library","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Using a variable library","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Create VL_ORCH_HUB_Config in the hub workspace with variables such as EnvironmentName (String), DefaultNotifyTo (String) and KeyVaultUri (String), and a value set for each stage. In the master pipeline, add a library variable reference and use it in dynamic content in place of a hard-coded value, for example @pipeline().libraryVariables.DefaultNotifyTo as the default recipient instead of the default_notify_to paramet"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#deploy-steps","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"What to automate in the release","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Deploy the Warehouse objects. Promote the Warehouse through source control or a deployment pipeline. If you keep the Warehouse in Git, read the source-control limitations for adding columns and constraints first. Deploy pipelines, notebooks and the variable library. Follow the layer order from the Deployment Pipelines and Promotion playbook , and put the library with the items that consume it. Run the post-deployment"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#runbook","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Day-two runbook","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Frequency Task Every run Failure notification triage. Read the message, then the log. Daily Run the stale-task, stuck-task and failures queries, or look at the operator report. After any rename, delete or recreate of an item Run the inventory notebook, usp_sync_object_ids and the guard. Weekly Review the slowest tasks and the capacity trend; adjust batch counts and timeouts. Monthly Purge old log rows (below), review"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#go-live","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Go-live checklist","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#check-config","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Configuration","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Every orchestrated script is a procedure that is idempotent, wrapped in a transaction and rethrows its error. The seed script is in Git, holds names and not IDs, and has been reviewed. IDs are filled by the sync procedure and the guard passes in the target environment. Order is set with stage and sequence_no , never by name, and dependencies are recorded."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#check-pipeline","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Pipeline","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The Switch has both a success path and a failure path, and a default case that fails. A deliberate failure fails the stage and blocks the next one. A restart with restart_run_id reruns only the tasks that did not succeed. Batch counts and activity timeouts are set from capacity tests, not left at defaults. The overlap check is in place, or schedules cannot overlap."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#check-notify","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Notifications","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Failure notification goes through one notification pipeline, tested on its own. The mail sender is a team-owned mailbox or a non-user identity, and the connection will be recreated after each deployment."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#check-security","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Security","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Only the platform team can edit the control table; operators have read-only access. The identities have the least workspace roles that work, through a security group per environment. No secret appears in the control table, parameters, variables or notebooks."},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#check-observe","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"Observability and operations","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"The health-check queries or an operator report exist, and someone owns them. Alerts cover failure and the absence of success. Log retention is decided and the purge runs when the pipeline is idle. The release includes the five post-deployment steps and a smoke test. Key takeaways Watch the framework at two layers: the run log for what the process did, and platform telemetry for how the pipelines behaved. Join them on"},{"u":"playbooks/10-control-table-pipelines/operations.html#refs","t":"Operations, security and checklist","s":"References","p":"Control-Table-Driven Pipelines and Asset Discovery","x":"Choose how to monitor pipeline runs Browse pipeline runs in the Monitoring hub Enable workspace monitoring for pipelines Create alerts for pipeline runs Install the Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app Security for data warehousing in Microsoft Fabric Source control with Warehouse Variable libraries overview Introduction to deployment pipelines Activity overview (secure input and output, retries, timeouts)"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Playbook 11 · Organizational Apps Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric An org app packages reports, notebooks, real-time dashboards and other Fabric items into a branded, audience-aware experience for people who never open the workspace. This playbook covers what you need before you start, how to build and govern an app, how access flows from the app to the items beneath it, and how to promote it through environme"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html#what-is","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"What an org app is","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"An org app is a Fabric item type that content creators use to package content for consumers in their organization. You create it inside a workspace ( New > Org app ), pick items from that same workspace, arrange them into a navigation structure, brand it and share it. The people who open the app do not need a role in the workspace: access to the app item grants them what they need on the included items and on the sem"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html#why-adopt","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"Why teams adopt org apps","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Capability What changes for you Watch for Multiple apps per workspace Targeted apps from one set of items, instead of duplicating content or shipping one monolithic app Every app needs an owner and a naming standard Wider content mix Reports, paginated reports, notebooks, real-time dashboards, maps and links in one navigation; Fabric Apps (preview) Classic dashboards and scorecards cannot be included Branding and nav"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html#when-to-use","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"When to use an org app, and when not to","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Use an org app when You are curating content for people outside the workspace, inside your tenant One set of items must serve several audiences with different views You want notebooks, real-time dashboards or maps next to reports Reports depend on semantic models in other workspaces and you want access handled for you You want Git and pipeline-based promotion for the app itself Prefer something else when The consumer"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html#status","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"Status of the capabilities this playbook relies on","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Status labels change. The table records what the documentation says and where this playbook covers it; confirm on Microsoft Learn before you rely on a label. Capability Documented status Covered in Org app item: create, share, edit, delete GA Build Audiences (child items of an org app) Supported in new org apps only Audiences Power BI reports, paginated reports, notebooks, Real-Time Dashboards, Maps as included items"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html#prereq-glance","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"Prerequisites at a glance","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Tenant. The org app tenant setting is on for the creators, and \"Users can create Fabric items\" is on if you include non-Power BI items. Workspace type. The workspace is a Pro, Fabric trial, Premium capacity or Fabric capacity workspace; notebooks and Real-Time Dashboards need a Fabric or trial capacity workspace. Creator license. Power BI Pro or Premium Per User, because org apps are Power BI items. Role. Contributor"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html#outcomes","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"What you will be able to do","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Choose between an org app, a workspace app, direct sharing, embedding and a template app, and justify the choice. Configure the tenant, capacity, license and role layers so creation and distribution do not fail late. Design an app around its audience, then build, brand and test it. Include each supported item type and know exactly where access is, and is not, handled for you. Share through audiences and groups, revok"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html#map","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"Map of this playbook","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"02 Concepts and choosing an app model Anatomy, live references, access propagation and a decision table against the alternatives. Concepts Decisions 03 Prerequisites and governance Tenant settings, workspace types, licenses, roles and a pre-flight checklist. Admin Checklist 04 Build and customize an org app Audience-first design, creation, navigation, overview page, branding, preview and save. Step by step 05 Integra"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html#related","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"How this fits with other playbooks","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Deployment Pipelines and Promotion and GitHub Integration and Branching describe the mechanics that the ALM section of this playbook applies to org apps. Power BI Report Development Standards and Paginated Report Migration cover the content you put inside an app. Tenant Settings Reference is the place to cross-check the admin settings named here. Power BI to Fabric Migration covers moving existing Power BI content, i"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/index.html#refs","t":"Organizational apps in Microsoft Fabric","s":"References","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Get started with org apps CI/CD for org apps in Fabric Publish a workspace app in Power BI Understand Microsoft Fabric licenses and capacity Microsoft Fabric tenant settings"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"Concepts and choosing an app model","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Playbook 11 · Organizational Apps Concepts and choosing an app model Before you build anything, understand what an org app actually stores, why saved changes are live, and how access moves from the app to the items and models underneath it. Then use the decision table to check that an org app is the right distribution model at all."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html#anatomy","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"Anatomy of an org app","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"An org app is a thin layer. It does not hold copies of reports or notebooks; it holds a structure, an appearance and a set of audiences, and it points at items that live in the same workspace. Org app item A Fabric item created with New > Org app . It carries the app's name, description, endorsement, icon, theme color and navigation behavior. Elements The ordered entries in the app navigation: an overview page (at mo"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html#live-references","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"Live references, not snapshots","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"A workspace app is a versioned copy: you edit content in the workspace, and consumers see nothing new until you select Update app . An org app has no such step. Included items remain the original items in the workspace, and after you select Save on the app, changes are immediately visible to the people who have access. What changes. The staging step disappears, and with it the safety net. In an org app the only way t"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html#access-propagation","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"How access propagates","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"The access model is the main reason to use org apps, and also the main source of surprises. When a person is given access to an app, the platform grants, at a minimum, Read on: the org app item itself; each included Power BI report and paginated report, and the underlying semantic model of each, whether the model is in the same workspace or another one; each included notebook, Map and Real-Time Dashboard; each includ"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html#comparison","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"Org apps compared with the alternatives","p":"Organizational Apps","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html#vs-workspace-apps","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"Org apps versus workspace apps","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Capability Org app Workspace app Apps per workspace Many One; audiences control who sees what Versioning and visibility of changes No versioned copies; saved changes are visible at once Versioned; users see changes after an app update Opening included items outside the app Possible with a direct link if the user has app access Not possible without separate access to the source item Semantic model access for paginated"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html#gaps","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"Gaps to plan around","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Some workspace app behavior has no org app equivalent. Check this list against your existing apps before you promise parity. Area Org app behavior Classic dashboards and scorecards Not eligible items; a report-only app is the way forward Report toolbar Not every workspace app toolbar capability is supported; comments are an example Email subscriptions Supported for Power BI reports only, tied to the report rather tha"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html#decision-table","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"Decision table","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Option Best for Content scope Access and licensing Watch out Org app Curated, branded, multi-audience experiences inside your tenant Reports, paginated reports, notebooks, Real-Time Dashboards, Maps, links, Fabric Apps (preview); same workspace only Item access propagated from the app and audiences; consumer license follows the capacity rules Live references; no classic dashboards or scorecards Workspace app Existing"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html#migration","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"Migrating a workspace app to an org app","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"There is no migration tool and nothing converts automatically. Because audiences are available only in new org apps, you recreate the app rather than upgrade it. Plan it as a parallel run. Inventory the existing app List audiences, the Entra groups behind them, included content, links, sections, branding and whether the app is installed automatically. Flag dashboards and scorecards, which cannot be included in an org"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/concepts.html#refs","t":"Concepts and choosing an app model","s":"References","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Get started with org apps Publish a workspace app in Power BI What are Power BI template apps? Power BI usage scenarios: Embed for your organization Embed a report in a secure portal or website What is Fabric Embedded? What is Fabric Apps (preview)? Understand Microsoft Fabric licenses and capacity"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/prerequisites.html","t":"Prerequisites and governance","s":"Prerequisites and governance","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Playbook 11 · Organizational Apps Prerequisites and governance Creating and distributing an org app depends on five layers, from the tenant down to the permissions on each included item. A gap in any layer fails late and confusingly, usually when someone tries to save or share. This page names each layer, who owns it, and how to prove it works before you build."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/prerequisites.html#layers","t":"Prerequisites and governance","s":"The five layers","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Control narrows as you go down: a Fabric administrator opens the platform-wide gates, a capacity administrator provides the capacity, a workspace administrator decides who builds, and the people who own the items decide who can reshare them. That layering supports delegated administration. Central IT sets policy; domain teams create and run their own apps inside it. Governance funnel. Layers 1 to 4 are configuration "},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/prerequisites.html#tenant","t":"Prerequisites and governance","s":"Tenant settings","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"The settings below are the ones that matter for org apps. Names are given as Microsoft Learn documents them and can change, so confirm each one in your admin portal. Setting (documented label) What it does Notes Users can discover and create org apps (preview) Lets users create org apps as items; users with access can view them. If it is turned off, org app items are hidden until it is turned on again. Workspace apps"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/prerequisites.html#capacity","t":"Prerequisites and governance","s":"Capacity and workspace type","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Org apps live in workspaces, and the workspace has to be one of four types: Pro, Fabric trial, Premium capacity or Fabric capacity. Learn calls this setting the workspace type ; older material calls it the license mode. You change it in Workspace settings , on the License info tab or the Premium tab depending on your tenant, with Edit . Workspace type Power BI items Non-Power BI Fabric items Notes Pro Yes No Reports "},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/prerequisites.html#licenses","t":"Prerequisites and governance","s":"Per-user licenses","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Licensing has two sides. Creators need a license that lets them manage Power BI items. Consumers need a license that depends on the capacity behind the workspace. Person License needed Why Creator or editor Power BI Pro or Premium Per User Org apps are Power BI items, and a Free license cannot create, update, delete or manage Power BI items outside My workspace Consumer, workspace on F64 or larger, P or trial capacit"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/prerequisites.html#roles","t":"Prerequisites and governance","s":"Workspace roles and the write-and-reshare rule","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Even with the right tenant setting, capacity and license, what a person can do in a given workspace depends on their role. Users with the Admin, Member or Contributor role can create org app items; a Viewer cannot create items. Creating the item is the easy part. Anything that changes who has access is stricter. When you add or remove an item, change audience visibility or otherwise affect access management, you need"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/prerequisites.html#preflight","t":"Prerequisites and governance","s":"Pre-flight checklist","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Level Requirement Required state Who configures Tenant Org app setting Enabled for the creators' security group Fabric administrator Tenant Fabric items setting Enabled for authors of notebooks, Real-Time Dashboards and Maps Fabric administrator Tenant Real-Time Dashboard and Fabric App settings Enabled only if you include those item types Fabric administrator Capacity Capacity for the workspace F SKU (or P or trial)"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/prerequisites.html#smoke-test","t":"Prerequisites and governance","s":"Smoke test before you invest","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"An hour spent on a throwaway app is cheaper than discovering a gap while stakeholders wait. Use a test workspace that matches the production workspace type. Create a minimal app As a Member, create an org app with one report whose semantic model lives in a different workspace. Add a notebook or Real-Time Dashboard if your design needs them. Add one audience member Share the app with a free-licensed test account that "},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/prerequisites.html#refs","t":"Prerequisites and governance","s":"References","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Get started with org apps Understand Microsoft Fabric licenses and capacity Microsoft Fabric tenant settings Tenant settings index Enable Microsoft Fabric for your organization Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric Create a Real-Time Dashboard What is Fabric Apps (preview)?"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/build.html","t":"Build and customize an org app","s":"Build and customize an org app","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Playbook 11 · Organizational Apps Build and customize an org app An org app is a curated, branded data product, not a shared folder. This page starts with the design questions that decide what goes in, then walks through creation, content, navigation, the overview page, branding, preview and save, and ends with a table of what takes effect when."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/build.html#design","t":"Build and customize an org app","s":"Design the app before you build it","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Because one workspace can host many apps, the useful question is no longer \"which reports should we share?\" but \"which experience does this audience need?\" Answer these first; they decide the items, the audiences and the roles. Question Why it matters Example answer Who is the audience and what decision do they make? Defines the audience and keeps the app small Executives running a weekly revenue review Which items a"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/build.html#flow","t":"Build and customize an org app","s":"The build flow","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Build flow. Adding items and saving are the two moments that change who has access, so they are where permission errors surface. Preview is not a substitute for testing as an audience member."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/build.html#steps","t":"Build and customize an org app","s":"Step by step","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Create the item Open the workspace that holds the content and select New > Org app . Enter the consumer-facing name and select Create . The app editor opens. If your tenant has Fabric trials enabled and the workspace is not on a capacity, you are prompted to start a trial. Add content Select Add content , choose items from the workspace in the picker, and select Add to app . Only supported item types appear (see Inte"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/build.html#navigation","t":"Build and customize an org app","s":"Navigation, overview page and links","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"A flat list of fifteen reports is not an app. Group elements into sections that match how the audience thinks, put the page they need first, and give each element a name they would use in conversation. Landing page. Whatever is first in the navigation opens first. If you add an overview page, put it first when you want a welcome and an index; otherwise put the report that answers the audience's main question first. O"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/build.html#branding","t":"Build and customize an org app","s":"Branding and behavior","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"The Customize pane controls the visual identity and how the navigation behaves. App image Upload a .jpg or .png of up to 45 KB. Reset to default removes it. On the previous Apps page, Gallery view shows the default org app icon rather than the custom one. Theme color Pick any color with the color picker or enter a hexadecimal or RGB value. The definition stores a small palette: background, foreground and hover, selec"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/build.html#preview-save","t":"Build and customize an org app","s":"Preview, save and what takes effect when","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Preview app shows the app as a consumer would see it, but it is rendered for users who have workspace access, so it reflects the broader access that a workspace role gives, across all audiences. It is a layout check, not an access test. Row-level security is not a good test here either, because workspace Admins, Members and Contributors hold write permission on the models, and write permission bypasses model-level da"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/build.html#quality","t":"Build and customize an org app","s":"Quality bar before you share","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"The app name and every navigation label make sense to the audience without explanation. The landing element answers the audience's main question or explains what the app contains. The icon is under 45 KB and the theme passes a contrast check in all four states. Each audience has been tested with an account that belongs to that audience only, not with a workspace Admin. Every report in the app opens for a test consume"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/build.html#refs","t":"Build and customize an org app","s":"References","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Get started with org apps CI/CD for org apps in Fabric (definition properties) Integrate Direct Lake security (row-level security and write permission) Navigate the Fabric home page (Focus mode)"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Playbook 11 · Organizational Apps Integrating Fabric and Power BI items An org app is only as useful as the items you can put in it and only as safe as your understanding of what each item type does to access. This page covers every supported item type, what consumers experience, what the app grants automatically, and what you still have to grant yourself."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#supported","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"What you can include","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Items come from the app's own workspace and are picked with Add content . The documentation lists Power BI reports, paginated reports, Fabric notebooks, Real-Time Dashboards, Maps and Fabric Apps (preview), plus navigation elements you add yourself: overview, sections and links. Item type Documented status Consumer experience Granted by the app Still your job Power BI report Supported Fully interactive report Read on"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#reports","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Power BI reports and their models","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Reports are the common case and the simplest: add them from the picker. When a user is given access to the app, the platform grants Read on the report and on the semantic model behind it. The part that used to be painful is cross-workspace models. If a report in workspace A is built on a model in workspace B, sharing the app from workspace A grants the user access to the model in B, provided the app's author has the "},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#direct-lake","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Direct Lake models: what consumers need","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"A common assumption is that consumers always need ReadData on the lakehouse. That is only one of several cases. The identity the model uses to read the data decides what consumers need, and Direct Lake uses single sign-on (the consumer's identity) by default. Direct Lake configuration Identity used What a consumer needs beyond the app's grants On SQL analytics endpoint, single sign-on (default) The consumer Read on t"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#chained","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Chained and composite models, and the paginated visual exception","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Chained models. The documentation describes propagation to the semantic model behind each included report. If a model is itself built on other models, treat the upstream models as dependencies, grant or verify access to them explicitly, and test with a consumer account. Reports that embed a paginated report visual. The report depends on a paginated report item that the app does not grant. Include the paginated report"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#paginated","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Paginated reports","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Paginated reports are the pixel-perfect output for statements, order confirmations and other operational documents. You build them with Power BI Report Builder, typically against a semantic model or a lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint. They are a supported item type in an org app: they appear in the item picker, and access to the report grants access to its semantic model automatically, which workspace apps did not do"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#notebooks","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Notebooks","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"A notebook in an app is a document: markdown, code, tables, charts and widgets that explain a method or tell a story next to a report. Consumers see it in a read-only mode. Interactive outputs such as rich dataframe previews, built-in charts and outputs from libraries like Plotly remain interactive, but anything a consumer changes is not saved and resets when the page is refreshed. Read, not run. The documentation li"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#realtime","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Real-Time Dashboards","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"A Real-Time Dashboard turns streaming or near-real-time data into KQL-driven tiles, typically over a KQL database in an eventhouse fed by an eventstream. In an app it lets one navigation hold both strategic analysis (reports) and operational monitoring (live tiles) for use cases such as store traffic, device telemetry or site activity. Prerequisites: a workspace on a Fabric-enabled capacity, a KQL database with data,"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#rtd-identity","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Who the dashboard queries as","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"The app grants Read on the dashboard item. Whether tiles show data depends on a separate setting: the identity model for the data source. Two identity models. Pass-through enforces source security per viewer; the editor's identity gives every viewer the same view of the data without direct database access. Dashboard permission Data source model Result for a viewer View Pass-through identity Opens the dashboard; sees "},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#maps","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Maps","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"A Fabric map is an item that visualizes real-time event data from an eventhouse and historical or static spatial data from a lakehouse as layers. In an app it gives an operations audience geographic context next to reports. The app grants Read on the map item; viewers also need access to the data sources behind its layers, so apply the same reasoning as for Real-Time Dashboards and test with a consumer account. Check"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#fabric-apps","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Fabric Apps (preview)","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"A Fabric App is a different artifact from an org app: a hosted application with a data model defined in TypeScript, a generated GraphQL API and authentication through Fabric single sign-on. You can include one in an org app so it sits in the same navigation as your reports. Adding a user to the org app grants Read and Execute on the Fabric App and removing the user revokes them. The app does not manage access to the "},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#combining","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Combining item types well","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Pattern Elements Why it works Watch out Insight with method Overview, report, notebook explaining the calculations Builds trust in numbers without a separate document Notebook outputs must be safe for the whole audience Strategic plus operational KPI report, Real-Time Dashboard, map One place for the weekly review and the live view Identity model of the live items; capacity load Statements and analysis Interactive re"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#access-checks","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"Access you must still verify","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Every Direct Lake model has a documented identity mode, and consumers hold exactly the data-layer rights that mode requires. Every Real-Time Dashboard and map has a decided identity model and a named owner for any cloud connection it uses. Reports that contain a paginated report visual have the paginated report included or separately granted. Fabric Apps have their related items granted directly. Notebook outputs hav"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/content.html#refs","t":"Integrating Fabric and Power BI items","s":"References","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Get started with org apps Integrate Direct Lake security How to integrate notebooks with Org app Create a Real-Time Dashboard Real-Time Dashboard permissions What is Fabric Maps? What is Fabric Apps (preview)? Email subscriptions for reports"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Playbook 11 · Organizational Apps Access, ALM and lifecycle Once an app is built, three things decide whether it stays trustworthy: who has access and why, how changes are reviewed and promoted, and how the app is retired. This page covers sharing and audiences, Git integration and deployment pipelines, and the draft-to-deleted lifecycle."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#sharing","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Sharing and managing access","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Sharing an org app is item sharing with a propagation layer on top. After you save the app, the Share button gives you four options. Option What it does Use it for Copy link to this app Copies the app link; the recipient lands on the first element. Recipients must already have access. Telling existing users where the app is Link to this app page Copies a link to the element you are viewing, with the same access requi"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#reshare","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Reshare permission","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"When you add a person, you can also grant reshare permission for the audiences you select, which lets them pass the app on. Users with share permission on the org app item can share it; a consumer without it can only send links to people who already have access. Reshare is convenient and easy to over-grant. Keep it to named group owners, record who holds it, and review it when you review membership."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#revoke","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Revoking access","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"From the user or group menu on the permissions page you have two choices. Manage access changes which audiences the user belongs to and their reshare rights. Remove access removes the org-app-derived access to the app, the included items and the underlying semantic models, and Read and Execute on an included Fabric App. Access granted independently of the app remains. Revocation removes one input. The app withdraws o"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#audiences","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Audiences in practice","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Audiences let one app serve several groups. They are child items of the org app, and you can manage their access without opening the app in edit mode. Audiences are available only in new org apps: if your app predates them, create a new app. Open Manage audiences in the editor. From there you create audiences with New audience and, from each audience tab's menu, rename, move the tab left or right, duplicate, change t"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#audience-design","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Mapping audiences to groups","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Assign Entra security groups, not individuals, to audiences, and keep one group per audience per environment so test and production membership never mix. Audience Entra group (production) Sees Group owner Executives sg-sales-exec-prd Overview, revenue report, methodology notebook Sales analytics lead Managers sg-sales-managers-prd Overview, regional report, live dashboard, notebook Regional operations lead Analysts s"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#alm","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Application lifecycle management","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"An org app is a workspace item, so it can be versioned in Git and promoted through deployment pipelines like any other. That matters more here than for most items, because saved changes are live. The practical pattern is: build in a development workspace, review the change in Git, promote through test to production with a pipeline, and assign access separately in each stage. Verify against Microsoft Learn Org app ite"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#lifecycle","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"The lifecycle","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Lifecycle states. There is no separate \"unpublish\" step in the org app model: removing access withdraws the app, and deleting the item retires it. Each editing session is another draft that ends with Save."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#git","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Git integration","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"When a workspace is connected to a Git repository, the org app is part of the synchronization. Supported providers are Azure DevOps, GitHub and GitHub Enterprise (cloud-based only). Connect from workspace settings and use the source control panel to see the branch, the status of each item and the time of the last sync. Each org app is stored as a set of artifacts: one for the app, which controls content and appearanc"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#git-review","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Reviewing definition changes","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"The definition is human-readable, so review it like code. Route changes through a pull request, ideally with owners for the app folder (see Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS ), and branch as described in GitHub Integration and Branching . hasAccessToHiddenContent did not flip to true without an explicit approval, because it widens access across the whole app. Every elementReferences entry in each audience st"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#pipelines","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Deployment pipelines","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Deployment pipelines promote the app and its content through stages. A pipeline has two to ten stages, three by default (development, test, production). Items in adjacent stages are paired , and a deployment overwrites the paired item in the target stage. Unpaired items with the same name and type are not overwritten; a duplicate is created. Pair stages before the first deployment. Promotion path. Git is connected to"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#stage-access","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Access per environment","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Stage Audience members Purpose Who can edit Development Developers and test accounts Build and review layout, navigation and audience visibility Developers (Contributor), app owner (Member) Test UAT group and one test account per audience Validate access, content and performance as a real consumer Release owner only Production Business groups Serve the audience App owner and release owner; no direct item edits"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#updating","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Updating an org app","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Users with Admin or Member permissions can edit an app when the update adds or removes items or otherwise changes access management. Contributors can edit when the update does not affect access management. Start from the workspace list ( ... > Edit ) or from the viewing experience ( Edit app ), change what you need and select Save . Kind of change Where Visible to consumers Edit an included report or notebook The ite"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#retiring","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"Unpublishing and deleting","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"In the org app model, unpublishing is deleting the org app item: select ... > Delete on the item in the workspace. Users lose the access the app gave them to the included items and to the underlying models of reports and paginated reports, and Read and Execute on an included Fabric App. Included items stay in the workspace, and access granted independently of the app stays in place. Plan for deletion as irreversible "},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/access-alm.html#refs","t":"Access, ALM and lifecycle","s":"References","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Get started with org apps CI/CD for org apps in Fabric Overview of Fabric Git integration Overview of Fabric deployment pipelines Introduction to CI/CD in Microsoft Fabric fabric-cicd documentation"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Playbook 11 · Organizational Apps Practices, pitfalls and roadmap The practices that keep an org app estate manageable, an operating model with named owners, a symptom-to-fix table for the problems you will actually be asked about, and a method for tracking how the feature set changes, because in this area the documentation moves faster than any playbook."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#practices","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Best practices","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Practice Why it matters How Isolate development from production Saved changes to a report are live in every app that includes it Separate DEV_ , TST_ and PRD_ workspaces and a deployment pipeline; no direct edits in production ( ALM ) Design for the audience One monolithic app forces everyone through content that is not theirs Several small apps from one workspace, or one app with audiences; sections, an overview pag"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#operating-model","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Operating model","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Role Responsibility for org apps Fabric administrator Org app and related tenant settings, group scoping, terminology and label checks after changes Capacity administrator Capacity size and assignment; load from live dashboards and concurrent consumers Workspace administrator Workspace type, roles (Contributor for developers, Member for app owners), pipeline pairing App owner Content and navigation, audiences, releas"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#troubleshooting","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Troubleshooting: symptom, cause, fix","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix No Org app entry under New The org app tenant setting is off for the user, the workspace type is unsupported, or the user is a Viewer Check the setting and its group scope, the workspace type and the role ( Prerequisites ) Save fails with a permissions message Missing write and reshare on the app, an included item or a semantic model, including a model in another workspace Have a Member or Ad"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#consumer-surfaces","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Where consumers meet the app","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Surface Behavior Apps page Lists org apps, workspace apps and Fabric Apps. On the new page they are labeled Org app , Workspace app and App . Reachable from both the Power BI and Fabric experiences. Home Appears under Recents once viewed and under Favorites once favorited. The separate My apps list does not show org apps. OneLake catalog Org apps the user can access appear like other items. Email on share Recipients "},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#roadmap","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Tracking capability changes","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Org apps have changed quickly and will keep changing. Statements about what is \"coming\" age badly and should not drive design. Some capabilities that were once described as upcoming, audiences and paginated report support among them, are now documented in the product articles, while others still carry limitations, such as audience tabs on mobile. The durable skill is not memorizing a feature list; it is having a chea"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#checklist","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Final checklist","p":"Organizational Apps","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#check-before-build","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Before you build","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"An org app is the right model for this audience (decision table in Concepts ). Tenant setting, capacity, workspace type, roles and licenses pass the pre-flight checklist and a smoke test. Audiences, Entra groups and group owners are defined for each environment. The workspace layout separates development, test and production, with a pipeline planned."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#check-before-share","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Before the first share","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Navigation labels, overview page, icon and theme are reviewed; contrast is checked. Every item opens for an account in each audience, including models in other workspaces. Direct Lake identity and Real-Time Dashboard identity are decided and tested. Notebook outputs and links are reviewed for sensitive content. Access to hidden content is off unless a documented decision says otherwise."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#check-before-promotion","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"Before each promotion","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"The definition diff was reviewed, including hasAccessToHiddenContent and audience references. The app and its included items deploy in the same run, under a user account with the needed rights. Audience membership is assigned and verified in the target stage. A rollback is possible from Git or the previous stage."},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#check-operations","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"In operation","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Manage access, reshare holders and models' Access via related items are reviewed on a schedule. The capability register is re-checked on a regular cadence and after announcements. There is a documented retirement path: capture the definition, withdraw access, delete, clean up. Key takeaways Run each org app as a product: an owner, a release path, group-based access and scheduled reviews. Most support tickets trace ba"},{"u":"playbooks/11-org-apps/practices.html#refs","t":"Practices, pitfalls and roadmap","s":"References","p":"Organizational Apps","x":"Get started with org apps CI/CD for org apps in Fabric Workspace apps in Power BI Add the Power BI app to Microsoft Teams Email subscriptions for reports Understand Microsoft Fabric licenses and capacity What's new in Microsoft Fabric Microsoft Fabric roadmap"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/index.html","t":"Power BI Report Development Standards","s":"Power BI Report Development Standards","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Playbook 12 · Analytics Power BI Report Development Standards A working standard for building semantic models and reports that stay fast, readable and safe to change. It assumes a Fabric data platform underneath: a curated Gold layer feeds a small number of shared semantic models, and many thin reports sit on top of them. Level Intermediate Audience Power BI developers, BI leads, reviewers Reading time 75 min"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/index.html#purpose","t":"Power BI Report Development Standards","s":"What this playbook is for","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Power BI makes it easy to produce a working report in an afternoon. The cost shows up later: every analyst ships their own copy of the model, measures are redefined in each report, a column rename breaks forty visuals, and nobody can tell which \"Sales\" number is the right one. Most of that is avoidable with a handful of agreed settings and conventions applied before the first visual is drawn. This playbook sets those"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/index.html#when-to-use","t":"Power BI Report Development Standards","s":"When to use it","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Apply it when You build or review semantic models and reports that other people will maintain. Several reports should share one governed model instead of each owning a copy. You are moving reports onto Fabric and want the model design to be right the second time. Report performance or \"which number is correct\" disputes are already costing time. Look elsewhere when You need operational, print-ready output. That is a p"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/index.html#prerequisites","t":"Power BI Report Development Standards","s":"Prerequisites","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"An up-to-date Power BI Desktop build and access to a Fabric workspace on a capacity. Direct Lake models require a Fabric capacity. Gold-layer tables that already look like facts and dimensions. The standards below cannot repair a flat, denormalized source; they only avoid making it worse. A workspace layout that separates models from reports. The house naming convention ( <ENV>_<DOMAIN>_CNSM for models, <ENV>_<DOMAIN"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/index.html#layers","t":"Power BI Report Development Standards","s":"How the layers fit together","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Three layers carry the standards, and each has a single accountable owner. The data team owns the Gold tables, a model owner owns the shared semantic model, and report authors own thin reports that connect to it. A report author who needs a new column does not patch it into the report; the request goes to the model owner, who adds it once for everyone. One model, many thin reports. The model owner holds the tables, r"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/index.html#at-a-glance","t":"Power BI Report Development Standards","s":"The standards at a glance","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"If you read nothing else, these are the rules that prevent the most rework. Each is explained, with its trade-offs, on the page named in the last column. # Rule What goes wrong without it Where 1 Turn off auto date/time and use one marked date table Hidden date tables per date column inflate the model and cannot be reused or reached from other tools Model 2 Turn off relationship autodetection; define every relationsh"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/index.html#how-to-read","t":"Power BI Report Development Standards","s":"How to read this playbook","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"02 Semantic model standards Storage mode decision, the two Desktop settings to change on day one, star schema, hiding and documenting fields, PBIP and TMDL. Model owners 20 min 03 Report design and performance Layout, accessibility, query reduction and a repeatable Performance Analyzer workflow with a triage path. Report authors 15 min 04 Naming, DAX and measures tables The naming rules with a worked example, DAX pat"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/index.html#refs","t":"Power BI Report Development Standards","s":"References","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Optimization guide for Power BI Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI Direct Lake overview Connect to semantic models in Power BI Power BI Desktop projects (PBIP)"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Semantic model standards","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Playbook 12 · Power BI Report Development Standards Semantic model standards Everything a report inherits is decided here. Pick the storage mode deliberately, change two Desktop defaults before loading any data, shape the tables as a star, hide and document what authors should not guess at, and keep the model in a project format that Git can diff."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#storage-mode","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Choose the storage mode first","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Storage mode is a property of each table, and the choice drives refresh cost, latency, which features you can use, and how you secure the data. On a Fabric platform with a curated Gold layer there are five realistic options. Decide before you build, because switching later changes how measures behave and what has to be re-tested. Storage mode decision path. Illustrative. Read the first question as \"did the data team "},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#direct-lake-notes","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Direct Lake details that change design","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Refresh means framing. A Direct Lake refresh re-points the model at the Delta table's current files; it does not copy data. Table maintenance (compaction, V-Order, sensible row group sizes) therefore decides query speed. See Delta Lake partitioning and the Microsoft guidance on table optimization. Guardrails depend on the capacity SKU (files, row groups and rows per table, and model size). Queries are evaluated per q"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#settings","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Two Desktop settings to change before you load data","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Power BI Desktop ships with conveniences that are helpful in a ten-minute demo and harmful in a shared model. Both are options, not model properties, so a new file silently picks them up again unless you change the global default. Change them in File > Options and settings > Options , on the Global page (affects new files) and on the Current File page (affects the open model)."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#auto-date-time","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Disable auto date/time","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"With the option on, Desktop creates a hidden calculated date table for every Import date column that is not on the many side of a relationship. Each table is a CALENDAR -based table with Year, Quarter, Month and Day columns, joined to its source column. A model with a dozen date columns carries a dozen hidden calendars, none of which you can see, edit, mark, or reference from non-Power BI tools or Analyze in Excel. C"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#auto-relationships","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Disable relationship autodetection","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"On load, Desktop compares column names and may create relationships on its own. In a narrow tutorial model that is convenient. In a model with surrogate keys, role-playing dimensions and a few similarly named columns, it produces relationships you did not choose, and a wrong relationship returns numbers that look reasonable. Three options govern this under Options > Data Load > Relationships : Option Default Standard"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#star-schema","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Model as a star","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Fact tables hold events or measurements at one grain, narrow and tall, with integer keys and numeric columns. Dimension tables hold descriptive attributes, wide and short. Every dimension relates to the fact with a one-to-many relationship that filters from the dimension. This is the shape VertiPaq compresses best, the shape DAX assumes, and the shape that keeps filter paths unambiguous. Flat versus star. The star st"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#relationship-rules","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Relationship rules","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"One-to-many, single direction, from the dimension. Bidirectional filtering is the exception. It adds ambiguity, can slow queries, and Microsoft's relationship guidance recommends it only when needed. If you need a filter to flow from a fact back to a dimension, a measure with CROSSFILTER or a purpose-built bridge is usually safer than a model-wide switch. Many-to-many relationships sparingly. They are limited relatio"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#date-table","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"One date table, marked","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Time intelligence functions need a contiguous date table with one row per day and a column of unique dates. Build it once in the Gold layer (or as a single calculated table if the model is Import), mark it with Table tools > Mark as date table and choose the date column, hide the technical columns, and give it columns for the periods your organization reports on (fiscal year, fiscal period, week). For a fiscal calend"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#expose","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Decide what authors see","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"An author who opens the model sees every field you leave visible and has to guess which is right. The model owner's job is to remove the guesses. Property Standard Why Hidden Hide surrogate and foreign keys, sort-order helpers, technical columns, and numeric columns that a measure already aggregates. Stops authors from summing a key or building visuals on implicit aggregations. Hiding is a usability control, not secu"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#model-features","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Perspectives, calculation groups and field parameters","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Feature Use it for Limits to know Perspectives Trimmed views of a large model for a subject area or for personalized visuals. They are a viewing convenience, not security. Desktop has no graphical editor for them, so create them in TMDL view or an external tool. Calculation groups Reusing one calculation across many measures: time intelligence (current, YTD, prior year), currency conversion, unit switching. Create th"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#projects","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Keep the model in a project format","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"A PBIX file is a single binary that Git cannot diff or merge. A Power BI project (PBIP) saves the semantic model and the report as separate folders of text files. The semantic model is stored as TMDL, one file per table plus files for relationships, expressions and the model; the report definition can be stored in the enhanced report format, PBIR, with one file per page, visual and bookmark. Three practices make the "},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#checklist","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"Semantic model checklist","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Storage mode chosen on purpose and recorded in the model description. Auto date/time off (global and current file); one date table marked. Relationship autodetection off; every relationship reviewed, one-to-many, single direction unless justified. Star shape: no flat tables, no snowflakes without a reason, Unknown members present in dimensions. Keys and helper columns hidden; summarization set to none where needed. E"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/semantic-model.html#refs","t":"Semantic model standards","s":"References","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Auto date/time in Power BI Desktop Create and manage relationships in Power BI Desktop Model relationships in Power BI Desktop Bi-directional relationship guidance Design guidance for date tables Direct Lake overview Power BI semantic models in Fabric Data Warehouse Create calculation groups in Power BI Use field parameters in Power BI reports Use TMDL view in Power BI Tabular Model Definition Language (TMDL) Power B"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Report design and performance","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Playbook 12 · Power BI Report Development Standards Report design and performance A report is a set of queries with a layout on top. Design it so the number of queries is small and predictable, the layout is consistent across the estate, and anyone can use it. When it is still slow, measure which of four time buckets is responsible before changing anything."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#layout","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Layout, theme and consistency","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Consistency is the cheapest quality control a report team has. A reader who learns where filters, headline numbers and detail live in one report can use the next one without help, and a reviewer can check a report against a template instead of against taste. Standard Rule Reason One theme Ship a single theme file (colors, fonts, visual defaults) and apply it to every report with View > Themes > Browse for themes . Ke"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#accessibility","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Accessibility","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Power BI gives you the features; accessibility is the author's responsibility. Treat the items below as acceptance criteria, not polish. Text and data labels have at least 4.5:1 contrast against their background; use fewer, higher-contrast colors rather than many pale ones. Color is never the only carrier of meaning: add a label, icon or pattern. Every non-decorative visual has alt text that states the insight to tak"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#mobile","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Mobile layout","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Mobile is a separate canvas, not a shrunk copy. Open it from the View ribbon ( Mobile layout ), then either let Desktop auto-create a layout and curate it, or drag visuals in yourself. Auto-create reads the page left to right from the top, so a page that depends on a left-hand navigation column translates poorly. Include only the visuals people actually need on a phone. A mobile page that mirrors a ten-visual desktop"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#interaction","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Bookmarks, drillthrough and tooltips","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Drillthrough sends a reader from a summary to a detail page filtered to what they selected. It is the default way to give detail without putting detail on the first page. Field parameters cannot be the linked field of a drillthrough page; use the underlying fields. Report page tooltips give a small contextual visual on hover. Keep them light: a tooltip page runs queries too. Bookmarks capture the state of a page, inc"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#visual-budget","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Set a visual budget","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Every visual on a page issues at least one query when the page loads and again when a filter changes. Microsoft's optimization guidance is plain: limit the visuals on a page to what is necessary and use drillthrough pages and report tooltips for the rest. It publishes no numeric limit. A number you may have been given Some internal guidance quotes a figure such as \"10 to 20 fields per visual\" as a Microsoft recommend"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#query-reduction","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Query reduction settings","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Query reduction trades some immediacy for fewer queries. It matters most for DirectQuery sources and for pages with many slicers. For a fast Import or Direct Lake model on a modest page, the default interactive behavior is usually right, so apply it where Performance Analyzer shows a problem. Open File > Options and settings > Options > Current File > Query reduction . The settings are stored per report: Group Settin"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#performance-analyzer","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Measure with Performance Analyzer","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Do not tune by intuition. Performance Analyzer records, for each visual, how long each stage took, so you can see whether the DAX, the visual or the queue is the problem. Open it In Desktop, Optimize > Performance analyzer (also on the View ribbon). When editing a report in the service, View > Performance analyzer . Record a real interaction Select Start recording , then do what a reader does: open the page, change a"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#optimization","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Optimization levers","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Most slow reports are slow for one of a handful of reasons. The table orders the levers by how often they pay off on a Fabric platform. Lever Applies to What to do Trade-off Pre-aggregate upstream All modes; the main option for Direct Lake Build summary tables in the Gold layer for the grains reports actually use, and point headline visuals at them. More tables to maintain; define the grain and owner for each. User-d"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#troubleshooting","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Symptom Likely cause What to do Each visual is fast alone but the page feels slow Too many visuals queue behind each other Reduce the visual count, consolidate cards, move detail to drillthrough, add Apply all slicers. Changing a slicer freezes the page Instant slicers with many dependent visuals Apply all slicers button; consider a single Apply button for the filter pane. A Direct Lake report became slow after a dat"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#checklist","t":"Report design and performance","s":"Report design checklist","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Theme applied from the shared file; page grid and slicer placement match the template. Alt text on every non-decorative visual; contrast and keyboard navigation checked. Mobile layout curated if the report is used on phones. Visual budget respected; detail behind drillthrough or tooltip pages; tables filtered by default. Query reduction settings chosen and recorded; Apply all slicers present on slicer-heavy pages. Pe"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/report-design.html#refs","t":"Report design and performance","s":"References","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Optimization guide for Power BI Use Performance Analyzer to examine report performance Optimize ribbon in Power BI Desktop Create Apply all and Clear all slicers buttons Design Power BI reports for accessibility Mobile layout view Best practices for mobile-optimized reports Data reduction techniques for Import modeling DirectQuery model guidance in Power BI Desktop Direct Lake overview External tools in Power BI Desk"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Playbook 12 · Power BI Report Development Standards Naming, DAX and measures tables Names are an interface: reports, other developers and Copilot all depend on them, and renaming later breaks things. This page fixes the conventions for measures, columns and tables, the DAX patterns that keep models fast and readable, and the structure that stops measures from scattering."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#principles","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"General naming principles","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"The naming rules below are house conventions, not Microsoft requirements. They are chosen so that a name tells a report author what a field is, where it comes from and whether it is shared. Be descriptive. The name states what the measure or column does. Total Sales beats Measure 1 ; Microsoft's own guidance asks for descriptive table, column and measure names because they are what report authors and other consumers "},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#measure-naming","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Report measures","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Measures defined in the shared semantic model are the governed definitions. A thin report may add a few report-level measures of its own for report-specific needs. To make the two distinguishable at a glance, every report-level measure carries the prefix Rpt . Practice Example Why Prefix report-level measures with Rpt Rpt Total Sales , Rpt Margin % Shows the logic lives in the report, not the model, and avoids a name"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#column-naming","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Calculated columns","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Calculated columns are the exception, not the pattern. They are computed at refresh, stored in memory, and are not available on Direct Lake on SQL endpoints. Create one only when the value must be a column (for example to slice or sort by) and cannot come from the Gold layer or Power Query. When you do, make it obvious. Practice Example Prefix with Col Col Region Group , Col Customer Segment Describe the transformati"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#table-naming","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Tables in semantic models","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Use clear, descriptive Pascal Case names, and be consistent across models. One rule needs more thought than the rest: prefix tables to show which model or domain they belong to when more than one model can appear in the same report. This is the situation in a composite model that combines several shared models, and also in a report that uses more than one."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#table-example","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Worked example","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Suppose the Sales model and the HR model each contain a date table and each is used by a report that combines them. Without a prefix, the report's field list contains two tables called Date , and authors cannot tell which relates to which fact. Because you cannot rename a table from a remote model inside a thin report, the prefix has to be in the model itself. Why prefix. The prefix is a cheap, permanent label for \"w"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#dax","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"DAX patterns that stay fast","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"These are the patterns that matter most in review. Format DAX consistently, with a DAX formatter (for example the Format query command in DAX query view), so diffs show logic changes rather than whitespace."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#dax-variables","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Use variables","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"A variable is evaluated once, outside the filter context its RETURN expression applies, and can be reused. That improves readability, avoids repeated evaluation, and removes most reasons to use EARLIER . Microsoft's example of reusing a prior-period calculation roughly halves the query time. You can also debug by temporarily returning a variable."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#dax-divide","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Use DIVIDE when the denominator can be zero","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"DIVIDE returns BLANK (or an alternate result you supply) instead of an error. Microsoft recommends it whenever the denominator is an expression that could be zero or BLANK, and the plain / operator when the denominator is a constant, because it is faster and cannot fail. Returning BLANK is usually the better behavior, because visuals drop groupings that summarize to BLANK instead of printing zeros."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#dax-iterators","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Avoid nested iterators and unnecessary row-by-row work","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Iterators ( SUMX , AVERAGEX , FILTER ) evaluate an expression per row. One iterator at the grain of the fact table is fine; an iterator inside an iterator multiplies the work. Better still, do the row arithmetic once in the Gold layer and use a plain aggregation."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#dax-filter","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Prefer Boolean filter arguments to FILTER","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"In an Import model, filtering a column with a simple Boolean expression is optimized by the storage engine. Passing FILTER over a whole table forces the engine to evaluate row by row. Keep FILTER for the cases a Boolean cannot express: conditions on a measure, on several columns, or with OR logic. KEEPFILTERS preserves the existing filters on the column the way FILTER over the table would."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#dax-time","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Time intelligence needs a marked date table","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Time intelligence functions read a contiguous date table. Mark it, relate it to every fact by date, and reference its date column. Do not use the date column of the fact table. Each base measure multiplied by each time variant becomes a long list. A calculation group removes the multiplication: define the time variants once and apply them to whichever measure is selected."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#dax-calc-groups","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Calculation groups for repeated variants","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Create the group in Model view with the Calculation group button. If Discourage implicit measures is off, Desktop prompts you to turn it on, because calculation groups require it. Once a calculation group exists, reports treat measure values as the variant data type, so give each item a format string (a dynamic format string expression) when the variant changes the unit, as a percentage does. Calculation item Express"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#dax-other","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Other rules","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Reference columns with the table name and measures without it : Sales[Net Amount] and [Sales Amount] . The convention makes the difference visible when you read a formula. Document logic in the description field of each measure and column, and organize measures with display folders. Centralize shared logic in the model. A measure used in two reports is defined once, in the model. Prefer measures to calculated columns"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#measures-table","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Create a measures table","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Keep every measure in a dedicated table that holds no data, so authors find measures in one place, the table sorts to the top of the field list, and the logic is not scattered across fact tables. Name it _Measures in the shared model, and Local Measures in a thin report that needs its own. The two names also make it obvious in a composite model which measures are shared and which are local. Where measures live. Share"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#measures-table-ui","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"In Power BI Desktop","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Create an empty table On the Home ribbon, select Enter data . Leave the grid empty (it has a single column named Column1 ), name the table _Measures (or Local Measures in a thin report) and select Load . The new table appears in the Data pane. Add measures to it Select the table, then Home > New measure (or Table tools in Table view). Enter the DAX, then set the format, display folder and description in the Propertie"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#measures-table-tmdl","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Alternative: with TMDL","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"For a repeatable result, script the table. In Power BI Desktop open TMDL view , paste the script and select Apply . The same text works as a tables/_Measures.tmdl file in a PBIP project. The table uses a calculated partition so no source data is involved, and the placeholder column is hidden. Confirm property names for your model TMDL property names depend on the model's compatibility level. Right-click a calculated "},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#checklist","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"Naming and DAX checklist","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Names are descriptive Pascal Case; abbreviations are only YoY , MTD , QTD , YTD . Report-level measures use Rpt ; calculated columns use Col ; shared measures have no prefix. No hard-coded years, people or temporary filters in names. Tables in any model that may be combined carry a short domain prefix, consistently. Every measure is in a measures table and has a description; display folders group them. Variables used"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/naming-dax.html#refs","t":"Naming, DAX and measures tables","s":"References","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Use variables to improve your DAX formulas DIVIDE function vs divide operator in DAX Avoid using FILTER as a filter argument in DAX Column and measure references in DAX Create calculation groups in Power BI Implement time-based calculations in Power BI Use TMDL view in Power BI Optimization guide for Power BI"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Playbook 12 · Power BI Report Development Standards Rebinding reports to a new semantic model Moving reports onto a new Gold-based model is routine in a Fabric migration. The mechanics are simple (change which model a report points at). The risk is everything inside the report that referred to a field the new model names differently. This page gives you four ways to do it, a gate that finds the gaps first, and a matr"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#when","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"When you rebind","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"A legacy Import model is replaced by a Direct Lake or consolidated model with the same business meaning. Reports that carry their own embedded model are being converted into thin reports on a shared model. A shared model is split or merged, or moves to a different workspace. Rebinding is not how you promote a report between Dev, Test and Production. Deployment pipelines handle that by binding a deployed report to the"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#approaches","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"Four ways to rebind","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Approach Use it when Strength Limit REST or semantic-link-labs rebind Thin reports whose model changes under them; same or compatible schema; many reports. Scriptable and repeatable; keeps the report item, so its ID, URL and dependents stay; supports service principals. Needs Write on the report and Build on the target model. Does nothing about mismatched field names. Not supported for paginated reports. Edit the PBI"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#schema-diff","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"Gate: compare the schemas first","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"A rebind to a model with different names produces a report that opens and then shows errors on every affected visual. Find the gaps before you touch the report: list the columns and measures the old model exposes, list what the new model exposes, and decide what to do with every difference. The snippet below runs in a Fabric notebook. The output is your work list. For each missing field choose one of three actions: a"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#rest","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"Rebind with the REST API or semantic-link-labs","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"The Power BI REST API Reports - Rebind Report In Group binds a report to a target semantic model. Learn documents three conditions that matter: you need Write permission on the report and Build permission on the target model, a model in a different workspace is exposed to the report's workspace as a shared model, and a report that had a live connection ends up with a direct binding to the target. The call does not su"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#pbip","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"Rebind in a PBIP project","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"In a Power BI project the pointer is a property of definition.pbir in the report folder, named datasetReference . It takes one of two forms: byPath : a relative path to a semantic model folder in the same repository. Desktop opens the report and the model together in full edit mode. Fabric Git integration always exports a byPath reference when the model and report share a workspace. byConnection : a connection to a m"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#manual","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"Remap manually in Desktop","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Use this route when a report owns a local model and must become a thin report, or when the new model's fields differ and every affected visual needs a decision. Always work on a copy. The broken window is expected. Between disconnecting the old source and finishing the remap, visuals error out. That is why you work on a copy and why you do not publish until the verification step passes. Copy the report Save the repor"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#verify","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"Verify every dependency","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Confirm that every remapped element is fixed. This includes fields, filters, hierarchies, specific column settings, bookmarks, URLs, row-level security and any other dependency that broke after the switch. Element What can go wrong How to check Field wells and values Column renamed, moved table, different data type or grain. Compare totals for each headline visual against the old report using the same filters. Visual"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#bulk","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"Rebinding at scale","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"For dozens of reports, treat the rebind as a release with gates, not as a loop. Gates and a way back. The old model stays in place until sign-off, so a failed smoke test is a second rebind call, not a rebuild. Pilot first. Rebind two or three representative reports, verify them with the matrix, and only then run the bulk call. Batch by owner. Group reports by the team that can sign them off and agree a window; people"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#troubleshooting","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Symptom Likely cause What to do Rebind call returns an authorization error Caller lacks Write on the report or Build on the target model Grant the missing permission, or run as a service principal that has both. Report opens but visuals show \"Something is wrong\" or empty fields Fields differ in the new model Use the schema gap list; rename in the model (hide the old name, add the new) or remap the visual. Numbers dif"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/rebinding.html#refs","t":"Rebinding reports to a new semantic model","s":"References","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Reports - Rebind Report In Group Power BI Desktop project report folder (definition.pbir, PBIR) Connect to semantic models in Power BI Use composite models in Power BI Desktop Build permission for shared semantic models Overview of Fabric Git integration"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Playbook 12 · Power BI Report Development Standards Collaboration, endorsement and QA The earlier pages describe a good model and a good report. This page covers what keeps them good once several people depend on them: where shared models live, who may build on them, how a change travels to production, how you signal trust, and the checklist every release passes before it is published."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#shared-models","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Shared semantic models and thin reports","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"The default pattern is one governed semantic model per subject area, owned by the data team, with many thin reports built on it by whoever needs a view. A thin report has no model of its own: it connects live to a shared model, or at most adds report-level measures or a small local measures table as described on the rebinding page . The benefits are a single definition of every measure, one refresh to schedule and se"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#who-needs-what","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Who needs what","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Person Needs How it is granted Model owner Contributor or higher on the model workspace Workspace role. A Contributor or higher also has Build on the models in that workspace. Report author Build permission on the shared model, and Contributor or higher on the workspace where the report is saved The model owner assigns Build to a user or a group under Manage permissions ; an app can also grant Build to its audiences."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#endorsement","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Endorsement and certification","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Endorsement tells people which content is safe to reuse. Items with a badge are labelled in the interface and, in some lists, shown first. A badge is a promise, so it has to be backed by the checklist at the end of this page. Badge Meaning Who can apply it Use it for Promoted The creators consider the item ready for sharing and reuse. Any user with write permission on the item. Reports that passed review; shared mode"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#release-flow","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"From change to production","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"A report or model change follows the same route as any other asset: author in a project, review a pull request, sync to Development, promote through the pipeline stages, and publish only after sign-off. The mechanics are in the dedicated playbooks; this page fixes the order and the gates. One route to production. Nothing reaches Production without passing through the repository, a reviewed pull request, a Test stage "},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#deployment-carries","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"What a deployment carries and what it does not","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Deployment pipelines copy metadata, not data or the settings that belong to a stage. Plan the follow-up work for every first deployment and after every change that touches these items. Item Copied by deployment What you do in the target stage Report pages and visuals, model metadata, relationships Yes Nothing; verify the deployment compare view. Data in the model No Refresh the model after deployment. Data source cre"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#change-management","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Changing a shared model safely","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Every column, measure and table name in a shared model is a contract with every report that uses it, because report visuals, filters and bookmarks refer to fields by name. Changing the contract without a process is how a single rename breaks a dozen reports. Change Effect on reports Rule Add a column, measure or table None Release freely after review. Hide a field Existing visuals keep working; new use is discouraged"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#checklist","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Pre-publish QA checklist","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Every model and report passes this list before its first publish to Production and after any material change. The reviewer records the result with the change. Items already covered in detail on earlier pages link back to them."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#qa-model","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Semantic model","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Auto date/time and auto relationship detection are off; the date table is marked and covers the needed range. Settings Star schema with single-direction relationships; no ambiguous paths; no bi-directional filtering without a recorded reason. Star schema Storage mode chosen and documented, including the Direct Lake variant. Storage mode Keys, foreign keys, technical columns and unused columns are hidden; descriptions"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#qa-dax","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"DAX and measures","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Measures live in a dedicated measures table, grouped in display folders, none in dimension or fact tables. Measures table Variables used for repeated expressions; DIVIDE for ratios; no unnecessary iterators or FILTER over whole tables. DAX guidance Headline measures reconciled to a source figure or to the previous report, using fixed filters."},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#qa-design","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Report design","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Shared theme applied; one grid; consistent slicer placement; titles state the question the visual answers. Layout Alt text, contrast, tab order and keyboard operation checked. Accessibility Mobile layout curated where the report is used on phones. Mobile layout Drillthrough and tooltip pages work from every source visual; bookmarks hold no sensitive selections. Interaction"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#qa-performance","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Performance","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Visual budget respected on every page; tables and matrices are filtered or Top N by default. Visual budget Performance Analyzer log reviewed for the busiest page, with no unexplained DAX query bucket. Performance Analyzer Query reduction options set, with Apply all slicers where pages carry many slicers. Query reduction For Direct Lake models, no fallback to DirectQuery and no guardrail failure at production data vol"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#qa-security","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Security and governance","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Row-level security roles defined in the model, members assigned in the target stage, and tested with View as for each role. Sensitivity label applied; no personal or client data in filter values, bookmarks or alt text stored in the project. Build permission granted to the authorised group only; the model description names an owner and a support route. Refresh schedule, credentials and failure notification set in the "},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#qa-publishing","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"Publishing","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Item names, workspace and endorsement match the plan; certification requested for shared production models. App updated where an app exposes the report; the audience list matches the intended consumers. Organizational apps Change note published, and the rollback point (the commit or tag) recorded. Key takeaways Keep models in a consumption workspace and reports in delivery workspaces; grant Build to groups. Promote f"},{"u":"playbooks/12-power-bi-report-development/collaboration-qa.html#refs","t":"Collaboration, endorsement and QA","s":"References","p":"Power BI Report Development Standards","x":"Build permission for shared semantic models Endorsement overview The Microsoft Fabric deployment pipelines process Overview of Fabric Git integration Power BI Desktop projects Optimization guide for Power BI"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/index.html","t":"Paginated Report Migration","s":"Paginated Report Migration","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Playbook 13 · Analytics Paginated Report Migration Paginated reports are the print-ready, pixel-controlled reports built from RDL files. This playbook moves them from workspaces on Power BI Premium capacity to workspaces on Fabric capacity, with the same reports, the same data and no surprises for the people who open them on Monday morning."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/index.html#purpose","t":"Paginated Report Migration","s":"Purpose and scope","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Paginated reports do not hold data. Each report is a layout plus queries that run at render time against the sources it names, which makes a migration mostly a question of where the report lives , which capacity runs it and how it reaches its data . This playbook covers all three: Where it lives. Keep the legacy workspace and change its capacity, or publish the RDL files into new development, test and production work"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/index.html#phases","t":"Paginated Report Migration","s":"The three phases","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Assess Inventory every report, its sources, parameters and features; decide each report's fate. Migrate Reassign the workspace or republish the RDL files; recreate credentials and gateway bindings. Promote Apply data source rules so each stage reads its own sources. Validate Prove layout, data, exports, performance, subscriptions and access match. Cut over Switch users, keep a rollback, then retire the old capacity."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/index.html#when-to-use","t":"Paginated Report Migration","s":"When to use this playbook","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Use it when Paginated reports live in workspaces assigned to Power BI Premium capacity and the capacity is going away. You are standardising on Fabric workspaces named by environment, with promotion through a pipeline. Reports come from SQL Server Reporting Services or Power BI Report Server and must land in the Power BI service. Look elsewhere when You are moving Power BI reports and semantic models, not RDL files: "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/index.html#before-you-start","t":"Paginated Report Migration","s":"Before you start","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"A Fabric (F SKU) capacity exists, sized from the Premium capacity it replaces, with capacity administrators assigned. See the sizing notes on the migration methods page . You can publish: a Power BI Pro or Premium Per User license is needed to publish paginated reports to workspaces other than My workspace , and the publisher needs at least the Contributor role in the target workspace. You know who owns each report. "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/index.html#choose","t":"Paginated Report Migration","s":"Choosing a path","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Path Use it when Effort Reversal Method A: reassign the workspace The whole workspace moves as it is, and the environments are handled elsewhere. Low. A setting change, then validation. Reassign back while the Premium capacity is still active. Method B: republish RDL files You want new workspaces, different data sources, per-report changes or a pipeline from the start. Per report, scriptable through REST. The legacy "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/index.html#map","t":"Paginated Report Migration","s":"Map of this playbook","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"02 Phase 1: Inventory and assessment Catalog reports with REST and an RDL parser, classify features that need work, and decide the fate of each report. Migration leads 15 min 03 Phase 2: Migration methods Reassign or republish, the SSRS and Report Server path, REST import code and a decision flow. Administrators 20 min 04 Pipelines, gateways and sources Data source rules per stage, gateway mapping, Fabric SQL endpoin"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/index.html#related","t":"Paginated Report Migration","s":"How this fits with the other playbooks","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"The wider move of datasets, reports and capacities is planned in Power BI to Fabric migration ; use this playbook for the paginated slice of that plan. Environment promotion follows Deployment pipelines , and the workspace names used here come from the house convention in the reference architecture . Paginated reports that read from semantic models inherit the standards in Power BI report development . Key takeaways "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/index.html#refs","t":"Paginated Report Migration","s":"References","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Plan to migrate .rdl reports to Power BI Paginated reports in Power BI: FAQ Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric migration overview Migrate workspaces from Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric Power BI Report Builder"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/assessment.html","t":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","s":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Playbook 13 · Paginated Report Migration Phase 1: Inventory and assessment The inventory decides everything that follows: which method you use, how much rework you face, who has to sign off, and how big the target capacity needs to be. Build it from the platform's own metadata and from the RDL files themselves, not from memory."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/assessment.html#what-to-record","t":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","s":"What to record for each report","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"A spreadsheet is enough to start, but build the inventory with scripts so you can rerun it after every change. One row per report, with these columns: Column Why it matters Where it comes from Name, workspace, report ID Identity and traceability through the migration. Reports API Owner Data source rules, credentials and take-over depend on the owning identity. The owner also signs off the result. Workspace settings a"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/assessment.html#list-reports","t":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","s":"Step 1: list the reports and their data sources","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"The Power BI REST API lists the items in a workspace, and the report type separates paginated reports from Power BI reports. For paginated reports, the Get Datasources In Group call returns each data source, its connection details and, if bound, the gateway it uses. A data source ID that is empty means the data source is not bound to a gateway. The notebook cell below builds the first version of the inventory. For a "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/assessment.html#export-rdl","t":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","s":"Step 2: export and parse the RDL","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"The API shows how reports are bound, not what is inside them. The RDL file is XML, so a short script can read every file and report the features that decide effort. First export the files; the Export Report In Group call returns the report definition for a paginated report. Then parse each file. The cell below reads the XML with namespace wildcards, because the RDL namespace differs by schema version, and it delibera"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/assessment.html#usage","t":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","s":"Step 3: measure usage","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Two sources tell you whether a report is worth moving. The audit log records report views for paginated reports, with a report type field that distinguishes them from Power BI reports, and the Fabric Capacity Metrics app shows what the paginated workload costs on your current capacity. Use the audit data to find reports nobody opens, and the capacity data, with peak render times, to size the target. Microsoft's guida"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/assessment.html#feature-differences","t":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","s":"Step 4: check the feature differences","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"These are the differences that most often change a report's disposition. Check each flagged report against this table. Feature or behavior In the Power BI service Action Shared data sources ( .rds ) and shared datasets ( .rsd ) Not supported. The RDL Migration Tool converts them to embedded data sources and datasets. Use embedded sources, cloud connections or shared Power BI semantic models. Custom assemblies and DLL"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/assessment.html#source-support","t":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","s":"Data source support","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Source type Reached how Note Azure SQL Database, Azure Synapse, Azure SQL Managed Instance, Dataverse Directly from the service No gateway needed for supported cloud sources. Power BI semantic models, Direct Lake models, XMLA endpoints Directly from the service Semantic model queries carry a fixed timeout; use the XMLA endpoint for longer queries. SQL Server, SQL Server Analysis Services, Oracle, Teradata, ODBC Throu"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/assessment.html#disposition","t":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","s":"Step 5: decide each report's fate","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Apply the same four dispositions to every report and record the reason. Three questions, four outcomes. Usage comes first because retiring a report is the cheapest migration. Only reports that are used and blocked by an unsupported feature need rework. Disposition What it means Typical examples Migrate Publish or reassign without changes to the layout. Reports on supported sources with embedded data sources. Modify E"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/assessment.html#refs","t":"Phase 1: Inventory and assessment","s":"References","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Plan to migrate .rdl reports to Power BI Paginated reports in Power BI: FAQ Supported data sources for Power BI paginated reports Capacity planning for paginated reports Find and retire unused reports Reports - Get Reports In Group Reports - Export Report In Group"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"Phase 2: Migration methods","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Playbook 13 · Paginated Report Migration Phase 2: Migration methods There are two ways to put paginated reports on Fabric capacity. Reassigning moves the whole workspace in one step and is the simplest. Republishing moves reports one at a time and is the only way to change them on the way. A third route covers reports that still live on a report server."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html#prepare","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"Prepare the target capacity","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Migration is not a tenant-wide switch. You move workspaces, one at a time or in waves, and the Premium capacity keeps serving everything you have not moved. That is what makes a pilot and a rollback possible, and it means both capacities run side by side until you finish. Buy the Fabric capacity first Create the F SKU capacity and add capacity administrators before you reassign anything. Keep the Premium capacity run"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html#method-a","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"Method A: reassign the workspace","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Choose this when the workspace should move as it stands. The paginated reports, their permissions and their settings stay in the workspace; only the capacity underneath changes. A capacity change, not a copy. Nothing is republished. That is why it is quick, and why the checks that matter are the ones about connectivity and load."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html#method-a-steps","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"Steps","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Announce a window Reassigning a workspace interrupts active queries and refreshes, and running jobs are cancelled when the move happens. Pick a quiet hour and tell the report owners. Reassign As a capacity administrator, open the admin portal, go to Capacity settings , open the Fabric capacity tab and select the target capacity. Choose Assign workspaces and select the specific workspaces to move. As a workspace admin"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html#method-b","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"Method B: republish the RDL files","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Choose this when you want new workspaces, different data sources, edits to individual reports or promotion through a pipeline from the start. The goal is to move each paginated report from the legacy workspace into the development workspace of the new environment set, then promote it. The RDL file is only the layout and queries. Credentials and gateway bindings are stored in the service, so they have to be set again "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html#method-b-steps","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"Steps in Report Builder","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Download the RDL Open the report in the legacy workspace, choose File and then Download report to save the RDL file. For many reports, use the export call in the assessment page instead. Open it in Power BI Report Builder Use an up-to-date version, and sign in to the service with your Microsoft Entra account so you can publish from the tool. Review the data sources Confirm that every data source and dataset is embedd"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html#method-b-rest","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"Scripted publishing with the REST API","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"The Power BI REST API imports an RDL file into a workspace. The call is asynchronous: it returns an import ID, and you poll until the import succeeds or fails. The nameConflict parameter decides what happens when a report with the same name already exists: Abort stops, Overwrite replaces. Who owns the result. The identity that imports owns the report. If a service principal imports, run the take-over call so that a p"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html#report-server","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"Reports on SSRS or Power BI Report Server","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Reports that still live on a report server follow the same plan, with a different first mile. The server instances can run on premises or on virtual machines in a cloud. Discover them with a free inventory tool, then use the publishing route that fits the version. Source Route What it does for you Power BI Report Server and SQL Server Reporting Services 2022 Built-in publish to the Power BI service, for all reports o"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html#decision","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"Choosing a method","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Questions in order of how much they narrow the choice. The two methods are not exclusive: a common pattern is to reassign workspaces whose reports are clean and republish the exceptions. Consideration Method A: reassign Method B: republish Unit of work A workspace A report Changes to reports on the way None Any, in Report Builder New environment workspaces and pipeline Added afterwards Built in from the start Effort "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/migration-methods.html#refs","t":"Phase 2: Migration methods","s":"References","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Plan to migrate .rdl reports to Power BI Publish .rdl files to Power BI from Reporting Services Use the RDL Migration Tool Migrate workspaces from Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric Power BI Premium P SKU to Fabric F SKU migration decision guide Manage your Fabric capacity Workspaces - Assign To Capacity Imports - Post Import In Group"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Playbook 13 · Paginated Report Migration Pipelines, gateways and sources A migrated report is only as good as its connections. This page covers the three things that change per environment: which server and database each stage reads, which gateway or cloud connection carries the query, and which identity runs it."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#rules","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Environment-specific sources with data source rules","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"The recommended Fabric-native approach keeps the RDL file untouched. The report is developed against the development source with a static connection, and the deployment pipeline carries a data source rule for each later stage. The rule tells the service to replace the server and database with the stage's own values during deployment. For general pipeline setup and promotion, see Deployment pipelines ; this page cover"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#rules-options","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Three ways to vary a source by environment","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Option Strength Cost Pipeline data source rules No change to the RDL; configured once per stage; visible in the pipeline. Report Builder lock-in after deployment; limits listed below. Scripted update after each deployment Works for any number of reports; can be part of a release job. You run it every time; the identity must own the data sources. One RDL per environment Simple to understand. Copies drift apart; every "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#rules-steps","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Configure the rules","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Paginated reports support data source rules only. Parameter rules and default lakehouse rules do not apply to them. A rule is created in the stage it applies to, never in the first stage. Create the pipeline and assign workspaces Create DPLY_SALES_DLV with development, test and production stages (the number and names of stages cannot be changed afterwards), and assign DEV_SALES_DLV to the first. Workspaces in a pipel"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#rules-limits","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Limits that shape how you work","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Constraint Consequence What to do Rules need the item owner Only the owner of the report can create or view its rules. Use one named deployment identity so ownership is stable. The deployer becomes the owner After each deployment of a paginated report, the user who deployed it is the owner, and needs credentials for its data sources. Deploy as the same account every time; keep its source access current. Report Builde"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#gateways","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Gateways and connections","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Each data source in a published report needs a route to its data. For a source in the cloud, the report connects directly or through a shareable cloud connection. For a source on a private network, it goes through a gateway. Where each query goes. The report runs its own queries when it renders, so connectivity and identity decide whether it works, not a refresh schedule."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#gateway-rules","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Gateway rules that catch migrations","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Binding needs rights on both sides. The person who binds a report to a gateway needs permission on the gateway and on the data source in it. Plan who does this for each environment. Names must match. The server and database in the report have to match the gateway data source exactly, including the form of the server name. A rule that changes the server also changes which gateway data source matches. Separate data sou"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#auth","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Which identity runs the query","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"The identity a report uses decides what data it can see, and security applied at the source (row-level rules, warehouse permissions) is applied to that identity. Choose it on purpose. Authentication Queries run as Source-side security Use when Single sign-on Each viewer Applied per viewer The source has per-user permissions and every viewer is meant to see only their rows. Stored credential (for example OAuth2 or bas"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#cloud-connections","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Cloud connections","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"A shareable cloud connection holds a source and its credential once, and several reports and items can use it. A connection can be created by someone with the right workspace role and used by others who are granted permission on it. The connections that apply to a workspace's paginated reports are managed from the workspace's Manage experience; confirm the exact navigation and the permissions needed to create and use"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#fabric-sources","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Fabric Warehouse and Lakehouse SQL endpoints as sources","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Many migrations repoint reports from an on-premises SQL Server to the Gold layer in Fabric. The report reaches a Warehouse or a Lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint over the same T-SQL connection that other tools use. Copy the connection string Open the Warehouse or SQL analytics endpoint in the Fabric portal and copy its SQL connection string . Use it as the server name. The string is unique to the item's workspace, so "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#scripting","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"Script the post-publish steps","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"When you publish many reports, set sources and bindings with the REST API rather than by hand. Four calls cover it. Call Purpose Limits Get Datasources In Group List a report's data sources, with data source and gateway IDs (empty when not bound). Paginated reports only. Take Over In Group Transfer ownership of the report's data sources to the caller. Paginated reports only. Update Datasources In Group Change the ser"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/pipelines-gateways.html#refs","t":"Pipelines, gateways and sources","s":"References","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Create deployment rules The Microsoft Fabric deployment pipelines process Supported data sources for Power BI paginated reports Connectivity to data warehousing in Microsoft Fabric Reports - Get Datasources In Group Reports - Take Over In Group Reports - Update Datasources In Group Reports - Bind To Gateway In Group What is an on-premises data gateway?"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Playbook 13 · Paginated Report Migration Phase 3: Validation and cutover A report that opens is not a report that matches. This phase proves parity with the original, recreates what the platform does not carry over, and ends in a cutover that you can undo until the day you retire the old capacity."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html#strategy","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"Validation strategy","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Reuse the inventory from Phase 1 as the test list. Every migrating report gets a set of parameter values (typical, edge and empty), and every test compares the migrated report with the original under the same inputs. Business owners then confirm the result in user acceptance testing; the automated checks exist so that the owners spend their time on judgement, not on counting rows. Same inputs, two outputs, one verdic"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html#test-matrix","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"What to test","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Area Compare How Pass when Layout and pagination Page count, page breaks, headers and footers, fonts, images Export PDF from both; compare page counts; review a sample side by side Same page count for the same inputs, or a documented reason Data Row counts, totals, nulls, ordering Export CSV from both; compare after sorting Identical, apart from explained differences Parameters Defaults, cascading lists, multi-value,"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html#parity","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"Automate parity checks","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"The REST API can render a paginated report to a file with given parameter values, which is exactly what a parity harness needs. The call is asynchronous: you start an export, poll its status and then fetch the file. Supported formats for paginated reports include PDF, accessible PDF, Excel, Word, PowerPoint, CSV, XML, MHTML and several image formats. Reports that read a semantic model need an identity. The export cal"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html#performance","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"Performance and capacity","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Compare render times against the baseline you recorded before the move, then watch the capacity for a couple of days under real use. Adding the paginated reports workload to a capacity can slow paginated reports and everything else on it, so treat the target capacity as a shared resource and check both. Symptom Check Action Report is slower than before Time the dataset query alone at the source, then the render Tune "},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html#subscriptions","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"Subscriptions, delivery and links","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Subscriptions are service settings, not part of the RDL, so they do not move with a report. Recreate each one and run it once. Email subscriptions are supported for paginated reports, with the report attached in a chosen format. Large attachments are limited in size; for big outputs, deliver to OneDrive or SharePoint instead. Per-recipient reports. The service supports subscriptions that vary the report by recipient,"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html#access","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"Permissions and licensing","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Roles. Under Method A the workspace stays in place, so confirm its roles are intact. New workspaces from Method B start with only the roles you grant, so recreate them from the legacy workspace. Viewers. Users with free licenses can view paginated reports only on a Fabric capacity of F64 or larger, or a Premium capacity of P1 or larger; otherwise they need a Pro or Premium Per User license. Confirm this with a free-l"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html#cutover","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"Cutover","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Cutover is the moment users stop using the old reports. For a workspace reassignment, the move itself is the cutover. For republished reports, it is the day links and apps point at the new items. In both cases, keep the old state intact for a defined rollback window. Every step before retirement is reversible. Define the rollback triggers in advance and give someone the authority to use them. Freeze changes Stop edit"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html#decommission","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"Retire the old environment","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Archive first. Export and store every legacy RDL file in source control before you delete a legacy workspace. Keep the inventory and the test results with it. Check no workspace remains on the Premium capacity before you cancel it, and cancel the subscription only after the last wave has passed validation. Do not let the subscription lapse first. Learn describes how a capacity behaves when its subscription ends: thro"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/validation-cutover.html#refs","t":"Phase 3: Validation and cutover","s":"References","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Plan to migrate .rdl reports to Power BI Reports - Export To File In Group Export paginated report to file Capacity planning for paginated reports Email subscriptions for reports and dashboards in the Power BI service Migrate workspaces from Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Playbook 13 · Paginated Report Migration Risks, troubleshooting and checklist Most paginated report migrations fail in the same handful of places: a feature the service does not support, a credential nobody recreated, a rule applied to the wrong stage, or a license assumption about viewers. This page lists the risks to manage up front, a fast way to localise a failure, and the checklist to run before each wave."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html#risk-register","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"Risk register","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Risk Early signal Mitigation Viewers lose access because the target capacity is below F64 Free-license users report that reports will not open after the move Size at F64 or larger, or confirm that every viewer holds a Pro or Premium Per User license. Test with a free-license user in the pilot. Unsupported features block publishing Publish fails with a list of reasons; the inventory flags assemblies, shared sources, f"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html#triage","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"Localise the failure","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"When a migrated report misbehaves, first decide at which step it fails. The step points at the cause far faster than the error text. The failing step is the clue. Five steps, five families of cause. Start with the family, then use the table below to find the fix."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html#symptoms","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"Symptom to fix","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix Publish from Report Builder is refused with a list of reasons The report uses a feature the service does not support: a custom assembly reference, shared data source or dataset, custom font, or too many data sources Revise the RDL: move logic into a code block, embed the sources, change the font, then publish again. Report opens and shows a data source or credential error Credentials not set,"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html#checklist","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"Migration readiness checklist","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html#check-before","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"Before a wave","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Inventory complete, with owner, sources, features, usage and disposition for each report. Target capacity bought, sized and administered; sized at F64 or larger if free-license users view reports. Pilot workspace migrated, validated and rolled back once as a rehearsal. Gateway data sources exist per environment; the people who bind them have rights. A deployment identity is chosen, licensed and able to read every sou"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html#check-report","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"For each report","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Unsupported features resolved; the RDL publishes without errors. Data sources are embedded, supported and named so that each can have its own rule. Credentials set; gateway or connection bound; data source IDs confirmed through the API. Parity tests pass for layout, data, parameters and every export format in use. Subscriptions recreated and tested; links, apps and embedded views updated."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html#check-env","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"For each environment","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Pipeline DPLY_SALES_DLV deploys development to test to production with the data source rules in place. Each stage reads its own source, verified by opening a report and checking the data source. Workspace roles match the plan; a typical viewer and a free-license viewer have been tested."},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html#check-cutover","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"Cutover and retirement","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Final validation passed and signed off by each owner. Rollback route documented: assign back, or repoint links to the legacy workspace. Hypercare period set, with a named person watching errors and capacity. Legacy RDL files, inventory and test results archived in source control. No workspace remains on the Premium capacity; its subscription is cancelled by a named person after the last wave. Key takeaways Most failu"},{"u":"playbooks/13-paginated-report-migration/troubleshooting.html#refs","t":"Risks, troubleshooting and checklist","s":"References","p":"Paginated Report Migration","x":"Plan to migrate .rdl reports to Power BI Paginated reports in Power BI: FAQ Supported data sources for Power BI paginated reports Capacity planning for paginated reports Create deployment rules Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric migration FAQ Migrate workspaces from Power BI Premium to Microsoft Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/index.html","t":"Deployment Checklist","s":"Deployment Checklist","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Playbook 14 · Deployment Checklist Deployment Checklist This is the page you keep open on release day. It condenses the deployment pipelines playbook into an ordered runbook: what to deploy, what to configure, what to hydrate and what to verify for each layer, in the order that keeps bindings and data correct. Audience Release managers, data engineers Level Intermediate Reading time about 45 minutes"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/index.html#purpose","t":"Deployment Checklist","s":"What this runbook is for","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"A release across a medallion platform touches seven kinds of workspace and many items. The risk is rarely a single failed deployment; it is a successful deployment of an empty layer, or an item bound to the wrong stage, discovered a layer later. The runbook prevents that by fixing three things: the order in which layers move, the checks that gate each move, and the evidence you leave behind. It assumes the layout fro"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/index.html#order","t":"Deployment Checklist","s":"The sequence in one table","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Run the whole table for the DEV to TST hop, finish every row, then run it again for TST to PRD. Inside a row, the steps run left to right. Layer Deploy Configure Hydrate Bronze Lakehouse; then variable library, environment, notebooks, data pipelines Activate the stage's variable library values Populate tables Silver Same as Bronze; redeploy anything deployed before its dependency existed Same as Bronze Populate table"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/index.html#ground-rules","t":"Deployment Checklist","s":"Ground rules","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"One layer at a time. Deploy, hydrate and verify a layer before you touch the next one. TST completes before PRD starts. All seven rows in TST, then all seven in PRD, with the same order and the PRD values. A deployment copies definitions, not data. An empty layer is the normal result of a deployment; hydration is what makes it a working layer. Every deployment gets a note that names the release, the layer and the sta"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/index.html#prerequisites","t":"Deployment Checklist","s":"Prerequisites","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Seven pipelines exist (or as many as you use), each with its three stage workspaces assigned and items paired. See Pipeline setup and pairing . The release manager has pipeline admin on every pipeline and at least Contributor on every stage workspace; the automation identity, if any, has the same. Connections used by pipelines, notebooks and models are owned by a service principal and shared with the deployment group"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/index.html#map","t":"Deployment Checklist","s":"Map of this playbook","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"01 Layer checklists Deploy, configure, hydrate and verify lists for each of the seven layers. Runbook 02 Tips and watch-outs The five practices that prevent most failures, with the mechanism behind each. Practice 03 Pre-flight and verification What to check before, during and after, and the evidence to keep. Gates 04 Troubleshooting Symptom, cause and fix, with a triage path. Operations"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/index.html#related","t":"Deployment Checklist","s":"Related playbooks","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Deployment pipelines and promotion explains pairing, autobinding, rules and the REST API behind each step. GitHub integration and branching covers how a change reaches the DEV workspace before the runbook starts. Variable libraries covers the per-stage values you activate in the Configure column. Key takeaways Deploy, hydrate and verify one layer at a time, and finish every layer in TST before you start PRD. A green "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/index.html#refs","t":"Deployment Checklist","s":"References","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Overview of Fabric deployment pipelines The deployment pipelines process Best practices for lifecycle management in Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html","t":"Layer checklists","s":"Layer checklists","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Playbook 14 · Deployment Checklist Layer checklists One checklist per layer. Each item is a single verifiable action; tick them in order, and do not start the next layer until the last item of this one is ticked. The tick state is remembered in your browser, so you can pause and return."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html#how-to-use","t":"Layer checklists","s":"How to use the checklists","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Run every list twice per release: once for the DEV to TST hop, once for TST to PRD with the PRD values. Reload the page or clear the ticks between hops. Each list has four groups in this order: Deploy , Configure , Hydrate , Verify . Use the same deployment note format everywhere: release reference, layer, from-stage to to-stage . If a verify item fails, stop. Fix the cause, repeat the step that produced it and re-ru"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html#bronze","t":"Layer checklists","s":"Bronze","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The Bronze layer ingests from external sources into raw tables. Pipeline: DPLY_<DOMAIN>_BRZ . Deploy. Deploy the lakehouse and wait for success; the stage lists it as paired and the target workspace holds a new, empty lakehouse of the same name. Deploy. Deploy the environment and confirm its published state (libraries and Spark settings) matches DEV. Deploy. Deploy the variable library. Deploy. Deploy notebooks, data"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html#silver","t":"Layer checklists","s":"Silver","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Silver cleans and conforms Bronze data. It depends on Bronze being deployed and hydrated in the same stage. Pipeline: DPLY_<DOMAIN>_SLV . Precondition. The Bronze verify group passed in this stage. Deploy. Repeat the Bronze deploy order: lakehouse, environment, variable library, then notebooks, pipelines, shortcuts. Deploy. Redeploy any item that was deployed before the item it depends on existed; binding is evaluate"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html#gold","t":"Layer checklists","s":"Gold","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Gold holds business-ready tables. Pipeline: DPLY_<DOMAIN>_GLD . Precondition. The Silver verify group passed in this stage. Deploy. Repeat the Silver deploy order and the redeploy check for items deployed ahead of their dependencies. Configure. Activate the stage's variable library value set and check references to Silver, as for Silver. Hydrate. Run the Gold transformations. Verify. Dimension and fact tables exist; "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html#warehouse","t":"Layer checklists","s":"Warehouse","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The consumption warehouse reads Gold and serves SQL. Pipeline: DPLY_<DOMAIN>_CNSM_WH . Precondition. The Gold verify group passed in this stage. Deploy. Deploy the lakehouse, if the layer has one (for example for staging or shortcuts). Deploy. Deploy the warehouse, then the load pipelines and any stored procedures. Deploy. Redeploy items that were deployed before their dependencies, to fix bindings. Configure. Set pe"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html#semantic-model","t":"Layer checklists","s":"Semantic model","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Semantic models are usually deployed twice: the rule is defined on the target stage's copy of the model, and it applies only at a deployment. Pipeline: DPLY_<DOMAIN>_CNSM_SM . Why semantic models are deployed twice. A rule is defined on an item that already exists in the target stage, and it applies only at a deployment. Until the second deploy the new model still points at the source stage, so refreshing it early lo"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html#consumption-lakehouse","t":"Layer checklists","s":"Consumption lakehouse","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Pipeline: DPLY_<DOMAIN>_CNSM_LH . If a Direct Lake model reads this lakehouse, deploy and verify it before the semantic model layer instead. Precondition. The Gold layer is verified in this stage. Deploy. Deploy the lakehouse; its SQL analytics endpoint and default semantic model are provisioned with it. Configure. Check each shortcut: internal shortcuts in the same workspace are remapped, but their target tables and"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html#delivery","t":"Layer checklists","s":"Delivery","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Reports and paginated reports. Pipeline: DPLY_<DOMAIN>_DLV . Precondition. The models the reports read are deployed and refreshed in this stage. Deploy. Deploy the reports. If you deploy selectively, use Select related so each report's model dependency is included or checked. Configure. For paginated reports with a Fabric semantic model as source, apply the data source rule; they do not autobind. Configure. Update th"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/layer-checklists.html#refs","t":"Layer checklists","s":"References","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Lakehouse Git integration and deployment pipelines Create deployment rules The deployment pipelines process Variable library CI/CD Environment Git integration and deployment pipelines"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Tips and watch-outs","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Playbook 14 · Deployment Checklist Tips and watch-outs Each tip follows the same shape: the rule, why it exists, what to do, and what to do if you already broke it. Four are documented platform behaviour; one (do not click during comparison) is operational experience that Microsoft does not document, and it is labelled as such."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#connections","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Tip 1: own connections with a service principal","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The rule. Every team member who deploys or supports the platform must have access to the connections, models and items it uses. Prefer connections owned by a service principal. If that is impossible, make sure everyone involved has been granted access to the connections that others created. Who owns the connection decides who can deploy. Connections carry their own roles (User, User with resharing, Owner). Items that"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#connections-why","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Why","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"A connection is an item with its own access list. It has roles (User, User with resharing, Owner), and the person who created it is its owner. Someone who is not on the list cannot use it, so their deployment, refresh or pipeline run fails even though they have every workspace role. When the owner leaves the organization, the connection is orphaned. Only a Fabric administrator can reassign it, through the admin conne"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#connections-do","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"What to do","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Create connections for pipelines, notebooks and models with the service principal authentication method where the connector supports it. Share each connection with the deployment group (User role), and with a second owner who is not the service principal. List connections per layer in the runbook, with the group that has access. Keep a note of the exceptions: where a connector or item type does not support a service "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#order","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Tip 2: lakehouse and environment first","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The rule. Deploy the lakehouse and the environment, and check they succeeded, before notebooks, shortcuts and the rest. The environment is an item in its own right: select it explicitly as part of the deployment. Why the lakehouse and environment go first. Autobinding looks for the paired lakehouse and environment in the target workspace when a notebook is deployed. If they are not there yet, the notebook is not boun"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#order-why","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Why","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Notebooks bind to the default lakehouse and to the attached environment when the lakehouse and environment are in the same workspace. The pipeline looks for the paired items in the target workspace at the moment the notebook is deployed. If they are not there, there is nothing to bind to: the deployment can fail on the missing dependency, or the notebook is left unbound until it is redeployed. A lakehouse deployment "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#order-do","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"What to do","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Select the lakehouse, environment and variable library in the first deployment of a layer. Wait for success, then select notebooks, data pipelines and shortcuts in the second deployment. For a selective deployment of a notebook, use Select related to include its dependencies. If it already happened. Redeploy the affected items now that their dependencies exist. Binding is evaluated at every deployment, so a second de"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#deletions","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Tip 3: remove deleted objects downstream","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The rule. If an object is deleted in one layer, delete it, and anything that depends on it, in the subsequent layers and in every downstream stage. Remove consumers first, then the object. Deletions do not travel with a deployment. A deployment never deletes. Remove consumers first, then the object itself, in every downstream stage and in layer order."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#deletions-why","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Why","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"A deployment copies new and changed items and overwrites paired ones. It never deletes. An item deleted in DEV is still in TST, labelled Not in source , and cannot be selected for deployment. Items that still reference the removed object end in error. A shortcut whose target no longer exists is moved to the Unidentified section of the lakehouse, and a \"missing target\" error is the symptom reported in practice when de"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#deletions-do","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"What to do","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Record deletions in the release note, in layer order. In each target stage, delete consumers first (later layers), then the stale item (earlier layers). After clean-up, open the lakehouse and confirm the Unidentified section is empty. Compare the stages and confirm nothing unexpected is left under Not in source . If it already happened. Items listed under Unidentified at the bottom of the lakehouse are the ones with "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#comparison","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Tip 4: do not interact during the comparison","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Operational experience, not documented behaviour Microsoft Learn does not describe this limitation. It is reported by practitioners using the new, preview deployment pipelines UI. Treat it as a precaution and check the troubleshooting article and the new UI overview for any change in status. The rule. While the pipeline is comparing two stages, wait. Do not click items, filters or stage cards until the status labels "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#comparison-why","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Why","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The compare is computed after you open the stage. The reported failure is that interacting while it runs stops the comparison and leaves the page unresponsive, and that refreshing the browser does not recover it. Because the compare feeds the item list you select from, a stuck compare blocks the deployment."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#comparison-do","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"What to do","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Open the stage and wait until every item shows a status label. If the page freezes, start again in a new browser session or a private window. If it still freezes, toggle to the original UI with the switch in the upper right corner; this restarts the comparison in most cases. If the UI is not workable, run the deployment through the REST API, which does not depend on the comparison page. See Automation and limits ."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#hydration","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Tip 5: verify hydration after every layer","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The rule. After a layer is deployed and its pipelines have run, open the workspace you deployed to and confirm the tables and shortcuts before you deploy the next layer. For example, after deploying DEV_SALES_BRZ to TST_SALES_BRZ through DPLY_SALES_BRZ , check the Bronze lakehouse in TST_SALES_BRZ before you start Silver."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#hydration-why","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"Why","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Deployment copies definitions, never data. A freshly deployed lakehouse is empty and a freshly deployed model has nothing to read. The next layer will run against empty inputs and succeed with empty outputs, so a green run proves nothing about data. The check has to be explicit. Compare the output with the expected counts for the stage. For a warehouse use a count query per table instead."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#hydration-do","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"What to do","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Define, per layer, the tables to count and the range each count must fall in. Run the counts in the target workspace, not in DEV, and attach the output to the deployment record. Check shortcuts and the Unidentified section in the same pass. For semantic models, compare one measure from the model with the same figure from its source."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#more","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"More watch-outs","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Watch-out Why it matters Do this Variable library comes up with Default active in a new stage Pipelines and notebooks read the default values, which usually belong to DEV Activate the stage's value set and save before anything runs Rules apply only at a deployment A model looks right in the rule pane but still reads the source stage Deploy the model again, and do not refresh it in between A dataflow refreshing during"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/tips.html#refs","t":"Tips and watch-outs","s":"References","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Data source management (connections and roles) Lakehouse Git integration and deployment pipelines Notebook source control and deployment Environment Git integration and deployment pipelines Compare content in different stages Troubleshoot the Fabric lifecycle management tools"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"Pre-flight and verification","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Playbook 14 · Deployment Checklist Pre-flight and verification The layer checklists say what to do for each layer. This page wraps them: the pre-flight that makes a failed deployment unlikely, the discipline during the window, and the verification that turns \"deployed\" into \"working\", plus the record you leave behind."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#phases","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"Three phases around every release","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Pre-flight Once per release, before the window opens. During For every layer and every stage hop. After Gates before the next layer, then sign-off. Who does what, in order. The release manager gates each hand-off; the pipeline and the target workspace do the rest. The same lanes apply to every layer and every stage hop. The release manager owns the hand-offs. The pipeline and the target workspace do the mechanical wo"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#preflight","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"Pre-flight checklist","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Complete this list before the first deployment of the release. If an item cannot be ticked, fix it or postpone the window."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#pf-scope","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"Scope and approvals","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The release content is merged to the branch DEV is connected to, and DEV has been synced. Nothing is deployed from an unmerged change. The release is the same content that passed testing: promote through the pipeline, and never edit TST or PRD by hand. A release reference exists, and every person involved knows the layer order and who signs off each layer. Deleted objects are listed, in layer order, so the downstream"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#pf-access","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"Access and identities","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The deployer is a pipeline admin and has at least Contributor on both the source and target workspaces of every pipeline in scope. Replacing an existing semantic model also needs the Member role, and a dataflow can be replaced only by its owner. Connections used by pipelines, notebooks and models exist in the target stage, are shared with the deployment group, and have a named second owner (Tip 1). The semantic model"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#pf-state","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"Platform state","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Compare each pipeline's stages first. Every item you expect to overwrite sits on the same row as its source; anything labelled Not in source or Only in source is expected. No dataflow is refreshing in the stages you will compare or deploy: a deployment fails while a dataflow in scope refreshes, and a comparison made during a refresh is unpredictable. Variable libraries have a value set for the target stage, and you k"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#pf-plan","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"Test plan","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"For each layer: the tables and expected row-count ranges, the shortcuts, the reports to open and the measure to reconcile. A named person for each sign-off."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#during","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"During the deployment window","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Repeat for each layer, in order. The details of each layer are in the layer checklists . Open the layer's pipeline and wait for the comparison Confirm every item has a status label before you select anything. Select the items for the first deployment Foundations first: lakehouse, environment and variable library. For a selective deployment, use Select related so dependencies are included. Write the deployment note Us"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#gates","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"After: three gates between layers","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Three gates between layers. Do not carry a failed gate into the next layer. Fix, repeat the failed step, and re-run the gate from the start. Deployment succeeded The history entry for this deployment shows success, no item failed, and the stage compare shows the deployed items as the same as the source (except items you know cannot match, such as an environment with a custom pool). Hydration completed Every load for "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#verify-by-type","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"What to verify for each item type","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Item type Verify in the target workspace Evidence to keep Lakehouse Expected tables exist, counts are in range, shortcuts resolve, nothing under Unidentified , the SQL analytics endpoint answers a query Count output; the shortcut list Environment Published state; libraries and Spark runtime as in DEV. A custom pool is not carried over, so a difference there is expected Environment details Variable library The active "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#evidence","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"Evidence and the deployment note","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"The deployment history is a log of past deployments in a pipeline. Each row records the target stage, the end time, who deployed (a person or a service principal), the item counts by category, any failed items, the note, a deployment ID to quote when you ask for help, and the status. It lists up to 1000 recent deployments, and a renamed item is treated as a new item in the log. Use the note field to tie each row to t"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#rollback","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"Restoring a previous version","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"A deployment overwrites the paired items in the target stage with the source stage's versions. Microsoft's guidance for going back is Git-based: there is no direct way to return to a previous commit in the interface, so promote the older commit to be the head with git revert or git reset , update the DEV workspace from it, and promote it through the pipeline again. Because data is not stored in Git, reverting a data "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/preflight-verify.html#refs","t":"Pre-flight and verification","s":"References","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Best practices for lifecycle management in Fabric Deployment history Compare content in different stages The deployment pipelines process Troubleshoot the Fabric lifecycle management tools"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Playbook 14 · Deployment Checklist Troubleshooting Start from what you see, not from what you think is wrong. The triage path points to one of four groups; each table gives the symptom, the usual cause and the fix, and links to the page that explains the mechanism."},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/troubleshooting.html#triage","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"Triage","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Start from what you see. Pick the symptom, read the first check, then use the matching rows in the table below. Before you work through a table, collect three facts: the deployment ID and status from the deployment history, the list of items that failed or differ, and the deployment note. They tell you which table to open and are what support will ask for. Deploy blocked or failed : the deployment cannot start, or en"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/troubleshooting.html#blocked","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"Deploy blocked or failed","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix The deploy button is disabled, or the stage is not listed The pipeline and the workspaces are permissioned separately. You are missing the pipeline admin permission, or the role on the source or target workspace Get pipeline admin and the workspace roles listed in Permissions and roles , then reload the page \"Can't assign the workspace\" An adjacent stage holds two items of the same type and n"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/troubleshooting.html#items","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"Item duplicated or missing","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix A second copy of an item appears in the target stage The items were not paired: the target item was created by hand or added after the workspace was assigned, or the source item was deleted and recreated Delete the unpaired copy in the target, deploy again, and check pairing first next time. See pairing consequences The first deployment of a Power BI item fails The target stage already holds "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/troubleshooting.html#wrong-source","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"Reads the wrong source","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix A notebook in TST reads the DEV lakehouse It was deployed before its lakehouse existed in the target, the lakehouse is in another workspace (not autobound), or no default lakehouse rule is set Deploy the lakehouse first and redeploy the notebook, or set a default lakehouse rule. See Autobinding A rule is set but the model still reads the source stage Rules apply only at the next deployment of"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/troubleshooting.html#empty-data","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"Empty or stale data","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix Tables are missing or empty after a deployment A deployment copies definitions only, never data Run the layer's loads, then the verify gate ( three gates ) Report visuals are empty after deploying a model or dataflow The data of models and dataflows is not copied Refresh the dataflow, then the semantic model, in the target stage A model refresh fails in the target Credentials are not copied, "},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/troubleshooting.html#escalate","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"When to escalate","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"You have the deployment ID, the history entry, the failed item list and the note. You have confirmed the pair, the permissions and the active value set, and the problem persists. The behaviour contradicts the page you were following. Check the status of the Learn article, because some of these features are in preview. Open a support request or a community post with the deployment ID, and describe what you did in orde"},{"u":"playbooks/14-deployment-checklist/troubleshooting.html#refs","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"References","p":"Deployment Checklist","x":"Troubleshoot the Fabric lifecycle management tools The deployment pipelines process Create deployment rules Deployment history"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Playbook 15 · QA Validation QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines A reusable, notebook-based way to prove that data survived each hop of a medallion pipeline: counts reconcile, transformations did what the specification says, schemas are intact, data is fresh and business metrics add up. Every check ends as a row in a results table that a pipeline can gate on, an alert can watch and a report can trend. A"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#what-you-get","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"What the framework gives you","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Most teams start validation the same way: someone writes an ad hoc notebook that compares two row counts after a deployment, eyeballs the output and moves on. It catches the first regression and then rots, because nothing records the result, nothing stops a bad load from being promoted and nobody can tell whether last week's check ran at all. This playbook describes the opposite approach: a small, deliberate framewor"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#three-frameworks","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"Three frameworks, one pattern","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The playbook documents three variants of the same pattern. They differ in how much business logic each check has to restate, not in plumbing. Architecture and design principles compares them in detail and explains how to choose. Framework Use it when Typical checks Complex metric validation (workforce headcount and FTE) A Gold table is the result of windowed aggregation, joins and derived measures that must be reprod"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#when-to-use","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"When to use it, and when not to","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Good fit Layer-to-layer promotion (Bronze to Silver, Silver to Gold) with transformations you can restate in SQL Release gates in a DEV to TST to PRD promotion path Regression suites that run after every load or every deployment Teams that want evidence in a table rather than a pasted result in a ticket Reconciling a rebuilt Gold layer against the data it replaced (see Power BI to Fabric Migration for report-level UA"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#scope","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"Scope and prerequisites","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#in-scope","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"In scope","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Layer-to-layer validation of Silver-to-Gold transformations, with the same patterns applied to Bronze-to-Silver. Validation types: row count, transformation, schema, freshness and complex metric checks, extended here with uniqueness, completeness, referential integrity, distribution drift, layer reconciliation and late-arriving data. Environments: built and tuned in DEV, then promoted unchanged to TST and PRD with en"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#out-of-scope","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"Out of scope","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Performance and load testing. The UAT sign-off process and test-case management (covered in playbook 01 ). Designing the medallion layers themselves (covered in Medallion Architecture )."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#prerequisites","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"Prerequisites","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Workspaces and lakehouses for the layers you validate (for example DEV_HR_SLV with LH_HR_SLV and DEV_HR_GLD with LH_HR_GLD ) and a place for results, ideally a small schema-enabled lakehouse in a dedicated QA workspace. The identity that runs the notebook can read the source and target tables and write to the results lakehouse. In a pipeline that identity comes from the Notebook activity's connection. A Fabric Spark "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#layers","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"Where checks sit in the medallion flow","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A check is a statement about the relationship between two relations (or between one relation and a contract). Place checks on the hop where the relationship is created, so a failure points at the transformation that broke it. Schema and freshness checks are cheap enough to run on every hop. Checks follow the transformation. Row-count and key checks guard the cleansing hop; parity and referential checks guard the mode"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#outcomes","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"What good looks like","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Design Pick the framework, define checks, tolerances and severities with the data owner. Build Write the notebook, run it in DEV, and prove each check can fail. Orchestrate Call it from the pipeline, gate promotion on the exit value, alert on blockers. Operate Trend results, retire noisy checks, govern change like any other code. Every promoted dataset has at least one row-count, one schema and one freshness check, a"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#map","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"How the playbook is organized","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"02 Architecture and design principles Frameworks, type matrix, principles, workspace layout, naming and four-part names. 03 Results model and tolerances Result and run tables, statuses, severities, tolerance design. 04 Harness and core checks The notebook skeleton plus row count, transformation, schema and freshness. 05 Complex and extended checks Metric reconciliation, uniqueness, nulls, referential integrity, drift"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/index.html#refs","t":"QA validation frameworks for Fabric data pipelines","s":"References","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Implement medallion lakehouse architecture in Fabric What are lakehouse schemas? Develop, execute, and manage notebooks NotebookUtils for Fabric Apache Spark runtimes in Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Architecture and design principles","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Playbook 15 · QA Validation Architecture and design principles The three framework variants, what each can check, the four principles that keep the notebooks simple and safe to run in parallel, how the workspaces fit together, and the naming rules that decide whether a table reference resolves. The naming section includes the Fabric specifics that cause most first-run failures."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#frameworks","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"The three frameworks","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"All three frameworks share the same plumbing (parameters, helpers, configuration list, runner, results writer, exit value). What differs is the kind of statement each check has to make about the data, and therefore how much domain logic you restate in SQL. Pick by the hardest thing you need to prove, not by table count."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#framework-a","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Framework A: complex metric validation (workforce headcount and FTE)","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Purpose. Prove that a Gold measure that is the product of windowing, joins and aggregation equals what an independent calculation from Silver produces. The running example is a workforce fact table where full-time-equivalent (FTE) is hours worked divided by a standard number of hours per period. Rolling fiscal-year window. The window is derived from an as-of date and the fiscal calendar, not typed in. See dynamic fis"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#framework-b","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Framework B: standard validation suite","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Purpose. Broad, cheap coverage for tables that are copied or lightly transformed between layers. Three or four check types applied consistently beat one clever check applied to one table. Row count between source and target, with a stated filter when the pipeline legitimately drops rows. Transformation checks for derived columns such as customer_label = customer_code || ' - ' || customer_name . Schema contract for ta"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#framework-c","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Framework C: financial statement reconciliation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Purpose. Validate fact and dimension tables behind a financial statement where totals have to tie out and the data has to be recent and well-formed. Row count and transformation as in framework B, with null checks on the transformed column. Freshness against a load timestamp with an explicit service level. Integrity rules : period labels match a format ( YYYY-MM ), flags only hold allowed values (0 or 1), optional fl"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#type-matrix","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Validation type matrix","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The first six rows are the types each framework implements. The remaining rows are common additions that apply to all three; Complex and extended checks gives their implementation. Validation type A. Complex metric B. Standard suite C. Statement reconciliation Page Row count Yes Yes Yes Core checks Transformation Yes Yes Yes Core checks Schema contract Calendar tables Yes Yes Core checks Freshness No No Yes Core chec"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#principles","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Design principles","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#p-self-contained","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"1. Self-contained notebooks","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Each framework is one notebook that carries its own helpers, configuration and writer. It imports only what the Fabric runtime already provides ( pyspark , the standard library, notebookutils ). The reasons are operational: deployment is a single item, a review shows the whole behavior in one diff, and a notebook can be run from a pipeline or interactively without checking that a package or configuration file is pres"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#p-sql-first","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"2. SQL first","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"State the comparison in Spark SQL and let the engine execute it. Python receives a handful of numbers (counts, sums, violation totals) and a small sample of offending rows; it never receives the data. A common misconception about speed Calling DataFrame.count() and running SELECT COUNT(*) compile to the same Spark plan, so switching between them does not make a check faster. What makes checks fast is scanning each ta"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#p-temp-views","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"3. The temporary-view pattern","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A check names the relations it compares as session-scoped temporary views whose names carry a random suffix, runs its SQL against those names, and drops them in a finally block. A view is just a named query, so creating one reads no data; the point is to define the filtered or projected relation once, keep the comparison SQL short, and make the statements easy to run by hand. Unique names matter whenever more than on"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#p-results","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"4. Structured results","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Every check returns one or more Result objects with the same fields, and the notebook writes them in a single append to a Delta table. The fields are designed so that a failure can be understood from the row alone: what was compared, expected versus actual, the variance, the tolerance applied and the rule that decided the status. Results model and tolerances defines the schema. Fail closed A check that cannot run (mi"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#stack","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Technology stack and workspace configuration","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Component Technology Notes Platform Microsoft Fabric Lakehouses for data, a Spark notebook for checks, a Data Factory pipeline for orchestration, a semantic model for reporting. Compute Fabric Spark runtime Runtime 2.0 is Spark 4.1 with Delta 4.2 GA and is the runtime to prefer for new work. Runtime 1.3 is Spark 3.5 with Delta 3.2, and its release stage on the runtime page is end of support announced. Write checks th"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#workspaces","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Workspace configuration","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Keep validation in its own workspace (or at least its own lakehouse) so that checks never write into the layers they read and so that read-only access to the data layers is enough. The names below follow the house convention; replace HR with your domain and the environment prefix with TST or PRD . Workspace Item Role Access for the run identity DEV_HR_SLV LH_HR_SLV Source: Silver tables Read DEV_HR_GLD LH_HR_GLD Targ"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#data-flow","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Data flow","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"One notebook run is one pass through six stages. Nothing is written to the data layers; the only writes are the results and run tables. Anatomy of a run. Only the runner touches the data layers, and it only reads. The writer and the exit value are the two outputs everything downstream depends on."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#naming","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Naming conventions and fully qualified names","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#object-names","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Object names","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Workspaces follow <ENV>_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER>[_<Qualifier>] and items follow <ARTIFACT>_<DOMAIN>_<LAYER>_<Function> , as in the rest of this site ( DEV_HR_SLV , LH_HR_GLD , NB_HR_GLD_QaComplexMetrics ). For the tables themselves, avoid encoding the layer, the source system or the pipeline in the table name: the layer is already in the workspace and lakehouse name. Using the same logical table name in Silver and Gold where"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#four-part","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Four-part names and cross-workspace queries","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A table in a schema-enabled lakehouse is addressed as workspace.lakehouse.schema.table . Omitted parts are resolved from the notebook's defaults, so a bare table name means the dbo schema of the default lakehouse. Because validation reads two lakehouses that usually live in two other workspaces, always write all four parts in check configuration. Target Spark SQL name Notes Schema-enabled lakehouse, any workspace you"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#sql-dialects","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"T-SQL versus Spark SQL","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Validation SQL is mostly portable, but the dialect differences below decide whether a statement written for a lakehouse runs unchanged against a warehouse. When the same check must run on both, keep the check configuration engine-neutral (object names, columns, expressions) and generate the statement per engine. Task Spark SQL (notebook) T-SQL (Warehouse, SQL analytics endpoint) Quote an identifier `name` [name] Name"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#learnings","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"Key learnings","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The name depends on the lakehouse type. Four parts for a schema-enabled lakehouse ( workspace.lakehouse.schema.table ), two or three for one without schemas. Writing dbo explicitly is harmless in both cases and future-proofs the code; leaving it out is only safe for bare names resolved through the default lakehouse. Always include the workspace for cross-lakehouse queries. Without it Spark resolves against the notebo"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/architecture.html#refs","t":"Architecture and design principles","s":"References","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"What are lakehouse schemas? Query the warehouse or SQL analytics endpoint T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse Data Warehouse collation Spark connector for Microsoft Fabric Data Warehouse Apache Spark runtimes in Fabric Runtime 2.0 in Fabric NotebookUtils for Fabric Spark SQL migration guide"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"Results model and tolerances","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Playbook 15 · QA Validation Results model and tolerances Define the shape of a result before you write any check. This page specifies the results and run tables, the status and severity model that drives promotion gates, and how to choose tolerances so that checks fail when the data is wrong and stay quiet when it is merely imperfect."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#expected-actual","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"Every check is expected versus actual","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The most useful design decision in the framework is that every check, however different its SQL, reduces to two numbers and a rule. A row count compares two counts. A uniqueness check expects zero duplicate keys and reports how many there are. A schema check expects zero missing columns. A freshness check expects data no older than a limit and reports its age. Once everything is a pair of numbers, one table, one stat"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#schema","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"The results schema","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Three tables live in the results lakehouse, in a qa schema. Only the first two are required. Data model. One run produces one row in validation_runs and many rows in validation_results , joined on run_id . The optional configuration table is the control table that drives the runner (see Control-Table-Driven Pipelines )."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#results-table","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"validation_results","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Column Type Meaning run_id STRING Identifier shared by all rows of one run. Pass the pipeline run ID when orchestrated; the notebook generates a GUID otherwise. suite , environment STRING The configuration suite that ran and the environment ( DEV , TST , PRD ). test_name STRING Stable, human-readable identifier such as dim_customer.rowcount . Trends and alerts key on it, so renaming a test breaks its history. check_t"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#runs-table","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"validation_runs","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"One row per run, written after the results. It makes \"what was the last run and did it block?\" a one-row lookup instead of an aggregation, and it is what an alert or a dashboard card should read."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#config-table","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"validation_config (optional)","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Hold the check definitions in Python while the suite is small, because the configuration is then reviewed and versioned together with the notebook. Move to a control table when non-developers need to switch checks on and off, when the list is long, or when different environments need different tolerances. The harness page shows both forms."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#status","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"Status and severity","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Status says what happened; severity says how much it matters. Keeping them separate lets the same failing check be a blocker in PRD and a warning in DEV without changing its logic. Status Meaning Set by Counts against the gate? PASS Breach is within the tolerance. evaluate() No WARN Breach is beyond tolerance but within the fail threshold. evaluate() No; reported and alertable FAIL Breach is beyond the fail threshold"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#tolerance","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"Threshold and tolerance design","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#breach","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"How a status is derived","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The evaluator computes variance = actual - expected , converts it to a non-negative breach that depends on the rule, optionally expresses the breach as a percentage of expected, and then compares it with two limits. One scale for every check. Equality checks breach in both directions; a freshness check ( le ) breaches only when data is older than the limit; a completeness check ( ge ) only when the percentage falls s"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#tolerance-guidance","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"Choosing tolerances","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Situation Rule and tolerance Reasoning Row count of a copy or lossless transformation eq , 0 Any difference is a defect. A tolerance here hides lost rows. Row count where the pipeline deliberately filters or dedupes eq , 0, with the same filter on the expected side Express the intended drop in the check (see reject accounting ) rather than loosening the tolerance. Sum of decimals recalculated independently eq , absol"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#reject-accounting","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"Reject accounting instead of fuzzy counts","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"When a pipeline legitimately drops rows (duplicates, rows failing a quality rule, late data routed to a quarantine table), do not compare raw counts with a loose tolerance. State the equation: expected = source rows - duplicates - rejected rows , where each term is its own count from the same snapshot. The check then passes with a tolerance of zero when the pipeline does what it claims, and fails when it drops anythi"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#precision","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"Precision and timing pitfalls","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Doubles. The results table stores numbers as doubles, which are exact for integers up to about 9 x 10 15 and for decimals to roughly 15 significant digits. For balances beyond that, compute the variance in SQL as a decimal, compare there and store the comparison outcome. Data in flight. A check that runs while a load is writing may read the source at one version and the target at another. Run validation after the loa"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#queries","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"Reading the results","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"These queries are the contract that dashboards and alerts build on. They run in a notebook SQL cell or in the lakehouse SQL editor. Illustrative rows for the first query might look like this: test_name check_type severity status expected actual variance message fact_orders.rowcount rowcount blocker FAIL 1,204,331 1,204,299 -32 source=1,204,331 target=1,204,299 dim_customer.label transformation blocker FAIL 0 5 5 miss"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#maintenance","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"Retention and maintenance","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Append-only. Never update or delete result rows to \"fix\" a history; add a new run instead. Small files. One append per run creates small files. Schedule OPTIMIZE on both tables, and VACUUM within your retention policy, as part of regular lakehouse table maintenance. Retention. Keep detailed results for as long as you need trends and audit evidence, and keep validation_runs indefinitely; it is tiny. Evolution. Add col"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/results-model.html#refs","t":"Results model and tolerances","s":"References","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"What are lakehouse schemas? Delta Lake table optimization and V-Order Lakehouse table maintenance (OPTIMIZE and VACUUM) Direct Lake overview Delta Lake constraints"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Harness and core checks","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Playbook 15 · QA Validation Harness and core checks The notebook skeleton that every framework shares, followed by the four checks that give the broadest coverage for the least effort: row count, transformation, schema and freshness. Each check has its purpose, copy-ready code, success criteria and example output. Paste the cells into a notebook in order and they run as a complete standard suite. Before you paste The"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#harness","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"The notebook harness","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Six cells, in this order. Cells 1 to 3 are the same in every framework; cell 4 holds the checks and is where frameworks differ; cells 5 and 6 run the suite, write results and return the exit value."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#cell-parameters","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Cell 1: parameters","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Mark this cell with Toggle parameter cell in the cell menu. A pipeline's Notebook activity overrides these values through its base parameters; the notebook runs with the defaults when you execute it interactively. Parameters support simple types (string, int, float, bool), which is why anything structured is passed as JSON text."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#cell-helpers","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Cell 2: helpers, result type and evaluator","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#cell-registry","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Cell 3: check registry and runner","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A check is a function that takes one configuration dictionary and returns a Result or a list of them. The decorator registers it under its check_type . The runner turns any exception into an ERROR result, runs independent checks in parallel, and runs checks that declare depends_on only if the check they depend on passed (one level of dependency is supported)."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#config","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Cell 4a: configuration","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Configuration is a list of dictionaries. Fully qualified names are built from env , so the same notebook runs in every environment. Keys common to all checks are suite , test_name , check_type , source and target ; optional keys are severity , rule , tolerance , tolerance_type , fail_threshold , enabled and depends_on . Everything else is specific to the check type. Where the configuration lives Choose it when Trade-"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#rowcount","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Row count validation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#rowcount-purpose","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Purpose","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Confirm that the target holds exactly the rows the transformation is supposed to keep. It is the cheapest check, catches the largest class of defects (truncated loads, failed merges, join fan-out and fan-in, unintended filters) and should be on every table."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#rowcount-impl","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Implementation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Both sides are defined as temporary views so that optional filters apply to each relation once. One statement counts both, so the two numbers come from one planning step. The min_rows guard exists because 0 = 0 passes an equality check: an empty source and an empty target are consistent and almost always wrong."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#rowcount-criteria","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Success criteria","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Target count equals the source count after the stated filters (or the tolerance in the configuration). Both relations have at least min_rows rows when the guard is set. No technical error reading either table."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#rowcount-output","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Example output","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"When the count is off, find the rows A failing count is a symptom. Put a second, dependent check behind it that anti-joins on the business key ( LEFT ANTI JOIN in Spark SQL) and stores a sample of missing keys in detail . Troubleshooting has the diagnostic queries."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#transformation","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Transformation validation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#transformation-purpose","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Purpose","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Prove that a derived column in the target equals the expression applied to the source, for every row. Typical cases are concatenated labels, code-to-description mappings, trimmed or case-normalized text, and simple arithmetic."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#transformation-impl","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Implementation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The check restates the transformation as a SQL expression over the source, then joins the result to the target on the business key and counts three kinds of disagreement. Casting both sides to text makes the comparison independent of numeric types; use the metric reconciliation check when you need tolerance on numbers. Keyed comparison. Every row ends in exactly one bucket. Because the join is keyed, the failure is a"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#transformation-criteria","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Success criteria","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The target column exists (a missing column surfaces as an ERROR , and the schema check says which one). No key is missing on either side and no value differs. The target column has no unexpected NULLs: null_targets is reported so a NULL that the expression would not produce is visible. The expression in the configuration matches the specification, not the implementation. If you copy the pipeline's own code into the c"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#transformation-output","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Example output","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#schema","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Schema validation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#schema-purpose","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Purpose","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Confirm that the columns downstream consumers depend on are present with the expected types. A renamed or retyped column breaks semantic models and reports without touching a single row count, so this is the check that catches contract drift between teams."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#schema-impl","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Implementation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The check reads the table's schema from the catalog and Delta log, so it does not scan data and costs almost nothing. Names are compared case-insensitively. Types are compared when the configuration states one. With strict set, columns that are not in the contract are reported too; leave it off for tables that are allowed to grow."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#schema-criteria","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Success criteria","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Every contracted column exists in the target. Every contracted type matches (when stated). With strict , no uncontracted columns exist. Derived columns that were added by transformation logic (flags, labels, sort keys) are part of the contract, not an afterthought."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#schema-output","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Example output","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Generate contracts, do not type them For wide tables, generate the column list once from the approved table ( spark.table(...).schema ), review it, and commit it. Typing 35 column names by hand introduces its own defects. If a semantic model reads the table, the contract is the list of columns the model uses."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#freshness","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Freshness validation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#freshness-purpose","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Purpose","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Confirm that data arrived on time. A pipeline can succeed, counts can match and the data can still be a day old because a source stopped delivering. Freshness is also the check that turns \"the load ran\" into \"the data is current\"."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#freshness-impl","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Implementation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The check computes the age of the newest value in a timestamp column, in hours, and compares it with a limit. It is a le check: expected is the allowed age, actual is the observed age, and only an older table breaches. A fail_threshold adds a WARN band so that the team hears about a late load before consumers do. Which timestamp? A column the pipeline stamps ( loaded_at ) proves the pipeline ran. A column carried fro"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#freshness-criteria","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Success criteria","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The newest timestamp is within max_age_hours of the run (PASS), or within the WARN band (WARN). The column has at least one non-NULL value; an all-NULL or empty column is an ERROR , not a pass. NULL timestamps are counted and reported in detail ; add a completeness check if any NULL is unacceptable."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#freshness-output","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Example output","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#freshness-pitfalls","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Freshness pitfalls","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Time zones. current_timestamp() is an instant, and Spark compares instants. The risk is a column that stores local wall-clock time as if it were UTC. Store timestamps as UTC, and if a legacy column cannot be changed, document the offset next to the check. Calendars. A 6-hour limit fails every weekend for a source that does not deliver on weekends. Pass max_age_hours from the pipeline, vary it by day, or schedule the "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#run","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"Run, write and exit","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Cell 5 selects the suite, runs it and writes both tables. Cell 6 hands a compact summary to the pipeline, and is deliberately its own cell because notebookutils.notebook.exit overwrites that cell's output and ends the run. Dependencies keep failures honest. A keyed comparison on a table with duplicate keys fails in confusing ways. Running the uniqueness check first, and skipping the dependent check with a recorded re"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#tsql","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"T-SQL variant for a Warehouse","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"When the target is a Warehouse table, the same checks can run in the warehouse itself with T-SQL, for example from a Script activity or a T-SQL notebook. Three-part names work within one workspace; expose Silver tables from other workspaces through a OneLake shortcut first. Writing the results to a warehouse table is an INSERT ; a lakehouse results table remains the better default because it feeds Direct Lake directl"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/core-checks.html#refs","t":"Harness and core checks","s":"References","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Develop, execute, and manage notebooks (parameter cell, reference runs) NotebookUtils for Fabric ( notebook.exit , runMultiple ) What are lakehouse schemas? Query the warehouse or SQL analytics endpoint Data Warehouse collation Spark SQL reference PySpark API reference"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Complex and extended checks","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Playbook 15 · QA Validation Complex and extended checks The first half covers the checks that make framework A heavier than the standard suite: recalculating a windowed, aggregated metric independently, validating enrichment joins, sentinel rows and derived calendar columns. The second half adds the checks that every framework should have and the source frameworks lacked: uniqueness, completeness, referential integri"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#complex","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Complex metric validation (framework A)","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The running example is a workforce fact table in the HR domain. Silver holds timesheet entries ( timesheet_entries : employee, department, period, hours). Gold holds fact_workforce_fte , where full-time equivalent (FTE) is the hours worked in a fiscal period divided by the standard hours in a period, restricted to a rolling window of fiscal years. The validation recomputes that table from Silver and compares it key b"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#fiscal-window","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Dynamic fiscal-year filtering","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Gold tables for planning and workforce reporting are usually windowed: current fiscal year, a year or two back for comparison, sometimes a year ahead for budget. The validation has to apply the same window to the Silver recalculation. Typing the year range into the check (for example a literal range of three fiscal years) makes the check correct on the day it is written and wrong when the fiscal year rolls over, and "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#metric","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Aggregation logic and the metric check","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The check applies the Gold table's aggregation to Silver at a stated grain (the set of keys that identifies one Gold row), applies the same window to both sides, aggregates Gold at the same grain (so extra detail columns in Gold cannot cause false differences), and joins the two on the grain with a full outer join. It reports three things for each measure: grain rows present on only one side, grain rows whose measure"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#enrichment","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Enrichment-join coverage","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A fact is often enriched by a left join to an attribute table, for example employee tenure bands taken from the latest headcount snapshot. Two things can go wrong without changing any single-table count in an obvious way: the lookup has duplicate keys and the join multiplies rows (fan-out), or the join silently stops matching (a changed key format, an empty snapshot) and the attribute becomes NULL for most rows. A ch"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#sentinel","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Sentinel-row (unknown member) union","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Dimensions commonly union one synthetic row (key 0, description \"Unknown\") to the real rows so that facts with missing references still join. The expected row count is the source count plus one, and exactly one sentinel must exist. Two failure modes matter: the sentinel is missing (facts drop out of reports) and it is duplicated (every join against it doubles). Configure it with \"sentinel_predicate\": \"project_key = 0"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#derived","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Derived calendar columns","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Labels and flags that were authored in a dataflow or in Power Query M (a period label, a \"past period\" flag, a default sort order, current-year flags) are easy to get subtly wrong when they are re-implemented, and they are easy to validate: restate each as a SQL expression and run the keyed transformation check on it. The wrapper below expands one configuration row into one transformation check per column. Time-depen"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#extended","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Extended checks for every framework","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"These checks apply to all three frameworks. They are cheap, catch defects that count comparisons miss, and share the same result shape. The table summarizes them; code follows. Check Catches Cost Default severity Key uniqueness Duplicate business keys, failed deduplication, join fan-out One group-by scan blocker Completeness NULLs in columns that must be populated, broken joins One scan for many columns blocker for k"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#keys","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Key uniqueness","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Expected is zero surplus rows: the number of rows beyond the first for each key value. Run this before any keyed comparison and declare the keyed checks as depends_on it, so duplicates produce one red result instead of many confusing ones. NULL key values are grouped together by GROUP BY , so a table with many NULL keys reports them as one large duplicate. That is the right signal, and the completeness check below re"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#completeness","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Null and completeness","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"One scan computes the NULL count of every listed column. Each column becomes its own result, so a dashboard can show which column regressed. The rule is ge : expected is the minimum percent populated (100 for keys, lower for optional attributes), derived from the source rather than guessed."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#integrity","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Format and domain integrity rules","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A rule is a SQL predicate that is true for valid rows. The check counts rows for which the predicate is not true. NULL evaluates to \"not true\", so write col IS NULL OR ... when NULL is allowed; that makes nullability an explicit part of the rule rather than an accident of three-valued logic."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#ri","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Referential integrity","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Spark does not enforce foreign keys, so a fact can reference a dimension row that does not exist. The check counts fact rows whose key is missing from the dimension. The dimension side is de-duplicated first so a duplicate dimension key cannot multiply fact rows. NULL foreign keys are reported separately because whether they are acceptable is a modeling decision (often they should point at the sentinel row instead). "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#drift","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Distribution drift","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Some defects keep every count intact and change what the data means: a unit conversion applied twice, a currency column loaded in the wrong scale, an upstream system that starts sending zeros. The drift check compares a statistic (an average, a NULL percentage, a distinct count) with the mean and spread of the same statistic in recent runs, read from the results table itself. There is no separate baseline store to ma"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#reconcile","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Reconciliation between layers","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A row-count comparison between Bronze and Silver is rarely meaningful on its own, because the Silver pipeline deduplicates and quarantines rows. Reconciliation states the equation: Bronze rows minus duplicates minus rejected rows equals Silver rows. Each term is a scalar query, and the check compares the two sides with the same tolerance logic as every other check. Because each term is explicit, a failure tells you w"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#late","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Late-arriving data","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Late-arriving rows are normal in event and transaction feeds. They become a defect when they arrive after the period they belong to was closed and published, because reports already issued no longer match the table. The check measures two things over a recent ingest window: the share of rows that arrived later than an allowed lag, and, optionally, how many arrived for a period that is already closed. What to do about"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#bi-parity","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"Semantic model parity","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A correct Gold table can still produce a wrong report: a measure with a filter nobody remembers, a relationship that fans out, a model that was never refreshed. A parity check evaluates a measure in the semantic model and compares it with an aggregate computed straight from the lakehouse. Semantic link runs the DAX query from the notebook, so the comparison fits the same harness and the same results table. Run it aft"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/advanced-checks.html#refs","t":"Complex and extended checks","s":"References","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Spark SQL reference ( EXCEPT ALL , count_if , timestampdiff , parameter markers) What are lakehouse schemas? NotebookUtils for Fabric (variable library utilities) Delta Lake constraints (per-row alternatives to rule checks) Data quality in materialized lake views Semantic link (sempy.fabric) API reference"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Playbook 15 · QA Validation Orchestration, gating and reporting A validation suite that nobody runs, or whose failure nobody acts on, is documentation. This page connects the notebooks to the rest of the platform: how a pipeline runs them and reads the verdict, how a failed gate stops promotion, how the right people hear about it, and how results are reported over time."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#patterns","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"Ways to run a suite","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Pattern How Use it when Watch out for Pipeline Notebook activity One activity per suite, parameters in, exit value out Default for scheduled and post-load validation, and for gating Each activity starts a Spark session unless sessions are shared (see scheduling ) Parent notebook with runMultiple notebookutils.notebook.runMultiple runs several QA notebooks as a DAG in one Spark session Several suites for the same rele"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#pipeline","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"The validation pipeline","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The standard shape is a short pipeline named per the house convention (for example PL_SALES_GLD_RunQa ) with a Notebook activity, an If Condition on its exit value, and notification and failure activities. It can run on its own schedule, be invoked by the data pipeline that loaded the data, or be invoked as a spoke by an orchestration hub (see Cross-Workspace Pipeline Orchestration ). Write, audit, publish. The load "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#gate","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"Gating promotion on results","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A gate is a decision with a cost: a false block delays a release, a false pass ships bad data. Three ways to implement it: Approach Mechanism Strengths Weaknesses Exit value and If Condition The notebook exits with a JSON summary; the pipeline parses blocking_failures Explicit, richer than pass or fail, no dependency on any table being readable Keep the payload small; confirm exit values propagate for your notebook t"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#release-gate","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"Release gates across environments","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The same suite can gate a promotion from TST to PRD. Deploy to TST, run the QA pipeline there, and let the release continue only if the run completed. Deployment pipelines deploy items, not data, so each environment keeps its own results tables, and a QA notebook promoted with the release finds its own environment's tables through the env parameter (see Deployment Pipelines and Promotion and the Deployment Checklist "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#alerts","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"Alerting on failure","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Alert on state changes that need a human : the gate going from open to blocked, an ERROR in a blocker, or a freshness failure. Send a digest for WARN results rather than a message per check, or the channel becomes noise and gets muted, which is worse than no alert. Channel How Good for Limits to know Teams activity Pipeline activity posts to a channel or group chat, message built with expressions Team-visible blocked"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#reporting","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"Reporting results in a semantic model","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Notifications tell you something is wrong now. A report tells you whether the suite is healthy over time, which checks are flaky, which tables drift, and what evidence exists for an audit. Build it on the results tables, not on notebook output. Reporting path. The notebook only writes Delta tables. A semantic model reads them, a refresh at the end of the pipeline keeps it current, and an alert can watch a card on the"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#model","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"Model design","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Tables. Import validation_runs and validation_results , plus a date table. Relate validation_results[run_id] to validation_runs[run_id] (one to many). That only works if run_id is unique per suite execution, which is why the pipeline appends the suite name to it. Storage mode. Use Direct Lake: the results are Delta tables in a lakehouse, and Microsoft's guidance favors Direct Lake on OneLake for new models. Direct La"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#report","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"Report pages worth building","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Status. Cards for the latest gate and blocking failures per suite and environment; a table of failing checks in the latest run (filter Is In Latest Run equal to 1) with message and variance. Trend. Pass rate and blocking failures by day, split by suite; average check duration to spot cost creep. Check health. Checks that flip between PASS and FAIL (the flaky-check query from the results page), checks that have never "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#scheduling","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"Scheduling and capacity","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Run the cheap suite often and the expensive suite when it matters. Validation is Spark work and consumes the same capacity as the pipelines it protects, so place it deliberately. Suite Trigger Why Smoke (counts, schema, freshness) After every load, in the loading pipeline Cheap, and it is the gate for publishing the new data Full (metrics, reconciliation, referential, rules) Nightly, or after the day's last load Heav"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/orchestration.html#refs","t":"Orchestration, gating and reporting","s":"References","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Transform data by running a notebook If Condition activity Fail activity Teams activity Office 365 Outlook activity Semantic model refresh activity Refresh SQL analytics endpoint activity Expressions and functions in pipelines NotebookUtils for Fabric ( runMultiple , exit ) Configure high concurrency mode for notebooks in pipelines Direct Lake overview What is Fabric Activator? Job Scheduler: Run On Demand Item Job"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Governance and troubleshooting","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Playbook 15 · QA Validation Governance and troubleshooting A validation framework earns trust slowly and loses it quickly. This page covers what keeps it trustworthy: who owns what, how checks change, how the framework itself is tested and versioned, what it costs to run, and how to diagnose it when a verdict is missing, wrong or slow."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#governance","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Ownership and roles","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The roles below are generic. One person can hold several, and in a small team all of them. What matters is that each decision has exactly one accountable role, written down, so that a tolerance is never relaxed by whoever happens to be annoyed by a red run. Role Responsible for Typically held by Framework owner The harness, the results schema, the framework version, the self-test suite and the reporting model Platfor"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#change-control","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Change control","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Treat checks and configuration as production code, because a changed check changes what the platform is allowed to publish. They live in source control, change through pull requests (see GitHub Integration and Branching ) and reach TST and PRD through the same promotion path as everything else (see Deployment Pipelines and Promotion ). Where the harness is shared across workspaces, package it as a library rather than"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#lifecycle","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Check lifecycle","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"New checks rarely belong in front of a release on day one. A check that blocks releases and turns out to be wrong damages trust in all checks. Introduce it quietly, watch it, and promote it when the evidence supports it. Promotion by evidence. The text under each stage is the exit criterion. The demotion path matters as much as the promotion path: a flaky blocker must go back to shadow while it is fixed, not be muted"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#adding","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Adding a validation","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"State the claim in one sentence For example, \"every order that is not void in Silver exists once in Gold\". If you cannot, the check has no defined expected value yet. Pick the check type and the severity Use the type matrix to choose. Start as info unless the data owner already knows the check is stable. Write the expected and actual queries Both must reduce to one number each, built from source-side facts, not from "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#selftest","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Testing the framework itself","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The quality of the verdicts is the quality of the framework. After every harness change, and when a check is added, run each check type against a copy of a clean table and against copies with a known defect injected. Each must return PASS on the clean copy and the expected status on the damaged copy. Run this only in DEV and write only to a scratch schema. Injected defect Check that must catch it About 1 % of rows re"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#versioning","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Versioning","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Artifact How it is versioned Rule Harness (cells 1-3, runner, writer) FRAMEWORK_VERSION in semantic versioning, written to every row of validation_runs Patch: bug fix with no change in verdicts. Minor: new check type or optional field. Major: changed meaning of a status, rule or tolerance, or a changed results schema. Check configuration Source control history, or Delta history for a control table Every change tracea"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#blocked","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"When the gate blocks","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Read the alert and open the run The alert carries environment, suite, run id and counts. Query the failing rows for that run. Classify the failure A data defect, an environment problem ( ERROR , SKIPPED ), a defective check (false positive), or an accepted deviation. Each has a different owner and a different fix. Act on the class Data defect: fix upstream, reload, rerun the suite. Environment: fix and rerun the suit"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#performance","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Performance and cost","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Validation competes with the pipelines it protects. Almost all of its cost is scanning large tables, plus Spark session start-up. Measure before you optimize: duration_s in the results table is the baseline. Durations of checks that ran in parallel overlap, so their sum overstates the wall-clock time of the run. Use the run's started_at and finished_at for that. Lever What to do Caution Scan less For a per-load check"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#troubleshooting","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Troubleshooting","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Sort the symptom first. It usually belongs to one of three families: no verdict at all (an environment problem), a verdict you do not believe (a check design problem), or a verdict that costs too much (a scan problem). Triage first. Each family has its own tables below."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#ts-env","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"No verdict: environment and naming","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix Table or view not found, or schema not found, for a four-part name The notebook's default lakehouse is not schema-enabled, so schema-qualified names do not resolve as expected; the target lakehouse has no schemas; or a workspace or lakehouse name containing a space or hyphen is not back-quoted Use schema-enabled lakehouses (or no default lakehouse) when you address schemas in code, build name"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#ts-verdict","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Verdicts you do not believe","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix A check passes on obviously wrong data Empty source and target ( 0 = 0 ), a filter that removes everything, a table compared with itself, or a tolerance wider than the defect Set min_rows , review the configuration, run the self-test and compare both sides' row counts in the message. The same data gives different verdicts on different days Expressions that use the current date, late-arriving "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#ts-ops","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Slow, stale or silent","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix The suite takes much longer than it used to A table grew, a plan changed, small files accumulated, or concurrency is oversubscribed Compare duration_s by check over time, then see the performance levers . Session start dominates a short suite Every activity starts its own session Use a session tag with high concurrency mode, or run suites together with runMultiple . A query through the SQL an"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#diagnostics","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Diagnostic queries","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A failing count is a symptom; these find the rows. Use the same filters as the check, and keep the results to a sample."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#pitfalls","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"Common pitfalls","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Pitfall Why it hurts Instead Treating a temporary view as a cache Each use re-runs the query, so \"read once\" designs read many times and can see data that changed in between Use views as names; cache explicitly only where measured to help Replacing COUNT(*) with DataFrame.count() for speed They compile to the same plan, so there is nothing to gain Choose the form that is clearer; spend effort on scan size instead Com"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/operations.html#refs","t":"Governance and troubleshooting","s":"References","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Lakehouse table maintenance (OPTIMIZE and VACUUM) What are lakehouse schemas? Apache Spark runtimes in Fabric NotebookUtils for Fabric Develop, execute, and manage notebooks SQL analytics endpoint metadata sync Delta Lake: review table history Spark SQL: ANSI compliance Semantic Versioning"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Comparison with other tools","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Playbook 15 · QA Validation Comparison with other tools Building your own validation framework is a choice, not a default. Fabric and the open-source ecosystem already cover parts of the problem. This page says what each option does, where it stops, how to decide, and how to combine them so that one results table and one gate remain."},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#landscape","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Where each option works","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The options differ first in when they run. Some act inside the write or refresh and can reject rows. Some run as notebooks or jobs inside your pipeline and can stop a release. Some run in the catalog after the fact and report. Choosing means matching the moment to the failure you are trying to prevent. Different moments, different jobs. Constraints sit inside the write or refresh and can refuse rows. The tools undern"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#native","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Native Fabric and Microsoft options","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#delta-constraints","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Delta constraints","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Delta tables support NOT NULL and CHECK constraints. A write that violates one fails, so bad rows never land. They are the cheapest control there is, because they cost nothing at run time that you schedule or monitor. Good for: invariants of a single row that must never be violated by any writer: required keys, non-negative amounts, allowed ranges. Stops at: one table, one row at a time. A constraint cannot compare t"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#mlv","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Materialized lake view constraints","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"A materialized lake view can declare CHECK constraints with an action for rows that do not satisfy them. ON MISMATCH DROP removes the row and records the count in the view's data quality report. ON MISMATCH FAIL , the default, stops the refresh with an error. If a view has both, the failing action takes precedence. Good for: declarative cleansing in a medallion flow that already uses materialized lake views, with dro"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#profiling","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Profiling in notebooks and Data Wrangler","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Fabric notebooks can profile a DataFrame from the output table (the Inspect pane gives per-column statistics and distributions, limited to the rows the output widget holds, up to 10,000), and Data Wrangler summarizes missing values and column statistics and generates code. These are the right tools for understanding unfamiliar data and for deciding which checks to write. They work on a sample, are interactive, and le"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#purview","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Microsoft Purview data quality","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Purview Unified Catalog can profile Fabric lakehouse tables, apply data quality rules and run scans that produce quality scores for data products, owned by stewards in governance domains rather than by pipeline engineers. It answers \"how healthy is this data product, and is it improving?\" for the whole estate, in a place business owners already use. Prerequisites to plan for: tables in Delta (or Iceberg) format; a Da"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#open-source","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Open-source and dbt options","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"These tools run as code, so they fit the same place in a pipeline as the framework does. None of them is Fabric-specific, and the Fabric documentation covers some of them only lightly. Treat the notes below as a starting point for your own proof of concept, and pin versions. Tool What it is How it runs in Fabric Strengths Watch out Great Expectations Python library: expectations grouped into suites, run by checkpoint"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#when-lib","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Why a library is not automatically better","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Column rules are the easy part. Most libraries excel at \"this column is never null, unique, in this set, in this range\". The expensive defects in the three frameworks of this playbook are different: a Gold measure that no longer equals the Silver aggregate, a window that is off by one period, a join that fans out, a transformation that changes a label. Those need business-specific SQL, which any library can run only "},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#matrix","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Feature comparison","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Option Runs Authored as Cross-table and cross-layer checks Can refuse or stop data History you can report on Owned by non-developers QA framework (this playbook) Notebook in a pipeline SQL and Python configuration Yes Yes, through the gate Yes, own tables With a control table Delta constraints Every write SQL DDL No Yes, the write fails No No MLV constraints View refresh SQL in the view Row rules only Drop rows or fa"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#decision","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Decision table","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Ask in this order. The answers are not exclusive: most platforms end up with constraints on critical tables, the framework as the release gate, and a catalog tool for stewardship. The path tells you what to try first for a given defect. You need to… Reach for Why, and the caveat Never let a negative amount or NULL key land in a table, whoever writes it Delta constraints Enforced at write. Cannot say anything about ot"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#combine","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Combining them behind one gate","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"The cleanest arrangement keeps one results schema and one gate, and treats every other tool as a producer of findings. Native constraints protect the tables, libraries and dbt supply extra column rules, Purview provides stewardship on top, and the framework turns everything that runs in the pipeline into validation_results rows. One schema, many producers. The adapter is the only place that knows another tool's outpu"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#checklist","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"Before you adopt a tool","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Runtime fit. Does the exact version run on the Fabric runtime you use, including the next one? Test it in a notebook or environment before design work. Where results land. Can the output be turned into your results schema, so there is one gate and one report? Can it stop a release? If it only reports, who acts on its findings, and how fast? Identity and permissions. Which identity runs it, and what does it need on th"},{"u":"playbooks/15-qa-validation/tools-comparison.html#refs","t":"Comparison with other tools","s":"References","p":"QA Validation Frameworks","x":"Delta Lake: constraints Spark SQL reference for materialized lake views Data quality in materialized lake views Notebook visualization (Inspect pane and profiling) Accelerate data prep with Data Wrangler Set up data quality for Fabric Lakehouse data Configure and run data profiling Configure and run a data quality scan dbt job in Microsoft Fabric Tutorial: Validate data using SemPy and Great Expectations Great Expect"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Playbook 16 · Tenant Settings Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide Tenant settings are the switchboard that decides which Microsoft Fabric and Power BI features people in your organization can use. This guide explains how the switchboard works, gives you a repeatable way to decide each switch, and documents the settings that matter most with a recommended posture for security-conscious enterprises. Audience F"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#purpose","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"What this guide gives you","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"A new Fabric tenant arrives with defaults chosen for broad adoption. Some of those defaults are fine, some quietly open data paths that a regulated or security-conscious organization would not choose, and a few change in meaning as features mature. Administrators then face dozens of switches with short descriptions and no guidance on who should get each one. This playbook closes that gap in three ways. A mental model"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#where","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"Where tenant settings live","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Tenant settings are managed by someone holding the Fabric Administrator role (assigned in Microsoft 365 or Microsoft Entra ID). Fabric organizes them in groups such as Export and sharing, Integration, Developer, OneLake and Copilot. Open the OneLake catalog Select OneLake catalog , then the Govern tab. Go to Configurations Choose Configurations , then Tenant settings . The same area also surfaces capacity and workspa"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#states","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"The five states of a setting","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Most settings can be shaped with groups. A setting is evaluated for each user in the order shown below. How one setting is evaluated for one user. Three checks decide the outcome. Exception groups always win because the most restrictive setting for a user applies; settings that apply to the entire organization skip the group check. State Meaning Typical use Disabled for the entire organization Nobody gets the feature"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#org-only","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"Settings that apply to the whole organization","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Some settings show a note that they apply to the entire organization and offer no group picker. For these, the toggle is the only control, so the decision is all or nothing. Plan compensating controls such as policy, review and monitoring where you cannot scope them. Block users from reassigning personal workspaces (My Workspace) Create and use Gen1 dataflows Block ResourceKey Authentication Use ArcGIS Maps for Power"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#hierarchy","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"Hierarchy and delegation","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"The tenant is the top of a four-level hierarchy. A Fabric administrator can leave a setting at tenant level, or delegate it so that lower-level administrators can override it for their own scope. Delegation path. The Fabric administrator owns every setting. Where a setting supports delegation, it passes to domain admins or capacity admins (not both for the same setting) and can reach workspace admins; the dashed rout"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#propagation","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"Propagation, audit and the API","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Propagation. Changes can take up to about 15 minutes to reach every user. Some features take longer: Block Public Internet Access can take 10 to 20 minutes. Tell users to sign out and in before testing. The settings API. The Fabric administration API returns every tenant setting with its state and group lists, which makes inventory and drift detection scriptable. See the next table. Audit. Delegation changes and many"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#not-a-boundary","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"Tenant settings are not a security boundary","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"A tenant setting switches a feature on or off for a population. It does not revoke the underlying permission. A user with Read and Build permission on a semantic model can still query it through any path whose own setting is open, and row-level security still filters the result on every path. Each egress path has its own switch. Closing one path does not close the others. Row-level security still filters what each pa"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#reading","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"How the reference pages read","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Each reference page uses the same fields so settings can be compared quickly. Field What it tells you Setting The exact name used in the tenant settings page and index. Where Learn uses two spellings, the alternative is mentioned in the notes. Default On or Off when the documentation states it; Not stated otherwise. Capture your own defaults with the API before you change anything. Typical posture My recommended star"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#map","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"Where to go next","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"02 Decision framework and rollout Security-group pattern, rollout phases and review cadence. 03 Export and sharing Exports, links, publish to web, guests and external data sharing. 04 Content, models and discovery Labels, discovery, apps, visuals, legacy dataflows and datamarts. 05 Integration, developer and APIs XMLA, Execute Queries, service principals and admin APIs. 06 Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items Fabric it"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/index.html#refs","t":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","s":"References","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"About tenant settings Tenant settings index Delegate tenant settings Manage your Fabric capacity: delegated tenant settings Tenants: List Tenant Settings (Fabric REST API) What is the admin portal?"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Decision framework and rollout","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Playbook 16 · Tenant Settings Decision framework and rollout A repeatable method for deciding each tenant setting, enabling it through security groups, piloting it and keeping it reviewed, so that every switch has an owner, a reason and a way back. Audience Fabric administrators, governance leads Outcome Decided, owned, reviewable settings Pattern Allow group plus exception group"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#principles","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Principles","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Least exposure for egress, generous guardrails. Anything that moves data, code or tokens outside governed stores starts disabled or scoped. Anything that protects (labels, certified-visual enforcement, workspace guardrails) starts enabled for everyone. Decide capabilities, not toggles. \"Bulk data extraction\" is one decision that spans export, Analyze in Excel, XMLA, Execute Queries and download settings. Decide the f"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#risk-classes","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Classify settings by risk","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Seven classes cover almost every setting. The class sets the starting posture; the use case decides whether to depart from it. Class What it covers Starting posture Examples A. Egress and external exposure Data leaves governed stores or the tenant. Specific groups, or disable Publish to web , organization-wide links , CSV export , download reports , external data sharing , guest settings, external OneLake apps B. Ide"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#decision-path","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"The decision path","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Apply three questions in order. If the setting has no egress and no new identity, enable it for all. Otherwise ask whether there is an approved use case with a named owner; if not, disable it. If there is, enable for specific groups when scoping is supported. When scoping is not supported, either find a compensating control or leave the setting off. Decision path. Three questions place most settings. Anything that fa"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#worked-example","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Worked example: Export to .csv","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"A retailer (Contoso) wants analysts to continue exporting sales detail to CSV for reconciliation, but not store-employee or payroll data. The decision record looks like this. Setting Export to .csv, Export and sharing settings Class A, egress. CSV has no label-based access control on the exported file. Owner Data governance lead Decision Enabled for specific groups: SG-FAB-ExportCsv-Users , except SG-FAB-Restricted-D"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#security-groups","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Enable through security groups","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"For any setting you enable for specific groups, use a pair of Microsoft Entra security groups: an allow group that carries the capability and an exception group that carves out restricted populations. The tenant setting lists the allow group under Apply to and the exception group under Except specific security groups . Security-group enablement pattern. Names are examples. One pair of groups per capability keeps owne"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#naming","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Naming and ownership","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Group Purpose Membership Owner SG-FAB-<Capability>-Users Allow list for one capability, such as ExportCsv , XmlaClients , GitSync , CopilotPilot . Access package with approval and expiry, or dynamic rule on a job attribute. Business capability owner SG-FAB-<Capability>-Exceptions People who must never get it, regardless of other membership. Rule-based where possible, for example a restricted-data attribute. Data gove"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#granularity","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"How fine should the groups be?","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Option Strength Cost Use when One group per setting Precise audit and ownership. Dozens of groups to review. High-risk settings such as publish to web, admin API access or external sharing. One group per capability family Matches how decisions are made (for example all extraction paths). Coarser; users in the group get several switches at once. Export and download family, developer tooling family. One group per perso"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#rollout","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Rollout phases","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Rollout in five phases. Snapshot first so every later change can be compared. Pilot by group rather than by switching a setting on for everyone, and keep the review loop alive after the rollout ends. Snapshot Call the Get Tenant Settings API and store the JSON. List capacity, domain and workspace overrides with the companion operations. Record who holds the Fabric administrator role. This is your baseline and your ro"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#tightening","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Tightening a setting that is already open","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Many tenants start with defaults on. Moving a setting from enabled for everyone to specific groups can break workflows, so sequence it. Find real usage Use audit events for the capability (for example ExportReport or ExportArtifact for exports) to list who actually uses it. Seed the allow group Create the allow group from that list, remove clear mismatches, and set an owner. Announce a window Tell users the date thei"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#anti-patterns","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Anti-patterns","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Anti-pattern Why it hurts Instead Enabling everything to unblock a pilot The pilot's risk becomes the tenant's risk and rarely gets reversed. Enable for the pilot group only. One mega-group for all settings Nobody can explain or review why a person has a switch. Capability or persona groups with owners. Groups without owners or expiry Entitlements accumulate and survive role changes. Access packages and periodic acce"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#cadence","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"Review cadence","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Frequency Activity Output Monthly Compare the current snapshot with the last approved baseline. Scan the tenant settings index for new, renamed or retired settings and for preview items that changed status. Drift report and a short list of decisions needed. Quarterly Group membership reviews, exception expiry, review of capacity, domain and workspace overrides, and Copilot and API usage. Signed review and updated exc"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/framework.html#refs","t":"Decision framework and rollout","s":"References","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"About tenant settings Delegate tenant settings Governance and compliance in Microsoft Fabric Learn about groups and access rights in Microsoft Entra ID Create an access package in entitlement management What are access reviews?"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Export and sharing settings","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Playbook 16 · Tenant Settings Export and sharing settings Export, download, link-sharing, guest and external-collaboration settings decide how data leaves the service and who can receive it. They carry the highest exposure in most tenants, so they get the most careful scoping. Portal group Export and sharing settings Default stance Specific groups or disable Pairs with Sensitivity labels, audit"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#egress-map","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"How data leaves the service","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Every export or sharing setting opens a different door. The useful question is not \"is it on\" but \"how much control travels with the data once it is out\". Group the doors by that answer and set each posture accordingly. Egress map. Group paths by how much control travels with the data, then set each setting's posture accordingly. The tenant setting decides whether the path exists; labels, permissions and audit decide"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#export","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Export and download","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"These settings apply to the Fabric and Power BI service. Several of them (Excel, CSV, PowerPoint, PDF) also depend on sensitivity labels being deployed first, because the label is what protects the exported file."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#export-persona","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Recommended allow groups by persona","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"A practical way to apply the cards below is to decide which persona gets which format, then create one allow group per column. Persona Excel .csv PowerPoint, PDF Download reports Analyze in Excel Report consumers Group Block Group Block Block Analysts, self-service authors Group Group Group Group Group Report and model developers Group Group Group Group Group Restricted-data populations Exception Exception Exception "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-copy-paste","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Copy and paste visuals","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Useful for business communication, but it is an uncontrolled copy path. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users copy a visual from a tile or report and paste it as a static image into another application. Risk or benefit The pasted image carries no lineage, label"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-export-excel","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Export to Excel","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — High business value; the label applied to the file is the real control. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users export the data behind a visualization (or a paginated report) to an Excel file. Risk or benefit The file is a static copy that leaves the service. Fab"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-export-csv","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Export to .csv","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Plain-text output with no label enforcement. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users export data from a tile, visualization or paginated report to a .csv file. Risk or benefit CSV is the weakest egress path: sensitivity-label-based access control is not enforced "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-download-reports","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Download reports","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Restrict the full-copy path to the people who build reports. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users download .pbix files and paginated report (.rdl) definitions from the service so they can be edited in Power BI Desktop or Report Builder. Risk or benefit A .pbix"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-excel-live","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Users can work with semantic models in Excel using a live connection","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default On Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Governed live access beats static exports, but start with analysts and widen after training. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users export data to Excel from a report visual or semantic model, or export a semantic model to an Excel workbook with Analyze in Excel, both w"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-export-pptx-pdf","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Export reports as PowerPoint presentations or PDF documents","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Common business need; labels and audit make it manageable for named groups. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. Portal offers delegation to domain admins and workspace admins. What it controls Lets users export a report from the Fabric or Power BI portal to a PowerPoint file (one slide "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-export-mhtml","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Export reports as MHTML documents","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Legacy format with no modern use case. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. Portal offers delegation to domain admins and workspace admins. What it controls Lets users export paginated reports (.rdl) as MHTML, a single-file web archive. It does not apply to standard Power BI reports. Risk or benefit MHTML "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-export-word","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Export reports as Word documents","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Editable output undermines report integrity. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. Portal offers delegation to domain admins and workspace admins. What it controls Lets users export paginated reports as Word documents (.docx). Risk or benefit The output is editable, so recipients can change numbers and layo"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#export-other","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Other export formats and outputs","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"These settings behave like the cards above but see less use. XML, image, MHTML and Word exports apply to paginated reports. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Export reports as XML documents Not stated Disable Lets users export paginated reports as XML. Another uncontrolled, machine-readable copy of report data. Disable unless an integration consumes it. Export reports as image fi"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#links","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Sharing links and Office integration","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"A link is the most common way content spreads inside a tenant. Three scopes exist, and one tenant setting controls the widest. Link scopes. Disable the organization scope for most people and the other two remain. The label guard is a separate setting that stays useful for the groups you do allow."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-shareable-links","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Allow shareable links to grant access to everyone in your organization","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Default to explicit, named access; org-wide links are hard to audit. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Controls whether users can create People in your organization links. When the setting is off, users with share permission can share a report only by link to Specific people or People w"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-ppt-addin","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Enable Power BI add-in for PowerPoint","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default On Typical posture Enable for all — Prefer live, permission-checked embedding to static exports. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets people embed live, interactive Power BI data in PowerPoint. When on, entry points that open a new presentation with the add-in loaded are available in Power BI. It requires the Office admi"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-directquery-pbi","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Allow DirectQuery connections to Power BI semantic models","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default On Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Powerful for developers, a source of dependency sprawl for everyone else. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users use DirectQuery to connect to Azure Analysis Services or Power BI semantic models. When off, users can no longer publish new composite models on Power BI sem"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#email-teams","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Email subscriptions, Teams and promotion","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"These settings shape how people discover and receive content. The two external-recipient settings are the ones with real exposure, because they deliver content to inboxes outside your control. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Users can set up email subscriptions Not stated Enable for all Lets users create e-mail subscriptions to reports and dashboards. Subscriptions deliver cont"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#public","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Publish to web","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Publish to web creates a public embed code that anyone, including search engines, can open without signing in. It is the one setting where a mistake becomes world-readable."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-publish-to-web","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Publish to web","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Public, unauthenticated access cannot be reconciled with a least-privilege model. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets people publish public reports on the web. Published reports need no authentication to view. Admins review and delete embed codes on the Embed codes page, and can set "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#guests","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Guests and external collaboration","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Three different mechanisms are easy to confuse. They use different settings and move data differently, so decide each on its own. Mechanism What it is Controlled by Does data move? B2B guests in your tenant External people are added to your Microsoft Entra directory and given access to items. Guest users can access Microsoft Fabric , Users can invite guest users to collaborate through item sharing and permissions , G"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#s-external-data-sharing","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"External data sharing","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Export and sharing settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Cross-tenant sharing needs a deliberate, partner-by-partner decision. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets specified users share read-only links to data in OneLake (lakehouses and additional Fabric items) with collaborators in other Fabric tenants. The link creates a shortcut in the r"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#endorsement","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"Certification and master data","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"These two settings decide who can mark content as a trusted source. They are trust signals rather than access controls, so scope them to named stewards with a written standard. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Certification Not stated Enable for specific groups Chooses who can certify items such as apps, reports and datamarts as trusted sources. The certifier's contact details s"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/export-sharing.html#refs","t":"Export and sharing settings","s":"References","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Export and sharing tenant settings External data sharing Distribute Power BI content to external guest users with Microsoft Entra B2B Publish to web from Power BI Manage embed codes Sensitivity labels in Power BI Create Excel workbooks with refreshable Power BI data"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Content, models and discovery","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Playbook 16 · Tenant Settings Content, models and discovery Settings that govern how content is created, protected, discovered and distributed: shared semantic models, sensitivity labels, discovery, apps, custom visuals, legacy dataflows and datamarts, and semantic model behavior. Portal groups Workspace, Information protection, Discovery, App, Power BI visuals, Dataflow, Datamart Default stance Enable guardrails, sc"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#cross-workspace","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Shared semantic models across workspaces","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Building reports on a model in another workspace is the backbone of a governed, shared-model approach: one certified model, many reports. Without it every report needs its own copy. Gate chain for cross-workspace models. The tenant setting is the weakest gate in the chain: leaving it on costs little because Build permission and row-level security still decide who sees which rows."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#s-cross-workspace-models","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Use semantic models across workspaces","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Workspace settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for all — It is the foundation of shared, governed models; the real gate is Build permission. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users build a report in one workspace on a semantic model in another. Users still need Build permission on the specific semantic model. Risk or benefit Without it eve"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#models","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Semantic model behavior","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"These settings change how models are edited, published, refreshed and connected. Most are low risk individually; together they define how tightly you control the model lifecycle. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Users can edit semantic models in the Power BI service Not stated Enable for specific groups Lets users edit semantic models in the service. It does not apply to Direct "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#protection","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Information protection and sensitivity labels","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Sensitivity labels from Microsoft Purview Information Protection travel with items and exports. The settings below decide whether people can apply labels and whether labels propagate automatically. Where label enforcement applies. Labels are strongest inside the tenant and on supported exports. Treat the right-hand column as places where policy, DLP and the posture of the related tenant setting must do the work. What"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#label-order","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Suggested rollout order","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Complete the Purview prerequisites Publish labels to users and enable sensitivity labels for Fabric in the Microsoft Purview portal, following the Learn prerequisites. Enable applying labels for a pilot group Use an allow group first and gather feedback on label names and defaults. Turn on propagation Enable downstream inheritance and data-source labels so content is at least as protected as its sources. Lock down ov"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#discovery","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Discovery","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Discovery lets people find content they cannot yet open and request access. They see metadata (name, description, owner) only. That shortens the path to the right data and reduces duplicated work, at the cost of exposing metadata beyond the item's audience."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#s-promoted-discoverable","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Make promoted content discoverable","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Discovery settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Self-service promotion is a weak trust signal for broad discovery. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users who can promote content make it discoverable by people who do not have access to it. They see metadata only (name, description, owner) and must request access. Risk "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#apps","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Apps and template apps","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Apps distribute curated content to consumers. Two feature families use the word \"app\", which is a frequent source of misconfiguration. Feature Purpose Settings Workspace apps and organizational apps Distribute reports and models inside the organization. Push apps to end users , Publish apps to the entire organization , Users can discover and create org apps Template apps Packaged solutions built from a data source, s"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#s-template-org-apps","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Create template organizational apps","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where App settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Needed by solution builders only. Scope and delegation Group scoping is not stated in the reference; check the setting panel. What it controls Lets users create template apps that use semantic models built on one data source in Power BI Desktop. Template apps are meant for distribution as a packaged solution, not for everyday sharing inside the organizat"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#s-push-apps","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Push apps to end users","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where App settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Distribution rights should sit with accountable publishers. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets app publishers install an app automatically for selected end users or groups, with no AppSource installation step by the recipient. Risk or benefit Automatic installation puts content "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#s-publish-apps-org","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Publish apps to the entire organization","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where App settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Organization-wide publication needs a named owner. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Decides who can publish an app to the entire organization rather than to chosen groups. Some documents refer to this setting as pushing apps to the entire organization; the portal name is Publish ap"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#visuals","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Custom visuals","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Custom visuals are JavaScript that runs inside reports. The tenant settings control the service; Power BI Desktop needs matching group policy settings, and the Organizational visuals store bypasses the tenant switches by design. Approved-visual workflow. Pair a strict tenant posture with a fast review route. Without the route, report authors work around the block."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#s-certified-visuals-only","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Add and use certified visuals only (block uncertified)","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Power BI visuals Default Off Typical posture Enable for all — Certification is the cheapest available control over visual code. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls When on, only certified Power BI visuals render in reports and dashboards; uncertified visuals from AppSource or .pbiviz files show an error. Visuals added to the Organizational vi"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#visual-gpo","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Align Power BI Desktop with group policy","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"The tenant settings for visuals apply to the Power BI service. For Power BI Desktop, administrators set matching registry values through group policy under Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power BI Desktop\\ . Each value is 0 to disable and 1 to enable, and Learn documents 1 as the default. Registry value Aligns with tenant setting EnableCustomVisuals Allow visuals created using the Power BI SDK EnableUncertifiedVisuals Ad"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#visual-org","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Organizational visuals store","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Adding a visual to the Organizational visuals page makes it available whether or not it is certified and regardless of the SDK and certified-only switches. Organizational visuals deploy to Power BI Desktop automatically. Use the store as the controlled release channel: review the visual for data flow and licensing, add it, and tell authors. Teams building report standards can find guidance in Power BI Report Developm"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#legacy","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Legacy objects: Gen1 dataflows and datamarts","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Two features have modern replacements. Both should be retired on a plan, because live content depends on them."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#s-gen1-dataflows","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Create and use Gen1 dataflows","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Gen1 dataflow settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Retire legacy dataflows on a plan, not by surprise. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Lets users create and use Gen1 dataflows, the original Power BI self-service ETL (Power Query Online with storage managed by Power BI). The setting applies to the entire organization and c"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#s-datamarts","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Create Datamarts","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Datamart settings Status Preview Labeled preview on Microsoft Learn. Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Retired feature; build on Warehouse instead. Scope and delegation Group scoping is not stated in the reference; check the setting panel. What it controls Lets specified users create datamarts. Learn still lists the setting with a preview label. Risk or benefit Datamarts are superseded by Fabric Ware"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#other","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"Other creation features","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Smaller settings for Q&A, scorecards, insights, R and Python visuals and dashboards. Each has a narrow footprint; the two to question are R and Python visuals (scripts run in the service) and web content on dashboard tiles (a malicious content risk that Learn calls out). Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Review questions Not stated Enable for specific groups Lets semantic model o"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/content-models.html#refs","t":"Content, models and discovery","s":"References","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Workspace tenant settings Information protection tenant settings Sensitivity labels in Power BI Discovery tenant settings Manage Power BI visuals admin settings App tenant settings Template app tenant settings Gen1 dataflow tenant settings Upgrade a datamart to a warehouse Semantic model security tenant settings"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Integration, developer and APIs","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Playbook 16 · Tenant Settings Integration, developer and APIs Settings that open programmatic and tool-based access to data: XMLA endpoints, the Execute Queries API, service principals and admin APIs, maps, single sign-on to third-party sources, OneDrive and SharePoint integration, and streaming authentication. Portal groups Integration, Developer, Admin API Default stance Specific groups, owners named Key risk Non-h"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#xmla","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"XMLA endpoints, Analyze in Excel and Execute Queries","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Three tenant settings decide which tools and APIs can read semantic models directly. They do not replace permissions: each path still needs Build permission on the model, and row-level security applies on every path. Connectivity gates. Read the rows independently. A tenant setting opens the door; the capacity mode and Build permission decide what a connected user can do."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-xmla","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Allow XMLA endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises semantic models","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Integration settings Default On Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Developer tools need it, but it is an open data path. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets client tools connect to semantic models through XMLA endpoints (Excel, SSMS, DAX Studio, Tabular Editor, ALM Toolkit, third-party tools and scripts) and allows Analyze in Excel. Despite the "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#xmla-truth","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"How the two tenant settings combine","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"XMLA setting Excel live-connection setting XMLA client tools Analyze in Excel Off Off Not allowed Not allowed Off On Not allowed Allowed On Off Allowed Not allowed On On Allowed Allowed Both tenant settings are documented as on by default. The capacity-level XMLA endpoint property is a third gate: it is read-only by default, and a capacity admin must set it to read-write before client tools can write to models, deplo"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-execute-queries","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Semantic Model Execute Queries REST API","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Integration settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Valuable for automation, but a data egress channel that needs ownership. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users query semantic models with DAX through the Power BI REST API (executeQueries). Also described in the API reference as the Dataset Execute Queries REST API se"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#service-principals","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Service principals and admin APIs","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Service principals are non-human identities. They are the right way to automate, but each switch below hands a program power over tenant metadata or workspace creation, so these are the settings that most need an owner, a dedicated security group and a credential lifecycle. Service principal automation pattern. Three separate decisions: which group may call which APIs (tenant settings), which workspaces it may touch "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#sp-gates","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Which switch gates which API family","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"API family Gated by Fabric public APIs with a permission model (create, read, update and delete items) Service principals can call Fabric public APIs Create workspace, connection and deployment pipeline , which have no item-level permission model Service principals can create workspaces, connections, and deployment pipelines Read-only admin APIs such as metadata scanning and activity events Service principals can acc"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-sp-public-apis","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Service principals can call Fabric public APIs","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Developer settings Default On — For new customers. Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Automation identities should be enumerated, not implied. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets service principals with suitable roles and item permissions call Fabric public APIs that do create, read, update and delete operations and are protected by a Fabric per"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-sp-create-workspaces","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Service principals can create workspaces, connections, and deployment pipelines","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Developer settings Default Off — For new customers. Typical posture Enable for specific groups — No item-level permission model protects these APIs. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets service principals call the Create Workspace, Create Connection and Create Deployment Pipeline APIs, which are not protected by a Fabric permission model. Risk or benefit B"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-sp-admin-updates","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Service principals can access admin APIs used for updates","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Admin API settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Least-privilege automation identities with a review cycle. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets service principals in allowed security groups call Fabric admin APIs used for updates, such as Workspaces - Restore Workspace. Service principals in allowed groups have full acces"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#sp-practice","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Operating service principals safely","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Create a group per purpose For example SG-FAB-SP-Provisioning and SG-FAB-SP-Governance . List only those groups in the tenant settings. Choose strong credentials Prefer a federated credential or a certificate to a client secret. Record where the credential lives and who rotates it. Keep the app registration clean The app must not hold Power BI permissions that need admin consent when it is used for admin APIs. Grant "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#maps","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Maps and location services","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Map visuals send location data to a mapping service. The decision is who processes it: Microsoft (Azure Maps) or a third party (Esri). Both settings apply to the whole organization."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-arcgis","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Use ArcGIS Maps for Power BI","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Integration settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Third-party processing with no group scoping. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Enables the ArcGIS Maps for Power BI visual provided by Esri. The visual is a third-party service: data used by the visual is sent to Esri, processed under Esri's terms, and may sit outside your t"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-azure-maps-visual","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Users can use the Azure Maps visual","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Integration settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for all — Stays inside Microsoft's boundary; review the two companion data-residency settings. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Lets users create and view reports that use the Azure Maps visual. Microsoft temporarily stores and processes the data for essential services such as "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#sso","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Single sign-on and data sources","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Each single sign-on setting approves sending the signed-in user's token information, including name and e-mail address, to a data source. Leave them off until a source is actually in use and its owner agrees. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes SSO for third-party sources: Dremio SSO, Snowflake SSO, Redshift SSO, Google BigQuery SSO, Enable Denodo SSO, Enable Starburst SSO Not stat"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#onedrive","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"OneDrive and SharePoint","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"These two preview settings move .pbix files into a permission model that Fabric administrators do not control. Both apply to the whole organization."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-onedrive-view","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Users can view Power BI files saved in OneDrive and SharePoint","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Integration settings Status Preview Labeled preview on Microsoft Learn. Default On — Applies to all users when enabled. Typical posture Disable — Moves data into a permission model Fabric admins do not control. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Lets users view .pbix files stored in OneDrive for Business or SharePoint document libraries in t"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-onedrive-share","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Users can share links to Power BI files stored in OneDrive and SharePoint through Power BI Desktop","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Integration settings Status Preview Labeled preview on Microsoft Learn. Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Ungoverned file distribution. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Lets users share links to .pbix files in OneDrive or SharePoint from Power BI Desktop. Sharing uses standard OneDrive and SharePoint functionality and applies to"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#streaming","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Streaming authentication","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":""},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#s-eventstream-key-auth","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Allow non-Entra ID auth in Eventstream","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Integration settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Key-based streaming cannot be attributed or revoked per identity. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Controls whether Eventstream custom endpoints can use key-based authentication alongside Microsoft Entra ID. When off, only Entra ID authentication is allowed. Learn names the "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#other-integration","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"Other integration settings","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Use global search for Power BI Not stated Enable for all Lets users use the global search bar, which relies on external Azure Search features. Respects item permissions. Users can connect to data sources by using Apache Arrow database connectivity (ADBC) Not stated Decide per organization When on, data connections to supported sources use ADBC by"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/integration-api.html#refs","t":"Integration, developer and APIs","s":"References","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Integration tenant settings Developer tenant settings Admin API tenant settings Enable service principal authentication for admin APIs Datasets: Execute Queries Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint ArcGIS Maps for Power BI Get started with Azure Maps Power BI visual"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Playbook 16 · Tenant Settings Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items Settings that control who can create Fabric items and workspaces, how deleted content is recovered, how workspaces connect to Git, how OneLake data is reached from outside Fabric, and how network and encryption controls apply. Portal groups Microsoft Fabric, Workspace, Git integration, OneLake, Advanced networking, Encryption Delegation Capacity and wor"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#creation","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Creating items and workspaces","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Three switches shape sprawl: who may create Fabric items, who may create workspaces, and whether people can move their personal workspace. They interact, and the first one is delegated to capacity admins automatically. Capacity override of Fabric item creation. The tenant selection is a default for capacities, not a hard limit. Decide who may override and monitor the capacity-level overrides."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#s-fabric-items","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Users can create Fabric items","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Microsoft Fabric Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Controls who can create governed data assets at all. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. Automatically delegated to capacity admins. What it controls Lets users use production-ready Fabric features to create Fabric items (lakehouses, warehouses, notebooks and more). Turning it off does not affect "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#s-block-myworkspace","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Block users from reassigning personal workspaces (My Workspace)","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Workspace settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for all — Keeps personal content on the capacity you chose. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Stops My workspace owners from changing the capacity assignment of their personal workspace. By default owners can reassign it, for example back from a Premium or Fabric capacity to share"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#recovery","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Retention and recovery","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Two settings define how long a deleted workspace or item can be restored. Retention is the safety net for mistakes, and also for malicious deletion. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Define workspace retention period Not stated Enable for all Sets how long a deleted collaborative workspace can be restored before permanent deletion. The minimum is 7 days, My workspaces are kept 30"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#git","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Git integration","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Git integration connects a workspace to a repository so item definitions can be committed, reviewed and deployed. Four tenant switches plus two supporting settings decide whether a user can do it. Git setting dependencies. Solid lines are required; dashed lines apply to Fabric item types. Turning the base switch off also stops GitHub because GitHub needs both."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#s-git-ado","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Users can synchronize workspace items with their Git repositories","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Git integration Default On Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Source control is desirable but should follow an approved repository standard. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. Capacity admins and workspace admins (delegation options in the portal). What it controls Lets users sync workspace items with an Azure DevOps Git repository, commit workspace changes and upda"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#s-git-github","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Users can sync workspace items with GitHub repositories","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Git integration Default Off Typical posture Enable for specific groups — Enabled only for groups that follow the approved repository model. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. Capacity admins and workspace admins (delegation options in the portal). What it controls Lets users pick GitHub as their Git provider and sync workspace items with GitHub repositories. Risk or benefit D"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#onelake","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"OneLake access","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"OneLake is where Fabric data lives. Tenant settings decide whether people can reach it with tools outside Fabric; workspace roles, item permissions and OneLake security decide what they can read once they are in. OneLake access gates. Tenant settings decide whether a path is open; roles and OneLake security decide what travels through it. Diagnostics cover the data plane, while the Fabric audit log covers control-pla"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#s-onelake-external","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Users can access data stored in OneLake with apps external to Fabric","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where OneLake settings Default Not stated Typical posture Enable for specific groups — External engines are a legitimate but separately governed access path. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. What it controls Lets users reach OneLake data with applications outside Fabric, such as custom ADLS API apps, OneLake file explorer and Databricks. Internal engines such as Spark, Data Engin"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#s-onelake-file-explorer","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Users can sync data in OneLake with the OneLake File Explorer app","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where OneLake settings Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — File-level sync outside the ingestion pattern. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Lets users run the OneLake File Explorer Windows app to browse OneLake items as folders and drag files in and out, similar to OneDrive. Risk or benefit Users can move files in and out of OneLake from"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#network","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Networking and encryption","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Network controls change how users, gateways and services reach the tenant. They are strong, and they can lock you out. Treat them as projects, not toggles. Network layers. Controls tighten from the outside in. Blocking public access is the highest-impact switch: enable it only after Private Link is proven for every user population and background service."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#s-private-link","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Tenant-level Private Link","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Advanced networking Default Not stated Typical posture Decide per organization — Depends on your network strategy; do not enable without a pilot. Scope and delegation Group scoping is not stated in the reference; check the setting panel. What it controls Lets people use Azure Private Link to reach your Fabric tenant. Someone must complete the setup in Azure; the setting can grant permission to the right person "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#s-block-public","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Block Public Internet Access","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Advanced networking Default Not stated Typical posture Decide per organization — High impact; enable only after a successful private-link pilot. Scope and delegation Group scoping is not stated in the reference; check the setting panel. What it controls Blocks access to your Fabric tenant over the public internet. People without Private Link access cannot connect. It can take 10 to 20 minutes to take effect. Ri"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#network-steps","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Safe sequence","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Read the considerations Review the Private Link considerations and limitations; some features and tools behave differently over private endpoints. Enable tenant-level Private Link Complete the Azure side (private endpoints, name resolution) and test with a pilot subnet and users. Prove every population Test desktop users, Power BI Desktop, gateways, pipelines, notebooks and background services over the private path. "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#workspace-identity","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Workspace identity","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"A workspace identity is a Microsoft Entra service principal that belongs to a workspace, so the workspace can authenticate to data sources without a person's credentials. It is generally available, created by workspace admins, and not available in My workspace. Review. Fabric administrators can see identities under Govern , Configurations , Fabric identities . Limit. The tenant setting Define maximum number of Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#delegation","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Capacity and domain delegation in practice","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Delegation hands a decision to the people closest to the workload. It works best for settings where the right answer differs by workload and where the delegate has context you lack. Setting Delegation Why delegate Users can create Fabric items Delegated to capacity admins automatically. Capacities host different teams; sandbox capacities can allow creation while production capacities stay restricted. Users can use Co"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#domain-assignment","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Domain assignment","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Domains group workspaces for ownership and delegation. One preview setting controls whether administrators can move workspaces between domains. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Allow tenant and domain admins to override workspace assignments Preview Not stated Decide per organization Lets tenant and domain admins reassign workspaces that were previously assigned to another domai"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#workloads-help","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Additional workloads, trial and help","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Additional workloads are partner-built workloads that send user data and access tokens to the publisher, and that sensitivity labels and encryption do not cover. Keep them off until the platform team has approved a publisher. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Workspace admins can add and remove additional workloads Preview Not stated Disable Lets workspace admins add and remove p"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#trial-help","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"Trial, alerts and help","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Users can try Microsoft Fabric paid features Not stated Enable for specific groups Lets users start a Fabric trial to try paid features for a limited period. Trial capacities can create unmanaged data assets; restrict to a sandbox group or disable. All Power BI users can see \"Set alert\" button to create Fabric Activator alerts Not stated Enable f"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/workspace-git-onelake.html#refs","t":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","s":"References","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Enable Microsoft Fabric for your organization Workspace tenant settings Workspace retention Git integration tenant settings OneLake tenant settings OneLake security Advanced networking tenant settings Private links for Fabric tenants Private links for Fabric workspaces Workspace identity Manage your Fabric capacity"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"AI, preview and experimental settings","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Playbook 16 · Tenant Settings AI, preview and experimental settings Settings that govern Copilot and Azure OpenAI features, agents, preview item types, experiments, Microsoft 365 data sharing, and audit and usage reporting. These change fastest, so confirm status before widening access. Portal groups Copilot, Microsoft Fabric, Explore, Audit and usage Default stance Pilot groups, sandbox for preview Verify Status cha"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#copilot","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Copilot and Azure OpenAI","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Copilot features run on Azure OpenAI models and use the signed-in user's permissions to read data. Several independent switches decide whether a user gets them: the tenant setting, capacity settings, region settings and an optional subprocessor setting. Copilot gating. Gates stack: the tenant setting, the capacity, the region and the subprocessor choices are all independent decisions. Copilot never widens a user's it"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#s-copilot-main","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Users can use Copilot and other features powered by Azure OpenAI","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Copilot and Azure OpenAI Service Default On Typical posture Enable for specific groups — On by default, so a deliberate decision is needed. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. Managed at tenant and capacity levels. What it controls Lets users use Copilot and Fabric AI agents and other Azure OpenAI powered features. Data agents depend on this setting. The service may run backgr"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#s-copilot-subprocessor","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Users can use Copilot, AI Agents and other AI experiences powered by OpenAI as a Microsoft Subprocessor","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Copilot and Azure OpenAI Service Default Off Typical posture Disable — Distinct subprocessor terms need a legal decision. Scope and delegation Supports specific security groups and exceptions. Tenant and capacity; if disabled at tenant a capacity cannot enable it. What it controls Lets users use Copilot, agents and other AI experiences powered by OpenAI-operated models, where OpenAI acts as a Microsoft subproce"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#copilot-rollout","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"A rollout approach for Copilot","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Choose the capacity Designate a Copilot capacity (if you enable that setting) or choose an existing one so usage and billing are visible in one place. Create a pilot group Enable the tenant setting for a pilot allow group, and decide in advance what data-handling rules apply to prompts. Check the region settings If your capacity is outside the covered regions, decide whether the cross-geography processing and storage"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#agents","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Agents","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Fabric agents build on the same AI settings. Learn documents no separate tenant setting for data agents: they depend on the Copilot and Azure OpenAI setting and on Users can create Fabric items . The settings below govern what leaves the agent's boundary. Agents can be consumed outside Fabric Users can configure a data agent to be consumed from other services, such as Microsoft Foundry, Microsoft Copilot Studio, Micr"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#ai-services","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Azure AI services and Azure Maps services","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Prebuilt Azure AI services and Azure Maps powered features can run outside the capacity's region when the service is not available there. Both settings are managed at tenant and, for AI services, capacity level. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Allow data processing outside geographic region for Azure AI services Not stated Disable Applies to prebuilt Azure AI services in Fabric"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#m365","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Microsoft 365 integration and privacy","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"These settings decide how much Fabric metadata flows into Microsoft 365 services, and whether Microsoft prompts users for feedback. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Share Fabric data with your Microsoft 365 services On Enabled automatically only when the Fabric and Microsoft 365 tenants are in the same geographic region. Decide per organization Lets Fabric metadata and some cont"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#preview-items","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Preview item types","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Preview item types appear in the Microsoft Fabric tenant settings group. Preview features can change, move or be retired, may not honor every governance control, and often carry separate terms. Keep them off for the organization and enable them for a sandbox group on an isolated capacity. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Users can create Ontology items Preview Not stated Disable"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#rti","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Real-Time Intelligence","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Allow Real-Time Dashboards to include embedded app visuals Not stated Disable Lets Real-Time Dashboards accept embedded app visuals, which are external apps embedded as iframes. Disable unless a vetted app needs it. Eventstream key authentication is covered with the integration settings: Allow non-Entra ID auth in Eventstream . Verify against Mic"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#experimental","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Exploration and experiments","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Two organization-wide settings affect the interface people see rather than data paths. Both are easy to underestimate because they leak into training and support."},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#s-explore","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Users with view permission can launch Explore","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where Explore settings Status Preview Labeled preview on Microsoft Learn. Default On — By default people with View permission can launch Explore. Typical posture Disable — Preview feature that widens ad hoc access for viewers. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Explore is a lightweight visual tool for ad hoc analysis without building a report. Thi"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#s-ux-experiments","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Help Power BI optimize your experience","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Where User experience experiments Default Not stated Typical posture Disable — Consistency for training and support. Scope and delegation Applies to the entire organization only; groups cannot be selected. What it controls Lets the Power BI team show some users minor variations in content, layout and design before they go live for everyone. Different users in one tenant may see different interfaces. Risk or benefit I"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#audit-usage","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"Audit and usage settings","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"These settings decide who can see usage information and where diagnostic data goes. Several expose user names and e-mail addresses, so they are privacy decisions as well as monitoring ones. Setting Default Typical posture What it controls; risk or benefit Notes Usage metrics for content creators Not stated Enable for all Lets content creators see usage metrics for items they have permission to. Useful for retiring un"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/ai-preview-other.html#refs","t":"AI, preview and experimental settings","s":"References","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Copilot and Azure OpenAI tenant settings Overview of Copilot in Fabric OpenAI as a subprocessor in Microsoft Fabric Audit and usage tenant settings Share Fabric data with your Microsoft 365 services Microsoft Fabric tenant settings User experience experiments tenant setting Explore data in the service"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/matrix.html","t":"Quick reference matrix","s":"Quick reference matrix","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Playbook 16 · Tenant Settings Quick reference matrix Every setting covered in this playbook in one place: its portal group, documented default, a recommended starting posture, whether it can be scoped to security groups, and a link to the full entry. Use it for Reviews, audits, onboarding Posture is Recommendation, not a Microsoft default"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/matrix.html#summary","t":"Quick reference matrix","s":"At a glance","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"165 settings covered 33 enable for all 51 enable for specific groups 63 disable 18 decide per organization 12 organization-wide only 29 labeled preview Postures are a starting point for a security-conscious enterprise. Default shows only what the documentation states; a dash means the default is not stated, so capture your tenant's real values with the API snapshot before changing anything. Scope reads Groups when th"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/matrix.html#first-changes","t":"Quick reference matrix","s":"Where to start","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"If your tenant is still at its defaults, these ten changes remove the largest exposures first. Take the snapshot before the first change. # Change Why it comes first 1 Snapshot every setting with the administration API. Gives you a baseline and a rollback for everything below. 2 Publish to web : disable, then delete existing embed codes. Public, unauthenticated, and not retired by group scoping. 3 Organization-wide s"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/matrix.html#matrix-export","t":"Quick reference matrix","s":"Export and sharing","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Setting Portal group Default Typical posture Scope Copy and paste visuals Export and sharing settings — Enable for specific groups Groups Export to Excel Export and sharing settings — Enable for specific groups Groups Export to .csv Export and sharing settings — Enable for specific groups Groups Download reports Export and sharing settings — Enable for specific groups Groups Users can work with semantic models in Exc"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/matrix.html#matrix-content","t":"Quick reference matrix","s":"Content, models and discovery","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Setting Portal group Default Typical posture Scope Use semantic models across workspaces Workspace settings — Enable for all Groups Automatically convert and store reports using Power BI enhanced metadata format (PBIR) Preview Workspace settings On Enable for specific groups — Allow users to apply sensitivity labels for content Information protection — Enable for all Groups Apply sensitivity labels from data sources "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/matrix.html#matrix-integration","t":"Quick reference matrix","s":"Integration, developer and APIs","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Setting Portal group Default Typical posture Scope Allow XMLA endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises semantic models Integration settings On Enable for specific groups Groups Semantic Model Execute Queries REST API Integration settings — Enable for specific groups Groups Users can use the Power BI Model Context Protocol server endpoint Preview Integration settings — Enable for specific groups — Use ArcGIS Ma"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/matrix.html#matrix-platform","t":"Quick reference matrix","s":"Workspaces, Git, OneLake and items","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Setting Portal group Default Typical posture Scope Users can create Fabric items Microsoft Fabric — Enable for specific groups Groups Create workspaces Workspace settings — Enable for specific groups Groups Block users from reassigning personal workspaces (My Workspace) Workspace settings — Enable for all Org only Define workspace retention period Workspace settings — Enable for all — Fabric item recovery Workspace s"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/matrix.html#matrix-ai","t":"Quick reference matrix","s":"AI, preview and experimental","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Setting Portal group Default Typical posture Scope Allow Microsoft Purview to secure AI interactions Information protection — Decide per organization — Users can use Copilot and other features powered by Azure OpenAI Copilot and Azure OpenAI Service On Enable for specific groups Groups Users can use Copilot, AI Agents and other AI experiences powered by OpenAI as a Microsoft Subprocessor Copilot and Azure OpenAI Serv"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/matrix.html#refs","t":"Quick reference matrix","s":"References","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Tenant settings index About tenant settings Tenants: List Tenant Settings (Fabric REST API)"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"Change control and troubleshooting","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Playbook 16 · Tenant Settings Change control and troubleshooting How to change tenant settings safely, detect drift with a snapshot-and-diff job, find who changed what in the audit log, handle exceptions, and trace a \"user cannot do X\" report back to the setting that gates it. Audience Fabric administrators, service desk Includes Python snapshot script, audit event list, troubleshooting table"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html#change-control","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"Change control","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"A tenant setting change affects every user in scope within minutes, and several are hard to notice when they go wrong. A light process is enough: every change has a record, a snapshot and a check. Request The requester names the setting, the capability and the population affected, and the business reason. Assess The Fabric administrator classifies the setting (egress, identity, third-party processing, creation, guard"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html#raci","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"Roles","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Activity Fabric admin Setting owner Security or governance Group owner Capacity or domain admin Decide the posture C A, R C I C Apply the change R A I I I Manage group membership I C I A, R - Review overrides R A C - R Approve an exception C A C R - R is responsible, A accountable, C consulted, I informed. A single person can hold several roles in a small team; the point is that each activity has exactly one accounta"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html#snapshot","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"Snapshot-and-diff","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"The administration API turns tenant settings into data. A scheduled job that stores each result and compares it with the last approved baseline gives you drift detection, an inventory and the evidence for reviews. Snapshot-and-diff pipeline. The job turns tenant settings into reviewable configuration. The API shows the state; the audit log shows who and when for changes that the API exposes as events. Verify against "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html#snapshot-ops","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"Running it","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Store snapshots in Git. One commit per run gives you history, review and rollback evidence. Update the approved baseline through a pull request. Schedule it. Daily is usually enough; run it manually before and after every planned change. Include the overrides. Add calls to the list-overrides operations for capacities, domains and workspaces, and diff those too. Alert on drift. Send a ticket to the setting owner when "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html#audit","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"Audit events","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Changes and data-movement events appear in the Microsoft Purview audit log and in the activity events API. The operation names below are the ones most useful for tenant-setting governance. Operation What it tells you Related setting GetTenantSettingsViaAdminApi Someone or something read tenant settings through the admin API. Admin API access UpdateCapacityTenantSettingDelegation , UpdateDomainTenantSettingDelegation "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html#exceptions","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"Handling exceptions","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Exceptions are normal; untracked exceptions are the problem. Give them a short path and an end date. Request A form captures person or team, capability, justification and the date the need ends. Approve The setting owner approves; the group owner adds the person to the allow group or removes them from the exception group. Time-box Use an access package with expiry so the membership ends without a manual step. Review "},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html#troubleshooting","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"Troubleshooting: from symptom to setting","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"When someone says \"I cannot do X\", work down the flow first, then use the table to find the likely gate. Troubleshooting flow. Work top to bottom. Most cases end at group membership or an override; the last step hands over to item-level checks that tenant settings cannot explain. Symptom Settings to check Also check Export to Excel or .csv is missing or fails Export to Excel , Export to .csv Allow and exception group"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html#checklist","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"Operational checklist","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"A snapshot exists for every planned change, before and after. Every setting in the standard has an owner and a decision record. Allow and exception groups have owners, and membership is reviewed on schedule. Capacity, domain and workspace overrides are listed and reviewed. Service principals for admin APIs are in dedicated groups with rotating credentials. The audit log is searched for export, sharing, publish-to-web"},{"u":"playbooks/16-tenant-settings/operations.html#refs","t":"Change control and troubleshooting","s":"References","p":"Tenant Settings Reference and Decision Guide","x":"Tenants: List Tenant Settings (Fabric REST API) Delegate tenant settings Operation list Track user activities in Microsoft Fabric Enable service principal authentication for admin APIs Create an access package in entitlement management"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Playbook 17 · PR Notifications Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS You cannot make GitHub send an email. What you can do is guarantee that the right people are asked to review every change to the files they own, and that nothing merges until they say yes. Whether that request then reaches an inbox, a mailbox or a phone is decided by settings that belong to the team and to each person. Audience repository admin"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html#problem","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"What this playbook solves","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"In a Fabric repository, a pull request can change a notebook, a pipeline, a semantic model and a deployment parameter file in one go. Each of those has a different owner, and the owner is rarely the person who opened the pull request. Relying on authors to remember whom to tag fails in predictable ways: the reviewer is a friend rather than the owner, the owner learns about the change after it has merged, or nobody is"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html#flow","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"From pull request to notification","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Three layers, three owners. The repository decides who is asked, the team and the person decide how they are told, and the ruleset decides whether the answer matters for the merge."},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html#who-controls","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"Who controls what","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Most confusion about CODEOWNERS notifications comes from expecting one person to control everything. Three different people control three different layers, and each layer can silently stop the chain. The chain is only as strong as its weakest layer. A perfect CODEOWNERS file does nothing for an owner who has turned off both email and On GitHub notifications."},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html#mechanisms","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"Two native mechanisms, two different jobs","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"GitHub has two built-in ways to ask a named person or team for a decision. They are often confused, and the second is not a notification feature at all. CODEOWNERS review requests Environment required reviewers What is asked \"Please review this change to files you own.\" \"Please approve this deployment to an environment.\" When When a pull request that touches owned paths is opened or marked ready. When a workflow job "},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html#concepts","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"Vocabulary","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Term Meaning CODEOWNERS file A text file that maps path patterns to owners. One file applies to one branch. Code owner A user or team named in that file. It must have Write access to the repository. Review request GitHub adds the owner to the pull request's Reviewers list. This is the event that generates a notification. Require review from code owners A ruleset option on the pull-request rule that makes an owner's a"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html#prerequisites","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"Before you start","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"A GitHub organization with teams that mirror ownership, ideally synced from Entra ID groups, as described in the identity section of the GitHub integration playbook. Every owner team has Write access to the repository, and is visible to the people who will open pull requests. A ruleset on main that requires pull requests, so that a pull request is the only way a change lands. See rulesets . A repository layout where "},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html#page-map","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"How the pages fit together","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"02 Creating the CODEOWNERS file Locations, syntax, why order matters, owners and teams, protecting and validating the file. Procedure 10 min 03 Fabric folders and enforcement A worked file for a Fabric repository and enforcing approval with a ruleset. Worked example 12 min 04 Notifications and troubleshooting Team and personal settings, the pull request UI, and what to check when nobody is notified. Operations 12 min"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html#related","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"How this fits with the other playbooks","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Playbook Relationship 05 · GitHub Integration and Branching Defines the repository layout, the ruleset on main and the pull-request workflow that this playbook plugs into. 05 · The pull-request process Shows what reviewers look for in a Fabric diff, which is what the owners you define here will be doing. 07 · Deployment Pipelines and Promotion Takes over after the merge. Owners of deployment files in this playbook de"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/index.html#refs","t":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","s":"References","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"About code owners Available rules for rulesets Managing code review settings for your team Configuring notifications Managing environments for deployment"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Playbook 17 · PR Notifications Creating the CODEOWNERS file The file is a dozen lines of text, and almost every way it goes wrong is silent: a pattern that matches nothing, an owner without Write access, a second owner on a second line. Nothing errors out; nobody is simply asked. This page covers the rules that matter. Audience repository admins, platform leads Use it for writing, placing and validating the file"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html#location","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"Where the file lives","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"GitHub looks for a file named CODEOWNERS (all capitals, no extension) in three places, in this order, and uses the first one it finds: Use .github/ . It keeps the file next to workflows and the pull-request template, and it is the one place you can protect together with them. One file, one branch. A CODEOWNERS file assigns owners for the branch it sits on. Review requests are generated from the copy on the base branc"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html#create","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"Creating it","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Start a branch. Because main is protected by a ruleset, open the repository and create a branch, for example chore/1301-add-codeowners , rather than committing to main . Create the file. Choose Add file , then Create new file . In the name box type .github/CODEOWNERS ; typing the slash creates the folder. Use capitals and no extension. Write the rules. Start with the catch-all and add specific lines below it (next se"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html#syntax","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"Syntax","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Each line is a path pattern followed by one or more owners. The patterns follow most of the .gitignore rules; # starts a comment. Pattern Matches * Every file, unless a later line also matches. The usual catch-all. *.sql Every file with that extension, at any depth. /docs/ The docs directory at the repository root and everything inside it, including subdirectories. docs/* Files directly in docs , but not files in its"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html#ordering","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"Order matters: the last match wins","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"For each changed file GitHub reads the file from top to bottom and uses the owners of the last line that matches . Earlier matches are discarded, not added. That is what lets you write a broad default and refine it, and it is also the usual reason a pull request goes to the wrong team. Go from general to specific. Put the catch-all first and the most specific rules last. If a later line is meant to add an owner rathe"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html#owners","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"Owners: users, teams and email","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Form Example Notes Team @contoso-org/data-engineering Recommended. Membership changes without editing the file. The team must be visible and have Write access to the repository, even if every member already has access some other way. User @example-user Must have Write access. Use sparingly: when the person leaves, the line silently stops working. Email user@contoso.com Resolves to the account that has that address. N"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html#same-line","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"Several owners belong on one line","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Owners listed on the same line share the pattern, and an approval from any one of them satisfies the requirement. Owners on separate lines do not combine: the last line replaces the earlier ones. A silent failure that is easy to copy A common first draft lists the members of the owning team one per line under a single * . Only the last person is then the owner, and everyone else is never asked. Put the people in a te"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html#protect-file","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"Protect the file itself","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Whoever controls CODEOWNERS controls who must review everything else. If a developer can change the file and approve their own change, the whole mechanism is optional. Close the loop by owning the file, and the folder that holds your workflows: With the code-owner rule enabled, a pull request that edits CODEOWNERS or a workflow now needs the platform team's approval, exactly as it should."},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html#validate","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"Validate before you rely on it","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Open the file on GitHub. Invalid lines are highlighted with an explanation. Browse to a file you expect to be owned and hover over the shield icon in its header. The tooltip names the owner and the line of CODEOWNERS it came from. List the errors programmatically if you manage many repositories: the REST API has an endpoint that lists CODEOWNERS errors for a repository. Open a test pull request that touches one path "},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/codeowners-file.html#refs","t":"Creating the CODEOWNERS file","s":"References","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"About code owners gitignore pattern format Managing team access to an organization repository Creating new files"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/fabric-example.html","t":"Fabric folders and enforcement","s":"Fabric folders and enforcement","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Playbook 17 · PR Notifications Fabric folders and enforcement A Fabric repository has a natural ownership map: one folder per workspace, and inside each folder one subfolder per item. This page turns that map into a working CODEOWNERS file, shows what a real pull request does with it, and makes the owners' approval mandatory. Audience repository admins, workspace and domain leads Use it for a ready-to-adapt file and "},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/fabric-example.html#scenario","t":"Fabric folders and enforcement","s":"The example repository","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"The repository follows the layout recommended in the GitHub integration playbook : one folder per workspace, named like the workspace without its environment prefix, plus a few folders that are not connected to any workspace. Ownership follows the people who answer for each layer. Team Owns Why @contoso-org/data-engineering Bronze and silver workspaces, and the tests They know the ingestion and cleansing logic and wh"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/fabric-example.html#file","t":"Fabric folders and enforcement","s":"The CODEOWNERS file","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Line order is the design. parameter.yml comes after the folder lines, so a parameter file in SALES_GLD/ goes to the platform team and not to analytics engineering. One line per team per path. Where two teams should share a folder, put both on the same line. Matching an item folder. A Fabric item is a folder named <display name>.<Type> , for example NB_SALES_GLD_ChurnScore.Notebook . A pattern such as *.Notebook/ matc"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/fabric-example.html#walkthrough","t":"Fabric folders and enforcement","s":"What a real pull request does","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"A developer's pull request changes a notebook in the gold folder, a semantic model definition, the gold parameter file and a runbook: Changed file Matching lines Owner requested SALES_GLD/NB_SALES_GLD_ChurnScore.Notebook/notebook-content.py * , /SALES_GLD/ analytics-engineering (last match) SALES_CNSM_SM/SM_SALES_CNSM_Sales.SemanticModel/definition/model.tmdl * , /SALES_CNSM_SM/ bi-developers SALES_GLD/parameter.yml "},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/fabric-example.html#axis","t":"Fabric folders and enforcement","s":"Folders or item types?","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"You can organise ownership by where an item lives, as above, or by what kind of item it is. Pick one as the main axis, because the last-match rule means the two do not combine. By workspace folder By item type Example lines /SALES_GLD/ *.Notebook/ , *.SemanticModel/ , *.Report/ Fits when Teams are organised by data domain or layer, which matches the workspace layout. Teams are organised by skill: engineers for code, "},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/fabric-example.html#enforce","t":"Fabric folders and enforcement","s":"Making owner approval mandatory","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Out of the box, a CODEOWNERS review request is only a request: the owner is asked, but the merge does not wait for them. To make the approval a requirement, turn on one option in the ruleset that already protects main . One checkbox separates a suggestion from a control. Everything on the previous pages only routes the request; this setting is what stops a merge without it. Merge the CODEOWNERS file first. The option"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/fabric-example.html#both-teams","t":"Fabric folders and enforcement","s":"When two teams must both approve","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"CODEOWNERS answers \"who is responsible for these files\", and a single line cannot demand two independent approvals. If a path needs sign-off from two specific teams, for example gold changes that need both analytics engineering and platform, use the ruleset's own required reviewers rule, either instead of or alongside CODEOWNERS. CODEOWNERS with the code-owner option Ruleset required reviewers Defined in A file in th"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/fabric-example.html#maintain","t":"Fabric folders and enforcement","s":"Keeping it true","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Add a line when you add a workspace and its folder, in the same pull request, with its owning team already granted Write access. When a team is renamed, merged or retired, update the file before the old name stops resolving. Review the file with the quarterly access review described in the operations page : every team still exists, still has Write access and still owns what it should. Treat a pull request that change"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/fabric-example.html#refs","t":"Fabric folders and enforcement","s":"References","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"About code owners Available rules for rulesets Creating rulesets for a repository Git integration source code format Basic concepts in Git integration"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/notifications.html","t":"Notifications and troubleshooting","s":"Notifications and troubleshooting","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Playbook 17 · PR Notifications Notifications and troubleshooting Once GitHub has asked an owner to review, the message is no longer yours to shape. It passes through the team's code review settings and then through the person's own notification settings. Knowing both layers is the difference between \"it should have worked\" and a fix. Audience repository admins, team maintainers, reviewers Use it for routing, personal"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/notifications.html#request-vs-email","t":"Notifications and troubleshooting","s":"A review request is not an email","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"When a pull request is opened, or moved out of draft, GitHub adds each matching owner to the pull request's Reviewers list. That is the review request. GitHub's notification system then takes the request and delivers it to the people concerned, on the channels they have enabled. An email is one possible result, not a guaranteed one. You, as repository admin You cannot Decide who is requested, with the CODEOWNERS file"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/notifications.html#team-routing","t":"Notifications and troubleshooting","s":"Team settings: who in the team is told","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"When the owner on a CODEOWNERS line is a team , the request goes to the team, and the team's own Code review settings decide how many of its members are told. There are three behaviours: Team settings narrow the audience. They cannot widen it past what each person has switched on for themselves. Setting Effect Only notify requested team members If someone from the team is also requested by name on the pull request, o"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/notifications.html#personal","t":"Notifications and troubleshooting","s":"Personal settings: how each owner is reached","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"The last layer belongs to each person, and no administrator can change it for them. Every code owner should check the following once. Open the settings. Select the notifications inbox icon, then Manage notifications , then Notification settings . Enable both channels. For Participating and for Watching , select both Email and On GitHub . The notifications inbox only works when both are enabled. Choose a verified addr"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/notifications.html#pr-ui","t":"Notifications and troubleshooting","s":"What the pull request page shows","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Where What you see Reviewers list, right sidebar The requested owners and teams, each with a status icon. A pending review shows a yellow indicator; an approval or requested changes replaces it once the person responds. Merge box The ruleset's verdict. With the code-owner option on, it reports that a review is required until an owner approves. A file's header Hover over the shield icon to see who owns the file and wh"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/notifications.html#environments","t":"Notifications and troubleshooting","s":"Deployment approvals are a different notification","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Environment required reviewers receive a request when a deployment job is waiting for approval, not when code is merged. They can be notified by email, in the inbox, or on GitHub Mobile, which supports alerts for deployment approval requests. If you want people to hear about every merge to main , use a repository watch setting with pull requests selected, or a workflow step that posts to a chat channel. See environme"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/notifications.html#troubleshooting","t":"Notifications and troubleshooting","s":"Troubleshooting: nobody was notified","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Work through the layers in order. Each question rules out one layer. The first \"no\" is the layer to fix. Check the pull request's Reviewers list before anything else: if the owner is not on it, no notification setting can help. Symptom Likely cause What to do Owner is not on the Reviewers list The file is not on the base branch; the path pattern does not match (case, renamed folder); the team or user lacks Write acce"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/notifications.html#checklist","t":"Notifications and troubleshooting","s":"Go-live checklist","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"The CODEOWNERS file is in .github/ on main , with the catch-all first and specific lines last. Every owner is a team, every team has Write access, and every team is visible to contributors. A test pull request touching one path per rule produced exactly the expected Reviewers list. Require review from Code Owners is enabled in the ruleset for main , together with stale-approval dismissal. /.github/ is owned by the pl"},{"u":"playbooks/17-pr-notifications-codeowners/notifications.html#refs","t":"Notifications and troubleshooting","s":"References","p":"Pull-Request Notifications with CODEOWNERS","x":"Managing code review settings for your team Configuring notifications About notifications Email notification headers About code owners Managing environments for deployment"}];
